    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "1.3.3 Sketch parametric equation"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "To sketch the parametric equation in MAPLE. We use MAPLE “plot” as discussed in the following example."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Example 5"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Sketch the graph of parametric equation \\(x=\\cos(t)\\) and \\(x=\\sin(t)\\)."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Where \\(-4\\leq t\\leq4\\), x = −2 to 2 and y = −2 to 2"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Solution"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "(a) Command"
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": "plot([cos(t),sin(t),t=-4..4],-2..2,-2..2);",
        "result": null,
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "diagram",
        "id": "math12-source-figure-1-15",
        "title": "Parametric circle given by the printed cosine and sine coordinate pair",
        "caption": "Figure 1.15",
        "diagram": {
          "title": "Figure 1.15",
          "type": "unit-circle",
          "xMin": -2,
          "xMax": 2,
          "yMin": -2,
          "yMax": 2,
          "xTicks": [
            -2,
            -1,
            0,
            1,
            2
          ],
          "yTicks": [
            -2,
            -1,
            1,
            2
          ],
          "colour": "#075985"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "(b) Context menu result"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Enter an expression or function, right click on it and select plots < plot builder < 2-D parametric plot and domain for the parametric t."
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": "cos(t), sin(t)",
        "result": null,
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": "plot([cos(t),sin(t),t=-4..4]);",
        "result": null,
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "figure",
        "src": "assets/math12/figure-1-16.jpg",
        "caption": "Figure 1.16",
        "alt": "Original partial-circle plot shown for the context-menu parametric example"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "1.3.4 Know plotting options"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The plotting options listed below can provide the commands that create 2-D plots. These options can be used with the “plot” command and are generally available to all Maple commands that generate two dimensional plots. The help page of Maple for a particular command provides more detail about the plotting options that accepts."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Options must be added at the end of the given sequence."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "We can use an interactive method of exploring the options in the “plot” command by using the Interactive Plot Builder."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Adaptive"
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": "adaptive = n or true/false",
        "result": null,
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "When we are plotting a function over an interval. The interval is sampled at a number of points, controlled by “sample” and “numpoints”. Adaptive plotting, where necessary, subdivides these intervals to attempt to get a better representation of the function. This option is subsampling can be turned off by setting the “adaptive” option to false. By default, this option is true and intervals are subdivided at most six times in trying to improve the plot. By setting this option to a non-negative integer, we can control the maximum number of six times the subintervals are divided."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Annotation"
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": "annotation = t",
        "result": null,
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The annotation option allows us to add descriptive text to a 2-D curve or point plot. The term \"point plot\" in this context means a collection of points that are treated as a single plot element, such that"
      }
    ],
    "sourceDefects": [],
    "visualStatus": "Figure 1.15 digitally recreated; Figure 1.16 retained as a cropped original pending exact vector reconstruction",
    "sourceNotes": [
      "Example 5 prints x=cos(t) and x=sin(t); the intended second coordinate is y=sin(t). Original question preserved; explanation separately labelled.",
      "Figure 1.16 shows partial arcs rather than the full curve determined by the printed parameter interval; preserve the scan rather than invent endpoints."
    ]
