    "file": "027_page_1.jpg",
    "page": 45,
    "title": "FUNCTIONS AND LIMITS",
    "paragraphs": [],
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "2.6 Limit of Function"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The algebraic problems considered in earlier sections dealt with static situations:"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "What is the revenue when x items are sold?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "How much interest is earned in 2 years?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Calculus, on the other hand, deals with dynamic situations:"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "At what rate is the economy growing?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "How fast is a rocket going at any instant after lift-off?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The techniques of calculus will allow us to answer many questions like these that deal with rates of change."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The key idea underlying the development of calculus is the concept of limit. So we begin by studying limits after explaining the location of intervals on the real number line."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "2.6.1 Identification of a real number by a point on the number line"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The various types of numbers used in this book can be illustrated with a diagram called a number line. Each real number corresponds to exactly one point on the line and vice-versa. A number line with several sample numbers located on it is shown in Figure 2.31:"
      },
      {
        "type": "diagram",
        "id": "math12-source-figure-2-31",
        "caption": "Figure 2.31",
        "diagram": {
          "type": "source-number-line",
          "title": "Figure 2.31",
          "xMin": -5,
          "xMax": 5,
          "ticks": [
            -4,
            -3,
            -2,
            -1,
            0,
            1,
            2,
            3,
            4
          ],
          "points": [
            {
              "x": -0.6666666666666666,
              "numerator": "−2",
              "denominator": "3"
            },
            {
              "x": 0.5,
              "numerator": "1",
              "denominator": "2"
            },
            {
              "x": 1.4142135623730951,
              "label": "√2"
            },
            {
              "x": 3.141592653589793,
              "label": "π"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "2.6.1 Representation of open interval, closed interval, half open and half closed intervals on the number line"
      },
      {
        "type": "clarification",
        "text": "The source prints 2.6.1 for both subsection headings on this page. Both printed labels are retained; the next heading on page 46 is 2.6.3."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "“A set that consists of all the real numbers between two points is called an interval.”"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A special notation will be used to indicate an interval on the real number line."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "For example, the interval including all numbers x, where −2 < x < 3 is written as (−2, 3). The parentheses indicate that the number −2 and 3 are not included."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "If −2 and 3 are to be included in the interval, square brackets are used, as in [−2, 3]."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The chart below shows several typical intervals, where a < b."
      },
      {
        "type": "dataTable",
        "headers": [
          "Inequality",
          "Interval Notation",
          "Explanation"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "a ≤ x ≤ b",
            "[a,b]: Closed",
            "Both a and b are included."
          ],
          [
            "a ≤ x < b",
            "[a,b): Half open/Closed",
            "a is included, b is not."
          ],
          [
            "a < x ≤ b",
            "(a,b]: Half Open/Closed",
            "b is included, a is not."
          ],
          [
            "a < x < b",
            "(a,b): Open",
            "Both a and b are not included."
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Interval notation is also used to describe sets such as the set of all numbers x, with x ≥ −2. This interval is written [−2, ∞)."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Example 24"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Represent the following intervals on number line.\n(a) [−2, ∞)\n(b) [4, ∞)\n(c) [−2, 1]"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Solution"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "a. Start at −2 and draw a heavy line to the right, as in graph: Use a solid hole at −2 to show that −2 is itself a part of the graph."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The symbol ∞, read “infinity” does not represent a number. It simply indicates that all numbers greater than −2 are in the interval. Similarly, the notation (−∞, 2) indicates the set of all real numbers with x < 2."
      }
    ],
