    "paragraphs": [],
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Example 37"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Discuss the continuity of \\(f(x)=x^3-2x^2-3x+5\\) at x = 1."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Solution"
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": "a. For x = 1",
        "result": "\\(f(1)=(1)^3-2(1)^2-3(1)+5=1-2-3+5=1\\)",
        "number": null,
        "prompt": false
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": "b.",
        "result": "\\(\\lim_{x\\to1^-}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to1^-}(x^3-2x^2-3x+5)=(1)^3-2(1)^2-3(1)+5=1\\)",
        "number": null,
        "prompt": false
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(\\lim_{x\\to1^+}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to1^+}(x^3-2x^2-3x+5)=(1)^3-2(1)^2-3(1)+5=1-2-3+5=1\\)",
        "number": null,
        "prompt": false
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": "c.",
        "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\quad\\lim_{x\\to1}f(x)=f(1)\\)",
        "number": null,
        "prompt": false
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "∴ f(x) is continuous at x = 1."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Remember"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A function is said to be a discontinuous function at a point ‘c’ if one of the three conditions of continuity does not satisfy."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Now, look at the following graph of function."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "It can be seen that the side limits consider with the value of the function with the point."
      },
      {
        "type": "clarification",
        "text": "No graph accompanies Example 37 on this source page. The polynomial value and both one-sided limits at 1 are all 1, proving continuity there."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "ii. Continuity of a function in an interval"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "“A function is said to be a continuous function in an interval when the function is defined at every point in that interval and no jumps or breaks involve.”"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "If some functions f(x) satisfies these criteria from x = a to x = b and we say that f(x) is continuous on the interval [a,b]."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Note"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "f(x) is continuous over the closed interval [a, b] if it is continuous on the (a, b) interval."
      },
      {
        "type": "clarification",
        "text": "For continuity on [a, b], continuity at every interior point is required, together with limₓ→a⁺ f(x) = f(a) and limₓ→b⁻ f(x) = f(b). Interior continuity alone does not establish continuity on the closed interval; both endpoint values must be defined and match the appropriate one-sided limits."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "2.8.3 Test of continuity and discontinuity of a function at a point and in an interval"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Example 38"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Discuss the continuity of \\(f(x)=\\frac{x^2-4}{x+2}\\), at x = −2."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Solution"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In order to check the continuity of the function f(x) at x = −2. We will have to check the function for all three conditions as we have done in Example 37."
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(f(x)=\\frac{x^2-4}{x+2}\\)",
        "number": null,
        "prompt": false
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(f(-2)=\\frac{(-2)^2-4}{(-2)+2}=\\frac{4-4}{-2+2}=\\frac{0}{0}=\\text{Undefined}\\)",
        "number": null,
        "prompt": false
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Hence, f(−2) is not defined. We know that if any of the three conditions of continuity does not satisfy, the function will discontinuous."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Therefore, f(x) is discontinuous function at x = −2."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "However, if we try to find the limit of f(x), we conclude that f(x) is continuous on all the values other than −2."
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(\\lim_{x\\to-2}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to-2}\\frac{x^2-4}{x+2}=\\lim_{x\\to-2}(x-2)=-4\\)",
        "number": null,
        "prompt": false
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "This implies that f(x) is continuous at all the values of x other than −2."
      },
      {
        "type": "clarification",
        "text": "The displayed 0/0 is an undefined expression, not a number. For x ≠ −2, factoring and cancelling gives f(x) = x − 2. Thus the limit at −2 exists and equals −4, but the original function has no value there. Its discontinuity is removable: defining f(−2) = −4 would make the extended function continuous."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Example 39 — continues on the next page"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "First-class postage in 1995 was $0.32 for the first ounce and $0.23 for each additional ounce up to 11 ounces. If p(x) is the amount of postage for a letter weighing in x ounces, then we write:"
      },
      {
        "type": "clarification",
        "text": "The piecewise postage model and its graph continue on printed page 59, transcribed below. These rates are the historical values stated in the textbook example."
      }
    ],
