    "page": 93,
    "title": "DIFFERENTIATION",
    "paragraphs": [],
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "3.6.3 Use of logarithmic differentiation to algebraic expressions involving product, quotient and power"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Logarithmic differentiation is a procedure in which logarithms are used to trade the task of differentiating products and quotients for that of differentiating sums and differences. It is especially valuable as a means for handling complicated product or quotient functions and power functions where variables appear in both the base and the exponent."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Example 20"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Differentiate the following functions: (a) \\(y=\\ln\\left[\\frac{x(x^2-3)^2}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}}\\right]\\); (b) \\(y=x^{\\sin x}\\)."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Solution"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "a. \\(y=\\ln\\left[\\frac{x(x^2-3)^2}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}}\\right]\\). If the given function after simplification is:"
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(y=\\ln\\left[\\frac{x(x^2-3)^2}{(x^2-4)^{1/2}}\\right]=\\ln[x(x^2-3)^2]-\\ln[(x^2-4)^{1/2}]\\)",
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "logarithms rules"
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(=\\ln x+\\ln(x^2-3)^2-\\ln(x^2-4)^{1/2}=\\ln x+2\\ln(x^2-3)-\\frac12\\ln(x^2-4)\\)",
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The derivative of y w.r.t. x is:"
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\ln x+2\\ln(x^2-3)-\\frac12\\ln(x^2-4)\\right]=\\frac{d}{dx}[\\ln x]+2\\frac{d}{dx}[\\ln(x^2-3)]-\\frac12\\frac{d}{dx}[\\ln(x^2-4)]\\)",
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(=\\frac1x+2\\frac1{x^2-3}(2x)-\\frac12\\frac1{x^2-4}(2x)=\\frac{4x^4-20x^2+12}{x(x^2-3)(x^2-4)}=\\frac{4(x^4-5x^2+3)}{x(x^2-3)(x+2)(x-2)}\\)",
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "b. \\(y=x^{\\sin x}\\). Taking ln of both sides:"
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(\\ln y=\\ln(x^{\\sin x}),\\quad\\ln y=\\sin x\\ln x\\)",
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Then on differentiation w.r.t. x it becomes:"
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y)=\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x\\ln x)\\)",
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(\\frac1y\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\sin x\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln x)+\\ln x\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)\\)",
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(\\frac1y\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\sin x}x+\\ln x\\cos x\\)",
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "command",
        "text": null,
        "result": "\\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=y\\left[\\frac{\\sin x}x+\\ln x\\cos x\\right]=x^{\\sin x}\\left[\\frac{\\sin x}x+\\ln x\\cos x\\right]\\)",
        "number": null
      },
      {
        "type": "clarification",
        "text": "For part (a), the denominator requires |x| > 2 and the logarithm argument must be positive. Since (x² − 3)² and the denominator are positive there, its sign is the sign of x, giving the original real domain x > 2. All separated logarithms are valid on that domain. For part (b), use x > 0 to define the differentiable real power x^(sin x) = exp(sin x ln x)."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "3.7 Differentiation of Hyperbolic and Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The concept of hyperbolic functions is completely discussed in Unit-2. The differentiation of hyperbolic functions can be found as follows:"
      }
    ],
