const math12Exercise23Revision=[{"id": "ex-2-3-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Evaluate the following limits:\n(a) \\(\\lim_{x\\to4}(\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-5})\\)\n(b) \\(\\lim_{x\\to1}(\\frac{x^2+3x+2}{x^2+x+2})^2\\)\n(c) \\(\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1}\\)\n(d) \\(\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}-1}{x-1}\\)\n(e) \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{1-\\sin x}{\\cos^2x}\\)\n(f) \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\tan x}{x}\\)\n(g) \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sec x-1}{x\\sec x}\\)\n(h) \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin^2(7x)}{7x}\\)", "steps": ["(a) Both denominators are nonzero at x = 4. Substitute: \\(3/4+1/(4-5)=3/4-1=-1/4\\).", "(b) The denominator at 1 is 4. Substitute and square: \\(((1+3+2)/(1+1+2))^2=(6/4)^2=9/4\\).", "(c) Multiply by the conjugate. For x ≠ 1, \\((\\sqrt{x}-1)/(x-1)=1/(\\sqrt{x}+1)\\), whose limit is 1/2.", "(d) Combine the numerator: \\((1/x-1)/(x-1)=(1-x)/(x(x-1))=-1/x\\) for x ≠ 1. Its limit is −1.", "(e) Direct substitution is valid since cos²0 = 1. The limit is (1 − 0)/1 = 1.", "(f) Write \\(\\tan x/x=(\\sin x/x)/\\cos x\\). In radians, the two factors tend to 1 and 1, so the quotient tends to 1.", "(g) For x ≠ 0 near 0, \\((\\sec x-1)/(x\\sec x)=(1-\\cos x)/x\\). Rationalize with 1 + cos x to get \\((\\sin x/x)\\sin x/(1+\\cos x)\\), which tends to 0.", "(h) Write \\(\\sin^2(7x)/(7x)=[\\sin(7x)/(7x)]\\sin(7x)\\). The factors tend to 1 and 0, so the limit is 0."], "solution": "(a) Both denominators are nonzero at x = 4. Substitute: \\(3/4+1/(4-5)=3/4-1=-1/4\\).\n(b) The denominator at 1 is 4. Substitute and square: \\(((1+3+2)/(1+1+2))^2=(6/4)^2=9/4\\).\n(c) Multiply by the conjugate. For x ≠ 1, \\((\\sqrt{x}-1)/(x-1)=1/(\\sqrt{x}+1)\\), whose limit is 1/2.\n(d) Combine the numerator: \\((1/x-1)/(x-1)=(1-x)/(x(x-1))=-1/x\\) for x ≠ 1. Its limit is −1.\n(e) Direct substitution is valid since cos²0 = 1. The limit is (1 − 0)/1 = 1.\n(f) Write \\(\\tan x/x=(\\sin x/x)/\\cos x\\). In radians, the two factors tend to 1 and 1, so the quotient tends to 1.\n(g) For x ≠ 0 near 0, \\((\\sec x-1)/(x\\sec x)=(1-\\cos x)/x\\). Rationalize with 1 + cos x to get \\((\\sin x/x)\\sin x/(1+\\cos x)\\), which tends to 0.\n(h) Write \\(\\sin^2(7x)/(7x)=[\\sin(7x)/(7x)]\\sin(7x)\\). The factors tend to 1 and 0, so the limit is 0.", "answer": "(a) −1/4; (b) 9/4; (c) 1/2; (d) −1; (e) 1; (f) 1; (g) 0; (h) 0.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 56, "jpg": "032_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF; project coefficient uncertainty separately recorded"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.3 Question 1", "verification": "visually inspected; discrepancies stated separately"}}, {"id": "ex-2-3-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Use algebra and the rules of limits to evaluate the following limits:\n(a) \\(\\lim_{x\\to4}\\frac{-6}{(x-4)^2}\\)\n(b) \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{x+3}-\\frac{1}{3}}{x}\\)\n(c) \\(\\lim_{x\\to5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{5}}{x-5}\\)", "steps": ["(a) For x ≠ 4 the squared denominator is positive, and tends to 0 from both sides. Therefore −6/(x − 4)² tends to −∞; it has no finite real limit.", "(b) Combine fractions: \\((1/(x+3)-1/3)/x=[-x/(3(x+3))]/x=-1/(3(x+3))\\) for x ≠ 0. Let x → 0 to obtain −1/9.", "(c) Rationalize: \\((\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{5})/(x-5)=1/(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{5})\\) for x ≠ 5. Its limit is \\(1/(2\\sqrt{5})\\)."], "solution": "(a) For x ≠ 4 the squared denominator is positive, and tends to 0 from both sides. Therefore −6/(x − 4)² tends to −∞; it has no finite real limit.\n(b) Combine fractions: \\((1/(x+3)-1/3)/x=[-x/(3(x+3))]/x=-1/(3(x+3))\\) for x ≠ 0. Let x → 0 to obtain −1/9.\n(c) Rationalize: \\((\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{5})/(x-5)=1/(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{5})\\) for x ≠ 5. Its limit is \\(1/(2\\sqrt{5})\\).", "answer": "(a) −∞, no finite limit; (b) −1/9; (c) 1/(2√5).", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 56, "jpg": "032_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF; project coefficient uncertainty separately recorded"}], "sourceClarification": "The key says “does not exist” for (a), meaning no finite real limit. The learning solution records the more precise divergence to −∞.", "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.3 Question 2", "verification": "visually inspected; discrepancies stated separately"}}, {"id": "ex-2-3-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Find the limit of the convergent of the following sequences:\n(a) \\(\\{\\frac{5n}{n+7}\\}\\)\n(b) \\(\\{\\frac{4-7n}{8+n}\\}\\)\n(c) \\(\\{\\frac{(-1)^n}{n^2}\\}\\)", "steps": ["(a) Divide by n: \\(5n/(n+7)=5/(1+7/n)\\). Since 7/n → 0, the limit is 5.", "(b) Divide by n: \\((4-7n)/(8+n)=(4/n-7)/(8/n+1)\\). The limit is −7.", "(c) The terms alternate in sign, but \\(-1/n^2\\leq(-1)^n/n^2\\leq1/n^2\\). Both bounds tend to 0; the sandwich theorem gives limit 0."], "solution": "(a) Divide by n: \\(5n/(n+7)=5/(1+7/n)\\). Since 7/n → 0, the limit is 5.\n(b) Divide by n: \\((4-7n)/(8+n)=(4/n-7)/(8/n+1)\\). The limit is −7.\n(c) The terms alternate in sign, but \\(-1/n^2\\leq(-1)^n/n^2\\leq1/n^2\\). Both bounds tend to 0; the sandwich theorem gives limit 0.", "answer": "(a) 5; (b) −7; (c) 0.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 56, "jpg": "032_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF; project coefficient uncertainty separately recorded"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.3 Question 3", "verification": "visually inspected; discrepancies stated separately"}}, {"id": "ex-2-3-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "Weekly sales (in rupees) at big store x weeks after the end of an advertising campaign are given by: \\(S(x)=5000+\\frac{3600}{x+2}\\). Find the sale for indicated weeks limits:\n(a) S(5). (b) \\(\\lim_{x\\to5}S(x)\\). (c) \\(\\lim_{x\\to16}S(x)\\).", "steps": ["(a) Substitute week 5: \\(S(5)=5000+3600/7=38600/7\\), approximately Rs 5514.29.", "(b) S is continuous at 5 because its denominator is 7 ≠ 0. Thus limₓ→5 S(x) = S(5) = 38600/7, approximately Rs 5514.29.", "(c) The source asks x → 16. The denominator is 18, so the limit is 5000 + 3600/18 = 5200 rupees."], "solution": "(a) Substitute week 5: \\(S(5)=5000+3600/7=38600/7\\), approximately Rs 5514.29.\n(b) S is continuous at 5 because its denominator is 7 ≠ 0. Thus limₓ→5 S(x) = S(5) = 38600/7, approximately Rs 5514.29.\n(c) The source asks x → 16. The denominator is 18, so the limit is 5000 + 3600/18 = 5200 rupees.", "answer": "(a) 38600/7 ≈ Rs 5514.29; (b) 38600/7 ≈ Rs 5514.29; (c) Rs 5200.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 56, "jpg": "032_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF; project coefficient uncertainty separately recorded"}], "sourceClarification": "The source key prints 5900 for (a). The stated model at week 5 gives 38600/7 ≈ 5514.29, matching its part (b). Part (c) approaches week 16, not infinity, and gives 5200.", "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.3 Question 4", "verification": "visually inspected; discrepancies stated separately"}}, {"id": "ex-2-3-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "Use MAPLE command “limit” to evaluate the limit of all parts of Q.1.", "steps": ["(a) Enter limit(3/x+1/(x-5), x=4); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is −1/4.", "(b) Enter limit(((x^2+3*x+2)/(x^2+x+2))^2, x=1); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 9/4.", "(c) Enter limit((sqrt(x)-1)/(x-1), x=1); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 1/2.", "(d) Enter limit((1/x-1)/(x-1), x=1); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is −1.", "(e) Enter limit((1-sin(x))/cos(x)^2, x=0); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 1.", "(f) Enter limit(tan(x)/x, x=0); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 1.", "(g) Enter limit((sec(x)-1)/(x*sec(x)), x=0); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 0.", "(h) Enter limit(sin(7*x)^2/(7*x), x=0); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 0.", "These are typed learning commands. Their expected results are calculated mathematically, not from a claimed Maple execution."], "solution": "(a) Enter limit(3/x+1/(x-5), x=4); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is −1/4.\n(b) Enter limit(((x^2+3*x+2)/(x^2+x+2))^2, x=1); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 9/4.\n(c) Enter limit((sqrt(x)-1)/(x-1), x=1); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 1/2.\n(d) Enter limit((1/x-1)/(x-1), x=1); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is −1.\n(e) Enter limit((1-sin(x))/cos(x)^2, x=0); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 1.\n(f) Enter limit(tan(x)/x, x=0); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 1.\n(g) Enter limit((sec(x)-1)/(x*sec(x)), x=0); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 0.\n(h) Enter limit(sin(7*x)^2/(7*x), x=0); The mathematical result checked in Question 1 is 0.\nThese are typed learning commands. Their expected results are calculated mathematically, not from a claimed Maple execution.", "answer": "(a) −1/4; (b) 9/4; (c) 1/2; (d) −1; (e) 1; (f) 1; (g) 0; (h) 0.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 56, "jpg": "032_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF; project coefficient uncertainty separately recorded"}]}, {"id": "ex-2-3-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "Use algebraic techniques to evaluate the following.\n(a) \\(\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+h}-\\sqrt{x}}{h}\\)\n(b) \\(\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^n-a^n}{x^m-a^m}\\)\n(c) \\(\\lim_{\\theta\\to0}\\frac{1-\\cos p\\theta}{1-\\cos q\\theta}\\)\n(d) \\(\\lim_{\\theta\\to0}\\frac{\\tan\\theta-\\sin\\theta}{\\sin^3\\theta}\\)\n(e) \\(\\lim_{n\\to+\\infty}(1-\\frac{1}{n})^n\\)\n(f) \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}(1+2x^2)^{\\frac{1}{x^2}}\\)\n(g) \\(\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}(\\frac{x}{1+x})^x\\)", "steps": ["(a) Treat x as fixed and positive. Rationalize: \\((\\sqrt{x+h}-\\sqrt{x})/h=1/(\\sqrt{x+h}+\\sqrt{x})\\) for h ≠ 0. Let h → 0 to obtain \\(1/(2\\sqrt{x})\\). At x = 0, only h → 0⁺ is real and the quotient tends to +∞.", "(b) For positive integers n, m and a ≠ 0, divide numerator and denominator by x − a. Their limits are \\(na^{n-1}\\) and \\(ma^{m-1}\\), so the ratio tends to \\((n/m)a^{n-m}\\). At a = 0 the ratio is xⁿ⁻ᵐ: its limit is 0 if n > m, 1 if n = m; if n < m it has no finite limit (even difference gives +∞; odd difference gives opposite one-sided infinities).", "(c) Assume q ≠ 0. Use \\(1-\\cos u=2\\sin^2(u/2)\\). For p ≠ 0, the ratio becomes \\((p/q)^2[\\sin(p\\theta/2)/(p\\theta/2)]^2/[\\sin(q\\theta/2)/(q\\theta/2)]^2\\), whose limit is p²/q². For p = 0 it is 0. If q = 0 the original denominator is identically zero.", "(d) Rewrite \\((\\tan\\theta-\\sin\\theta)/\\sin^3\\theta=(1-\\cos\\theta)/(\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta)=1/[\\cos\\theta(1+\\cos\\theta)]\\) near 0, excluding 0. The limit is 1/2.", "(e) Taking logs gives \\(n\\ln(1-1/n)=\\ln(1-u)/u\\) with u = 1/n → 0⁺. Since ln(1 − u)/u → −1, the log tends to −1. The original limit is e⁻¹.", "(f) Put u = 2x². Then \\((1+2x^2)^{1/x^2}=[(1+u)^{1/u}]^2\\). As x → 0, u → 0⁺, so the limit is e².", "(g) Rewrite \\((x/(1+x))^x=(1+1/x)^{-x}=1/[(1+1/x)^x]\\). As x → ∞, the denominator tends to e; the limit is e⁻¹."], "solution": "(a) Treat x as fixed and positive. Rationalize: \\((\\sqrt{x+h}-\\sqrt{x})/h=1/(\\sqrt{x+h}+\\sqrt{x})\\) for h ≠ 0. Let h → 0 to obtain \\(1/(2\\sqrt{x})\\). At x = 0, only h → 0⁺ is real and the quotient tends to +∞.\n(b) For positive integers n, m and a ≠ 0, divide numerator and denominator by x − a. Their limits are \\(na^{n-1}\\) and \\(ma^{m-1}\\), so the ratio tends to \\((n/m)a^{n-m}\\). At a = 0 the ratio is xⁿ⁻ᵐ: its limit is 0 if n > m, 1 if n = m; if n < m it has no finite limit (even difference gives +∞; odd difference gives opposite one-sided infinities).\n(c) Assume q ≠ 0. Use \\(1-\\cos u=2\\sin^2(u/2)\\). For p ≠ 0, the ratio becomes \\((p/q)^2[\\sin(p\\theta/2)/(p\\theta/2)]^2/[\\sin(q\\theta/2)/(q\\theta/2)]^2\\), whose limit is p²/q². For p = 0 it is 0. If q = 0 the original denominator is identically zero.\n(d) Rewrite \\((\\tan\\theta-\\sin\\theta)/\\sin^3\\theta=(1-\\cos\\theta)/(\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta)=1/[\\cos\\theta(1+\\cos\\theta)]\\) near 0, excluding 0. The limit is 1/2.\n(e) Taking logs gives \\(n\\ln(1-1/n)=\\ln(1-u)/u\\) with u = 1/n → 0⁺. Since ln(1 − u)/u → −1, the log tends to −1. The original limit is e⁻¹.\n(f) Put u = 2x². Then \\((1+2x^2)^{1/x^2}=[(1+u)^{1/u}]^2\\). As x → 0, u → 0⁺, so the limit is e².\n(g) Rewrite \\((x/(1+x))^x=(1+1/x)^{-x}=1/[(1+1/x)^x]\\). As x → ∞, the denominator tends to e; the limit is e⁻¹.", "answer": "(a) 1/(2√x), x > 0; (b) (n/m)aⁿ⁻ᵐ, a ≠ 0; (c) p²/q², q ≠ 0; (d) 1/2; (e) e⁻¹; (f) e²; (g) e⁻¹. See steps for exceptional parameter cases.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 56, "jpg": "032_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF; project coefficient uncertainty separately recorded"}], "sourceClarification": "The key prints “does not exist” for (d), but cancellation gives the finite limit 1/2. For (f), the printed question has exponent 1/x² and gives e²; the key prints e^(1/2). In (b), its generic formula needs a ≠ 0 and positive integer powers. Exceptional parameter cases are explained separately.", "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.3 Question 6", "verification": "visually inspected; discrepancies stated separately"}}, {"id": "ex-2-3-project", "qNo": "Project", "question": "What type of function are represented by the curves drawn on each image?", "steps": ["(i) Read the roof outline and the printed formula from the original crop. The two straight roof slopes meeting at a peak suggest an inverted absolute-value graph, equivalently two affine pieces.", "(ii) The displayed expression has a squared x-shift with a negative coefficient. It is a quadratic function with a downward-opening parabola.", "These classifications use the visible shapes and formula structure. The exact coefficients of the first printed formula are not reliably legible, so its full algebraic model remains unverified. Do not treat the whole photographed object as the graph."], "solution": "(i) Read the roof outline and the printed formula from the original crop. The two straight roof slopes meeting at a peak suggest an inverted absolute-value graph, equivalently two affine pieces.\n(ii) The displayed expression has a squared x-shift with a negative coefficient. It is a quadratic function with a downward-opening parabola.\nThese classifications use the visible shapes and formula structure. The exact coefficients of the first printed formula are not reliably legible, so its full algebraic model remains unverified. Do not treat the whole photographed object as the graph.", "answer": "(i) Suggested inverted absolute-value/piecewise affine profile; exact model unverified. (ii) Quadratic, downward-opening parabola.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/exercise-2-3-project-i.jpg", "caption": "Project (i)", "alt": "Original rooftop photograph with coordinate grid, highlighted roof profile and partially legible printed formula", "displayWidth": 400}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/exercise-2-3-project-ii.jpg", "caption": "Project (ii)", "alt": "Original photograph of a person with coordinate grid and a printed quadratic function", "displayWidth": 400}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "partial: first project model legibility pending", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 56, "jpg": "032_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF; project coefficient uncertainty separately recorded"}]}];
