const math12Exercise24Revision=[{"id": "ex-2-4-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Use properties of continuous function to test the continuity and discontinuity of the following functions:\n(a) \\(f(x)=2x-3\\). (b) \\(h(x)=\\frac{2}{x-5}\\). (c) \\(g(x)=\\frac{x-5}{(x-3)(x+2)}\\).", "steps": ["(a) 2x − 3 is a polynomial, so it is continuous at every real x.", "(b) A quotient of polynomials is continuous wherever its denominator is nonzero. Here x − 5 = 0 only at 5; the nonzero numerator 2 gives a pole there.", "(c) The denominator vanishes at 3 and −2. The numerator equals −2 and −7 at those points, so neither factor cancels; both are poles. At all other real values the rational function is continuous."], "solution": "(a) 2x − 3 is a polynomial, so it is continuous at every real x.\n(b) A quotient of polynomials is continuous wherever its denominator is nonzero. Here x − 5 = 0 only at 5; the nonzero numerator 2 gives a pole there.\n(c) The denominator vanishes at 3 and −2. The numerator equals −2 and −7 at those points, so neither factor cancels; both are poles. At all other real values the rational function is continuous.", "answer": "(a) Continuous on ℝ. (b) Continuous on ℝ ∖ {5}. (c) Continuous on ℝ ∖ {−2, 3}.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 60, "jpg": "034_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.4 Question 1", "verification": "visually inspected; Question 7 discrepancy separately explained"}}, {"id": "ex-2-4-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Show that the given function is continuous at x = 0.", "steps": ["The defined value is f(0) = 1. For x ≠ 0, f(x) = sin x/x.", "Using radians, \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}=1\\). Hence both one-sided limits exist and equal 1.", "The limit equals f(0), so all continuity conditions hold: f is continuous at 0."], "solution": "The defined value is f(0) = 1. For x ≠ 0, f(x) = sin x/x.\nUsing radians, \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}=1\\). Hence both one-sided limits exist and equal 1.\nThe limit equals f(0), so all continuity conditions hold: f is continuous at 0.", "answer": "Continuous at 0: limit and assigned value both equal 1.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "piecewise", "label": "f(x) =", "rows": [["\\(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\)", "\\(\\text{if }x\\ne0\\)"], ["\\(1\\)", "\\(\\text{if }x=0\\)"]]}], "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 60, "jpg": "034_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.4 Question 2", "verification": "visually inspected; Question 7 discrepancy separately explained"}}, {"id": "ex-2-4-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Use the graph of g(x) to answer: (a) Is g continuous on (−1, 2)? (b) Is g continuous from the right at −1; is limₓ→−1⁺ g(x) = g(−1)? (c) Is g continuous from the left at 2; is limₓ→2⁻ g(x) = g(2)? (d) Is g continuous on [−1, 2]?", "steps": ["(a) The curve has no jumps or holes between −1 and 2; alternatively, the printed formula g(x) = −x² + 2x + 2 is a polynomial. Thus g is continuous on (−1, 2).", "(b) The solid left endpoint is (−1, −1). Along the curve from the right, the values approach −1 = g(−1). Right continuity holds.", "(c) The solid right endpoint is (2, 2). Approaching from the left gives 2 = g(2). Left continuity holds.", "(d) Interior continuity and the two endpoint conditions prove continuity on [−1, 2]. All four answers are yes."], "solution": "(a) The curve has no jumps or holes between −1 and 2; alternatively, the printed formula g(x) = −x² + 2x + 2 is a polynomial. Thus g is continuous on (−1, 2).\n(b) The solid left endpoint is (−1, −1). Along the curve from the right, the values approach −1 = g(−1). Right continuity holds.\n(c) The solid right endpoint is (2, 2). Approaching from the left gives 2 = g(2). Left continuity holds.\n(d) Interior continuity and the two endpoint conditions prove continuity on [−1, 2]. All four answers are yes.", "answer": "(a)–(d) Yes; endpoint limits equal g(−1) = −1 and g(2) = 2.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "Question 3: g(x) = −x² + 2x + 2", "xMin": -2.5, "xMax": 4, "yMin": -2, "yMax": 5, "linearSegments": [[-1.0, -1.0, -0.95, -0.8024999999999998], [-0.95, -0.8024999999999998, -0.9, -0.6100000000000003], [-0.9, -0.6100000000000003, -0.85, -0.4224999999999999], [-0.85, -0.4224999999999999, -0.8, -0.2400000000000002], [-0.8, -0.2400000000000002, -0.75, -0.0625], [-0.75, -0.0625, -0.7, 0.1100000000000001], [-0.7, 0.1100000000000001, -0.65, 0.27749999999999986], [-0.65, 0.27749999999999986, -0.6, 0.43999999999999995], [-0.6, 0.43999999999999995, -0.55, 0.5974999999999999], [-0.55, 0.5974999999999999, -0.5, 0.75], [-0.5, 0.75, -0.44999999999999996, 0.8975000000000002], [-0.44999999999999996, 0.8975000000000002, -0.4, 1.04], [-0.4, 1.04, -0.35, 1.1775000000000002], [-0.35, 1.1775000000000002, -0.30000000000000004, 1.3099999999999998], [-0.30000000000000004, 1.3099999999999998, -0.25, 1.4375], [-0.25, 1.4375, -0.19999999999999996, 1.56], [-0.19999999999999996, 1.56, -0.15000000000000002, 1.6775], [-0.15000000000000002, 1.6775, -0.09999999999999998, 1.79], [-0.09999999999999998, 1.79, -0.050000000000000044, 1.8975], [-0.050000000000000044, 1.8975, 0.0, 2.0], [0.0, 2.0, 0.050000000000000044, 2.0975], [0.050000000000000044, 2.0975, 0.10000000000000009, 2.1900000000000004], [0.10000000000000009, 2.1900000000000004, 0.1499999999999999, 2.2775], [0.1499999999999999, 2.2775, 0.19999999999999996, 2.36], [0.19999999999999996, 2.36, 0.25, 2.4375], [0.25, 2.4375, 0.30000000000000004, 2.51], [0.30000000000000004, 2.51, 0.3500000000000001, 2.5775], [0.3500000000000001, 2.5775, 0.3999999999999999, 2.6399999999999997], [0.3999999999999999, 2.6399999999999997, 0.44999999999999996, 2.6975], [0.44999999999999996, 2.6975, 0.5, 2.75], [0.5, 2.75, 0.55, 2.7975000000000003], [0.55, 2.7975000000000003, 0.6000000000000001, 2.84], [0.6000000000000001, 2.84, 0.6499999999999999, 2.8775], [0.6499999999999999, 2.8775, 0.7, 2.91], [0.7, 2.91, 0.75, 2.9375], [0.75, 2.9375, 0.8, 2.96], [0.8, 2.96, 0.8500000000000001, 2.9775], [0.8500000000000001, 2.9775, 0.8999999999999999, 2.99], [0.8999999999999999, 2.99, 0.95, 2.9975], [0.95, 2.9975, 1.0, 3.0], [1.0, 3.0, 1.0499999999999998, 2.9975], [1.0499999999999998, 2.9975, 1.1, 2.99], [1.1, 2.99, 1.15, 2.9775], [1.15, 2.9775, 1.2000000000000002, 2.96], [1.2000000000000002, 2.96, 1.25, 2.9375], [1.25, 2.9375, 1.2999999999999998, 2.91], [1.2999999999999998, 2.91, 1.35, 2.8775], [1.35, 2.8775, 1.4, 2.84], [1.4, 2.84, 1.4500000000000002, 2.7975], [1.4500000000000002, 2.7975, 1.5, 2.75], [1.5, 2.75, 1.5499999999999998, 2.6975000000000002], [1.5499999999999998, 2.6975000000000002, 1.6, 2.6399999999999997], [1.6, 2.6399999999999997, 1.65, 2.5775], [1.65, 2.5775, 1.7000000000000002, 2.51], [1.7000000000000002, 2.51, 1.75, 2.4375], [1.75, 2.4375, 1.7999999999999998, 2.3600000000000003], [1.7999999999999998, 2.3600000000000003, 1.85, 2.2775], [1.85, 2.2775, 1.9, 2.19], [1.9, 2.19, 1.9500000000000002, 2.0974999999999997], [1.9500000000000002, 2.0974999999999997, 2.0, 2.0]], "closedPoints": [[-1, -1], [2, 2]], "openPoints": [], "xTicks": [-2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "yTicks": [-1, 0, 2, 4]}, "caption": "Question 3 — digital reconstruction of source graph"}], "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 60, "jpg": "034_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.4 Question 3", "verification": "visually inspected; Question 7 discrepancy separately explained"}}, {"id": "ex-2-4-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "Use the graph of f(x) to answer: (a) Is f continuous on (0, 3)? (b) Is f continuous from the right at 0; is limₓ→0⁺ f(x) = f(0)? (c) Is f continuous from the left at 3; is limₓ→3⁻ f(x) = g(3)? (d) Is g continuous on [0, 3]?", "steps": ["(a) The plotted curve has no jumps or holes on (0, 3), so it is continuous there.", "(b) The included point at 0 has value 2. The curve approaching it from the right tends to 2 = f(0); right continuity holds.", "(c) The included point at 3 has value 5. Approaching from the left tends to 5 = f(3); left continuity holds. The printed g(3) is read as f(3).", "(d) The interior and endpoint conditions hold, so the displayed f is continuous on [0, 3]. All four answers are yes."], "solution": "(a) The plotted curve has no jumps or holes on (0, 3), so it is continuous there.\n(b) The included point at 0 has value 2. The curve approaching it from the right tends to 2 = f(0); right continuity holds.\n(c) The included point at 3 has value 5. Approaching from the left tends to 5 = f(3); left continuity holds. The printed g(3) is read as f(3).\n(d) The interior and endpoint conditions hold, so the displayed f is continuous on [0, 3]. All four answers are yes.", "answer": "(a)–(d) Yes for the displayed f; endpoint limits equal f(0) = 2 and f(3) = 5.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "Question 4: source curve and included endpoints", "xMin": -1, "xMax": 6, "yMin": -1, "yMax": 6, "linearSegments": [[0.0, 2.0, 0.05, 1.9025], [0.05, 1.9025, 0.1, 1.81], [0.1, 1.81, 0.15, 1.7225000000000001], [0.15, 1.7225000000000001, 0.2, 1.6400000000000001], [0.2, 1.6400000000000001, 0.25, 1.5625], [0.25, 1.5625, 0.3, 1.49], [0.3, 1.49, 0.35, 1.4224999999999999], [0.35, 1.4224999999999999, 0.4, 1.3599999999999999], [0.4, 1.3599999999999999, 0.45, 1.3025], [0.45, 1.3025, 0.5, 1.25], [0.5, 1.25, 0.55, 1.2025], [0.55, 1.2025, 0.6, 1.1600000000000001], [0.6, 1.1600000000000001, 0.65, 1.1225], [0.65, 1.1225, 0.7, 1.09], [0.7, 1.09, 0.75, 1.0625], [0.75, 1.0625, 0.8, 1.04], [0.8, 1.04, 0.85, 1.0225], [0.85, 1.0225, 0.9, 1.01], [0.9, 1.01, 0.95, 1.0025], [0.95, 1.0025, 1.0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0, 1.05, 1.0025], [1.05, 1.0025, 1.1, 1.01], [1.1, 1.01, 1.15, 1.0225], [1.15, 1.0225, 1.2, 1.04], [1.2, 1.04, 1.25, 1.0625], [1.25, 1.0625, 1.3, 1.09], [1.3, 1.09, 1.35, 1.1225], [1.35, 1.1225, 1.4, 1.16], [1.4, 1.16, 1.45, 1.2025000000000001], [1.45, 1.2025000000000001, 1.5, 1.25], [1.5, 1.25, 1.55, 1.3025000000000002], [1.55, 1.3025000000000002, 1.6, 1.3600000000000003], [1.6, 1.3600000000000003, 1.65, 1.4224999999999999], [1.65, 1.4224999999999999, 1.7, 1.4899999999999998], [1.7, 1.4899999999999998, 1.75, 1.5625], [1.75, 1.5625, 1.8, 1.6400000000000001], [1.8, 1.6400000000000001, 1.85, 1.7225000000000001], [1.85, 1.7225000000000001, 1.9, 1.81], [1.9, 1.81, 1.95, 1.9024999999999999], [1.95, 1.9024999999999999, 2.0, 2.0], [2.0, 2.0, 2.05, 2.1025], [2.05, 2.1025, 2.1, 2.21], [2.1, 2.21, 2.15, 2.3225], [2.15, 2.3225, 2.2, 2.4400000000000004], [2.2, 2.4400000000000004, 2.25, 2.5625], [2.25, 2.5625, 2.3, 2.6899999999999995], [2.3, 2.6899999999999995, 2.35, 2.8225000000000007], [2.35, 2.8225000000000007, 2.4, 2.96], [2.4, 2.96, 2.45, 3.102500000000001], [2.45, 3.102500000000001, 2.5, 3.25], [2.5, 3.25, 2.55, 3.4025], [2.55, 3.4025, 2.6, 3.5600000000000005], [2.6, 3.5600000000000005, 2.65, 3.7225], [2.65, 3.7225, 2.7, 3.8900000000000006], [2.7, 3.8900000000000006, 2.75, 4.0625], [2.75, 4.0625, 2.8, 4.239999999999999], [2.8, 4.239999999999999, 2.85, 4.4225], [2.85, 4.4225, 2.9, 4.61], [2.9, 4.61, 2.95, 4.8025], [2.95, 4.8025, 3.0, 5.0]], "closedPoints": [[0, 2], [3, 5]], "openPoints": [], "xTicks": [0, 1, 3, 5], "yTicks": [0, 2, 5]}, "caption": "Question 4 — digital reconstruction of source curve"}], "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 60, "jpg": "034_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "sourceClarification": "The source switches from f to g in parts (c) and (d). These learning answers consistently refer to the displayed function f. The digital curve follows the visible source vertex and endpoints; no formula is printed for this graph.", "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.4 Question 4", "verification": "visually inspected; Question 7 discrepancy separately explained"}}, {"id": "ex-2-4-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "Graph and locate all points of discontinuity of the following piecewise functions (a) and (b).", "steps": ["(a) Each linear branch is continuous on its own interval. At 1, the left limit is 1 + 1 = 2 and the right limit is 5 − 1 = 4. The defined value f(1) = 4 belongs to the second branch.", "Since 2 ≠ 4, the two-sided limit at 1 does not exist. This is a jump discontinuity; every other point is continuous. Draw an open point (1, 2) and a closed point (1, 4).", "(b) Away from 0 the function is |x|, which is continuous. The left and right limits at 0 both equal 0, but f(0) = 1.", "Thus only 0 is discontinuous, and the discontinuity is removable. Draw an open point at (0, 0) and a closed isolated point at (0, 1). Defining f(0) = 0 would repair it."], "solution": "(a) Each linear branch is continuous on its own interval. At 1, the left limit is 1 + 1 = 2 and the right limit is 5 − 1 = 4. The defined value f(1) = 4 belongs to the second branch.\nSince 2 ≠ 4, the two-sided limit at 1 does not exist. This is a jump discontinuity; every other point is continuous. Draw an open point (1, 2) and a closed point (1, 4).\n(b) Away from 0 the function is |x|, which is continuous. The left and right limits at 0 both equal 0, but f(0) = 1.\nThus only 0 is discontinuous, and the discontinuity is removable. Draw an open point at (0, 0) and a closed isolated point at (0, 1). Defining f(0) = 0 would repair it.", "answer": "(a) Jump only at 1. (b) Removable discontinuity only at 0.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "piecewise", "label": "f(x) =", "rows": [["\\(1+x\\)", "\\(\\text{if }x<1\\)"], ["\\(5-x\\)", "\\(\\text{if }x\\geq1\\)"]]}, {"type": "piecewise", "label": "f(x) =", "rows": [["\\(-x\\)", "\\(\\text{if }x<0\\)"], ["\\(1\\)", "\\(\\text{if }x=0\\)"], ["\\(x\\)", "\\(\\text{if }x>0\\)"]]}], "solutionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "Question 5(a): jump at 1", "xMin": -3, "xMax": 5, "yMin": -2, "yMax": 7, "linearSegments": [[-3, -2, 1, 2], [1, 4, 5, 0]], "closedPoints": [[1, 4]], "openPoints": [[1, 2]], "xTicks": [-2, 0, 1, 3, 5], "yTicks": [0, 2, 4, 6]}, "caption": "SpaceBook learning graph — Question 5(a)"}, {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "Question 5(b): isolated value at 0", "xMin": -3, "xMax": 3, "yMin": -0.5, "yMax": 3.5, "linearSegments": [[-3, 3, 0, 0], [0, 0, 3, 3]], "closedPoints": [[0, 1]], "openPoints": [[0, 0]], "xTicks": [-2, 0, 2], "yTicks": [0, 1, 2, 3]}, "caption": "SpaceBook learning graph — Question 5(b)"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 60, "jpg": "034_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.4 Question 5", "verification": "visually inspected; Question 7 discrepancy separately explained"}}, {"id": "ex-2-4-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "Personal computer salesperson receives a base salary of $1,000 per month and a commission of 5% of all sales over $10,000 during the month. If monthly sales are $20,000 or more, the salesperson is given an additional $500 bonus. Let E(s) represent monthly earnings as a function of monthly sales. (a) Graph E(s) for 0 ≤ s ≤ 30,000. (b) Find limₛ→10,000 E(s) and E(10,000). (c) Find limₛ→20,000 E(s) and E(20,000). (d) Is E continuous at s = 10,000? At s = 2,000?", "steps": ["(a) For 0 ≤ s ≤ 10,000, E(s) = 1,000. For 10,000 < s < 20,000, E(s) = 1,000 + 0.05(s − 10,000) = 0.05s + 500. For s ≥ 20,000, add the $500 bonus: E(s) = 0.05s + 1,000.", "The graph stays at 1,000 until sales of 10,000, rises linearly toward the open point (20,000, 1,500), then starts at the closed point (20,000, 2,000) and rises to (30,000, 2,500).", "(b) At 10,000 both branches tend to 1,000 and E(10,000) = 1,000. The limit exists and earnings are continuous there.", "(c) At 20,000, the left limit is 1,500 and the right limit is 2,000. They differ, so no two-sided limit exists. E(20,000) = 2,000 because the bonus applies at the threshold.", "(d) Yes at 10,000. At the literal printed 2,000 the function is constant in a neighbourhood and is also continuous. At the intended bonus threshold 20,000 it is discontinuous, with a $500 jump."], "solution": "(a) For 0 ≤ s ≤ 10,000, E(s) = 1,000. For 10,000 < s < 20,000, E(s) = 1,000 + 0.05(s − 10,000) = 0.05s + 500. For s ≥ 20,000, add the $500 bonus: E(s) = 0.05s + 1,000.\nThe graph stays at 1,000 until sales of 10,000, rises linearly toward the open point (20,000, 1,500), then starts at the closed point (20,000, 2,000) and rises to (30,000, 2,500).\n(b) At 10,000 both branches tend to 1,000 and E(10,000) = 1,000. The limit exists and earnings are continuous there.\n(c) At 20,000, the left limit is 1,500 and the right limit is 2,000. They differ, so no two-sided limit exists. E(20,000) = 2,000 because the bonus applies at the threshold.\n(d) Yes at 10,000. At the literal printed 2,000 the function is constant in a neighbourhood and is also continuous. At the intended bonus threshold 20,000 it is discontinuous, with a $500 jump.", "answer": "Limit and value at 10,000: $1,000. No two-sided limit at 20,000; E(20,000) = $2,000. Continuous at 10,000 and the literal 2,000; discontinuous at 20,000.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "solutionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "Question 6: monthly earnings E(s)", "xMin": 0, "xMax": 30000, "yMin": 800, "yMax": 2800, "linearSegments": [[0, 1000, 10000, 1000], [10000, 1000, 20000, 1500], [20000, 2000, 30000, 2500]], "closedPoints": [[10000, 1000], [20000, 2000]], "openPoints": [[20000, 1500]], "xTicks": [0, 10000, 20000, 30000], "yTicks": [1000, 1500, 2000, 2500], "xGridStep": 5000, "yGridStep": 250}, "caption": "SpaceBook learning graph — Question 6"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 60, "jpg": "034_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "sourceClarification": "Part (d) prints 2,000, although the preceding bonus threshold and answer key use 20,000. The solution answers the literal 2,000 and the intended 20,000 separately.", "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.4 Question 6", "verification": "visually inspected; Question 7 discrepancy separately explained"}}, {"id": "ex-2-4-q7", "qNo": "7", "question": "Use MAPLE command “iscont” to test the continuity of \\(f(x)=\\frac{x^2+1}{x^3+2.7}\\) in closed interval [−5, 5].", "steps": ["The numerator \\(x^2+1\\) is strictly positive for real x. The denominator is zero when \\(x=-\\sqrt[3]{2.7}\\approx-1.39248\\), which lies in [−5, 5].", "At this point the numerator does not vanish, so there is no cancellation; the function is undefined and has a pole inside the requested interval.", "Enter iscont((x^2+1)/(x^3+2.7), x=-5..5, 'closed');. The expected result is false, based on the interior pole.", "The printed key gives true, contradicting the printed function. This solution retains the source question and explains the disagreement; no Maple execution is claimed."], "solution": "The numerator \\(x^2+1\\) is strictly positive for real x. The denominator is zero when \\(x=-\\sqrt[3]{2.7}\\approx-1.39248\\), which lies in [−5, 5].\nAt this point the numerator does not vanish, so there is no cancellation; the function is undefined and has a pole inside the requested interval.\nEnter iscont((x^2+1)/(x^3+2.7), x=-5..5, 'closed');. The expected result is false, based on the interior pole.\nThe printed key gives true, contradicting the printed function. This solution retains the source question and explains the disagreement; no Maple execution is claimed.", "answer": "Expected false: an interior pole at −∛2.7. The printed key says true.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 60, "jpg": "034_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "sourceClarification": "The printed answer key says true. For the printed denominator x³ + 2.7, there is a real zero −∛2.7 inside [−5,5], and the numerator there is positive. Thus the function is discontinuous on this interval; the expected iscont result is false. Maple has not been executed.", "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 170, "printedPage": 331, "scope": "Exercise 2.4 Question 7", "verification": "visually inspected; Question 7 discrepancy separately explained"}}, {"id": "ex-2-4-project", "qNo": "Project", "question": "Project (page 59): Create at least five functions randomly then use MAPLE command “iscont” to check their continuity on interval (0,1).", "steps": ["One possible selection is x² + 1, sin x, exp(x), 1/(x + 1), and 1/(x − 0.5). Many other choices satisfy the project request; these are sample selections.", "For each expression enter iscont(expression, x=0..1);. The omitted third argument selects the open interval (0, 1).", "Commands iscont(x^2+1,x=0..1);, iscont(sin(x),x=0..1); and iscont(exp(x),x=0..1); have expected result true, since these functions are continuous everywhere.", "Command iscont(1/(x+1),x=0..1); has expected result true because its only pole is at −1, outside the interval.", "Command iscont(1/(x-0.5),x=0..1); has expected result false because its pole at 0.5 lies inside the interval. These are mathematically derived expected outputs, not results from executing Maple."], "solution": "One possible selection is x² + 1, sin x, exp(x), 1/(x + 1), and 1/(x − 0.5). Many other choices satisfy the project request; these are sample selections.\nFor each expression enter iscont(expression, x=0..1);. The omitted third argument selects the open interval (0, 1).\nCommands iscont(x^2+1,x=0..1);, iscont(sin(x),x=0..1); and iscont(exp(x),x=0..1); have expected result true, since these functions are continuous everywhere.\nCommand iscont(1/(x+1),x=0..1); has expected result true because its only pole is at −1, outside the interval.\nCommand iscont(1/(x-0.5),x=0..1); has expected result false because its pole at 0.5 lies inside the interval. These are mathematically derived expected outputs, not results from executing Maple.", "answer": "Sample expected results: true, true, true, true, false. Maple not executed.", "diagram": null, "sourceQuestionBlocks": [], "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 59, "jpg": "034_page_1.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}];
