const math12Exercise35=[{"id": "ex-3-5-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Use the rule of first principle to find the derivative of the following functions: (a) \\(f(x)=e^{2x}\\); (b) \\(f(x)=\\frac13e^{3x}\\); (c) \\(f(x)=\\frac58e^{x^2}+1\\); (d) \\(f(x)=2^x\\); (e) \\(f(x)=4^{x+4}\\); (f) \\(f(x)=\\log(x+1)\\); (g) \\(f(x)=\\log_a(x^2)\\); (h) \\(f(x)=\\sinh2x\\).", "steps": ["Use the difference quotient with increment h ≠ 0 and limit h → 0. For fixed a > 0, lim[k→0](aᵏ − 1)/k = ln a; for a = e the limit is 1.", "(a) [e^(2x+2h) − e^(2x)]/h = 2e^(2x)[(e^(2h) − 1)/(2h)] → 2e^(2x).", "(b) [(e^(3x+3h)/3) − (e^(3x)/3)]/h = e^(3x)[(e^(3h) − 1)/(3h)] → e^(3x).", "(c) The +1 cancels in the difference. Let k = (x+h)² − x² = h(2x+h). The quotient becomes (5/8)e^(x²)[(eᵏ − 1)/k](2x+h), giving (5/4)x e^(x²). Extend the ratio at k = 0 by its limiting value 1; this also covers x = 0. The key incorrectly puts +1 inside the exponent.", "(d) [2^(x+h) − 2ˣ]/h = 2ˣ[(2ʰ − 1)/h] → 2ˣln2. (e) The same argument with 4^(x+4) gives 4^(x+4)ln4, equal to 256·4ˣln4.", "(f) For log base b > 0, b ≠ 1, the quotient is ln[1 + h/(x+1)]/(h ln b). Set k = h/(x+1); lim ln(1+k)/k = 1 gives 1/[(x+1)ln b], with x > −1. If log means natural logarithm, ln b = 1. The source does not specify the base; the key uses the natural-log result.", "(g) The quotient for logₐ(x²) is ln[(x+h)²/x²]/(h ln a). Put k = h/x; for small h and x ≠ 0 this becomes 2ln(1+k)/(x k ln a), tending to 2/(x ln a). Require a > 0, a ≠ 1 and x ≠ 0.", "(h) Use sinh2x = [e^(2x) − e^(−2x)]/2 and its difference quotient. The exponential limits give [2e^(2x) + 2e^(−2x)]/2 = 2cosh2x, for all real x.", "The key’s entries for 1(h) and 4(a) appear exchanged: 1(h) is 2cosh2x; 4(a), under the key’s natural-log interpretation of a cubed argument, is 3/(x+2)."], "solution": "Use the difference quotient with increment h ≠ 0 and limit h → 0. For fixed a > 0, lim[k→0](aᵏ − 1)/k = ln a; for a = e the limit is 1.\n(a) [e^(2x+2h) − e^(2x)]/h = 2e^(2x)[(e^(2h) − 1)/(2h)] → 2e^(2x).\n(b) [(e^(3x+3h)/3) − (e^(3x)/3)]/h = e^(3x)[(e^(3h) − 1)/(3h)] → e^(3x).\n(c) The +1 cancels in the difference. Let k = (x+h)² − x² = h(2x+h). The quotient becomes (5/8)e^(x²)[(eᵏ − 1)/k](2x+h), giving (5/4)x e^(x²). Extend the ratio at k = 0 by its limiting value 1; this also covers x = 0. The key incorrectly puts +1 inside the exponent.\n(d) [2^(x+h) − 2ˣ]/h = 2ˣ[(2ʰ − 1)/h] → 2ˣln2. (e) The same argument with 4^(x+4) gives 4^(x+4)ln4, equal to 256·4ˣln4.\n(f) For log base b > 0, b ≠ 1, the quotient is ln[1 + h/(x+1)]/(h ln b). Set k = h/(x+1); lim ln(1+k)/k = 1 gives 1/[(x+1)ln b], with x > −1. If log means natural logarithm, ln b = 1. The source does not specify the base; the key uses the natural-log result.\n(g) The quotient for logₐ(x²) is ln[(x+h)²/x²]/(h ln a). Put k = h/x; for small h and x ≠ 0 this becomes 2ln(1+k)/(x k ln a), tending to 2/(x ln a). Require a > 0, a ≠ 1 and x ≠ 0.\n(h) Use sinh2x = [e^(2x) − e^(−2x)]/2 and its difference quotient. The exponential limits give [2e^(2x) + 2e^(−2x)]/2 = 2cosh2x, for all real x.\nThe key’s entries for 1(h) and 4(a) appear exchanged: 1(h) is 2cosh2x; 4(a), under the key’s natural-log interpretation of a cubed argument, is 3/(x+2).", "answer": "(a) 2e^(2x); (b) e^(3x); (c) (5/4)x e^(x²); (d) 2ˣln2; (e) 4^(x+4)ln4; (f) 1/[(x+1)ln b] for stated log base b; (g) 2/(x ln a); (h) 2cosh2x, on the domains explained above.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 98, "jpg": "053_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Exercise 3.5 Questions 1–6 only; no separate Question 7 key, answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "In 1(c), +1 is outside the exponential in the question, but the key places it inside. The key’s 1(h) and 4(a) entries are exchanged. The unbased log in 1(f) is unspecified; the key assumes natural log."}, {"id": "ex-3-5-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Find f′(x): (a) \\(11^{3-4x^3}\\); (b) \\(e^{\\sqrt{x}-5}\\); (c) \\(x^5e^{1/x}\\); (d) \\(\\frac{e^{2x}}{e^{-2x}+1}\\); (e) \\(\\ln(e^{mx}-e^{-mx})\\); (f) \\(\\frac{e^{ax}-e^{-ax}}{e^{ax}+e^{-ax}}\\).", "steps": ["(a) The source exponent is 3 − 4x³. Its inner derivative is −12x², so f′ = −12x²11^(3 − 4x³)ln11. The key instead solves the different exponent 3 − 4x⁵, giving −20x⁴11^(3 − 4x⁵)ln11.", "(b) u = √x − 5 has u′ = 1/(2√x) for x > 0. Thus f′ = e^(√x − 5)/(2√x). At x = 0 the function exists, but the right slope is unbounded.", "(c) Product rule with d(e^(1/x))/dx = −e^(1/x)/x² gives f′ = 5x⁴e^(1/x) − x³e^(1/x) = (5x⁴ − x³)e^(1/x), with x ≠ 0.", "(d) N = e^(2x), N′ = 2e^(2x); D = e^(−2x) + 1, D′ = −2e^(−2x). The quotient numerator simplifies to 2(e^(2x) + 2). Hence f′ = 2(e^(2x) + 2)/(e^(−2x) + 1)², defined for every real x.", "(e) u = e^(mx) − e^(−mx), u′ = m[e^(mx) + e^(−mx)]. The logarithm requires u > 0, equivalent to mx > 0 for real constant m. For m = 0 there is no real domain.", "Thus f′ = m[e^(mx) + e^(−mx)]/[e^(mx) − e^(−mx)], on mx > 0. This equals m coth(mx).", "(f) Let E = e^(ax), F = e^(−ax). Then N = E − F, N′ = a(E + F), D = E + F, D′ = a(E − F).", "Quotient rule gives a[(E+F)² − (E−F)²]/(E+F)² = 4a EF/(E+F)² = 4a/(e^(ax)+e^(−ax))² = a sech²(ax), since EF = 1. This holds for all real a,x, including a = 0."], "solution": "(a) The source exponent is 3 − 4x³. Its inner derivative is −12x², so f′ = −12x²11^(3 − 4x³)ln11. The key instead solves the different exponent 3 − 4x⁵, giving −20x⁴11^(3 − 4x⁵)ln11.\n(b) u = √x − 5 has u′ = 1/(2√x) for x > 0. Thus f′ = e^(√x − 5)/(2√x). At x = 0 the function exists, but the right slope is unbounded.\n(c) Product rule with d(e^(1/x))/dx = −e^(1/x)/x² gives f′ = 5x⁴e^(1/x) − x³e^(1/x) = (5x⁴ − x³)e^(1/x), with x ≠ 0.\n(d) N = e^(2x), N′ = 2e^(2x); D = e^(−2x) + 1, D′ = −2e^(−2x). The quotient numerator simplifies to 2(e^(2x) + 2). Hence f′ = 2(e^(2x) + 2)/(e^(−2x) + 1)², defined for every real x.\n(e) u = e^(mx) − e^(−mx), u′ = m[e^(mx) + e^(−mx)]. The logarithm requires u > 0, equivalent to mx > 0 for real constant m. For m = 0 there is no real domain.\nThus f′ = m[e^(mx) + e^(−mx)]/[e^(mx) − e^(−mx)], on mx > 0. This equals m coth(mx).\n(f) Let E = e^(ax), F = e^(−ax). Then N = E − F, N′ = a(E + F), D = E + F, D′ = a(E − F).\nQuotient rule gives a[(E+F)² − (E−F)²]/(E+F)² = 4a EF/(E+F)² = 4a/(e^(ax)+e^(−ax))² = a sech²(ax), since EF = 1. This holds for all real a,x, including a = 0.", "answer": "(a) −12x²11^(3−4x³)ln11; (b) e^(√x−5)/(2√x); (c) (5x⁴−x³)e^(1/x); (d) 2(e^(2x)+2)/(e^(−2x)+1)²; (e) m coth(mx) on mx > 0; (f) a sech²(ax).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 98, "jpg": "053_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Exercise 3.5 Questions 1–6 only; no separate Question 7 key, answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "Question 2(a) has x³ in its exponent; the key answers a different exponent containing x⁵. The learning solution follows the question."}, {"id": "ex-3-5-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Find dy/dx using any suitable rule: (a) \\(y=x^3\\ln\\sqrt{x}\\); (b) \\(y=x^2\\sqrt{\\ln x}\\); (c) \\(y=\\ln\\sqrt{\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2-1}}\\); (d) \\(y=\\ln(x-\\sqrt{x^2+1})\\); (e) \\(y=e^{-2x}\\cos2x\\); (f) \\(y=x^2e^{\\sin x}\\).", "steps": ["(a) Require x > 0. Since ln√x = (1/2)ln x, differentiate (1/2)x³ln x: y′ = (3/2)x²ln x + (1/2)x² = (x²/2)(1 + 3ln x).", "(b) x²√ln x is real for x ≥ 1. On x > 1, product and chain rules give y′ = 2x√ln x + x/(2√ln x) = x(4ln x + 1)/(2√ln x). At x = 1 its right slope is unbounded.", "(c) Require (x² + 1)/(x² − 1) > 0, hence |x| > 1. Expand y = (1/2)[ln(x² + 1) − ln(x² − 1)].", "Then y′ = x/(x² + 1) − x/(x² − 1) = −2x/(x⁴ − 1) = 2x/(1 − x⁴), on |x| > 1.", "(d) As printed, √(x² + 1) > |x| for every real x, so x − √(x² + 1) < 0. Therefore the real logarithm and its real derivative do not exist at any real x.", "If the intended function is ln|x − √(x² + 1)|, it equals ln(√(x² + 1) − x) = −arsinh x. Its derivative is −1/√(x² + 1), the key’s answer. This is a clarified alternative, not a silent change to the question.", "(e) Product rule gives y′ = −2e^(−2x)cos2x − 2e^(−2x)sin2x = −2e^(−2x)(cos2x + sin2x), for all real x.", "(f) Product and chain rules give y′ = 2xe^(sin x) + x²e^(sin x)cos x = xe^(sin x)(xcos x + 2), for all real x."], "solution": "(a) Require x > 0. Since ln√x = (1/2)ln x, differentiate (1/2)x³ln x: y′ = (3/2)x²ln x + (1/2)x² = (x²/2)(1 + 3ln x).\n(b) x²√ln x is real for x ≥ 1. On x > 1, product and chain rules give y′ = 2x√ln x + x/(2√ln x) = x(4ln x + 1)/(2√ln x). At x = 1 its right slope is unbounded.\n(c) Require (x² + 1)/(x² − 1) > 0, hence |x| > 1. Expand y = (1/2)[ln(x² + 1) − ln(x² − 1)].\nThen y′ = x/(x² + 1) − x/(x² − 1) = −2x/(x⁴ − 1) = 2x/(1 − x⁴), on |x| > 1.\n(d) As printed, √(x² + 1) > |x| for every real x, so x − √(x² + 1) < 0. Therefore the real logarithm and its real derivative do not exist at any real x.\nIf the intended function is ln|x − √(x² + 1)|, it equals ln(√(x² + 1) − x) = −arsinh x. Its derivative is −1/√(x² + 1), the key’s answer. This is a clarified alternative, not a silent change to the question.\n(e) Product rule gives y′ = −2e^(−2x)cos2x − 2e^(−2x)sin2x = −2e^(−2x)(cos2x + sin2x), for all real x.\n(f) Product and chain rules give y′ = 2xe^(sin x) + x²e^(sin x)cos x = xe^(sin x)(xcos x + 2), for all real x.", "answer": "(a) (x²/2)(1+3ln x); (b) x(4ln x+1)/(2√ln x); (c) 2x/(1−x⁴); (d) no real function as printed; the clarified ln of the absolute value has derivative −1/√(x²+1); (e) −2e^(−2x)(cos2x+sin2x); (f) xe^(sin x)(xcos x+2).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 98, "jpg": "053_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Exercise 3.5 Questions 1–6 only; no separate Question 7 key, answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "Question 3(d) has a negative logarithm argument for every real x and therefore no real domain. The key’s result applies to the explicitly separated ln-of-absolute-value alternative."}, {"id": "ex-3-5-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "Differentiate: (a) \\(y=\\log(x+2)^3\\); (b) \\(y=\\cosh3x\\); (c) \\(y=\\sinh^{-1}(\\cos x)\\); (d) \\(y=\\tanh^{-1}(\\frac{x}{2})\\); (e) \\(y=\\ln(\\text{coth }x)\\); (f) \\(y=x\\cosh^{-1}x-\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\).", "steps": ["(a) The printed log(x+2)³ is ambiguous. Under the key’s natural-log interpretation of a cubed argument, y = ln((x+2)³), x > −2. Then y = 3ln(x+2) and y′ = 3/(x+2). For another log base b multiply by 1/ln b.", "If the cube instead belongs to the logarithm value, y = [log_b(x+2)]³ has derivative 3[log_b(x+2)]²/[(x+2)ln b]. These are different functions. The key’s 4(a) entry 2cosh2x belongs to 1(h), while 1(h)’s entry 3/(x+2) fits the natural log of the cubed argument.", "(b) Chain rule gives y′ = 3sinh3x, for all real x.", "(c) Let u = cos x, u′ = −sin x. Since d(arsinh u)/du = 1/√(1+u²), y′ = −sin x/√(1+cos²x), for all real x.", "(d) u = x/2; artanh requires |u| < 1, so |x| < 2. Then y′ = (1/2)/(1 − x²/4) = 2/(4 − x²).", "(e) ln(coth x) requires coth x > 0, giving x > 0. Then y′ = −cosech²x/coth x = −1/(sinh x cosh x) = −2cosech2x.", "(f) Principal arcosh x and √(x² − 1) require x ≥ 1. On x > 1, the product rule gives arcosh x + x/√(x² − 1), while the root derivative is x/√(x² − 1).", "The radical terms cancel, giving y′ = arcosh x for x > 1. At x = 1, y = 0 and the right derivative is 0; this is an endpoint one-sided derivative, not a two-sided derivative on an open real domain."], "solution": "(a) The printed log(x+2)³ is ambiguous. Under the key’s natural-log interpretation of a cubed argument, y = ln((x+2)³), x > −2. Then y = 3ln(x+2) and y′ = 3/(x+2). For another log base b multiply by 1/ln b.\nIf the cube instead belongs to the logarithm value, y = [log_b(x+2)]³ has derivative 3[log_b(x+2)]²/[(x+2)ln b]. These are different functions. The key’s 4(a) entry 2cosh2x belongs to 1(h), while 1(h)’s entry 3/(x+2) fits the natural log of the cubed argument.\n(b) Chain rule gives y′ = 3sinh3x, for all real x.\n(c) Let u = cos x, u′ = −sin x. Since d(arsinh u)/du = 1/√(1+u²), y′ = −sin x/√(1+cos²x), for all real x.\n(d) u = x/2; artanh requires |u| < 1, so |x| < 2. Then y′ = (1/2)/(1 − x²/4) = 2/(4 − x²).\n(e) ln(coth x) requires coth x > 0, giving x > 0. Then y′ = −cosech²x/coth x = −1/(sinh x cosh x) = −2cosech2x.\n(f) Principal arcosh x and √(x² − 1) require x ≥ 1. On x > 1, the product rule gives arcosh x + x/√(x² − 1), while the root derivative is x/√(x² − 1).\nThe radical terms cancel, giving y′ = arcosh x for x > 1. At x = 1, y = 0 and the right derivative is 0; this is an endpoint one-sided derivative, not a two-sided derivative on an open real domain.", "answer": "(a) 3/(x+2) under the stated natural-log, cubed-argument interpretation; other interpretations explained above. (b) 3sinh3x; (c) −sin x/√(1+cos²x); (d) 2/(4−x²); (e) −2cosech2x; (f) arcosh x, with the original domains.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 98, "jpg": "053_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Exercise 3.5 Questions 1–6 only; no separate Question 7 key, answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "Question 4(a) has ambiguous cube placement and no log base. The natural-log, cubed-argument interpretation matches the key’s misplaced 1(h) answer; both interpretations and a general log base are explained."}, {"id": "ex-3-5-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "A research group (used hospital records) developed the approximate mathematical model related to systolic blood pressure and age is: \\(p(x)=40+25\\ln(x+1)\\), 0 ≤ x ≤ 65, where p(x) is the pressure measured in millimeters of mercury and x is age in years. What is the rate of change of pressure at the end of 10 years? at the end of 30 years? at the end of 60 years?", "steps": ["This is an approximate textbook model on ages 0 ≤ x ≤ 65. Its derivative describes model pressure change per year, not a medical diagnosis.", "Differentiate 40 + 25ln(x+1): the constant gives zero and the logarithm gives 1/(x+1).", "Thus p′(x) = 25/(x+1) millimeters of mercury per year for interior ages, with one-sided endpoint rates where applicable.", "At x = 10, p′(10) = 25/11 ≈ 2.2727 mmHg/year.", "At x = 30, p′(30) = 25/31 ≈ 0.80645 mmHg/year.", "At x = 60, p′(60) = 25/61 ≈ 0.40984 mmHg/year.", "All three are positive, so modeled pressure is increasing; the rate decreases with age because its denominator increases. The key’s 2.27,0.81,0.41 are consistent rounded values."], "solution": "This is an approximate textbook model on ages 0 ≤ x ≤ 65. Its derivative describes model pressure change per year, not a medical diagnosis.\nDifferentiate 40 + 25ln(x+1): the constant gives zero and the logarithm gives 1/(x+1).\nThus p′(x) = 25/(x+1) millimeters of mercury per year for interior ages, with one-sided endpoint rates where applicable.\nAt x = 10, p′(10) = 25/11 ≈ 2.2727 mmHg/year.\nAt x = 30, p′(30) = 25/31 ≈ 0.80645 mmHg/year.\nAt x = 60, p′(60) = 25/61 ≈ 0.40984 mmHg/year.\nAll three are positive, so modeled pressure is increasing; the rate decreases with age because its denominator increases. The key’s 2.27,0.81,0.41 are consistent rounded values.", "answer": "p′(x) = 25/(x+1) mmHg/year. At ages 10,30,60: approximately 2.27,0.81,0.41 mmHg/year.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Exercise 3.5 Questions 1–6 only; no separate Question 7 key, answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-3-5-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "A single cholera bacterium divides every 0.5 hour to produce two complete cholera bacteria. If we start with a colony of 5,000 bacteria, then after t hours there will be \\(A(t)=5000\\,2^{2t}\\) bacteria. Find A′(t), A′(1) and A′(5). Interpret the results.", "steps": ["One doubling every 0.5 hour means 2t doublings after t hours, so A(t) = 5000·2^(2t). This agrees with the source model.", "For an exponential a^u, the derivative is a^u ln a times u′. Here a = 2 and u = 2t, so u′ = 2.", "Thus A′(t) = 10000ln2·2^(2t) bacteria per hour, for t ≥ 0 under the population model.", "At t = 1, 2^(2t) = 4. Hence A′(1) = 40000ln2 ≈ 27,725.887 bacteria/hour, about 27,726.", "At t = 5, 2^(2t) = 1024. Hence A′(5) = 10,240,000ln2 ≈ 7,097,827.129 bacteria/hour, about 7,097,827.", "These are instantaneous rates of increase, rather than population counts. The populations themselves are A(1) = 20,000 and A(5) = 5,120,000 bacteria.", "The exact finite one-hour increase is A(t+1) − A(t) = 3A(t), which differs from the instantaneous derivative 2ln2·A(t)."], "solution": "One doubling every 0.5 hour means 2t doublings after t hours, so A(t) = 5000·2^(2t). This agrees with the source model.\nFor an exponential a^u, the derivative is a^u ln a times u′. Here a = 2 and u = 2t, so u′ = 2.\nThus A′(t) = 10000ln2·2^(2t) bacteria per hour, for t ≥ 0 under the population model.\nAt t = 1, 2^(2t) = 4. Hence A′(1) = 40000ln2 ≈ 27,725.887 bacteria/hour, about 27,726.\nAt t = 5, 2^(2t) = 1024. Hence A′(5) = 10,240,000ln2 ≈ 7,097,827.129 bacteria/hour, about 7,097,827.\nThese are instantaneous rates of increase, rather than population counts. The populations themselves are A(1) = 20,000 and A(5) = 5,120,000 bacteria.\nThe exact finite one-hour increase is A(t+1) − A(t) = 3A(t), which differs from the instantaneous derivative 2ln2·A(t).", "answer": "A′(t) = 10000ln2·2^(2t) bacteria/hour. A′(1) ≈ 27,726 and A′(5) ≈ 7,097,827 bacteria/hour.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Exercise 3.5 Questions 1–6 only; no separate Question 7 key, answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-3-5-q7", "qNo": "7", "question": "Use MAPLE command “diff” to differentiate all the functions given in Q.3 and Q.4.", "steps": ["Maple commands differentiate the exact Q.3 and Q.4 expressions. Keep original-domain restrictions and source ambiguities from those worked solutions; a symbolic output does not make an undefined real logarithm valid.", "Q.3(a): diff(x^3*ln(sqrt(x)),x); gives (x²/2)(1 + 3ln x), x > 0.", "Q.3(b): diff(x^2*sqrt(ln(x)),x); gives x(4ln x + 1)/(2√ln x), x > 1.", "Q.3(c): diff(ln(sqrt((x^2+1)/(x^2-1))),x); gives 2x/(1 − x⁴), |x| > 1.", "Q.3(d): diff(ln(x-sqrt(x^2+1)),x); can give the formal result −1/√(x²+1). The printed real logarithm has no real domain. That derivative belongs to the separately stated real alternative ln|x−√(x²+1)|.", "Q.3(e): diff(exp(-2*x)*cos(2*x),x); gives −2e^(−2x)(cos2x + sin2x).", "Q.3(f): diff(x^2*exp(sin(x)),x); gives xe^(sin x)(xcos x + 2).", "Q.4(a), under the stated natural-log and cubed-argument interpretation: diff(ln((x+2)^3),x); gives 3/(x+2), x > −2. For a general base b, use ln((x+2)^3)/ln(b).", "Q.4(b): diff(cosh(3*x),x); gives 3sinh3x.", "Q.4(c): diff(arcsinh(cos(x)),x); gives −sin x/√(1+cos²x).", "Q.4(d): diff(arctanh(x/2),x); gives 2/(4−x²), |x| < 2.", "Q.4(e): diff(ln(coth(x)),x); gives −1/(sinh x cosh x) = −2cosech2x, x > 0.", "Q.4(f): diff(x*arccosh(x)-sqrt(x^2-1),x); gives arcosh x, x > 1."], "solution": "Maple commands differentiate the exact Q.3 and Q.4 expressions. Keep original-domain restrictions and source ambiguities from those worked solutions; a symbolic output does not make an undefined real logarithm valid.\nQ.3(a): diff(x^3*ln(sqrt(x)),x); gives (x²/2)(1 + 3ln x), x > 0.\nQ.3(b): diff(x^2*sqrt(ln(x)),x); gives x(4ln x + 1)/(2√ln x), x > 1.\nQ.3(c): diff(ln(sqrt((x^2+1)/(x^2-1))),x); gives 2x/(1 − x⁴), |x| > 1.\nQ.3(d): diff(ln(x-sqrt(x^2+1)),x); can give the formal result −1/√(x²+1). The printed real logarithm has no real domain. That derivative belongs to the separately stated real alternative ln|x−√(x²+1)|.\nQ.3(e): diff(exp(-2*x)*cos(2*x),x); gives −2e^(−2x)(cos2x + sin2x).\nQ.3(f): diff(x^2*exp(sin(x)),x); gives xe^(sin x)(xcos x + 2).\nQ.4(a), under the stated natural-log and cubed-argument interpretation: diff(ln((x+2)^3),x); gives 3/(x+2), x > −2. For a general base b, use ln((x+2)^3)/ln(b).\nQ.4(b): diff(cosh(3*x),x); gives 3sinh3x.\nQ.4(c): diff(arcsinh(cos(x)),x); gives −sin x/√(1+cos²x).\nQ.4(d): diff(arctanh(x/2),x); gives 2/(4−x²), |x| < 2.\nQ.4(e): diff(ln(coth(x)),x); gives −1/(sinh x cosh x) = −2cosech2x, x > 0.\nQ.4(f): diff(x*arccosh(x)-sqrt(x^2-1),x); gives arcosh x, x > 1.", "answer": "All twelve corresponding Maple commands and derivatives are given above, with real-domain restrictions and the clarified alternative for Q.3(d).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}];
MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[4].unreviewedProblems=MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[4].problems;MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[4].problems=math12Exercise35;MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[4].title=MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[4].exercise="Exercise 3.5";
MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[5].problems=[{"id": "ex-3-project-balloon", "qNo": "Project", "question": "Take a spherical balloon or ball and inflate it. Given \\(V=\\frac43\\pi r^3\\), find dV/dr and evaluate when r = 3.", "steps": ["Volume V depends on radius r, with r ≥ 0. The requested rate is with respect to radius.", "Differentiate V(r) = (4π/3)r³ using the power rule.", "dV/dr = (4π/3)·3r² = 4πr².", "At r = 3, substitute r² = 9 to obtain dV/dr = 36π ≈ 113.0973 square length units.", "If radius is measured in a particular length unit, the rate has units volume per radius, equivalent to that unit squared. The source does not specify centimeters or another length unit.", "Check geometrically: a small radius increase Δr adds an approximately thin spherical shell of volume 4πr²Δr, agreeing with the derivative.", "If a time rate is also needed, chain rule gives dV/dt = 4πr² dr/dt. No numerical dr/dt is given, so the source task determines dV/dr rather than a numerical volume-per-time rate."], "answer": "\\(\\frac{dV}{dr}=4\\pi r^2\\). At r = 3: 36π ≈ 113.0973 square length units.", "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/balloon-source100.jpg", "caption": "Spherical balloon — Project", "alt": "Original page 100 project balloon illustration", "displayWidth": 110}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 100, "jpg": "054_page_2.jpg", "verification": "full project and its exact illustration visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "solution": "Volume V depends on radius r, with r ≥ 0. The requested rate is with respect to radius.\nDifferentiate V(r) = (4π/3)r³ using the power rule.\ndV/dr = (4π/3)·3r² = 4πr².\nAt r = 3, substitute r² = 9 to obtain dV/dr = 36π ≈ 113.0973 square length units.\nIf radius is measured in a particular length unit, the rate has units volume per radius, equivalent to that unit squared. The source does not specify centimeters or another length unit.\nCheck geometrically: a small radius increase Δr adds an approximately thin spherical shell of volume 4πr²Δr, agreeing with the derivative.\nIf a time rate is also needed, chain rule gives dV/dt = 4πr² dr/dt. No numerical dr/dt is given, so the source task determines dV/dr rather than a numerical volume-per-time rate."}];MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[5].title=MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[5].exercise="Project — Spherical Balloon";
MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[6].problems=[{"id": "ex-3-review-i", "qNo": "i", "question": "If \\(f(t)=2t^2+3t+2\\), then f(−3) is: (a) 9 (b) 11 (c) 21 (d) 29", "steps": ["The input is −3; evaluate the function itself rather than its derivative.", "Square the input first: (−3)² = 9, so 2t² = 18.", "The linear term is 3(−3) = −9; the constant is +2.", "Add 18 − 9 + 2 = 11. Choose (b)."], "solution": "The input is −3; evaluate the function itself rather than its derivative.\nSquare the input first: (−3)² = 9, so 2t² = 18.\nThe linear term is 3(−3) = −9; the constant is +2.\nAdd 18 − 9 + 2 = 11. Choose (b).", "answer": "Option (b)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (i); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-3-review-ii", "qNo": "ii", "question": "The average rate of change for \\(f(x)=x^2-6x+5\\) is _____ if x increases in [1,3]. (a) 1 (b) −1 (c) 2 (d) −2", "steps": ["Average rate over [1,3] is [f(3) − f(1)]/(3 − 1).", "f(1) = 1 − 6 + 5 = 0, and f(3) = 9 − 18 + 5 = −4.", "The numerator is −4 and the interval length is 2.", "Divide: −4/2 = −2. Choose (d)."], "solution": "Average rate over [1,3] is [f(3) − f(1)]/(3 − 1).\nf(1) = 1 − 6 + 5 = 0, and f(3) = 9 − 18 + 5 = −4.\nThe numerator is −4 and the interval length is 2.\nDivide: −4/2 = −2. Choose (d).", "answer": "Option (d)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (ii); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-3-review-iii", "qNo": "iii", "question": "If y = f(x), then f′(x) = (a) \\(\\lim_{\\Delta x\\to0}\\frac{f(x)-f(\\Delta x)}{\\Delta x}\\) (b) \\(\\lim_{\\Delta x\\to0}\\frac{f(x+\\Delta x)-f(x)}{\\Delta x}\\) (c) \\(\\lim_{\\Delta x\\to0}\\frac{f(x-\\Delta x)+f(x)}{\\Delta x}\\) (d) \\(\\lim_{\\Delta x\\to0}\\frac{f(x-\\Delta x)+f(x)}{f(x)}\\)", "steps": ["Start from the change in input Δx and matching change in output f(x + Δx) − f(x).", "For Δx ≠ 0, divide output change by input change.", "The derivative is the limit of that quotient as Δx tends to zero, provided the limit exists.", "Only (b) has the correct difference, increment and denominator."], "solution": "Start from the change in input Δx and matching change in output f(x + Δx) − f(x).\nFor Δx ≠ 0, divide output change by input change.\nThe derivative is the limit of that quotient as Δx tends to zero, provided the limit exists.\nOnly (b) has the correct difference, increment and denominator.", "answer": "Option (b)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (iii); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-3-review-iv", "qNo": "iv", "question": "If \\(f(t)=2t^3-3t^2+4\\), then f′(3) is: (a) 54 (b) 45 (c) 36 (d) 27", "steps": ["Differentiate before substituting t = 3.", "The power rule gives f′(t) = 6t² − 6t; the constant gives zero.", "At t = 3, the two terms are 54 and −18.", "Their sum is 36. Choose (c)."], "solution": "Differentiate before substituting t = 3.\nThe power rule gives f′(t) = 6t² − 6t; the constant gives zero.\nAt t = 3, the two terms are 54 and −18.\nTheir sum is 36. Choose (c).", "answer": "Option (c)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (iv); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-3-review-v", "qNo": "v", "question": "If \\(f(x)=\\frac{h(x)}{g(x)}\\), then f′(x) = (a) \\(\\frac{h′(x)-g′(x)}{[g(x)]^2}\\) (b) \\(\\frac{g(x)h′(x)-h(x)g′(x)}{[g(x)]^2}\\) (c) \\(\\frac{f′(x)g(x)+g(x)f′(x)}{[g(x)]^2}\\) (d) \\(\\frac{f′(x)g(x)-f(x)g′(x)}{[g(x)]^2}\\)", "steps": ["Identify numerator h and denominator g, and require g(x) ≠ 0.", "The quotient rule differentiates the numerator in its first product and the denominator in its second.", "The numerator is gh′ − hg′; divide it by g².", "This is option (b). Options that introduce f′ inside the formula do not give the explicit quotient derivative."], "solution": "Identify numerator h and denominator g, and require g(x) ≠ 0.\nThe quotient rule differentiates the numerator in its first product and the denominator in its second.\nThe numerator is gh′ − hg′; divide it by g².\nThis is option (b). Options that introduce f′ inside the formula do not give the explicit quotient derivative.", "answer": "Option (b)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (v); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-3-review-vi", "qNo": "vi", "question": "If \\(h(t)=\\sqrt{t^3}\\), then h′(t) is: (a) \\(\\frac32\\sqrt t\\) (b) \\(\\frac23\\sqrt t\\) (c) \\(\\frac{3t}{2\\sqrt{t^2}}\\) (d) \\(\\frac{3t^2}{2\\sqrt{t^3}}\\)", "steps": ["The real function has t ≥ 0. On t > 0, write √(t³) = t^(3/2).", "Power rule gives h′(t) = (3/2)√t, which is option (a).", "Chain rule gives 3t²/[2√(t³)], option (d); for t > 0 this simplifies to the same value.", "Both (a) and (d) are correct on t > 0, although the key selects (d). At t = 0 the right-hand derivative is 0, obtained from the original function; expression (d) is undefined there."], "solution": "The real function has t ≥ 0. On t > 0, write √(t³) = t^(3/2).\nPower rule gives h′(t) = (3/2)√t, which is option (a).\nChain rule gives 3t²/[2√(t³)], option (d); for t > 0 this simplifies to the same value.\nBoth (a) and (d) are correct on t > 0, although the key selects (d). At t = 0 the right-hand derivative is 0, obtained from the original function; expression (d) is undefined there.", "answer": "Option (d) — also (a) on t > 0", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (vi); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "Two printed options are equivalent on the stated derivative domain; the key-selected option and the equivalent option are both identified."}, {"id": "ex-3-review-vii", "qNo": "vii", "question": "If \\(f(x)=x\\tan x\\), then f′(x) = (a) \\(\\tan x+x\\sec^2x\\) (b) \\(x\\tan x-x\\sec^2x\\) (c) \\(\\sec^2x\\) (d) \\(\\tan x+x[1+\\tan^2x]\\)", "steps": ["Require cos x ≠ 0 so the tangent function is defined.", "Product rule gives (x)′tan x + x(tan x)′ = tan x + xsec²x.", "Option (a) matches this directly. The identity sec²x = 1 + tan²x converts it into option (d).", "Therefore both (a) and (d) are equivalent correct answers; the key selects (a)."], "solution": "Require cos x ≠ 0 so the tangent function is defined.\nProduct rule gives (x)′tan x + x(tan x)′ = tan x + xsec²x.\nOption (a) matches this directly. The identity sec²x = 1 + tan²x converts it into option (d).\nTherefore both (a) and (d) are equivalent correct answers; the key selects (a).", "answer": "Option (a) — also (d)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (vii); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "Two printed options are equivalent on the stated derivative domain; the key-selected option and the equivalent option are both identified."}, {"id": "ex-3-review-viii", "qNo": "viii", "question": "\\(\\frac{d}{dy}\\cos^{-1}y=\\) (a) \\(\\frac1{\\sqrt{y^2-1}}\\) (b) \\(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2-y^2}}\\) (c) \\(\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}\\) (d) \\(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1+y^2}}\\)", "steps": ["cos⁻¹y denotes principal arccos, with input y.", "Its derivative is negative because principal arccos decreases as its input increases.", "On −1 < y < 1, the derivative is −1/√(1 − y²).", "Choose (c). At y = ±1 the function exists but has no finite derivative. The source’s unrelated x symbols in other options are retained."], "solution": "cos⁻¹y denotes principal arccos, with input y.\nIts derivative is negative because principal arccos decreases as its input increases.\nOn −1 < y < 1, the derivative is −1/√(1 − y²).\nChoose (c). At y = ±1 the function exists but has no finite derivative. The source’s unrelated x symbols in other options are retained.", "answer": "Option (c)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (viii); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-3-review-ix", "qNo": "ix", "question": "\\(\\frac{-1}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2+1}}=\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ \\ \\ )\\) (a) \\(\\sec^{-1}x\\) (b) \\(\\cos^{-1}x\\) (c) \\(\\text{cosech}^{-1}x\\) (d) \\(\\tanh^{-1}x\\)", "steps": ["The absolute value and √(x² + 1) identify the inverse hyperbolic cosecant rule.", "Let y = arsinh(1/x), the real inverse cosech for x ≠ 0.", "Chain rule gives y′ = (−1/x²)/√(1 + 1/x²) = −1/[|x|√(x² + 1)].", "Choose (c), inverse cosech, on x ≠ 0."], "solution": "The absolute value and √(x² + 1) identify the inverse hyperbolic cosecant rule.\nLet y = arsinh(1/x), the real inverse cosech for x ≠ 0.\nChain rule gives y′ = (−1/x²)/√(1 + 1/x²) = −1/[|x|√(x² + 1)].\nChoose (c), inverse cosech, on x ≠ 0.", "answer": "Option (c)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (ix); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-3-review-x", "qNo": "x", "question": "If f(x) = ln x, then f′(x) = (a) \\(\\frac1{\\ln x}\\) (b) \\(\\frac2x\\) (c) \\(\\frac1{x^2}\\) (d) \\(\\frac1x\\)", "steps": ["The real natural logarithm requires x > 0.", "Its derivative rule is d(ln x)/dx = 1/x.", "The derivative is the reciprocal of its input, rather than the reciprocal of its output ln x.", "Choose (d), for x > 0."], "solution": "The real natural logarithm requires x > 0.\nIts derivative rule is d(ln x)/dx = 1/x.\nThe derivative is the reciprocal of its input, rather than the reciprocal of its output ln x.\nChoose (d), for x > 0.", "answer": "Option (d)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 99, "jpg": "054_page_1.jpg", "verification": "complete question and all four options visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 334, "scope": "Review Exercise 3 Question 1 (x); answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}];MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[6].title=MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[6].exercise="Review Exercise 3";
const math12Exercise32Questions=["Differentiate f(x) + g(x) and f(x) − g(x) if: (a) f(x) = 3x + 7, g(x) = 6x² + 2x − 3; (b) f(x) = 17x² − 15, \\(g(x)=\\frac13x^2+\\frac12x-5\\); (c) \\(f(x)=x^3-\\frac32\\), g(x) = 3x³ − 4x² + 2; (d) f(x) = 4x³ − 5x, \\(g(x)=\\frac35x^2-2x\\).", "Use the product rule to find out the derivative of the following functions: (a) y = (x² − 2)(3x + 1); (b) y = (7x⁴ + 2x)(x² − 4); (c) \\(y=(2x-3)(\\sqrt{x}-1)\\); (d) \\(y=(-3\\sqrt{x}+6)(4\\sqrt{x}-2)\\).", "Use the quotient rule to find the derivative of the following functions: (a) \\(y=\\frac{3x-5}{x-4}\\); (b) \\(y=\\frac{-x^2+6x}{4x^3+1}\\); (c) \\(y=\\frac{5x+6}{\\sqrt{x}}\\); (d) \\(f(p)=\\frac{(2p+3)(4p-1)}{3p+2}\\).", "Find an equation of a tangent line to the graph of the function at the particular point in the following problems: (a) f(x) = 3x − 7 at (3, 2); (b) f(x) = x³ at x = −1/2; (c) \\(f(x)=\\frac1{x+3}\\) at x = 2; (d) \\(f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-2}\\) at (3, 3).", "For a thin lens of constant focal length P, the object distance x and the image distance y are related by the formula \\(\\frac1x+\\frac1y=\\frac1P\\). (a) Solve the above equation for y in terms of x and P. (b) Determine the rate of change of y with respect to x."];
