const math12Exercise32Steps=[["Use \\((f+g)′=f′+g′\\) and \\((f-g)′=f′-g′\\). Differentiate each term before combining.", "(a) f′ = 3 and g′ = 12x + 2. Sum: 12x + 5. Difference: 1 − 12x.", "(b) f′ = 34x and g′ = 2x/3 + 1/2. Sum: \\(\\frac{104}3x+\\frac12\\). Difference: \\(\\frac{100}3x-\\frac12\\).", "(c) f′ = 3x² and g′ = 9x² − 8x. Sum: 12x² − 8x. Difference: −6x² + 8x.", "(d) f′ = 12x² − 5 and g′ = 6x/5 − 2. Sum: \\(12x^2+\\frac65x-7\\). Difference: \\(12x^2-\\frac65x-3\\).", "All functions here are polynomials plus constants, so these derivatives hold for every real x."], ["Use \\((uv)′=u′v+uv′\\), keeping both terms. For square roots, work with x > 0 and use (√x)′ = 1/(2√x).", "(a) u = x² − 2, v = 3x + 1: y′ = 2x(3x + 1) + 3(x² − 2) = 9x² + 2x − 6.", "(b) u′ = 28x³ + 2, v′ = 2x. Thus y′ = (28x³ + 2)(x² − 4) + (7x⁴ + 2x)2x = 42x⁵ − 112x³ + 6x² − 8.", "(c) \\(y′=2(\\sqrt{x}-1)+\\frac{2x-3}{2\\sqrt{x}}=3\\sqrt{x}-2-\\frac3{2\\sqrt{x}}\\), for x > 0.", "(d) \\(y′=\\frac{-3}{2\\sqrt{x}}(4\\sqrt{x}-2)+(-3\\sqrt{x}+6)\\frac2{\\sqrt{x}}=\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{x}}-12\\), for x > 0.", "Check by expansion: (a) y = 3x³ + x² − 6x − 2; (b) y = 7x⁶ − 28x⁴ + 2x³ − 8x; (c) y = 2x√x − 2x − 3√x + 3; (d) y = −12x + 30√x − 12. Their derivatives agree.", "Parts (c) and (d) are defined at x = 0, but their right difference quotients are unbounded there. No finite ordinary derivative is added at that endpoint."], ["Use \\((u/v)′=\\frac{vu′-uv′}{v^2}\\), wherever the original denominator is nonzero.", "(a) \\(y′=\\frac{3(x-4)-(3x-5)}{(x-4)^2}=\\frac{-7}{(x-4)^2}\\), x ≠ 4.", "(b) u = −x² + 6x, u′ = −2x + 6; v = 4x³ + 1, v′ = 12x². The numerator is (4x³ + 1)(−2x + 6) − (−x² + 6x)12x².", "Expand that numerator: −8x⁴ + 24x³ − 2x + 6 + 12x⁴ − 72x³ = 4x⁴ − 48x³ − 2x + 6. Divide by (4x³ + 1)²; exclude x = −∛(1/4).", "(c) \\(y′=\\frac{5\\sqrt{x}-(5x+6)/(2\\sqrt{x})}{x}=\\frac{5x-6}{2x\\sqrt{x}}\\), x > 0.", "(d) Expand u = (2p + 3)(4p − 1) = 8p² + 10p − 3, so u′ = 16p + 10. With v = 3p + 2, v′ = 3, the numerator is (3p + 2)(16p + 10) − 3(8p² + 10p − 3) = 24p² + 32p + 29.", "Thus \\(f′(p)=\\frac{24p^2+32p+29}{(3p+2)^2}\\), p ≠ −2/3. The original denominator restrictions apply to every answer."], ["Find the point (a, f(a)), compute m = f′(a), and use y − f(a) = m(x − a).", "(a) f(3) = 2 and f′ = 3. Therefore y − 2 = 3(x − 3), giving y = 3x − 7.", "(b) a = −1/2, f(a) = −1/8, and f′(a) = 3a² = 3/4. Hence y + 1/8 = (3/4)(x + 1/2), giving y = 3x/4 + 1/4, or 3x − 4y + 1 = 0.", "(c) f(2) = 1/5 and f′(x) = −1/(x + 3)², so m = −1/25. Thus y − 1/5 = −(x − 2)/25, or x + 25y − 7 = 0.", "(d) f(3) = 3 and \\(f′(x)=\\frac{(x-2)-x}{(x-2)^2}=\\frac{-2}{(x-2)^2}\\). At x = 3 the slope is −2. Hence y − 3 = −2(x − 3), giving y = −2x + 9.", "The rational functions are differentiable at the specified points; (c) excludes x = −3 and (d) excludes x = 2. Each tangent passes through its corresponding point."], ["The original equation requires x ≠ 0, y ≠ 0 and constant P ≠ 0. Rearrange: \\(\\frac1y=\\frac1P-\\frac1x=\\frac{x-P}{Px}\\).", "Invert to obtain \\(y=\\frac{Px}{x-P}\\). The additional restriction x ≠ P is necessary: at x = P no finite y satisfies the original equation.", "Treat P as constant. The quotient rule gives \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(x-P)P-Px}{(x-P)^2}=\\frac{-P^2}{(x-P)^2}\\).", "Check by rewriting y = P + P²/(x − P). Its derivative is again −P²/(x − P)².", "The derivative is negative for real nonzero P and allowable x. It describes the change of image distance per change of object distance within this model; apply any physical distance/sign restrictions separately."]];
const math12Exercise32Answers=["(a) 12x + 5; 1 − 12x. (b) \\(\\frac{104}3x+\\frac12\\); \\(\\frac{100}3x-\\frac12\\). (c) 12x² − 8x; −6x² + 8x. (d) \\(12x^2+\\frac65x-7\\); \\(12x^2-\\frac65x-3\\). Each pair lists sum then difference.", "(a) 9x² + 2x − 6; (b) 42x⁵ − 112x³ + 6x² − 8; (c) \\(3\\sqrt{x}-2-\\frac3{2\\sqrt{x}}\\); (d) \\(\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{x}}-12\\). Parts (c),(d): x > 0.", "(a) \\(\\frac{-7}{(x-4)^2}\\); (b) \\(\\frac{4x^4-48x^3-2x+6}{(4x^3+1)^2}\\); (c) \\(\\frac{5x-6}{2x\\sqrt{x}}\\); (d) \\(\\frac{24p^2+32p+29}{(3p+2)^2}\\). Use the original-domain restrictions stated in the steps.", "(a) y = 3x − 7; (b) 3x − 4y + 1 = 0; (c) x + 25y − 7 = 0; (d) y = −2x + 9.", "\\(y=\\frac{Px}{x-P}\\); \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-P^2}{(x-P)^2}\\), with P ≠ 0, x ≠ 0, x ≠ P."];
MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[1].problems.forEach((p,index)=>Object.assign(p,{steps:math12Exercise32Steps[index],answer:math12Exercise32Answers[index],solution:math12Exercise32Steps[index].join('\n'),progressiveSolution:true,solutionOrigin:'SpaceBook learning enhancement',solutionStatus:'worked and checked',answerKeyReference:{pdf:'Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf',pdfPage:170,printedPage:332,scope:'Exercise 3.2 Questions 1–5 only; answer page not transcribed'}}));MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[1].problems[1].sourceClarification='The answer key prints the correct 9x² + 2x − 6, followed by an unrelated extra equality copied from Question 1(d). The independent product-rule solution uses the correct first result.';
MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[1].problems.push({"id": "ex-3-2-project", "qNo": "Project", "question": "Measure a ladder of length L feet. Its bottom moves away from the wall at 6 ft/min. How fast does its top move down when the bottom is 6 feet from the wall?", "steps": ["Take x as the horizontal distance from the wall and y as the height of the ladder top. The rigid ladder has fixed length L, so x² + y² = L².", "Differentiate with respect to time: \\(2x\\frac{dx}{dt}+2y\\frac{dy}{dt}=0\\). Thus \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}=-\\frac{x}{y}\\frac{dx}{dt}\\).", "At x = 6 feet and dx/dt = 6 ft/min, \\(y=\\sqrt{L^2-36}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}=\\frac{-36}{\\sqrt{L^2-36}}\\) ft/min.", "The negative sign means the height decreases. The downward speed is \\(\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{L^2-36}}\\) ft/min. A numerical answer requires the measured L > 6 feet; L = 6 is a degenerate horizontal position, where this finite-rate formula is undefined."], "answer": "Downward speed \\(\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{L^2-36}}\\) ft/min, for measured length L > 6 ft.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 76, "jpg": "042_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]});
const math12Exercise33=[{"id": "ex-3-3-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Find the derivative of the following functions w.r.t. involved independent variable: (a) \\(w=4(x^3-4x+2)^5\\); (b) \\(y=\\frac{(4x-x^3)^{11}}5\\); (c) \\(u=\\text{∛}(1-3t^2)\\); (d) \\(s=\\frac1{(3t+1)^7}\\).", "steps": ["For a constant multiple of a power, differentiate the outer power, retain the inner expression, then multiply by its derivative.", "(a) The inner derivative is 3x² − 4, so dw/dx = 4·5(x³ − 4x + 2)⁴(3x² − 4) = 20(x³ − 4x + 2)⁴(3x² − 4).", "(b) dy/dx = (11/5)(4x − x³)¹⁰(4 − 3x²). Both (a) and (b) are defined and differentiable for all real x.", "(c) Put r = ∛(1 − 3t²). For r ≠ 0, r³ = 1 − 3t² gives 3r²r′ = −6t, so \\(du/dt=\\frac{-2t}{[\\text{∛}(1-3t^2)]^2}\\).", "The cube-root function is real for every t, including negative radicands. Its finite derivative excludes t = ±1/√3, where the radicand vanishes with a nonzero derivative.", "(d) Write s = (3t + 1)⁻⁷. Then ds/dt = −7(3t + 1)⁻⁸·3 = −21/(3t + 1)⁸, with t ≠ −1/3."], "solution": "For a constant multiple of a power, differentiate the outer power, retain the inner expression, then multiply by its derivative.\n(a) The inner derivative is 3x² − 4, so dw/dx = 4·5(x³ − 4x + 2)⁴(3x² − 4) = 20(x³ − 4x + 2)⁴(3x² − 4).\n(b) dy/dx = (11/5)(4x − x³)¹⁰(4 − 3x²). Both (a) and (b) are defined and differentiable for all real x.\n(c) Put r = ∛(1 − 3t²). For r ≠ 0, r³ = 1 − 3t² gives 3r²r′ = −6t, so \\(du/dt=\\frac{-2t}{[\\text{∛}(1-3t^2)]^2}\\).\nThe cube-root function is real for every t, including negative radicands. Its finite derivative excludes t = ±1/√3, where the radicand vanishes with a nonzero derivative.\n(d) Write s = (3t + 1)⁻⁷. Then ds/dt = −7(3t + 1)⁻⁸·3 = −21/(3t + 1)⁸, with t ≠ −1/3.", "answer": "(a) 20(x³ − 4x + 2)⁴(3x² − 4); (b) (11/5)(4x − x³)¹⁰(4 − 3x²); (c) −2t/[∛(1 − 3t²)]², t ≠ ±1/√3; (d) −21/(3t + 1)⁸, t ≠ −1/3.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 81, "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 333, "scope": "Exercise 3.3 only; key discrepancies independently corrected"}}, {"id": "ex-3-3-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Determine the derivative f′(x) in each case: (a) \\(f(x)=(2x-5)^3(5x-7)\\); (b) \\(f(x)=\\frac{(x+2)^2}{x-1}\\); (c) \\(f(x)=\\left(\\frac{2x-5}{x-4}\\right)^4\\); (d) \\(f(x)=x\\sqrt{2x^2+11}\\).", "steps": ["(a) Use the product and chain rules: f′ = 6(2x − 5)²(5x − 7) + 5(2x − 5)³.", "Factor (2x − 5)². The remaining factor is 6(5x − 7) + 5(2x − 5) = 40x − 67; thus f′ = (2x − 5)²(40x − 67).", "(b) Quotient rule: \\(f′=\\frac{2(x+2)(x-1)-(x+2)^2}{(x-1)^2}=\\frac{(x+2)(x-4)}{(x-1)^2}=\\frac{x^2-2x-8}{(x-1)^2}\\), x ≠ 1.", "(c) For u = (2x − 5)/(x − 4), u′ = [2(x − 4) − (2x − 5)]/(x − 4)² = −3/(x − 4)².", "Apply the outer fourth power: \\(f′=4u^3u′=\\frac{-12(2x-5)^3}{(x-4)^5}\\), x ≠ 4.", "(d) Product rule: \\(f′=\\sqrt{2x^2+11}+x\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2x^2+11}}=\\frac{4x^2+11}{\\sqrt{2x^2+11}}\\). The radicand is positive for all real x."], "solution": "(a) Use the product and chain rules: f′ = 6(2x − 5)²(5x − 7) + 5(2x − 5)³.\nFactor (2x − 5)². The remaining factor is 6(5x − 7) + 5(2x − 5) = 40x − 67; thus f′ = (2x − 5)²(40x − 67).\n(b) Quotient rule: \\(f′=\\frac{2(x+2)(x-1)-(x+2)^2}{(x-1)^2}=\\frac{(x+2)(x-4)}{(x-1)^2}=\\frac{x^2-2x-8}{(x-1)^2}\\), x ≠ 1.\n(c) For u = (2x − 5)/(x − 4), u′ = [2(x − 4) − (2x − 5)]/(x − 4)² = −3/(x − 4)².\nApply the outer fourth power: \\(f′=4u^3u′=\\frac{-12(2x-5)^3}{(x-4)^5}\\), x ≠ 4.\n(d) Product rule: \\(f′=\\sqrt{2x^2+11}+x\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2x^2+11}}=\\frac{4x^2+11}{\\sqrt{2x^2+11}}\\). The radicand is positive for all real x.", "answer": "(a) (2x − 5)²(40x − 67); (b) (x² − 2x − 8)/(x − 1)²; (c) −12(2x − 5)³/(x − 4)⁵; (d) (4x² + 11)/√(2x² + 11). Use the domain restrictions in the steps.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 81, "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 333, "scope": "Exercise 3.3 only; key discrepancies independently corrected"}}, {"id": "ex-3-3-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Find dy/dx of the following function in terms of parameter t: (a) x = 1 + t², y = t³ + 2t² + 1; (b) x = 3at² + 2, y = 6t⁴ + 9; (c) \\(x=\\frac{a(1-t^2)}{1+t^2},\\quad y=\\frac{2bt}{1+t^2}\\); (d) \\(x=\\frac{3at}{1+t^3},\\quad y=\\frac{3at^2}{1+t^3}\\).", "steps": ["Compute x′(t) and y′(t), then use dy/dx = y′(t)/x′(t) where x′(t) ≠ 0.", "(a) x′ = 2t and y′ = 3t² + 4t. For t ≠ 0, dy/dx = (3t² + 4t)/(2t) = 3t/2 + 2.", "At t = 0 both parameter derivatives vanish. Cancellation is not valid there. The two branches y = 2x − 1 ± (x − 1)^(3/2), x ≥ 1, have the same limiting slope 2 at (1,1).", "(b) x′ = 6at and y′ = 24t³. Therefore dy/dx = 4t²/a for a ≠ 0, t ≠ 0. The key’s 12t²/a conflicts with the printed y = 6t⁴ + 9.", "For a ≠ 0, eliminate t in (b): y = 2(x − 2)²/(3a²) + 9. Its tangent is horizontal at t = 0, although the parameter ratio is 0/0 there. For a = 0 the x-coordinate is constant and this formula is unavailable.", "(c) x′ = −4at/(1 + t²)² and y′ = 2b(1 − t²)/(1 + t²)². Divide to obtain dy/dx = b(t² − 1)/(2at), for a ≠ 0 and t ≠ 0.", "(d) x′ = 3a(1 − 2t³)/(1 + t³)² and y′ = 3a(2t − t⁴)/(1 + t³)². Thus dy/dx = (2t − t⁴)/(1 − 2t³), requiring a ≠ 0, t ≠ −1 and 1 − 2t³ ≠ 0."], "solution": "Compute x′(t) and y′(t), then use dy/dx = y′(t)/x′(t) where x′(t) ≠ 0.\n(a) x′ = 2t and y′ = 3t² + 4t. For t ≠ 0, dy/dx = (3t² + 4t)/(2t) = 3t/2 + 2.\nAt t = 0 both parameter derivatives vanish. Cancellation is not valid there. The two branches y = 2x − 1 ± (x − 1)^(3/2), x ≥ 1, have the same limiting slope 2 at (1,1).\n(b) x′ = 6at and y′ = 24t³. Therefore dy/dx = 4t²/a for a ≠ 0, t ≠ 0. The key’s 12t²/a conflicts with the printed y = 6t⁴ + 9.\nFor a ≠ 0, eliminate t in (b): y = 2(x − 2)²/(3a²) + 9. Its tangent is horizontal at t = 0, although the parameter ratio is 0/0 there. For a = 0 the x-coordinate is constant and this formula is unavailable.\n(c) x′ = −4at/(1 + t²)² and y′ = 2b(1 − t²)/(1 + t²)². Divide to obtain dy/dx = b(t² − 1)/(2at), for a ≠ 0 and t ≠ 0.\n(d) x′ = 3a(1 − 2t³)/(1 + t³)² and y′ = 3a(2t − t⁴)/(1 + t³)². Thus dy/dx = (2t − t⁴)/(1 − 2t³), requiring a ≠ 0, t ≠ −1 and 1 − 2t³ ≠ 0.", "answer": "(a) 3t/2 + 2; (b) 4t²/a; (c) b(t² − 1)/(2at); (d) (2t − t⁴)/(1 − 2t³). Validity and exceptional parameter values are explained in the steps.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 81, "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 333, "scope": "Exercise 3.3 only; key discrepancies independently corrected"}, "sourceClarification": "The key prints 12t²/a for Question 3(b); the source y = 6t⁴ + 9 has derivative 24t³, so the correct ratio is 4t²/a."}, {"id": "ex-3-3-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "At a certain factor, the total cost of manufacturing q units during the daily production run is C(q) = 0.2q² + q + 900 dollars. From experience, it has been determined that approximately q(t) = t² + 100t units are manufactured during the first t hours of a production run. Compute the rate at which the total manufacturing cost is changing with respect to time one hour after production begins.", "steps": ["The cost depends on q, while production q depends on time. Use dC/dt = (dC/dq)(dq/dt).", "dC/dq = 0.4q + 1 dollars per production unit. dq/dt = 2t + 100 units/hour.", "At t = 1 hour, q = 1² + 100·1 = 101 and dq/dt = 102 units/hour.", "Thus dC/dq = 0.4·101 + 1 = 41.4 dollars/unit.", "Multiply: dC/dt = 41.4·102 = 4222.8 dollars/hour. The total manufacturing cost is increasing at that instantaneous rate."], "solution": "The cost depends on q, while production q depends on time. Use dC/dt = (dC/dq)(dq/dt).\ndC/dq = 0.4q + 1 dollars per production unit. dq/dt = 2t + 100 units/hour.\nAt t = 1 hour, q = 1² + 100·1 = 101 and dq/dt = 102 units/hour.\nThus dC/dq = 0.4·101 + 1 = 41.4 dollars/unit.\nMultiply: dC/dt = 41.4·102 = 4222.8 dollars/hour. The total manufacturing cost is increasing at that instantaneous rate.", "answer": "4222.8 dollars/hour, increasing.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 82, "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 333, "scope": "Exercise 3.3 only; key discrepancies independently corrected"}}, {"id": "ex-3-3-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "Use implicit differentiation to perform dy/dx for the following functions: (a) x² + y² = 25; (b) xy(2x + 3y) = 2; (c) (x + y)³ + 3y = 3; (d) \\(\\frac1y+\\frac1x=1\\).", "steps": ["(a) Differentiate x² + y² = 25: 2x + 2yy′ = 0, giving y′ = −x/y where y ≠ 0. At y = 0 the circle has vertical tangents.", "(b) Expand 2x²y + 3xy² = 2. Differentiate: 4xy + 2x²y′ + 3y² + 6xyy′ = 0.", "Therefore \\(y′=-\\frac{4xy+3y^2}{2x^2+6xy}\\), where the denominator is nonzero. The negative sign follows from moving the non-derivative terms to the other side.", "(c) Differentiate (x + y)³ + 3y = 3: 3(x + y)²(1 + y′) + 3y′ = 0.", "Collect y′ to obtain \\(y′=\\frac{-(x+y)^2}{(x+y)^2+1}\\). The denominator is strictly positive for real x and y.", "(d) Differentiate 1/y + 1/x = 1: −y′/y² − 1/x² = 0, hence y′ = −y²/x², with x ≠ 0 and y ≠ 0.", "Check (d) explicitly: y = x/(x − 1), x ≠ 0,1, and y′ = −1/(x − 1)². This agrees with the answer key’s −y²/x²."], "solution": "(a) Differentiate x² + y² = 25: 2x + 2yy′ = 0, giving y′ = −x/y where y ≠ 0. At y = 0 the circle has vertical tangents.\n(b) Expand 2x²y + 3xy² = 2. Differentiate: 4xy + 2x²y′ + 3y² + 6xyy′ = 0.\nTherefore \\(y′=-\\frac{4xy+3y^2}{2x^2+6xy}\\), where the denominator is nonzero. The negative sign follows from moving the non-derivative terms to the other side.\n(c) Differentiate (x + y)³ + 3y = 3: 3(x + y)²(1 + y′) + 3y′ = 0.\nCollect y′ to obtain \\(y′=\\frac{-(x+y)^2}{(x+y)^2+1}\\). The denominator is strictly positive for real x and y.\n(d) Differentiate 1/y + 1/x = 1: −y′/y² − 1/x² = 0, hence y′ = −y²/x², with x ≠ 0 and y ≠ 0.\nCheck (d) explicitly: y = x/(x − 1), x ≠ 0,1, and y′ = −1/(x − 1)². This agrees with the answer key’s −y²/x².", "answer": "(a) −x/y; (b) −(4xy + 3y²)/(2x² + 6xy); (c) −(x + y)²/[(x + y)² + 1]; (d) −y²/x². Apply the original relation and nonzero-denominator conditions.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 82, "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 333, "scope": "Exercise 3.3 only; key discrepancies independently corrected"}}, {"id": "ex-3-3-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "Arrange the following functions explicitly and implicitly to perform dy/dx: (a) x²y³ + y³ = 1; (b) xy + 2y = x²; (c) \\(x+\\frac1y=5\\); (d) xy − x = y + 2.", "steps": ["(a) Factor y³(x² + 1) = 1, so y = (x² + 1)^(−1/3). Explicitly, y′ = −2x/[3(x² + 1)^(4/3)], all real x.", "Implicitly, 2xy³ + 3y²(x² + 1)y′ = 0 gives y′ = −2xy/[3(x² + 1)]. Substituting the positive y above agrees with the explicit derivative.", "(b) y(x + 2) = x² gives y = x²/(x + 2), x ≠ −2. Quotient differentiation yields y′ = (x² + 4x)/(x + 2)².", "Implicitly, y + (x + 2)y′ = 2x gives y′ = (2x − y)/(x + 2), the same result after substitution.", "(c) 1/y = 5 − x gives y = 1/(5 − x), x ≠ 5. Explicitly y′ = 1/(5 − x)². Implicitly 1 − y′/y² = 0 gives y′ = y², which agrees.", "(d) y(x − 1) = x + 2 gives y = (x + 2)/(x − 1), x ≠ 1, and y′ = −3/(x − 1)².", "Implicitly, y + xy′ − 1 = y′ gives y′ = (1 − y)/(x − 1), which agrees after substitution."], "solution": "(a) Factor y³(x² + 1) = 1, so y = (x² + 1)^(−1/3). Explicitly, y′ = −2x/[3(x² + 1)^(4/3)], all real x.\nImplicitly, 2xy³ + 3y²(x² + 1)y′ = 0 gives y′ = −2xy/[3(x² + 1)]. Substituting the positive y above agrees with the explicit derivative.\n(b) y(x + 2) = x² gives y = x²/(x + 2), x ≠ −2. Quotient differentiation yields y′ = (x² + 4x)/(x + 2)².\nImplicitly, y + (x + 2)y′ = 2x gives y′ = (2x − y)/(x + 2), the same result after substitution.\n(c) 1/y = 5 − x gives y = 1/(5 − x), x ≠ 5. Explicitly y′ = 1/(5 − x)². Implicitly 1 − y′/y² = 0 gives y′ = y², which agrees.\n(d) y(x − 1) = x + 2 gives y = (x + 2)/(x − 1), x ≠ 1, and y′ = −3/(x − 1)².\nImplicitly, y + xy′ − 1 = y′ gives y′ = (1 − y)/(x − 1), which agrees after substitution.", "answer": "(a) −2x/[3(x² + 1)^(4/3)]; (b) (x² + 4x)/(x + 2)²; (c) 1/(5 − x)²; (d) −3/(x − 1)². Explicit and implicit forms agree.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 82, "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 333, "scope": "Exercise 3.3 only; key discrepancies independently corrected"}}, {"id": "ex-3-3-q7", "qNo": "7", "question": "Let \\(\\frac{u^2}{a^2}+\\frac{v^2}{b^2}=1\\), where a and b are nonzero constants. Find (a) du/dv; (b) dv/du.", "steps": ["a and b are fixed nonzero constants; the curve is an ellipse in coordinates u and v.", "(a) Treat u as a function of v. Differentiate: (2u/a²)(du/dv) + 2v/b² = 0.", "Solve: du/dv = −a²v/(b²u), wherever u ≠ 0.", "(b) Treat v as a function of u. Differentiate: 2u/a² + (2v/b²)(dv/du) = 0.", "Solve: dv/du = −b²u/(a²v), wherever v ≠ 0. The two derivatives are reciprocal where both are finite and nonzero. At zero denominators, use the appropriate tangent direction instead."], "solution": "a and b are fixed nonzero constants; the curve is an ellipse in coordinates u and v.\n(a) Treat u as a function of v. Differentiate: (2u/a²)(du/dv) + 2v/b² = 0.\nSolve: du/dv = −a²v/(b²u), wherever u ≠ 0.\n(b) Treat v as a function of u. Differentiate: 2u/a² + (2v/b²)(dv/du) = 0.\nSolve: dv/du = −b²u/(a²v), wherever v ≠ 0. The two derivatives are reciprocal where both are finite and nonzero. At zero denominators, use the appropriate tangent direction instead.", "answer": "(a) du/dv = −a²v/(b²u), u ≠ 0; (b) dv/du = −b²u/(a²v), v ≠ 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 82, "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 333, "scope": "Exercise 3.3 only; key discrepancies independently corrected"}}, {"id": "ex-3-3-q8", "qNo": "8", "question": "Determine the slope of the tangent line to the curve 3x² − 7y² + 14y = 27 at the point P(−3, 0).", "steps": ["Check the point: 3(−3)² − 7·0² + 14·0 = 27, so P is on the curve.", "Differentiate: 6x − 14yy′ + 14y′ = 0.", "Collect: (14 − 14y)y′ = −6x, giving y′ = 3x/[7(y − 1)] where y ≠ 1.", "At P(−3,0), y′ = −9/(−7) = 9/7.", "The requested slope is 9/7. As a learning extension, the tangent equation is y = (9/7)(x + 3)."], "solution": "Check the point: 3(−3)² − 7·0² + 14·0 = 27, so P is on the curve.\nDifferentiate: 6x − 14yy′ + 14y′ = 0.\nCollect: (14 − 14y)y′ = −6x, giving y′ = 3x/[7(y − 1)] where y ≠ 1.\nAt P(−3,0), y′ = −9/(−7) = 9/7.\nThe requested slope is 9/7. As a learning extension, the tangent equation is y = (9/7)(x + 3).", "answer": "Slope 9/7.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 82, "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 333, "scope": "Exercise 3.3 only; key discrepancies independently corrected"}}, {"id": "ex-3-3-q9", "qNo": "9", "question": "Suppose two motor boats leave from the same point at the same time. If one boat travels north at 15 miles per hour and the other boat travels east at 20 miles per hour. How fast will the distance between them be changing after 2 hours?", "steps": ["Let x be the eastward distance, y the northward distance and z the separation. After t hours, x = 20t and y = 15t, in miles.", "The right triangle gives z² = x² + y². Differentiating for t > 0: 2z dz/dt = 2x dx/dt + 2y dy/dt.", "At t = 2, x = 40 miles, y = 30 miles and z = √(40² + 30²) = 50 miles.", "Use dx/dt = 20 and dy/dt = 15 miles/hour: dz/dt = (40·20 + 30·15)/50 = 1250/50 = 25 miles/hour.", "The separation is increasing at 25 miles/hour. Independently, z(t) = √((20t)² + (15t)²) = 25t for t ≥ 0, confirming that rate for every t > 0."], "solution": "Let x be the eastward distance, y the northward distance and z the separation. After t hours, x = 20t and y = 15t, in miles.\nThe right triangle gives z² = x² + y². Differentiating for t > 0: 2z dz/dt = 2x dx/dt + 2y dy/dt.\nAt t = 2, x = 40 miles, y = 30 miles and z = √(40² + 30²) = 50 miles.\nUse dx/dt = 20 and dy/dt = 15 miles/hour: dz/dt = (40·20 + 30·15)/50 = 1250/50 = 25 miles/hour.\nThe separation is increasing at 25 miles/hour. Independently, z(t) = √((20t)² + (15t)²) = 25t for t ≥ 0, confirming that rate for every t > 0.", "answer": "25 miles/hour, increasing.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 82, "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 171, "printedPage": 333, "scope": "Exercise 3.3 only; key discrepancies independently corrected"}, "solutionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "Source boat diagram, reconstructed digitally", "diagram": {"type": "source-motor-boats", "title": "Motor boats"}}]}];
MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[2].unreviewedProblems=MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[2].problems;MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[2].problems=math12Exercise33;MATH_12_DATA[2].exercises[2].title='Exercise 3.3';
const math12Exercise31Revision=[{"id": "ex-3-1-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Find the average rate of change of the following functions over the indicated intervals:\n(a) y = x² + 4, from x = 2 to x = 3. (b) \\(y=x^2+\\frac13x\\), from x = −3 to x = 3. (c) s = 2t³ − 5t + 7, from t = 1 to t = 3. (d) \\(h=\\sqrt{2t}+4\\), from t = 8 to t = 8.5.", "steps": ["Use \\(\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}\\); each stated interval has distinct endpoints.", "(a) f(2) = 8 and f(3) = 13, so (13 − 8)/(3 − 2) = 5.", "(b) f(−3) = 9 − 1 = 8 and f(3) = 9 + 1 = 10. Thus (10 − 8)/(3 − (−3)) = 2/6 = 1/3.", "(c) s(1) = 2 − 5 + 7 = 4 and s(3) = 54 − 15 + 7 = 46. The average rate is (46 − 4)/(3 − 1) = 21.", "(d) h(8) = 8 and h(8.5) = √17 + 4. Thus the rate is \\(2(\\sqrt{17}-4)\\) ≈ 0.24621, or 0.25 to two decimal places."], "solution": "Use \\(\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}\\); each stated interval has distinct endpoints.\n(a) f(2) = 8 and f(3) = 13, so (13 − 8)/(3 − 2) = 5.\n(b) f(−3) = 9 − 1 = 8 and f(3) = 9 + 1 = 10. Thus (10 − 8)/(3 − (−3)) = 2/6 = 1/3.\n(c) s(1) = 2 − 5 + 7 = 4 and s(3) = 54 − 15 + 7 = 46. The average rate is (46 − 4)/(3 − 1) = 21.\n(d) h(8) = 8 and h(8.5) = √17 + 4. Thus the rate is \\(2(\\sqrt{17}-4)\\) ≈ 0.24621, or 0.25 to two decimal places.", "answer": "(a) 5; (b) 1/3; (c) 21; (d) 2(√17 − 4) ≈ 0.24621.", "diagram": null, "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 70, "jpg": "039_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "ex-3-1-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Use definition for the rate of change to find out the average rate of change over the specified interval:\n(a) s = 2t − 3, from t = 2 to t = 5. (b) y = x² − 6x + 8, from x = 3 to x = 3.1. (c) A = πr², from r = 2 to r = 2.1. (d) \\(h=\\sqrt t-9\\), from t = 9 to t = 16.", "steps": ["The definition divides the dependent-variable change by its corresponding nonzero input change.", "(a) s(2) = 1 and s(5) = 7; (7 − 1)/(5 − 2) = 2.", "(b) y(3) = −1 and y(3.1) = −0.99; (−0.99 + 1)/0.1 = 0.1.", "(c) \\(\\frac{\\pi(2.1)^2-\\pi(2)^2}{2.1-2}=\\frac{0.41\\pi}{0.1}=4.1\\pi\\).", "(d) h(9) = 3 − 9 = −6 and h(16) = 4 − 9 = −5. Thus (−5 + 6)/(16 − 9) = 1/7."], "solution": "The definition divides the dependent-variable change by its corresponding nonzero input change.\n(a) s(2) = 1 and s(5) = 7; (7 − 1)/(5 − 2) = 2.\n(b) y(3) = −1 and y(3.1) = −0.99; (−0.99 + 1)/0.1 = 0.1.\n(c) \\(\\frac{\\pi(2.1)^2-\\pi(2)^2}{2.1-2}=\\frac{0.41\\pi}{0.1}=4.1\\pi\\).\n(d) h(9) = 3 − 9 = −6 and h(16) = 4 − 9 = −5. Thus (−5 + 6)/(16 − 9) = 1/7.", "answer": "(a) 2; (b) 0.1; (c) 4.1π; (d) 1/7.", "diagram": null, "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 70, "jpg": "039_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "ex-3-1-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "A ball is thrown straight up. Its height after t seconds is given by h = −16t² + 80t. Use definition for the rate of change to determine the average velocity \\(\\frac{\\Delta h}{\\Delta t}\\): (a) from t = 2 to t = 2.1; (b) from t = 2 to t = 2.01.", "steps": ["For an increment u ≠ 0, \\(\\frac{h(t+u)-h(t)}u=-32t-16u+80\\). At t = 2 this becomes 16 − 16u.", "(a) u = 0.1 gives 16 − 1.6 = 14.4 height units per second.", "(b) u = 0.01 gives 16 − 0.16 = 15.84 height units per second. The smaller interval gives a value closer to the instantaneous velocity 16 at t = 2."], "solution": "For an increment u ≠ 0, \\(\\frac{h(t+u)-h(t)}u=-32t-16u+80\\). At t = 2 this becomes 16 − 16u.\n(a) u = 0.1 gives 16 − 1.6 = 14.4 height units per second.\n(b) u = 0.01 gives 16 − 0.16 = 15.84 height units per second. The smaller interval gives a value closer to the instantaneous velocity 16 at t = 2.", "answer": "(a) 14.4; (b) 15.84 height units per second.", "diagram": null, "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 70, "jpg": "039_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "ex-3-1-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "The rate of change of price is called inflation. The price p in rupees after t years is p(t) = 3t² + t + 1. Use definition for the rate of change to determine the average rate of change of inflation from t = 3 to t = 5 years. What the rate of change means? Explain.", "steps": ["Compute p(3) = 27 + 3 + 1 = 31 rupees and p(5) = 75 + 5 + 1 = 81 rupees.", "The interval is 2 years, so the average rate is (81 − 31)/2 = 25 rupees per year.", "This means the model price increases by 50 rupees across those two years, averaging 25 rupees of increase per year. It is a price-change rate, not a percentage inflation rate."], "solution": "Compute p(3) = 27 + 3 + 1 = 31 rupees and p(5) = 75 + 5 + 1 = 81 rupees.\nThe interval is 2 years, so the average rate is (81 − 31)/2 = 25 rupees per year.\nThis means the model price increases by 50 rupees across those two years, averaging 25 rupees of increase per year. It is a price-change rate, not a percentage inflation rate.", "answer": "25 rupees per year.", "diagram": null, "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 70, "jpg": "039_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "ex-3-1-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "A farmer plants x acres of sugar beets. The profit generated is f(x) = 1800x − 9x². Determine the average rate of change of the profit, when the planted area is between x = 20 acres and x = 50 acres. What the rate of change means? Explain.", "steps": ["The input increase is 50 − 20 = 30 acres. Profits are f(20) = 36,000 − 3,600 = 32,400 and f(50) = 90,000 − 22,500 = 67,500.", "The average rate is (67,500 − 32,400)/30 = 35,100/30 = 1,170 profit units per acre.", "Across this interval, an extra acre corresponds to an average profit increase of 1,170; this does not mean that every acre has the same marginal profit."], "solution": "The input increase is 50 − 20 = 30 acres. Profits are f(20) = 36,000 − 3,600 = 32,400 and f(50) = 90,000 − 22,500 = 67,500.\nThe average rate is (67,500 − 32,400)/30 = 35,100/30 = 1,170 profit units per acre.\nAcross this interval, an extra acre corresponds to an average profit increase of 1,170; this does not mean that every acre has the same marginal profit.", "answer": "1,170 profit units per acre.", "diagram": null, "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 70, "jpg": "039_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "ex-3-1-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "Use first principle rule to determine the derivative:\n(a) f(x) = 3x. (b) \\(f(x)=(5x+6)^{1/2}\\). (c) f(x) = x² + 1. (d) f(x) = 12 − x². (e) f(x) = 16x² − 7x. (f) \\(f(x)=\\frac7x\\).", "steps": ["Use \\(f'(x)=\\lim_{u\\to0}\\frac{f(x+u)-f(x)}u\\), simplifying only for u ≠ 0.", "(a) For 3x, the quotient is 3u/u = 3, so f′(x) = 3.", "(b) Multiply by the conjugate: \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+5u+6}-\\sqrt{5x+6}}u=\\frac5{\\sqrt{5x+5u+6}+\\sqrt{5x+6}}\\). Its limit is \\(\\frac5{2\\sqrt{5x+6}}\\), for x > −6/5.", "At the boundary x = −6/5 there is no finite ordinary derivative: the one-sided difference quotient grows without bound.", "For a quadratic Ax² + Bx + C, the difference quotient is 2Ax + Au + B, whose limit is 2Ax + B.", "(c) With A = 1, B = 0, the derivative is 2x. (d) With A = −1, B = 0, it is −2x. (e) With A = 16, B = −7, it is 32x − 7.", "(f) For x ≠ 0, \\(\\frac{7/(x+u)-7/x}u=-\\frac7{x(x+u)}\\). Taking u → 0 gives −7/x²."], "solution": "Use \\(f'(x)=\\lim_{u\\to0}\\frac{f(x+u)-f(x)}u\\), simplifying only for u ≠ 0.\n(a) For 3x, the quotient is 3u/u = 3, so f′(x) = 3.\n(b) Multiply by the conjugate: \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+5u+6}-\\sqrt{5x+6}}u=\\frac5{\\sqrt{5x+5u+6}+\\sqrt{5x+6}}\\). Its limit is \\(\\frac5{2\\sqrt{5x+6}}\\), for x > −6/5.\nAt the boundary x = −6/5 there is no finite ordinary derivative: the one-sided difference quotient grows without bound.\nFor a quadratic Ax² + Bx + C, the difference quotient is 2Ax + Au + B, whose limit is 2Ax + B.\n(c) With A = 1, B = 0, the derivative is 2x. (d) With A = −1, B = 0, it is −2x. (e) With A = 16, B = −7, it is 32x − 7.\n(f) For x ≠ 0, \\(\\frac{7/(x+u)-7/x}u=-\\frac7{x(x+u)}\\). Taking u → 0 gives −7/x².", "answer": "(a) 3; (b) 5/(2√(5x + 6)), x > −6/5; (c) 2x; (d) −2x; (e) 32x − 7; (f) −7/x², x ≠ 0.", "diagram": null, "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 70, "jpg": "039_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "ex-3-1-q7", "qNo": "7", "question": "Use f(x) = x² − 7x + 6: (a) Find the derivative at P(5, −4). (b) Find the tangent line on the curve at P(5, −4). (c) View the slope of the tangent line on the curve at P(6, 0).", "steps": ["For u ≠ 0, the difference quotient is \\(\\frac{(x+u)^2-7(x+u)+6-(x^2-7x+6)}u=2x+u-7\\). Taking its limit gives f′(x) = 2x − 7.", "(a) f(5) = 25 − 35 + 6 = −4 and f′(5) = 3.", "(b) The tangent through (5, −4) is y + 4 = 3(x − 5), or y = 3x − 19.", "(c) This part uses a different point: f(6) = 0 and f′(6) = 5. Its tangent is y = 5(x − 6) = 5x − 30.", "On the learning graphs, the tangents have the stated slopes and meet the parabola at their respective points."], "solution": "For u ≠ 0, the difference quotient is \\(\\frac{(x+u)^2-7(x+u)+6-(x^2-7x+6)}u=2x+u-7\\). Taking its limit gives f′(x) = 2x − 7.\n(a) f(5) = 25 − 35 + 6 = −4 and f′(5) = 3.\n(b) The tangent through (5, −4) is y + 4 = 3(x − 5), or y = 3x − 19.\n(c) This part uses a different point: f(6) = 0 and f′(6) = 5. Its tangent is y = 5(x − 6) = 5x − 30.\nOn the learning graphs, the tangents have the stated slopes and meet the parabola at their respective points.", "answer": "(a) 3; (b) y = 3x − 19; (c) slope 5 at (6, 0), tangent y = 5x − 30.", "diagram": null, "solutionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "SpaceBook learning graph — Question 7 at [5, -4]", "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "y = x² − 7x + 6 and tangent", "xMin": 0, "xMax": 8, "yMin": -8, "yMax": 15, "xTicks": [0, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8], "yTicks": [-5, 0, 5, 10, 15], "xGridStep": 1, "yGridStep": 2, "polynomial": [6, -7, 1], "linearSegments": [[3.6666666666666665, -8, 8, 5]], "points": [[5, -4, "[5, -4]"]]}}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "SpaceBook learning graph — Question 7 at [6, 0]", "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "y = x² − 7x + 6 and tangent", "xMin": 0, "xMax": 8, "yMin": -8, "yMax": 15, "xTicks": [0, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8], "yTicks": [-5, 0, 5, 10, 15], "xGridStep": 1, "yGridStep": 2, "polynomial": [6, -7, 1], "linearSegments": [[4.4, -8, 8, 10]], "points": [[6, 0, "[6, 0]"]]}}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 70, "jpg": "039_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "ex-3-1-q8", "qNo": "8", "question": "Use definition of derivative to determine the slope of the tangent at the given point, and find its equation: (a) f(x) = −x² + 7x, x = 3. (b) f(x) = 6x² − 11x − 10, x = 1. (c) f(x) = 3x² − 6x − 10, x = 0. (d) f(x) = 2x² + 3x − 4, x = 1.", "steps": ["For f(x) = Ax² + Bx + C, expand the first-principles quotient to obtain 2Ax + Au + B. Letting u → 0 gives f′(x) = 2Ax + B.", "(a) At 3, f(3) = 12 and f′(3) = −6 + 7 = 1. The tangent y − 12 = x − 3 is y = x + 9.", "(b) At 1, f(1) = −15 and f′(1) = 12 − 11 = 1. The tangent y + 15 = x − 1 is y = x − 16.", "(c) At 0, f(0) = −10 and f′(0) = −6. The tangent is y = −6x − 10.", "(d) At 1, f(1) = 1 and f′(1) = 4 + 3 = 7. The tangent y − 1 = 7(x − 1) is y = 7x − 6."], "solution": "For f(x) = Ax² + Bx + C, expand the first-principles quotient to obtain 2Ax + Au + B. Letting u → 0 gives f′(x) = 2Ax + B.\n(a) At 3, f(3) = 12 and f′(3) = −6 + 7 = 1. The tangent y − 12 = x − 3 is y = x + 9.\n(b) At 1, f(1) = −15 and f′(1) = 12 − 11 = 1. The tangent y + 15 = x − 1 is y = x − 16.\n(c) At 0, f(0) = −10 and f′(0) = −6. The tangent is y = −6x − 10.\n(d) At 1, f(1) = 1 and f′(1) = 4 + 3 = 7. The tangent y − 1 = 7(x − 1) is y = 7x − 6.", "answer": "(a) slope 1, y = x + 9; (b) slope 1, y = x − 16; (c) slope −6, y = −6x − 10; (d) slope 7, y = 7x − 6.", "diagram": null, "solutionBlocks": [], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "solutionStatus": "worked and checked", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 70, "jpg": "039_page_2.jpg", "verification": "visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}];
