const math12HigherExamples=[{"id": "we-4-source-1", "num": "1", "title": "Example 1", "problem": "Find the second derivative: (a) \\(8x^3-9x^2+6x+4\\); (b) \\(\\frac{4x+2}{3x-1}\\).", "given": "A cubic polynomial and a rational function.", "method": "Differentiate once, then differentiate the resulting derivative.", "steps": ["(a) Apply the power rule term by term: f′(x) = 24x² − 18x + 6.", "Differentiate again: f″(x) = 48x − 18, for every real x.", "(b) Require 3x − 1 ≠ 0, so x ≠ 1/3. Set N = 4x + 2 and D = 3x − 1.", "Quotient rule gives f′ = [4D − 3N]/D² = [12x − 4 − 12x − 6]/D² = −10/(3x − 1)².", "Write f′ = −10(3x − 1)⁻². Chain rule gives f″ = (−10)(−2)(3)(3x − 1)⁻³ = 60/(3x − 1)³.", "Check independently: f(x) = 4/3 + (10/3)/(3x − 1). The constant gives zero, and two differentiations of the reciprocal give the same f″.", "Both derivatives in (b) retain x ≠ 1/3; simplification does not restore the original pole."], "answer": "(a) \\(48x-18\\). (b) \\(\\frac{60}{(3x-1)^3}\\), x ≠ 1/3.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 102, "jpg": "055_page_2.jpg", "verification": "complete question and proof visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-2", "num": "2", "title": "Example 2", "problem": "An object’s position in feet is \\(s(t)=t^3-2t^2-7t+9\\), with t in seconds. Find (a) velocity, (b) acceleration, and (c) when velocity is zero for t ≥ 0.", "given": "Position as a differentiable function of time; physical time t ≥ 0.", "method": "First and second time derivatives, then solve v(t) = 0.", "steps": ["Velocity is the first derivative of position: v(t) = s′(t) = 3t² − 4t − 7 feet/second.", "Acceleration is the derivative of velocity: a(t) = v′(t) = 6t − 4 feet/second².", "For an instantaneous stop, set 3t² − 4t − 7 = 0.", "Factor as (3t − 7)(t + 1) = 0. The roots are t = 7/3 and t = −1.", "The model asks for t ≥ 0, so exclude −1. The required stopping instant is t = 7/3 seconds, approximately 2.3333 seconds.", "Check: v(7/3) = 49/3 − 28/3 − 7 = 0. At that instant acceleration is 6(7/3) − 4 = 10 ft/s².", "For 0 ≤ t < 7/3, t + 1 > 0 and 3t − 7 < 0, so v < 0. After 7/3, both are positive. Thus the object reverses direction at its instantaneous stop."], "answer": "v(t) = 3t² − 4t − 7 ft/s; a(t) = 6t − 4 ft/s²; velocity is zero at t = 7/3 s for t ≥ 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 102, "jpg": "055_page_2.jpg", "verification": "complete question and proof visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-3", "num": "3", "title": "Example 3", "problem": "Find the fifth derivatives: (a) \\((6x+4)^9\\); (b) \\(1/(4x+3)\\); (c) \\(\\ln(4x+7)\\); (d) \\(6^{4x}\\); (e) \\(e^{4x}\\); (f) \\(\\sin(5x+7)\\).", "given": "n = 5 for six functions with different outer forms.", "method": "Apply the appropriate nth-derivative formula and retain each original domain.", "steps": ["(a) Use m!aⁿ(ax+b)^(m−n)/(m−n)! with m = 9, a = 6 and n = 5. The product 9·8·7·6·5 = 15120, and 6⁵ = 7776.", "Thus f⁽⁵⁾(x) = 15120·7776(6x+4)⁴ = 117573120(6x+4)⁴, for all real x.", "(b) For 1/(ax+b), f⁽ⁿ⁾ = (−1)ⁿn!aⁿ/(ax+b)^(n+1). At n = 5, a = 4: f⁽⁵⁾ = −120·1024/(4x+3)⁶ = −122880/(4x+3)⁶, x ≠ −3/4.", "(c) For ln(ax+b), f⁽ⁿ⁾ = (−1)^(n−1)(n−1)!aⁿ/(ax+b)ⁿ. Thus f⁽⁵⁾ = 24·1024/(4x+7)⁵ = 24576/(4x+7)⁵, x > −7/4.", "(d) Rewrite 6^(4x) = exp(4x ln6). Each differentiation supplies the factor 4ln6. Therefore f⁽⁵⁾ = (4ln6)⁵6^(4x), all real x.", "(e) Each derivative of e^(4x) multiplies by 4, so f⁽⁵⁾ = 4⁵e^(4x) = 1024e^(4x), all real x.", "(f) Each derivative multiplies by 5 and shifts the sine phase by π/2. Thus f⁽⁵⁾ = 5⁵sin(5x+7+5π/2) = 3125cos(5x+7), all real x.", "Check the trigonometric cycle: sin → cos → −sin → −cos → sin → cos. The fifth derivative therefore has a positive cosine factor, agreeing with the phase-shift formula."], "answer": "(a) 117573120(6x+4)⁴. (b) −122880/(4x+3)⁶. (c) 24576/(4x+7)⁵. (d) (4ln6)⁵6^(4x). (e) 1024e^(4x). (f) 3125cos(5x+7), with the domains stated above.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 104, "pageEnd": 105, "verification": "complete questions and proofs visually compared with JPG and full PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-4", "num": "4", "title": "Example 4", "problem": "Find the second derivative of \\(x^2y+2y^3=3x+2y\\).", "given": "An implicit algebraic curve.", "method": "Differentiate implicitly twice and collect all terms containing y″.", "steps": ["Set D = x² + 6y² − 2. Differentiating the original equation gives 2xy + x²y′ + 6y²y′ = 3 + 2y′.", "Collect: D y′ = N, where N = 3 − 2xy. If D ≠ 0, y′ = N/D.", "Differentiate D y′ = 3 − 2xy: (2x + 12yy′)y′ + D y″ = −2y − 2xy′.", "Collect the terms: D y″ = −2y − 4xy′ − 12y(y′)².", "Substitute y′ = N/D and combine denominators: y″ = −2[yD² + 2xND + 6yN²]/D³.", "Expand the bracket: 36y⁵ + 12x²y³ − 24y³ − 36xy² − 3x⁴y + 4x²y + 58y + 6x³ − 12x. This agrees with the printed final polynomial.", "Check at the source curve point (0,1): 2 = 2, D = 4, N = 3, y′ = 3/4, and y″ = −2[16 + 54]/64 = −35/16.", "The formula is valid on the implicit curve where D ≠ 0. At D = 0, investigate the local curve separately instead of dividing by zero."], "answer": "\\(y″=-2[yD^2+2xND+6yN^2]/D^3\\), D = x² + 6y² − 2, N = 3 − 2xy, D ≠ 0.", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 105, "pageEnd": 106, "verification": "complete source proof compared with JPG and PDF"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement"}, {"id": "we-4-source-5", "num": "5", "title": "Example 5", "problem": "Find the second derivative of \\(\\cos^{-1}y+y=2xy\\).", "given": "Principal inverse cosine, with −1 ≤ y ≤ 1.", "method": "Retain the implicit chain rule and differentiate the first equation again.", "steps": ["For a finite standard differentiation, require |y| < 1. Set s = √(1 − y²) > 0.", "First derivative: (1 − 1/s)y′ = 2y + 2xy′. Therefore A y′ = 2y, where A = 1 − 2x − 1/s.", "If A ≠ 0, y′ = 2y/A = 2ys/(s − 1 − 2xs). The source Remember box has the opposite denominator sign.", "Differentiate A along the curve: A′ = −2 − y y′/s³. The chain factor y′ is necessary because y depends on x.", "Product rule for A y′ = 2y gives (−2 − y y′/s³)y′ + A y″ = 2y′.", "Collect: A y″ = 4y′ + y(y′)²/s³. The source proof loses one y′ factor in this square.", "Substitute y′ = 2y/A: y″ = 8y/A² + 4y³/(s³A³). This is the corrected second derivative.", "An independent implicit-function check uses F = acos(y) + y − 2xy: Fₓ = −2y, Fᵧ = A, Fₓᵧ = −2 and Fᵧᵧ = −y/s³. Thus y″ = −[2Fₓᵧy′ + Fᵧᵧ(y′)²]/Fᵧ, giving the same result."], "answer": "\\(y″=\\frac{8y}{A^2}+\\frac{4y^3}{s^3A^3}\\), s = √(1 − y²), A = 1 − 2x − 1/s, |y| < 1, A ≠ 0.", "sourceClarification": "Printed first-derivative box reverses the sign; printed second-derivative calculation omits an implicit chain factor. Original source proof remains in the lesson, with corrections separately identified.", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 106, "pageEnd": 107, "verification": "complete source proof compared with JPG and PDF"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement"}, {"id": "we-4-source-6", "num": "6", "title": "Example 6", "problem": "Find \\(d^2y/dx^2\\) for \\(x=1+t^2,\\ y=t^3+2t^2+1\\).", "given": "A parametric curve, with t as parameter.", "method": "Differentiate with respect to t and divide by dx/dt at each stage.", "steps": ["Compute x′ = 2t and x″ = 2; y′ₜ = 3t² + 4t and y″ₜ = 6t + 4.", "For t ≠ 0, dy/dx = (3t² + 4t)/(2t) = (3t + 4)/2.", "The derivative of this slope with respect to t is 3/2.", "Convert its rate from t to x: d²y/dx² = (3/2)/(2t) = 3/(4t).", "Check the general formula: [x′y″ₜ − y′ₜx″]/(x′)³ = [(2t)(6t + 4) − (3t² + 4t)(2)]/(8t³) = 6t²/(8t³).", "At t = 0, x′ = 0, so both original quotient formulas are unavailable. The branches are y = 2x − 1 ± (x − 1)³/² for x ≥ 1.", "The limiting first slope on either branch is 2 at x = 1. The second derivative becomes unbounded as t approaches zero, so the simplified first slope must not imply a finite second derivative there."], "answer": "dy/dx = (3t + 4)/2; d²y/dx² = 3/(4t), t ≠ 0.", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 107, "pageEnd": 108, "verification": "complete source proof compared with JPG and PDF"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement"}, {"id": "we-4-source-7", "num": "7", "title": "Example 7", "problem": "Differentiate \\(f(x)=x^4+x^2\\sin x+x+2\\) w.r.t. x, using MAPLE command diff repeatedly.", "given": "A polynomial plus a product of x² and sin x.", "method": "Differentiate once, then apply diff to the first derivative.", "steps": ["Differentiate x⁴ + x + 2 to obtain 4x³ + 1.", "Product rule for x²sin x gives 2xsin x + x²cos x.", "Combine: f′ = 4x³ + 2xsin x + x²cos x + 1.", "Differentiate again: the polynomial gives 12x². The term 2xsin x gives 2sin x + 2xcos x.", "The term x²cos x gives 2xcos x − x²sin x. Add these contributions: f″ = 12x² + 2sin x + 4xcos x − x²sin x.", "The single printed command diff(f,x) gives f′, while the source displayed answer is f″. Use diff(diff(f,x),x) to request the latter.", "At x = 0, the first derivative is 1 and the second derivative is 0; this also checks that the source display cannot be the output of its first-derivative command."], "answer": "\\(f′=4x^3+2x\\sin x+x^2\\cos x+1\\); \\(f″=12x^2+2\\sin x+4x\\cos x-x^2\\sin x\\).", "sourceClarification": "Source command and displayed derivative order disagree. Corrected commands have expected outputs verified by manual differentiation, not an executed Maple session.", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 108, "jpg": "058_page_2.jpg", "verification": "complete source example compared with JPG and PDF"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement"}, {"id": "we-4-source-8", "num": "8", "title": "Example 8", "problem": "Use the fourth-order Taylor polynomial of eˣ about 0 to estimate f(0.2).", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["Every derivative of eˣ equals eˣ, so the values through fourth order at zero are all 1.", "Insert these values into p₄(x) = Σ from k = 0 to 4 of f⁽ᵏ⁾(0)xᵏ/k!.", "The constant and linear terms are 1 + x; the remaining terms are x²/2 + x³/6 + x⁴/24.", "At x = 0.2 these contributions are 1,0.2,0.02,0.0013333333 and 0.0000666667.", "Their sum is exactly 1.2214 for the given decimal x.", "Check against e⁰·² ≈ 1.22140275816. The absolute error is about 2.75816 × 10⁻⁶.", "The source equality is correct after rounding to four decimal places; the finite polynomial does not equal the exponential exactly."], "answer": "p₄(0.2) = 1.2214; absolute error ≈ 0.00000275816.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 110, "pageEnd": 110, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-9", "num": "9", "title": "Example 9", "problem": "Use Taylor’s series for f(x) = eˣ about x₀ = 2.", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["Put h = x − 2.", "All derivatives of eˣ equal eˣ, so every derivative at 2 equals e².", "The constant is e²; the linear contribution is e²h.", "The quadratic and cubic contributions are e²h²/2 and e²h³/6.", "Thus eˣ = e²[1 + h + h²/2! + h³/3! + …].", "This agrees independently with eˣ = e²eʰ, and the exponential series converges for every real h.", "The source uses 7.3891 for e²; that coefficient is rounded. Use the exact e² for accurate computation."], "answer": "\\(e^x=e^2[1+(x-2)+(x-2)^2/2!+(x-2)^3/3!+...]\\).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 112, "pageEnd": 112, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-10", "num": "10", "title": "Example 10", "problem": "Use Maclaurin’s series for f(x) = aˣ about 0.", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["For a real exponential, require a > 0. Set L = ln a.", "Rewrite aˣ as exp(Lx).", "Each derivative multiplies by L: f⁽ⁿ⁾(x) = Lⁿaˣ.", "At zero, f⁽ⁿ⁾(0) = Lⁿ.", "Insert the constant 1, linear Lx, quadratic L²x²/2! and cubic L³x³/3!.", "The full series is Σ (Lx)ⁿ/n!, converging for every real x.", "If a = 1, L = 0 and the series is the constant 1, as expected."], "answer": "\\(a^x=1+x\\ln a+x^2(\\ln a)^2/2!+x^3(\\ln a)^3/3!+...\\).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 112, "pageEnd": 112, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-11", "num": "11", "title": "Example 11", "problem": "Use Maclaurin’s series for f(x) = eˣ about 0.", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["Every derivative of eˣ is eˣ.", "At zero all derivative values are 1.", "Taylor coefficient for xⁿ is therefore 1/n!.", "The constant and linear terms are 1 + x.", "Add x²/2! + x³/3! and the continuing higher terms.", "The ratio of successive absolute terms is |x|/(n + 1), approaching zero for each fixed x.", "Thus the infinite series equals eˣ for all real x; a finite truncation is an approximation."], "answer": "\\(e^x=1+x+x^2/2!+x^3/3!+...\\).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 112, "pageEnd": 112, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-12", "num": "12", "title": "Example 12", "problem": "Use Maclaurin’s series for sinx about 0.", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["The derivatives cycle sinx,cosx,−sinx,−cosx.", "At zero their values cycle 0,1,0,−1.", "Even powers have zero coefficients.", "The linear coefficient is 1; the cubic is −1/3!.", "The next nonzero coefficients are +1/5! and −1/7!.", "Hence sinx = x − x³/3! + x⁵/5! − x⁷/7! + …, for all real x.", "All powers are odd, consistent with the function being odd; use radians in these derivatives."], "answer": "\\(\\sin x=x-x^3/3!+x^5/5!-x^7/7!+...\\).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 112, "pageEnd": 112, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-13", "num": "13", "title": "Example 13", "problem": "Use Maclaurin’s series for cosx about 0.", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["The derivatives cycle cosx,−sinx,−cosx,sinx.", "At zero their values cycle 1,0,−1,0.", "Odd coefficients vanish.", "The constant is 1 and quadratic coefficient is −1/2!.", "The quartic is +1/4! and sixth-degree coefficient is −1/6!.", "Thus cosx = 1 − x²/2! + x⁴/4! − x⁶/6! + …, for every real x.", "The alternating even powers agree with cosine being even, and termwise differentiation gives the negative sine series."], "answer": "\\(\\cos x=1-x^2/2!+x^4/4!-x^6/6!+...\\).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 113, "pageEnd": 113, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-14", "num": "14", "title": "Example 14", "problem": "Use Maclaurin’s series for tanx about 0.", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["The value tan0 is 0 and the first derivative sec²0 is 1.", "The second derivative 2sec²x tanx vanishes at zero.", "The third derivative 2sec⁴x + 4tan²x sec²x equals 2 at zero.", "Insert these values: tanx = x + 2x³/3! + … = x + x³/3 + … .", "To check the next coefficient, use T′ = 1 + T². With T = x + Ax³ + Bx⁵, compare 3A = 1 and 5B = 2A.", "This gives A = 1/3,B = 2/15. Thus the next term is 2x⁵/15.", "The nearest poles to zero are ±π/2; the Maclaurin series applies for |x| < π/2, not for all real x."], "answer": "\\(\\tan x=x+x^3/3+2x^5/15+...\\), |x| < π/2.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 113, "pageEnd": 113, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-15", "num": "15", "title": "Example 15", "problem": "Use Maclaurin’s series for logₐ(1 + x) about 0.", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["Require a > 0,a ≠ 1 and x > −1.", "Change base: logₐ(1 + x) = ln(1 + x)/ln a.", "The first three derivatives at zero are 1/ln a,−1/ln a and 2/ln a.", "Insert them: logₐ(1 + x) = [x − x²/2 + x³/3 − …]/ln a.", "The general nth coefficient is (−1)^(n−1)/(n ln a) for n ≥ 1.", "The source factor logₐe equals 1/ln a, so its formula is equivalent.", "The series converges for −1 < x ≤ 1; at x = 1 it is the alternating harmonic series divided by ln a."], "answer": "\\(\\log_a(1+x)=\\frac1{\\ln a}(x-x^2/2+x^3/3-...)\\).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 113, "pageEnd": 114, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-16", "num": "16", "title": "Example 16", "problem": "Use Maclaurin’s series for ln(1 + x) about 0.", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["The value at zero is ln1 = 0.", "First derivative is 1/(1 + x), giving value 1 at zero.", "Second derivative is −1/(1 + x)², giving −1 at zero.", "Third derivative is 2/(1 + x)³, giving 2 at zero; the source formula omits the numerator 2.", "Insert the coefficients: x − x²/2! + 2x³/3! + … = x − x²/2 + x³/3 − … .", "For n ≥ 1 the coefficient is (−1)^(n−1)/n.", "The real function requires x > −1, while the series converges for −1 < x ≤ 1. A finite sum is an approximation."], "answer": "\\(\\ln(1+x)=x-x^2/2+x^3/3-x^4/4+...\\).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 114, "pageEnd": 114, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-17", "num": "17", "title": "Example 17", "problem": "Use Maple taylor for eˣ to order 4 and sinx to order 5 about zero.", "given": "Expansion about the stated point.", "method": "Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.", "steps": ["For eˣ, all derivatives at zero equal 1.", "The terms below degree 4 are 1 + x + x²/2 + x³/6, followed by O(x⁴).", "The source command is taylor(e^x,x=0,4); it displays ln(e) coefficients. These equal 1 if e is Euler’s constant.", "For sinx, the derivatives at zero give only the nonzero x and −x³/6 terms below degree 5.", "The source command taylor(sin(x),x=0,5); displays x − x³/6 + O(x⁵).", "The number 5 here is the cutoff order, not five nonzero terms. To show five nonzero sine terms, one would need degrees through 9.", "The Context Menu alternatives series(e^x,x,4) and series(sin(x),x,5) are preserved beside the printed outputs. These results are independently checked series, not an executed Maple session."], "answer": "\\(e^x=1+x+x^2/2+x^3/6+O(x^4)\\); \\(\\sin x=x-x^3/6+O(x^5)\\).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 114, "pageEnd": 114, "verification": "complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-18", "num": "18", "title": "Example 18", "problem": "Find tangent and normal lines to y = x² at P(2,4).", "given": "A parabola and a point on it.", "method": "Find the derivative slope and use point–slope form.", "steps": ["Check the point: 2² = 4, so (2,4) lies on the curve.", "Differentiate: y′ = 2x. At x = 2, the tangent slope is 4.", "The tangent equation is y − 4 = 4(x − 2).", "Simplify to y = 4x − 4, or 4x − y − 4 = 0.", "The perpendicular normal slope is −1/4. Its equation is y − 4 = −(x − 2)/4.", "Multiply by 4 and collect to obtain x + 4y − 18 = 0.", "Check both lines at (2,4): 8 − 4 − 4 = 0 and 2 + 16 − 18 = 0. Their slopes multiply to −1."], "answer": "Tangent: 4x − y − 4 = 0. Normal: x + 4y − 18 = 0.", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 115, "pageEnd": 116, "verification": "source question and complete proof compared with JPG/PDF"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement"}, {"id": "we-4-source-19", "num": "19", "title": "Example 19", "problem": "Find tangent and normal lines to y = 9 − x² where the curve crosses the x-axis.", "given": "A downward parabola; y = 0 at an x-axis crossing.", "method": "Find both crossings and differentiate for the two slopes.", "steps": ["Put y = 0: x² = 9, so the crossings are (3,0) and (−3,0).", "The derivative is −2x. At x = 3 it is −6; at x = −3 it is 6.", "At (3,0), the tangent is y = −6(x − 3), or 6x + y − 18 = 0.", "At (−3,0), the tangent is y = 6(x + 3), or 6x − y + 18 = 0.", "Normal slopes are 1/6 and −1/6 respectively.", "At (3,0), y = (x − 3)/6, so x − 6y − 3 = 0.", "At (−3,0), y = −(x + 3)/6, so x + 6y + 3 = 0.", "Each pair passes through its crossing and has perpendicular slopes; keep the positive and negative crossings associated with their own lines."], "answer": "At (3,0): tangent 6x + y − 18 = 0, normal x − 6y − 3 = 0. At (−3,0): tangent 6x − y + 18 = 0, normal x + 6y + 3 = 0.", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 116, "jpg": "062_page_2.jpg", "verification": "source question and complete proof compared with JPG/PDF"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement"}, {"id": "we-4-source-20", "num": "20", "title": "Example 20", "problem": "Find the angle between y = x³ − 2x + 1 and y = x² + 1 at (2,5).", "given": "Two curves and the specified common point.", "method": "Differentiate both curves and compare their tangent slopes.", "steps": ["Check the common point: 8 − 4 + 1 = 5 and 4 + 1 = 5.", "For all intersections, subtract the curve equations: x³ − x² − 2x = x(x − 2)(x + 1) = 0.", "The intersections are (0,1),(−1,2),(2,5). The question selects (2,5).", "First slope: 3x² − 2 = 10 at x = 2. Second slope: 2x = 4.", "The smaller angle has tangent |(10 − 4)/(1 + 10·4)| = 6/41.", "Thus θ = arctan(6/41) ≈ 0.145310011 rad ≈ 8.3256503°.", "The denominator is 41, so the tangents are not perpendicular; the source 0.1453 is a rounded radian value."], "answer": "θ = arctan(6/41) ≈ 0.14531 radians, or 8.32565°.", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 116, "pageEnd": 117, "verification": "source question and complete proof compared with JPG/PDF"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement"}, {"id": "we-4-source-21", "num": "21", "title": "Example 21", "problem": "Find all points on y = 2x³ + 4x² whose tangent is parallel to y = 8x − 4.", "given": "The line’s slope is 8.", "method": "Set the derivative equal to the line’s slope and evaluate the curve.", "steps": ["Differentiate: y′ = 6x² + 8x.", "Parallel tangents need 6x² + 8x = 8, or 3x² + 4x − 4 = 0.", "Factor: (x + 2)(3x − 2) = 0, so x = −2 or 2/3.", "At x = −2, y = 2(−8) + 4(4) = 0.", "At x = 2/3, y = 16/27 + 16/9 = 64/27.", "Check both slopes: 6(4) − 16 = 8 and 6(4/9) + 8(2/3) = 8.", "The requested points are (−2,0) and (2/3,64/27). Optional tangent equations are y = 8(x + 2) and y − 64/27 = 8(x − 2/3)."], "answer": "(−2,0) and (2/3,64/27).", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 117, "jpg": "063_page_1.jpg", "verification": "source question and complete proof compared with JPG/PDF"}], "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement"}, {"id": "we-4-source-22", "num": "22", "title": "Example 22", "problem": "Find where f(x) = x² is increasing or decreasing.", "given": "The source function and its real domain.", "method": "Use derivative signs or the source’s direct comparison argument.", "steps": ["Choose x₁ < x₂. The value difference is f(x₂) − f(x₁) = (x₂ − x₁)(x₂ + x₁).", "For 0 < x₁ < x₂, both factors are positive, so f(x₂) > f(x₁).", "Thus f is strictly increasing on (0,∞).", "For x₁ < x₂ < 0, the first factor is positive and the second negative, so f(x₂) < f(x₁).", "Thus f is strictly decreasing on (−∞,0).", "An independent derivative check gives f′(x) = 2x, negative for x < 0 and positive for x > 0.", "The minimum is at x = 0. Including 0 at the adjacent interval endpoint also preserves strict monotonicity on each half-line."], "answer": "Decreasing on (−∞,0); increasing on (0,∞).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 119, "pageEnd": 119, "verification": "complete source question and proof checked against JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-23", "num": "23", "title": "Example 23", "problem": "Determine where f(x) = x² + 2x − 3 increases/decreases, and its zero-slope point.", "given": "The source function and its real domain.", "method": "Use derivative signs or the source’s direct comparison argument.", "steps": ["Complete the square: f(x) = (x + 1)² − 4, so the vertex is (−1,−4).", "Differentiate: f′(x) = 2x + 2 = 2(x + 1).", "For x < −1, f′ < 0, so the function is decreasing.", "For x > −1, f′ > 0, so the function is increasing.", "At x = −1, f′ = 0 and f(−1) = −4.", "The positive leading coefficient shows this vertex is a minimum; the second derivative is 2 > 0.", "Check the source points: f(−4) = 5 and f(2) = 5, symmetric about x = −1."], "answer": "Decreasing (−∞,−1), increasing (−1,∞); zero slope/minimum at (−1,−4).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 120, "pageEnd": 120, "verification": "complete source question and proof checked against JPG/PDF"}], "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "Figure 4.5", "polynomial": [-3, 2, 1], "extraPolynomials": [[2, 2]], "xMin": -10, "xMax": 10, "yMin": -10, "yMax": 15, "xTicks": [-10, -5, 0, 5, 10], "yTicks": [-10, -5, 0, 5, 10], "points": [[-4, 5, "(−4,5)"], [2, 5, "(2,5)"], [-1, -4, "(−1,−4)"], [2.236, 6.472, "(2.236,6.472)"], [-2.236, -2.472, "(−2.236,−2.472)"]], "annotations": [[5, 11, "y"], [-4, -9, "y′"]]}}, {"id": "we-4-source-24", "num": "24", "title": "Example 24", "problem": "Find critical values: (a) \\(4x^3-5x^2-8x+20\\); (b) \\(x^2/(x-2)\\); (c) \\(12x^{1/2}-2x^{3/2}\\); (d) \\(3x^{4/3}-12x^{1/3}\\); (e) \\(6x^{2/3}-4x\\).", "given": "The source function and its real domain.", "method": "Use derivative signs or the source’s direct comparison argument.", "steps": ["A critical value must be in the function’s domain and have derivative zero or no derivative. A pole outside the domain is excluded.", "(a) f′ = 12x² − 10x − 8 = 2(3x − 4)(2x + 1), so the critical values are 4/3 and −1/2.", "(b) f′ = x(x − 4)/(x − 2)². The zeros are 0 and 4; x = 2 is excluded from the function and is not critical.", "(c) The domain is x ≥ 0. For x > 0, f′ = 3(2 − x)/√x, which vanishes at 2. At 0 the function is defined but the derivative is not finite, so the source definition includes 0.", "(d) Use real cube roots. For x ≠ 0, f′ = (4x − 4)/(∛x)², which vanishes at 1. The function is defined at 0 while its derivative is not, so 0 is also critical.", "(e) For x ≠ 0, f′ = (4 − 4∛x)/∛x, which vanishes at 1. The function is defined at 0 but its derivative is not; include 0.", "Part (e)’s last printed fraction has a wrong minus sign on its leading 4. The unsimplified derivative and following source sentence establish the correct numerator.", "Check all five original domains before accepting derivative zeros or singularities; in particular, never include the rational function’s pole x = 2."], "answer": "(a) −1/2,4/3; (b) 0,4; (c) 0,2; (d) 0,1; (e) 0,1.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 121, "pageEnd": 122, "verification": "complete source question and proof checked against JPG/PDF"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-25", "num": "25", "title": "Example 25", "problem": "Find where f(x) = x³ − 3x² − 9x + 1 is strictly increasing or decreasing.", "given": "The source function and its real domain.", "method": "Use derivative signs or the source’s direct comparison argument.", "steps": ["The derivative is f′ = 3x² − 6x − 9 = 3(x + 1)(x − 3).", "The critical values are −1 and 3; the derivative exists everywhere.", "For x < −1, both factors are negative, so f′ > 0; sample x = −2 gives 15.", "For −1 < x < 3, one factor is positive and one negative, so f′ < 0; sample 0 gives −9.", "For x > 3, both factors are positive, so f′ > 0; sample 4 gives 15.", "Therefore the increasing intervals are (−∞,−1),(3,∞), and the decreasing interval is (−1,3).", "At x = −1, f = 6 and the sign changes + to −, giving a relative maximum. At 3, f = −26 and the sign changes − to +, giving a relative minimum."], "answer": "Increasing (−∞,−1) and (3,∞); decreasing (−1,3).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 122, "pageEnd": 122, "verification": "complete source question and proof checked against JPG/PDF"}], "diagram": {"type": "source-critical-sign-pair", "title": "Figure 4.7", "critical": [-1, 3], "poly": [1, -9, -3, 1], "points": [[-1, 6, "(−1,6)"], [0, 1, "(0,1)"], [3, -26, "(3,−26)"]]}}, {"id": "we-4-source-26", "num": "26", "title": "Example 26", "problem": "Draw f(x) = x³ − 3x² − 9x + 1 and f′(x) = 3x² − 6x − 9. Explain (a) positive f′, (b) decreasing f.", "given": "The source function and stated conditions.", "method": "Use derivative signs, the second derivative test, and endpoint checks as appropriate.", "steps": ["The derivative factors as 3(x + 1)(x − 3), with zeros −1 and 3.", "For x < −1 or x > 3, f′ > 0; its graph lies above the x-axis.", "Positive derivative means the original function is increasing on those intervals.", "For −1 < x < 3, f′ < 0; its graph lies below the x-axis.", "The original function is decreasing on that interval.", "Plot the original function’s relative maximum (−1,6), relative minimum (3,−26), and intercept (0,1) together with the derivative parabola.", "Here all critical values are derivative zeros. This is specific to this differentiable polynomial; undefined-derivative critical points in other functions need separate treatment."], "answer": "(a) f is increasing; (b) f′ is negative on the decreasing interval.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 123, "pageEnd": 123, "verification": "complete source question and proof visually checked"}], "diagram": {"type": "source-derivative-graph-pair", "title": "Figure 4.8", "poly": [1, -9, -3, 1], "derivative": [-9, -6, 3], "points": [[-1, 6, "(−1,6)"], [3, -26, "(3,−26)"]]}}, {"id": "we-4-source-27", "num": "27", "title": "Example 27", "problem": "Examine f(x) = 2x³ + 3x² − 12x − 5 for relative extrema using the first derivative test.", "given": "The source function and stated conditions.", "method": "Use derivative signs, the second derivative test, and endpoint checks as appropriate.", "steps": ["Differentiate: f′ = 6x² + 6x − 12 = 6(x + 2)(x − 1).", "Critical values are −2 and 1. The derivative is defined everywhere.", "At sample −3 the derivative is 24 > 0, so f is increasing before −2.", "At 0 it is −12 < 0, so f is decreasing between −2 and 1.", "At 2 it is 24 > 0, so f increases after 1.", "The + to − sign change at −2 gives a maximum; f(−2) = −16 + 12 + 24 − 5 = 15.", "The − to + change at 1 gives a minimum; f(1) = 2 + 3 − 12 − 5 = −12.", "Second derivative 12x + 6 provides an independent check: −18 at −2 and 18 at 1."], "answer": "Relative maximum (−2,15); relative minimum (1,−12).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 123, "pageEnd": 124, "verification": "complete source question and proof visually checked"}], "diagram": {"type": "source-derivative-sign", "title": "Figure 4.9", "critical": [-2, 1], "samples": [-3, 0, 2], "ticks": [-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4]}}, {"id": "we-4-source-28", "num": "28", "title": "Example 28", "problem": "Classify critical values of f(x) = 3x⁵ − 5x³ + 2 using the second derivative test.", "given": "The source function and stated conditions.", "method": "Use derivative signs, the second derivative test, and endpoint checks as appropriate.", "steps": ["First derivative: f′ = 15x²(x − 1)(x + 1). Critical values are −1,0,1.", "Second derivative: f″ = 30x(2x² − 1).", "At −1, f″ = −30 < 0, giving a relative maximum; f(−1) = 4.", "At 1, f″ = 30 > 0, giving a relative minimum; f(1) = 0.", "At 0, f″ = 0, so the second derivative test is inconclusive.", "For −1 < x < 0 and 0 < x < 1, x² > 0 and x² − 1 < 0; therefore f′ < 0 on both sides of zero.", "There is no sign change at 0, so it is neither a maximum nor a minimum. Since f″ changes sign there, it is a stationary inflection point (0,2)."], "answer": "Maximum (−1,4); minimum (1,0); neither at 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 125, "pageEnd": 125, "verification": "complete source question and proof visually checked"}]}, {"id": "we-4-source-29", "num": "29", "title": "Example 29", "problem": "A truck uses \\(G(x)=(800+x^2)/(200x)\\) gallons/mile at x > 0 mph. Fuel costs $2/gallon for a 1000-mile trip. Find the minimum-cost speed. The printed final sentence asks for the maximum total cost.", "given": "The source function and stated conditions.", "method": "Use derivative signs, the second derivative test, and endpoint checks as appropriate.", "steps": ["Trip cost is C = G·1000·2 = 8000/x + 10x dollars, x > 0.", "Derivative: C′ = −8000/x² + 10. Set it to zero: x² = 800.", "The allowed root is x = √800 = 20√2 ≈ 28.2843 mph; the negative root is outside the domain.", "Second derivative is C″ = 16000/x³ > 0, confirming a minimum.", "Check global behavior: C tends to infinity near 0 and as x increases without bound, so the critical minimum is global.", "At x = 20√2, C = 8000/(20√2) + 200√2 = 400√2 ≈ $565.6854.", "For the printed maximum-cost request, no finite maximum exists on x > 0; the model is unbounded above."], "answer": "Minimum-cost speed 20√2 mph ≈ 28.2843; minimum $565.69. No finite maximum cost on x > 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 125, "pageEnd": 125, "verification": "complete source question and proof visually checked"}], "sourceClarification": "Source final sentence asks for maximum, but its worked calculation is the minimum; both requests are explicitly addressed."}, {"id": "we-4-source-30", "num": "30", "title": "Example 30", "problem": "For the pipeline cable y = 0.03125x² − 1.25x, find the low point relative to its left attachment, in meters.", "given": "The source function and stated conditions.", "method": "Use derivative signs, the second derivative test, and endpoint checks as appropriate.", "steps": ["Differentiate: y′ = 0.0625x − 1.25.", "Set y′ = 0: 0.0625x = 1.25, so x = 20 m.", "Second derivative y″ = 0.0625 > 0 shows the vertex is a minimum.", "Evaluate y(20) = 0.03125·400 − 1.25·20 = 12.5 − 25 = −12.5 m.", "Thus the low point is 20 m right of the left attachment and 12.5 m below it.", "Complete the square as an independent check: y = 0.03125(x − 20)² − 12.5.", "The vertex is (20,−12.5). Setting y = 0 gives x = 0 and 40, matching symmetric attachment elevations in the model."], "answer": "(20,−12.5) m: 20 m right and 12.5 m below the left attachment.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 125, "pageEnd": 126, "verification": "complete source question and proof visually checked"}], "diagram": {"type": "source-pipeline-cable", "title": "Figure 4.13"}}, {"id": "we-4-source-31", "num": "31", "title": "Example 31", "problem": "Use MAPLE commands to compute extrema of cosx, and of x⁴ − 2x² + 3 on [−1,2].", "given": "The source function and stated conditions.", "method": "Use derivative signs, the second derivative test, and endpoint checks as appropriate.", "steps": ["For all real x, −1 ≤ cosx ≤ 1. Maxima 1 occur at x = 2kπ; minima −1 at x = (2k + 1)π for any integer k.", "These give the source maximize(cos(x)) output 1 and minimize(cos(x)) output −1.", "The source numerical Context Menu examples choose points close to 0 and π; they do not list every periodic extremum.", "For the polynomial, f′ = 4x³ − 4x = 4x(x − 1)(x + 1). On the closed interval compare −1,0,1 and 2.", "Values are f(−1) = 2, f(0) = 3, f(1) = 2, f(2) = 11.", "Thus the global interval minimum is 2 at −1 and 1; the maximum is 11 at 2. These agree with the printed commands and outputs.", "An independent algebraic check writes f = (x² − 1)² + 2, making the minimum value 2 clear. Expected software results are verified mathematically, not through an executed Maple session."], "answer": "cosx: maximum 1, minimum −1. Polynomial on [−1,2]: maximum 11 at 2, minimum 2 at −1 and 1.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 126, "pageEnd": 126, "verification": "complete source question and proof visually checked"}]}];
const math12Higher=MATH_12_DATA[3].sections[0];
