math12HigherExamples.forEach(revision=>{const chapter=MATH_12_DATA[3];const existing=chapter.workedExamples.find(example=>example.num===revision.num);if(existing)Object.assign(existing,revision);else{const index=chapter.workedExamples.findIndex(example=>Number(example.num)>Number(revision.num));chapter.workedExamples.splice(index<0?chapter.workedExamples.length:index,0,revision);}const duplicates=chapter.workedExamples.filter(example=>example.num===revision.num&&example.id!==revision.id);chapter.unreviewedDuplicateExamples=(chapter.unreviewedDuplicateExamples||[]).concat(duplicates);chapter.workedExamples=chapter.workedExamples.filter(example=>example.num!==revision.num||example.id===revision.id);});
const math12Exercise41=[{"id": "ex-4-1-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Find the indicated higher derivatives: (a) f(x) = 3x³ + 4x + 5, f″(x); (b) \\(f(x)=x+\\frac1x\\), f‴(x); (c) \\(s(t)=\\sqrt{5t+7}\\), s″(t); (d) \\(y=\\frac{x+1}{x-1}\\), y″.", "steps": ["(a) Power rule: f′ = 9x² + 4, then f″ = 18x, all real x.", "(b) Write f = x + x⁻¹, with x ≠ 0. First derivative: 1 − x⁻².", "Second derivative is 2x⁻³; third derivative is −6x⁻⁴ = −6/x⁴.", "(c) Write s = (5t + 7)¹/². First derivative is (5/2)(5t + 7)⁻¹/².", "Chain rule again gives s″ = (5/2)(−1/2)(5)(5t + 7)⁻³/² = −25/[4(5t + 7)³/²], t > −7/5.", "(d) Rewrite y = 1 + 2/(x − 1), x ≠ 1. First derivative is −2/(x − 1)².", "The second derivative is 4/(x − 1)³. The original pole remains excluded."], "solution": "(a) Power rule: f′ = 9x² + 4, then f″ = 18x, all real x.\n(b) Write f = x + x⁻¹, with x ≠ 0. First derivative: 1 − x⁻².\nSecond derivative is 2x⁻³; third derivative is −6x⁻⁴ = −6/x⁴.\n(c) Write s = (5t + 7)¹/². First derivative is (5/2)(5t + 7)⁻¹/².\nChain rule again gives s″ = (5/2)(−1/2)(5)(5t + 7)⁻³/² = −25/[4(5t + 7)³/²], t > −7/5.\n(d) Rewrite y = 1 + 2/(x − 1), x ≠ 1. First derivative is −2/(x − 1)².\nThe second derivative is 4/(x − 1)³. The original pole remains excluded.", "answer": "(a) 18x; (b) −6/x⁴; (c) −25/[4(5t + 7)³/²]; (d) 4/(x − 1)³, with stated domains.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 109, "jpg": "059_page_1.jpg", "verification": "source question and derivative order visually compared with JPG and PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPages": [171, 172], "printedPages": [334, 335], "scope": "Exercise 4.1 Questions 1–6 only; answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-1-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Use implicit rule to find out the second derivative: (a) b²x² + a²y² = a²b²; (b) x² + y² = r²; (c) y² − 2xy = 0; (d) eˣ + x = eʸ + y.", "steps": ["(a) With fixed constants a,b, implicit differentiation gives b²x + a²yy′ = 0. If a ≠ 0 and y ≠ 0, y′ = −b²x/(a²y).", "Differentiate again: b² + a²[(y′)² + yy″] = 0. Substitute y′ and use b²x² + a²y² = a²b² to obtain y″ = −b⁴/(a²y³). For an ellipse, a,b are nonzero.", "(b) First derivative gives x + yy′ = 0, hence y′ = −x/y for y ≠ 0. Second derivative gives 1 + (y′)² + yy″ = 0.", "Use x² + y² = r² to obtain y″ = −r²/y³. Points with y = 0 require a separate tangent analysis.", "(c) The equation factors as y(y − 2x) = 0, the union of two straight lines. Away from their intersection, the local branches are y = 0 or y = 2x; both have y″ = 0.", "The implicit formula (2y − 2x)y′ = 2y divides by zero at (0,0). At that point the whole union is not a single graph, though each selected line has second derivative zero.", "(d) Differentiate: eˣ + 1 = (eʸ + 1)y′, so y′ = (eˣ + 1)/(eʸ + 1).", "Differentiate again: y″ = [eˣ(eʸ + 1)² − eʸ(eˣ + 1)²]/(eʸ + 1)³ = (eˣ − eʸ)(eˣ⁺ʸ − 1)/(eʸ + 1)³.", "Check the actual real curve: h(u) = eᵘ + u is strictly increasing since h′(u) = eᵘ + 1 > 0. Thus h(x) = h(y) implies y = x, so y′ = 1 and y″ = 0 on the curve. The unsimplified formula agrees."], "solution": "(a) With fixed constants a,b, implicit differentiation gives b²x + a²yy′ = 0. If a ≠ 0 and y ≠ 0, y′ = −b²x/(a²y).\nDifferentiate again: b² + a²[(y′)² + yy″] = 0. Substitute y′ and use b²x² + a²y² = a²b² to obtain y″ = −b⁴/(a²y³). For an ellipse, a,b are nonzero.\n(b) First derivative gives x + yy′ = 0, hence y′ = −x/y for y ≠ 0. Second derivative gives 1 + (y′)² + yy″ = 0.\nUse x² + y² = r² to obtain y″ = −r²/y³. Points with y = 0 require a separate tangent analysis.\n(c) The equation factors as y(y − 2x) = 0, the union of two straight lines. Away from their intersection, the local branches are y = 0 or y = 2x; both have y″ = 0.\nThe implicit formula (2y − 2x)y′ = 2y divides by zero at (0,0). At that point the whole union is not a single graph, though each selected line has second derivative zero.\n(d) Differentiate: eˣ + 1 = (eʸ + 1)y′, so y′ = (eˣ + 1)/(eʸ + 1).\nDifferentiate again: y″ = [eˣ(eʸ + 1)² − eʸ(eˣ + 1)²]/(eʸ + 1)³ = (eˣ − eʸ)(eˣ⁺ʸ − 1)/(eʸ + 1)³.\nCheck the actual real curve: h(u) = eᵘ + u is strictly increasing since h′(u) = eᵘ + 1 > 0. Thus h(x) = h(y) implies y = x, so y′ = 1 and y″ = 0 on the curve. The unsimplified formula agrees.", "answer": "(a) −b⁴/(a²y³); (b) −r²/y³; (c) 0 on either selected line; (d) 0 on its real curve, with implicit formulas and restrictions above.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 109, "jpg": "059_page_1.jpg", "verification": "source question and derivative order visually compared with JPG and PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPages": [171, 172], "printedPages": [334, 335], "scope": "Exercise 4.1 Questions 1–6 only; answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-1-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Use parametric differentiation to find out d²y/dx²: (a) x = 4t² + 1, y = 6t³ + 1; (b) x = 3at² + 2, y = 6t⁴ + 9; (c) x = a cos2t, y = b sin2t; (d) \\(x=\\frac{3at}{1+t^3},\\ y=\\frac{3at^2}{1+t^3}\\).", "steps": ["Use d²y/dx² = (d/dt[dy/dx])/(dx/dt), with dx/dt ≠ 0.", "(a) x′ = 8t, y′ₜ = 18t². Thus dy/dx = 9t/4 and its t derivative is 9/4. Divide by 8t: y″ = 9/(32t), t ≠ 0.", "(b) x′ = 6at and y′ₜ = 24t³. For a ≠ 0,t ≠ 0, dy/dx = 4t²/a; differentiating and dividing gives y″ = (8t/a)/(6at) = 4/(3a²).", "The selected curve also satisfies y = 2(x − 2)²/(3a²) + 9. Its one-sided second derivative extends to x = 2, though the parametric quotient at t = 0 is unavailable.", "(c) x′ = −2a sin2t, y′ₜ = 2b cos2t. For a ≠ 0 and sin2t ≠ 0, dy/dx = −(b/a)cot2t.", "Differentiate the slope: (2b/a)csc²2t. Divide by −2a sin2t to obtain y″ = −b/(a²sin³2t).", "(d) Require t ≠ −1. Quotient rule gives x′ = 3a(1 − 2t³)/(1 + t³)² and y′ₜ = 3at(2 − t³)/(1 + t³)².", "For a ≠ 0 and 1 − 2t³ ≠ 0, the slope is B/C with B = 2t − t⁴, C = 1 − 2t³.", "Differentiate B/C: B′ = 2C, C′ = −6t², so its derivative is [2C² + 6t²B]/C² = 2(1 + t³)²/C².", "Divide by x′ to obtain y″ = 2(1 + t³)⁴/[3a(1 − 2t³)³]. Keep a ≠ 0, t ≠ −1, t ≠ ∛(1/2)."], "solution": "Use d²y/dx² = (d/dt[dy/dx])/(dx/dt), with dx/dt ≠ 0.\n(a) x′ = 8t, y′ₜ = 18t². Thus dy/dx = 9t/4 and its t derivative is 9/4. Divide by 8t: y″ = 9/(32t), t ≠ 0.\n(b) x′ = 6at and y′ₜ = 24t³. For a ≠ 0,t ≠ 0, dy/dx = 4t²/a; differentiating and dividing gives y″ = (8t/a)/(6at) = 4/(3a²).\nThe selected curve also satisfies y = 2(x − 2)²/(3a²) + 9. Its one-sided second derivative extends to x = 2, though the parametric quotient at t = 0 is unavailable.\n(c) x′ = −2a sin2t, y′ₜ = 2b cos2t. For a ≠ 0 and sin2t ≠ 0, dy/dx = −(b/a)cot2t.\nDifferentiate the slope: (2b/a)csc²2t. Divide by −2a sin2t to obtain y″ = −b/(a²sin³2t).\n(d) Require t ≠ −1. Quotient rule gives x′ = 3a(1 − 2t³)/(1 + t³)² and y′ₜ = 3at(2 − t³)/(1 + t³)².\nFor a ≠ 0 and 1 − 2t³ ≠ 0, the slope is B/C with B = 2t − t⁴, C = 1 − 2t³.\nDifferentiate B/C: B′ = 2C, C′ = −6t², so its derivative is [2C² + 6t²B]/C² = 2(1 + t³)²/C².\nDivide by x′ to obtain y″ = 2(1 + t³)⁴/[3a(1 − 2t³)³]. Keep a ≠ 0, t ≠ −1, t ≠ ∛(1/2).", "answer": "(a) 9/(32t); (b) 4/(3a²); (c) −b/(a²sin³2t); (d) 2(1 + t³)⁴/[3a(1 − 2t³)³], with stated restrictions.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 109, "jpg": "059_page_1.jpg", "verification": "source question and derivative order visually compared with JPG and PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPages": [171, 172], "printedPages": [334, 335], "scope": "Exercise 4.1 Questions 1–6 only; answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "The lengthy source-key expression for the rational parametric case simplifies to 2(1 + t³)⁴/[3a(1 − 2t³)³]. Original parameter exclusions are retained."}, {"id": "ex-4-1-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "Find the indicated higher order derivative: (a) f(x) = (x³ + 4x − 5)⁴, f⁽ⁱᵛ⁾(x); (b) f(x) = tan²x, f⁽ⁱⁱⁱ⁾(x); (c) \\(f(x)=\\frac12e^{9x}\\), f⁽ⁱⁱⁱ⁾(x); (d) f(x) = x⁴ ln|x²|, f⁽ⁱᵛ⁾(x).", "steps": ["(a) Set u = x³ + 4x − 5, v = u′ = 3x² + 4, u″ = 6x, u‴ = 6, u⁽⁴⁾ = 0.", "First derivative of u⁴ is 4u³v; second is 12u²v² + 4u³u″.", "Third derivative is 24uv³ + 36u²v u″ + 4u³u‴.", "One more derivative gives 24v⁴ + 144uv²u″ + 36u²(u″)² + 48u²v u‴. Therefore f⁽⁴⁾ = 24v⁴ + 864xuv² + 1296x²u² + 288u²v. The answer key loses powers of v in its first two terms.", "(b) Put T = tan x, T′ = 1 + T². First derivative of T² is 2T(1 + T²); second is 2(1 + T²)(1 + 3T²).", "Third derivative is 16T(1 + T²) + 24T³(1 + T²), with cos x ≠ 0. This agrees with the key’s factored expression.", "(c) Each derivative multiplies e⁹ˣ by 9. Thus f‴ = (729/2)e⁹ˣ, all real x. The key adds a stray factor ln|e|³, which is 1 when its logarithm is natural.", "(d) Let L = ln(x²), x ≠ 0, so L′ = 2/x. Then f′ = x³(4L + 2), f″ = x²(12L + 14).", "Third derivative is x(24L + 52); fourth derivative is 24L + 100. The key prints the third derivative despite the fourth-order source request."], "solution": "(a) Set u = x³ + 4x − 5, v = u′ = 3x² + 4, u″ = 6x, u‴ = 6, u⁽⁴⁾ = 0.\nFirst derivative of u⁴ is 4u³v; second is 12u²v² + 4u³u″.\nThird derivative is 24uv³ + 36u²v u″ + 4u³u‴.\nOne more derivative gives 24v⁴ + 144uv²u″ + 36u²(u″)² + 48u²v u‴. Therefore f⁽⁴⁾ = 24v⁴ + 864xuv² + 1296x²u² + 288u²v. The answer key loses powers of v in its first two terms.\n(b) Put T = tan x, T′ = 1 + T². First derivative of T² is 2T(1 + T²); second is 2(1 + T²)(1 + 3T²).\nThird derivative is 16T(1 + T²) + 24T³(1 + T²), with cos x ≠ 0. This agrees with the key’s factored expression.\n(c) Each derivative multiplies e⁹ˣ by 9. Thus f‴ = (729/2)e⁹ˣ, all real x. The key adds a stray factor ln|e|³, which is 1 when its logarithm is natural.\n(d) Let L = ln(x²), x ≠ 0, so L′ = 2/x. Then f′ = x³(4L + 2), f″ = x²(12L + 14).\nThird derivative is x(24L + 52); fourth derivative is 24L + 100. The key prints the third derivative despite the fourth-order source request.", "answer": "(a) 24v⁴ + 864xuv² + 1296x²u² + 288u²v; (b) 16T(1 + T²) + 24T³(1 + T²); (c) (729/2)e⁹ˣ; (d) 24ln(x²) + 100.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 109, "jpg": "059_page_1.jpg", "verification": "source question and derivative order visually compared with JPG and PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPages": [171, 172], "printedPages": [334, 335], "scope": "Exercise 4.1 Questions 1–6 only; answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "The answer key drops powers in (a) and supplies a third derivative for the fourth-derivative request in (d). Corrections are separate from the source question."}, {"id": "ex-4-1-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "Use MAPLE command “diff” to find the indicated higher order derivative: (a) \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{\\sec(2x)}\\), f‴(x); (b) f(x) = sin(sin x), f‴(x).", "steps": ["(a) The real function √sec2x requires cos2x > 0. Put U = √sec2x and T = tan2x.", "Chain rule gives U′ = UT, and T′ = 2sec²2x = 2(1 + T²).", "The second derivative is U[T² + 2(1 + T²)] = U(2 + 3T²).", "The third derivative is UT(2 + 3T²) + U(6T)(2)(1 + T²) = U(14T + 15T³).", "Request this order with diff(diff(diff(sqrt(sec(2*x)),x),x),x); the expected result is √sec2x·tan2x·(14 + 15tan²2x). The answer key’s printed expression is inconsistent with this calculation.", "(b) Put S = sin x, C = cos x. First derivative is cos(S)C; second is −sin(S)C² − cos(S)S.", "Third derivative: −cos(S)C³ + 2sin(S)CS + sin(S)CS − cos(S)C.", "Thus f‴ = −cos(sin x)cos³x + 3sin(sin x)cos x sin x − cos(sin x)cos x, all real x.", "Use diff(diff(diff(sin(sin(x)),x),x),x); these are expected symbolic outputs independently checked by differentiation, not an executed Maple session."], "solution": "(a) The real function √sec2x requires cos2x > 0. Put U = √sec2x and T = tan2x.\nChain rule gives U′ = UT, and T′ = 2sec²2x = 2(1 + T²).\nThe second derivative is U[T² + 2(1 + T²)] = U(2 + 3T²).\nThe third derivative is UT(2 + 3T²) + U(6T)(2)(1 + T²) = U(14T + 15T³).\nRequest this order with diff(diff(diff(sqrt(sec(2*x)),x),x),x); the expected result is √sec2x·tan2x·(14 + 15tan²2x). The answer key’s printed expression is inconsistent with this calculation.\n(b) Put S = sin x, C = cos x. First derivative is cos(S)C; second is −sin(S)C² − cos(S)S.\nThird derivative: −cos(S)C³ + 2sin(S)CS + sin(S)CS − cos(S)C.\nThus f‴ = −cos(sin x)cos³x + 3sin(sin x)cos x sin x − cos(sin x)cos x, all real x.\nUse diff(diff(diff(sin(sin(x)),x),x),x); these are expected symbolic outputs independently checked by differentiation, not an executed Maple session.", "answer": "(a) √sec2x·tan2x·(14 + 15tan²2x); (b) −cos(sin x)cos³x + 3sin(sin x)cos x sin x − cos(sin x)cos x.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 109, "jpg": "059_page_1.jpg", "verification": "source question and derivative order visually compared with JPG and PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPages": [171, 172], "printedPages": [334, 335], "scope": "Exercise 4.1 Questions 1–6 only; answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "The printed key in (a) disagrees with three successive differentiations. Commands are provided with expected manually checked outputs."}, {"id": "ex-4-1-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "Find the indicated derivative by using rule: (a) y = (3x + 7)¹¹, 7th derivative; (b) f(x) = ln(2x − 4), 10th derivative; (c) g(x) = 4cos(3x + 8), 6th derivative; (d) h(x) = 7e⁵ˣ⁺⁴, 12th derivative.", "steps": ["(a) Use n = 7,m = 11,a = 3. The falling factorial is 11!/4! = 1663200.", "Hence y⁽⁷⁾ = 1663200·3⁷(3x + 7)⁴. The answer key substitutes (3x + 2)⁴, which is a different source function.", "(b) For ln(ax+b), f⁽ⁿ⁾ = (−1)^(n−1)(n−1)!aⁿ/(ax+b)ⁿ.", "At n = 10,a = 2: f⁽¹⁰⁾ = −9!2¹⁰/(2x − 4)¹⁰, x > 2. The key’s −2¹¹·181440 is equivalent because 2·181440 = 9!.", "(c) The nth cosine derivative shifts the phase by nπ/2 and supplies 3ⁿ. Thus g⁽⁶⁾ = 4·3⁶cos(3x + 8 + 3π) = −2916cos(3x + 8).", "(d) Each derivative of 7e⁵ˣ⁺⁴ supplies a factor 5, so h⁽¹²⁾ = 7·5¹²e⁵ˣ⁺⁴. The +4 remains in the exponent.", "The polynomial, cosine and exponential cases have all-real domains; the logarithmic case retains x > 2."], "solution": "(a) Use n = 7,m = 11,a = 3. The falling factorial is 11!/4! = 1663200.\nHence y⁽⁷⁾ = 1663200·3⁷(3x + 7)⁴. The answer key substitutes (3x + 2)⁴, which is a different source function.\n(b) For ln(ax+b), f⁽ⁿ⁾ = (−1)^(n−1)(n−1)!aⁿ/(ax+b)ⁿ.\nAt n = 10,a = 2: f⁽¹⁰⁾ = −9!2¹⁰/(2x − 4)¹⁰, x > 2. The key’s −2¹¹·181440 is equivalent because 2·181440 = 9!.\n(c) The nth cosine derivative shifts the phase by nπ/2 and supplies 3ⁿ. Thus g⁽⁶⁾ = 4·3⁶cos(3x + 8 + 3π) = −2916cos(3x + 8).\n(d) Each derivative of 7e⁵ˣ⁺⁴ supplies a factor 5, so h⁽¹²⁾ = 7·5¹²e⁵ˣ⁺⁴. The +4 remains in the exponent.\nThe polynomial, cosine and exponential cases have all-real domains; the logarithmic case retains x > 2.", "answer": "(a) 1663200·3⁷(3x + 7)⁴; (b) −9!2¹⁰/(2x − 4)¹⁰; (c) −2916cos(3x + 8); (d) 7·5¹²e⁵ˣ⁺⁴.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 109, "jpg": "059_page_1.jpg", "verification": "source question and derivative order visually compared with JPG and PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPages": [171, 172], "printedPages": [334, 335], "scope": "Exercise 4.1 Questions 1–6 only; answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "Question 6(a) uses +7 in the source; the key uses +2. Question 6(b) has an equivalent coefficient written in a different factorization."}, {"id": "ex-4-1-project-1", "qNo": "Project 1", "question": "\\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\frac{(x^2+4)^{10}\\sin^5(\\sqrt{x})}{\\sqrt{1+\\cos x}}\\right]\\)", "given": "By using the chain rule and other differential rules, some of the derivative computations can be radius to perform. For complicated derivatives mathematicians, scientists and engineers use computer softwares. Such as mathematica, maple and Matlab, use computer software to compute.", "steps": ["Write A = (x² + 4)¹⁰, B = sin⁵√x and C = (1 + cos x)⁻¹/², so the function is ABC.", "The real source domain is x ≥ 0 and 1 + cos x > 0. For ordinary chain differentiation take x > 0; sin√x = 0 is allowed.", "Differentiate A: A′ = 20x(x² + 4)⁹. Differentiate B: B′ = 5sin⁴√x cos√x/(2√x).", "Differentiate C: C′ = sin x/[2(1 + cos x)³/²].", "Product rule gives A′BC + AB′C + ABC′. This direct sum remains valid at positive zeros of sin√x, unlike a logarithmic-derivative form that divides by sin√x.", "The three terms are 20x(x² + 4)⁹sin⁵√x/√(1 + cos x), plus 5(x² + 4)¹⁰sin⁴√x cos√x/[2√x√(1 + cos x)], plus (x² + 4)¹⁰sin⁵√x sin x/[2(1 + cos x)³/²].", "At x = 0, use the right derivative separately: sin⁵√x behaves as x⁵/², so the function’s right derivative is zero. This limit is not a two-sided derivative on the real source domain.", "A software request is diff((x^2+4)^10*sin(sqrt(x))^5/sqrt(1+cos(x)),x); the expression above is its independently derived expected derivative."], "solution": "Write A = (x² + 4)¹⁰, B = sin⁵√x and C = (1 + cos x)⁻¹/², so the function is ABC.\nThe real source domain is x ≥ 0 and 1 + cos x > 0. For ordinary chain differentiation take x > 0; sin√x = 0 is allowed.\nDifferentiate A: A′ = 20x(x² + 4)⁹. Differentiate B: B′ = 5sin⁴√x cos√x/(2√x).\nDifferentiate C: C′ = sin x/[2(1 + cos x)³/²].\nProduct rule gives A′BC + AB′C + ABC′. This direct sum remains valid at positive zeros of sin√x, unlike a logarithmic-derivative form that divides by sin√x.\nThe three terms are 20x(x² + 4)⁹sin⁵√x/√(1 + cos x), plus 5(x² + 4)¹⁰sin⁴√x cos√x/[2√x√(1 + cos x)], plus (x² + 4)¹⁰sin⁵√x sin x/[2(1 + cos x)³/²].\nAt x = 0, use the right derivative separately: sin⁵√x behaves as x⁵/², so the function’s right derivative is zero. This limit is not a two-sided derivative on the real source domain.\nA software request is diff((x^2+4)^10*sin(sqrt(x))^5/sqrt(1+cos(x)),x); the expression above is its independently derived expected derivative.", "answer": "The three-term product-rule derivative is given above, with the original real domain.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 109, "jpg": "059_page_1.jpg", "verification": "exact source project expression compared with JPG and PDF"}]}, {"id": "ex-4-1-project-2", "qNo": "Project 2", "question": "\\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\frac{\\sqrt{1+\\csc x}}{(x^2+4)^{10}\\sin^5(\\sqrt{x})}\\right]\\)", "given": "By using the chain rule and other differential rules, some of the derivative computations can be radius to perform. For complicated derivatives mathematicians, scientists and engineers use computer softwares. Such as mathematica, maple and Matlab, use computer software to compute.", "steps": ["Write U = √(1 + csc x), A = (x² + 4)⁻¹⁰ and B = sin⁻⁵√x, so the function is UAB.", "The real source domain requires x > 0, sin x ≠ 0, sin√x ≠ 0 and 1 + csc x ≥ 0. For a smooth interval assume the last expression is strictly positive.", "U′ = −csc x cot x/[2√(1 + csc x)]; A′ = −20x(x² + 4)⁻¹¹.", "B′ = −5cos√x/[2√x sin⁶√x].", "The derivative is U′AB + UA′B + UAB′: −csc x cot x/[2√(1 + csc x)(x² + 4)¹⁰sin⁵√x], minus 20x√(1 + csc x)/[(x² + 4)¹¹sin⁵√x], minus 5√(1 + csc x)cos√x/[2√x(x² + 4)¹⁰sin⁶√x].", "At a source-domain point where sin x = −1, the numerator U is zero. Locally 1 + csc x is negative on either side, so there is no real open interval on which the source function is defined; the displayed smooth derivative does not apply there.", "A software request is diff(sqrt(1+csc(x))/((x^2+4)^10*sin(sqrt(x))^5),x); keep the original domain exclusions when simplifying its expected output."], "solution": "Write U = √(1 + csc x), A = (x² + 4)⁻¹⁰ and B = sin⁻⁵√x, so the function is UAB.\nThe real source domain requires x > 0, sin x ≠ 0, sin√x ≠ 0 and 1 + csc x ≥ 0. For a smooth interval assume the last expression is strictly positive.\nU′ = −csc x cot x/[2√(1 + csc x)]; A′ = −20x(x² + 4)⁻¹¹.\nB′ = −5cos√x/[2√x sin⁶√x].\nThe derivative is U′AB + UA′B + UAB′: −csc x cot x/[2√(1 + csc x)(x² + 4)¹⁰sin⁵√x], minus 20x√(1 + csc x)/[(x² + 4)¹¹sin⁵√x], minus 5√(1 + csc x)cos√x/[2√x(x² + 4)¹⁰sin⁶√x].\nAt a source-domain point where sin x = −1, the numerator U is zero. Locally 1 + csc x is negative on either side, so there is no real open interval on which the source function is defined; the displayed smooth derivative does not apply there.\nA software request is diff(sqrt(1+csc(x))/((x^2+4)^10*sin(sqrt(x))^5),x); keep the original domain exclusions when simplifying its expected output.", "answer": "The three-term product-rule derivative is given above, with the original real domain.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 109, "jpg": "059_page_1.jpg", "verification": "exact source project expression compared with JPG and PDF"}]}];
const math12Ex41=MATH_12_DATA[3].exercises.find(ex=>ex.title==='Exercise 4.1');math12Ex41.unreviewedProblems=math12Ex41.problems;math12Ex41.problems=math12Exercise41;math12Ex41.pageStart=math12Ex41.pageEnd=109;MATH_12_DATA[3].exercises=MATH_12_DATA[3].exercises.filter(ex=>ex!==math12Ex41);MATH_12_DATA[3].exercises.unshift(math12Ex41);
const math12Taylor=MATH_12_DATA[3].sections[1];math12Taylor.unreviewedTheory='';math12Taylor.sourceBlocks=MATH_12_DATA[3].sourcePages.filter(page=>page.page>=110&&page.page<=114).flatMap(page=>page.blocks);math12Taylor.sourceReferences=MATH_12_DATA[3].sourcePages.filter(page=>page.page>=110&&page.page<=114).map(page=>({page:page.page,jpg:page.file,verification:'visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}));math12Taylor.theory=math12Taylor.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');
MATH_12_DATA[3].exercises.push({id:"ex-4-taylor-challenge",title:"Challenge — Taylor Series at 3",exercise:"Challenge — Taylor Series at 3",pageStart:114,pageEnd:114,problems:[{"id": "ex-4-taylor-challenge", "qNo": "Challenge", "question": "Use Taylor’s theorem to compute series at x₀ = 3 for sinx,cosx,tanx,aˣ,eˣ,logₐ(1 + x),ln(1 + x).", "steps": ["Put h = x − 3. Taylor expansion about 3 uses f(3) + f′(3)h + f″(3)h²/2! + f‴(3)h³/3! + … .", "(i) Use sin(3 + h) = sin3 cos(h) + cos3 sin(h). Insert the sine/cosine Maclaurin series to obtain sin3 + h cos3 − h²sin3/2 − h³cos3/6 + … . The infinite series converges for all real h.", "(ii) cos(3+h) = cos3 − h sin3 − h²cos3/2 + h³sin3/6 + …, all real h.", "(iii) Set T = tan3,Q = sec²3. The first three derivatives at 3 are Q,2QT and 2Q² + 4QT². Thus tan(3+h) = T + Qh + QTh² + Q(1 + 3T²)h³/3 + … . Its interval is |h| < 3 − π/2, the distance to the nearest tangent pole.", "(iv) With a > 0,L = ln a, a^(3+h) = a³[1 + Lh + L²h²/2 + L³h³/6 + …], all real h.", "(v) e^(3+h) = e³[1 + h + h²/2 + h³/6 + …], all real h.", "(vi) For a > 0,a ≠ 1: logₐ(4+h) = logₐ4 + [h/4 − h²/32 + h³/192 − …]/ln a. The series converges for −4 < h ≤ 4, with h > −4 required by the real logarithm.", "(vii) ln(4+h) = ln4 + h/4 − h²/32 + h³/192 − …, since ln(4+h) = ln4 + ln(1+h/4). The same interval −4 < h ≤ 4 applies.", "For sine and cosine, h is an angle in radians. The nearest-singularity restriction is necessary for tangent and logarithms; listing derivative coefficients alone does not justify all-real convergence."], "answer": "The seven Taylor expansions about 3 and their convergence intervals are shown above.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 114, "jpg": "061_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all seven source challenge functions visually compared with JPG/PDF"}], "solution": "Put h = x − 3. Taylor expansion about 3 uses f(3) + f′(3)h + f″(3)h²/2! + f‴(3)h³/3! + … .\n(i) Use sin(3 + h) = sin3 cos(h) + cos3 sin(h). Insert the sine/cosine Maclaurin series to obtain sin3 + h cos3 − h²sin3/2 − h³cos3/6 + … . The infinite series converges for all real h.\n(ii) cos(3+h) = cos3 − h sin3 − h²cos3/2 + h³sin3/6 + …, all real h.\n(iii) Set T = tan3,Q = sec²3. The first three derivatives at 3 are Q,2QT and 2Q² + 4QT². Thus tan(3+h) = T + Qh + QTh² + Q(1 + 3T²)h³/3 + … . Its interval is |h| < 3 − π/2, the distance to the nearest tangent pole.\n(iv) With a > 0,L = ln a, a^(3+h) = a³[1 + Lh + L²h²/2 + L³h³/6 + …], all real h.\n(v) e^(3+h) = e³[1 + h + h²/2 + h³/6 + …], all real h.\n(vi) For a > 0,a ≠ 1: logₐ(4+h) = logₐ4 + [h/4 − h²/32 + h³/192 − …]/ln a. The series converges for −4 < h ≤ 4, with h > −4 required by the real logarithm.\n(vii) ln(4+h) = ln4 + h/4 − h²/32 + h³/192 − …, since ln(4+h) = ln4 + ln(1+h/4). The same interval −4 < h ≤ 4 applies.\nFor sine and cosine, h is an angle in radians. The nearest-singularity restriction is necessary for tangent and logarithms; listing derivative coefficients alone does not justify all-real convergence."}]});
const math12Tangent=MATH_12_DATA[3].sections[2];math12Tangent.unreviewedTheory='';math12Tangent.sourceBlocks=MATH_12_DATA[3].sourcePages.filter(page=>page.page>=115&&page.page<=117).flatMap(page=>page.blocks);math12Tangent.sourceReferences=MATH_12_DATA[3].sourcePages.filter(page=>page.page>=115&&page.page<=117).map(page=>({page:page.page,jpg:page.file,verification:'visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}));math12Tangent.theory=math12Tangent.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');
const math12Exercise42=[{"id": "ex-4-2-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "In each case, find the equation of the tangent line at the indicated x: (a) \\(y=\\sqrt{x+1}\\), x = 3; (b) y = sin(2x + π), x = 0; (c) y = x²e⁻ˣ, x = 1; (d) \\(y=\\frac{x}{x^2+1}\\), x = 1.", "steps": ["For each tangent use y − y₀ = f′(x₀)(x − x₀), after evaluating y₀.", "(a) At x = 3, y₀ = 2 and f′ = 1/(2√(x + 1)), so m = 1/4.", "The line is y − 2 = (x − 3)/4, or y = (x + 5)/4.", "(b) At x = 0, y₀ = sinπ = 0. The slope 2cos(2x + π) is −2, hence y = −2x.", "(c) Product rule gives f′ = e⁻ˣ(2x − x²). At x = 1, both y₀ and m are 1/e.", "Thus y − 1/e = (x − 1)/e, simplifying to y = x/e.", "(d) Quotient rule gives f′ = (1 − x²)/(x² + 1)². At x = 1, m = 0 and y₀ = 1/2, so the tangent is horizontal: y = 1/2."], "solution": "For each tangent use y − y₀ = f′(x₀)(x − x₀), after evaluating y₀.\n(a) At x = 3, y₀ = 2 and f′ = 1/(2√(x + 1)), so m = 1/4.\nThe line is y − 2 = (x − 3)/4, or y = (x + 5)/4.\n(b) At x = 0, y₀ = sinπ = 0. The slope 2cos(2x + π) is −2, hence y = −2x.\n(c) Product rule gives f′ = e⁻ˣ(2x − x²). At x = 1, both y₀ and m are 1/e.\nThus y − 1/e = (x − 1)/e, simplifying to y = x/e.\n(d) Quotient rule gives f′ = (1 − x²)/(x² + 1)². At x = 1, m = 0 and y₀ = 1/2, so the tangent is horizontal: y = 1/2.", "answer": "(a) y = (x + 5)/4; (b) y = −2x; (c) y = x/e; (d) y = 1/2.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 118, "jpg": "063_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source subparts and coefficients visually checked"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPage": 335, "scope": "Exercise 4.2 Questions 1–8 only; answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-2-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "In each case, find the equation of normal at the indicated x: (a) y = xeˣ, x = 1; (b) y = (2x + 1)⁶, x = 0; (c) y = cos(x − π), x = π/2; (d) y = x³lnx, x = 1.", "steps": ["At each point compute the tangent slope m and use the normal slope −1/m when m ≠ 0.", "(a) f′ = eˣ(x + 1). At 1, the point is (1,e) and m = 2e.", "The normal is y − e = −(x − 1)/(2e), or y = −x/(2e) + (1 + 2e²)/(2e).", "(b) f′ = 12(2x + 1)⁵. At 0, the point is (0,1) and m = 12, giving y = 1 − x/12.", "(c) f′ = −sin(x − π). At x = π/2, y₀ = 0 and m = 1.", "The normal is y = −(x − π/2) = −x + π/2.", "(d) Product rule gives f′ = 3x²lnx + x². At 1, the point is (1,0) and m = 1. The normal is y = −(x − 1) = −x + 1."], "solution": "At each point compute the tangent slope m and use the normal slope −1/m when m ≠ 0.\n(a) f′ = eˣ(x + 1). At 1, the point is (1,e) and m = 2e.\nThe normal is y − e = −(x − 1)/(2e), or y = −x/(2e) + (1 + 2e²)/(2e).\n(b) f′ = 12(2x + 1)⁵. At 0, the point is (0,1) and m = 12, giving y = 1 − x/12.\n(c) f′ = −sin(x − π). At x = π/2, y₀ = 0 and m = 1.\nThe normal is y = −(x − π/2) = −x + π/2.\n(d) Product rule gives f′ = 3x²lnx + x². At 1, the point is (1,0) and m = 1. The normal is y = −(x − 1) = −x + 1.", "answer": "(a) y − e = −(x − 1)/(2e); (b) y = 1 − x/12; (c) y = −x + π/2; (d) y = −x + 1.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 118, "jpg": "063_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source subparts and coefficients visually checked"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPage": 335, "scope": "Exercise 4.2 Questions 1–8 only; answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-2-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "(a) Find a tangent to x² + y² = 13 at (−2,3). (b) Find a tangent to sin(x − y) = xy at (0,π). (c) Find a normal to x² + 2xy = y³ at (1,−1). (d) Find a normal to \\(x^2\\sqrt{y-2}=y^2-3x-1\\) at (1,2).", "steps": ["(a) Differentiate x² + y² = 13 to obtain y′ = −x/y. At (−2,3), m = 2/3.", "The tangent is y − 3 = (2/3)(x + 2), or y = 2x/3 + 13/3.", "(b) Differentiate sin(x − y) = xy: cos(x − y)(1 − y′) = y + xy′.", "At (0,π), −1(1 − y′) = π, so y′ = 1 + π. The tangent is y − π = (1 + π)x.", "(c) Differentiate x² + 2xy = y³: 2x + 2y + 2xy′ = 3y²y′. Thus y′ = −(2x + 2y)/(2x − 3y²). At (1,−1) it is 0; the normal is x = 1.", "(d) The radical makes direct differentiation singular at y = 2. Introduce u = √(y − 2) ≥ 0, so y = u² + 2.", "The source equation becomes x²u = u⁴ + 4u² + 3 − 3x. At (x,u) = (1,0), differentiation with respect to u gives 2xu xᵤ + x² = 4u³ + 8u − 3xᵤ.", "Thus xᵤ = −1/3, while yᵤ = 2u = 0. The one-sided tangent slope on the real branch is 0, so the normal is x = 1.", "This point is an endpoint of the source real branch; it is not justified by substituting y = 2 into a derivative that divides by √(y − 2)."], "solution": "(a) Differentiate x² + y² = 13 to obtain y′ = −x/y. At (−2,3), m = 2/3.\nThe tangent is y − 3 = (2/3)(x + 2), or y = 2x/3 + 13/3.\n(b) Differentiate sin(x − y) = xy: cos(x − y)(1 − y′) = y + xy′.\nAt (0,π), −1(1 − y′) = π, so y′ = 1 + π. The tangent is y − π = (1 + π)x.\n(c) Differentiate x² + 2xy = y³: 2x + 2y + 2xy′ = 3y²y′. Thus y′ = −(2x + 2y)/(2x − 3y²). At (1,−1) it is 0; the normal is x = 1.\n(d) The radical makes direct differentiation singular at y = 2. Introduce u = √(y − 2) ≥ 0, so y = u² + 2.\nThe source equation becomes x²u = u⁴ + 4u² + 3 − 3x. At (x,u) = (1,0), differentiation with respect to u gives 2xu xᵤ + x² = 4u³ + 8u − 3xᵤ.\nThus xᵤ = −1/3, while yᵤ = 2u = 0. The one-sided tangent slope on the real branch is 0, so the normal is x = 1.\nThis point is an endpoint of the source real branch; it is not justified by substituting y = 2 into a derivative that divides by √(y − 2).", "answer": "(a) y = 2x/3 + 13/3; (b) y = π + (1 + π)x; (c) x = 1; (d) x = 1 on the real endpoint branch.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 118, "jpg": "063_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source subparts and coefficients visually checked"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPage": 335, "scope": "Exercise 4.2 Questions 1–8 only; answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "The radical curve at (1,2) needs a one-sided branch parameter to justify its normal; direct division by √(y − 2) is invalid there."}, {"id": "ex-4-2-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "Show the first four terms in Taylor series: (a) f(x) = tanx about x = π/4: \\(1+2(x-\\pi/4)+2(x-\\pi/4)^2+\\frac83(x-\\pi/4)^3\\). (b) f(x) = √x about x = 4: \\(2+\\frac14(x-4)-\\frac1{64}(x-4)^2+\\frac1{512}(x-4)^3\\). (c) f(x) = x + eˣ about x = 1: \\((1+e)x+e[\\frac{(x-1)^2}{2!}+\\frac{(x-1)^3}{3!}+\\frac{(x-1)^4}{4!}+...]\\).", "steps": ["Use p₃(x) = f(x₀) + f′(x₀)h + f″(x₀)h²/2 + f‴(x₀)h³/6, h = x − x₀.", "(a) For tanx at π/4, the derivative values are 1,2,4,16. Hence p₃ = 1 + 2h + 2h² + 8h³/3, matching the source.", "This tangent series is local with |h| < π/4, the distance to the nearest pole.", "(b) For √x at 4, the derivative values are 2,1/4,−1/32,3/256.", "Hence p₃ = 2 + h/4 − h²/64 + h³/512. The real function requires x ≥ 0, and the analytic expansion is valid locally about 4.", "(c) For x + eˣ at 1, values are f(1) = 1 + e, f′(1) = 1 + e, and every derivative of order at least 2 equals e at 1.", "The first four Taylor terms are (1 + e) + (1 + e)h + eh²/2 + eh³/6.", "Combine the constant and linear terms using h = x − 1: (1 + e)(1 + h) = (1 + e)x. The source expression adds the quartic term and continuing terms; it is a correct series after this grouping, though more than four original Taylor terms are shown."], "solution": "Use p₃(x) = f(x₀) + f′(x₀)h + f″(x₀)h²/2 + f‴(x₀)h³/6, h = x − x₀.\n(a) For tanx at π/4, the derivative values are 1,2,4,16. Hence p₃ = 1 + 2h + 2h² + 8h³/3, matching the source.\nThis tangent series is local with |h| < π/4, the distance to the nearest pole.\n(b) For √x at 4, the derivative values are 2,1/4,−1/32,3/256.\nHence p₃ = 2 + h/4 − h²/64 + h³/512. The real function requires x ≥ 0, and the analytic expansion is valid locally about 4.\n(c) For x + eˣ at 1, values are f(1) = 1 + e, f′(1) = 1 + e, and every derivative of order at least 2 equals e at 1.\nThe first four Taylor terms are (1 + e) + (1 + e)h + eh²/2 + eh³/6.\nCombine the constant and linear terms using h = x − 1: (1 + e)(1 + h) = (1 + e)x. The source expression adds the quartic term and continuing terms; it is a correct series after this grouping, though more than four original Taylor terms are shown.", "answer": "The three coefficient calculations above reproduce the printed expansions; part (c) combines its constant and linear terms.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 118, "jpg": "063_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source subparts and coefficients visually checked"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPage": 335, "scope": "Exercise 4.2 Questions 1–8 only; answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "Part (c) combines constant and linear contributions into (1 + e)x, then shows a quartic term beyond four original Taylor terms."}, {"id": "ex-4-2-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "Find Maclaurin series for: (a) \\(f(x)=\\frac1{1+x}\\); (b) f(x) = sin²x; (c) f(x) = coshx; (d) f(x) = ln(1 − 4x).", "steps": ["(a) Geometric series gives 1/(1 + x) = 1 − x + x² − x³ + …, |x| < 1.", "(b) Use sin²x = (1 − cos2x)/2.", "Insert cos2x = 1 − (2x)²/2! + (2x)⁴/4! − (2x)⁶/6! + … to obtain sin²x = x² − x⁴/3 + 2x⁶/45 − …, all real x.", "(c) By coshx = (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2, odd terms cancel and even terms add.", "Hence coshx = 1 + x²/2! + x⁴/4! + x⁶/6! + …, all real x.", "(d) Substitute u = −4x into ln(1 + u) = u − u²/2 + u³/3 − … .", "Thus ln(1 − 4x) = −4x − 8x² − 64x³/3 − 64x⁴ − … . Its real domain is x < 1/4; the series converges for −1/4 ≤ x < 1/4."], "solution": "(a) Geometric series gives 1/(1 + x) = 1 − x + x² − x³ + …, |x| < 1.\n(b) Use sin²x = (1 − cos2x)/2.\nInsert cos2x = 1 − (2x)²/2! + (2x)⁴/4! − (2x)⁶/6! + … to obtain sin²x = x² − x⁴/3 + 2x⁶/45 − …, all real x.\n(c) By coshx = (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2, odd terms cancel and even terms add.\nHence coshx = 1 + x²/2! + x⁴/4! + x⁶/6! + …, all real x.\n(d) Substitute u = −4x into ln(1 + u) = u − u²/2 + u³/3 − … .\nThus ln(1 − 4x) = −4x − 8x² − 64x³/3 − 64x⁴ − … . Its real domain is x < 1/4; the series converges for −1/4 ≤ x < 1/4.", "answer": "(a) 1 − x + x² − x³ + …; (b) x² − x⁴/3 + 2x⁶/45 − …; (c) 1 + x²/2! + x⁴/4! + …; (d) −4x − 8x² − 64x³/3 − …, with the stated intervals.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 118, "jpg": "063_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source subparts and coefficients visually checked"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPage": 335, "scope": "Exercise 4.2 Questions 1–8 only; answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-2-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "(a) Use Maclaurin series for eˣ to show that the sum of the infinite series 1 + 1/1! + 1/2! + 1/3! + … is e. (b) Use part (a) to find e accurate to 4 decimal places.", "steps": ["(a) Maclaurin expansion eˣ = Σ from n = 0 to infinity of xⁿ/n! converges for all real x.", "At x = 1, e = 1 + 1/1! + 1/2! + 1/3! + … .", "(b) Add the terms through n = 9: S₉ = 2.7182815255731922.", "The first omitted term is 1/10!; later ratios are at most 1/11. Thus the positive tail is less than (1/10!)/(1 − 1/11) ≈ 0.00000030313.", "Therefore 2.71828152557 < e < 2.71828182871.", "Every value in this interval rounds to 2.7183 at four decimal places.", "This proves the requested precision; merely stopping at an arbitrary number of terms does not establish a rounding guarantee."], "solution": "(a) Maclaurin expansion eˣ = Σ from n = 0 to infinity of xⁿ/n! converges for all real x.\nAt x = 1, e = 1 + 1/1! + 1/2! + 1/3! + … .\n(b) Add the terms through n = 9: S₉ = 2.7182815255731922.\nThe first omitted term is 1/10!; later ratios are at most 1/11. Thus the positive tail is less than (1/10!)/(1 − 1/11) ≈ 0.00000030313.\nTherefore 2.71828152557 < e < 2.71828182871.\nEvery value in this interval rounds to 2.7183 at four decimal places.\nThis proves the requested precision; merely stopping at an arbitrary number of terms does not establish a rounding guarantee.", "answer": "The series sums to e; e = 2.7183 to four decimal places.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 118, "jpg": "063_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source subparts and coefficients visually checked"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPage": 335, "scope": "Exercise 4.2 Questions 1–8 only; answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-2-q7", "qNo": "7", "question": "Find the angle of intersection between: (a) x² − y² = a² and x² + y² = a²√2; (b) y² = ax and x³ + y³ = 3axy.", "steps": ["(a) Assume a ≠ 0 for nondegenerate curves. Add and subtract the equations to obtain x² = a²(√2 + 1)/2 and y² = a²(√2 − 1)/2.", "At each of the four intersections y ≠ 0. The hyperbola slope is m₁ = x/y and circle slope m₂ = −x/y.", "Let m = x/y; then m² = (√2 + 1)/(√2 − 1) = 3 + 2√2, so |m| = √2 + 1.", "The smaller angle satisfies tanθ = |2m/(1 − m²)| = 1. Hence θ = π/4. If a = 0, the curves degenerate and this angle calculation is unavailable.", "(b) Assume a ≠ 0 and substitute x = y²/a into x³ + y³ = 3axy: y⁶/a³ + y³ = 3y³.", "Thus y³(y³ − 2a³) = 0. The intersections are (0,0) and (a∛4,a∛2).", "At the nonzero point, the parabola slope a/(2y) is 1/(2∛2). For the cubic, (3y² − 3ax)y′ = 3ay − 3x²; its y′ coefficient vanishes while the right side is nonzero. The cubic has a vertical tangent.", "The smaller angle from that vertical tangent to the parabola is arctan(2∛2) = arctan(∛16), matching the key for the regular intersection.", "At (0,0) the cubic is singular: its leading term −3axy = 0 has both horizontal and vertical tangent directions. The parabola has a vertical tangent there. Consequently the branch angles are 0 and π/2, with no single angle for the whole singular cubic."], "solution": "(a) Assume a ≠ 0 for nondegenerate curves. Add and subtract the equations to obtain x² = a²(√2 + 1)/2 and y² = a²(√2 − 1)/2.\nAt each of the four intersections y ≠ 0. The hyperbola slope is m₁ = x/y and circle slope m₂ = −x/y.\nLet m = x/y; then m² = (√2 + 1)/(√2 − 1) = 3 + 2√2, so |m| = √2 + 1.\nThe smaller angle satisfies tanθ = |2m/(1 − m²)| = 1. Hence θ = π/4. If a = 0, the curves degenerate and this angle calculation is unavailable.\n(b) Assume a ≠ 0 and substitute x = y²/a into x³ + y³ = 3axy: y⁶/a³ + y³ = 3y³.\nThus y³(y³ − 2a³) = 0. The intersections are (0,0) and (a∛4,a∛2).\nAt the nonzero point, the parabola slope a/(2y) is 1/(2∛2). For the cubic, (3y² − 3ax)y′ = 3ay − 3x²; its y′ coefficient vanishes while the right side is nonzero. The cubic has a vertical tangent.\nThe smaller angle from that vertical tangent to the parabola is arctan(2∛2) = arctan(∛16), matching the key for the regular intersection.\nAt (0,0) the cubic is singular: its leading term −3axy = 0 has both horizontal and vertical tangent directions. The parabola has a vertical tangent there. Consequently the branch angles are 0 and π/2, with no single angle for the whole singular cubic.", "answer": "(a) π/4 for a ≠ 0. (b) arctan(∛16) at the nonzero intersection; the origin has two branch angles, 0 and π/2.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 118, "jpg": "063_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source subparts and coefficients visually checked"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPage": 335, "scope": "Exercise 4.2 Questions 1–8 only; answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "The source/key angle for part (b) describes the regular nonzero intersection. The origin is a singular point with two cubic tangent directions; both branch angles are explained."}, {"id": "ex-4-2-q8", "qNo": "8", "question": "Find the points on y = 5x³ − 4x² where tangent is parallel to y = 5x − 3.", "steps": ["The given line has slope 5. The curve derivative is y′ = 15x² − 8x.", "Set 15x² − 8x = 5, or 15x² − 8x − 5 = 0.", "Quadratic formula gives x = (8 ± √364)/30 = (4 ± √91)/15.", "Let s = √91. Evaluate y = 5x³ − 4x² = x²(5x − 4).", "For the matching sign, x² = (107 ± 8s)/225 and 5x − 4 = (−8 ± s)/3.", "Multiply and use s² = 91: y = (−128 ± 43s)/675.", "The two points are ((4 + √91)/15,(−128 + 43√91)/675) and ((4 − √91)/15,(−128 − 43√91)/675). The key lists only their x-coordinates.", "Check both slopes through the quadratic equation: 15x² − 8x = 5 at both points."], "solution": "The given line has slope 5. The curve derivative is y′ = 15x² − 8x.\nSet 15x² − 8x = 5, or 15x² − 8x − 5 = 0.\nQuadratic formula gives x = (8 ± √364)/30 = (4 ± √91)/15.\nLet s = √91. Evaluate y = 5x³ − 4x² = x²(5x − 4).\nFor the matching sign, x² = (107 ± 8s)/225 and 5x − 4 = (−8 ± s)/3.\nMultiply and use s² = 91: y = (−128 ± 43s)/675.\nThe two points are ((4 + √91)/15,(−128 + 43√91)/675) and ((4 − √91)/15,(−128 − 43√91)/675). The key lists only their x-coordinates.\nCheck both slopes through the quadratic equation: 15x² − 8x = 5 at both points.", "answer": "((4 ± √91)/15,(−128 ± 43√91)/675), using the same sign in each coordinate.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 118, "jpg": "063_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source subparts and coefficients visually checked"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPage": 335, "scope": "Exercise 4.2 Questions 1–8 only; answer page not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "The question requests points, while the answer key supplies only x-coordinates. Both coordinates are provided in the learning solution."}];
const math12Ex42=MATH_12_DATA[3].exercises.find(ex=>ex.title==='Exercise 4.2');math12Ex42.unreviewedProblems=math12Ex42.problems;math12Ex42.problems=math12Exercise42;math12Ex42.pageStart=math12Ex42.pageEnd=118;math12Ex42.sourceContextBlocks=MATH_12_DATA[3].sourcePages.find(page=>page.page===118).blocks.slice(9);
const math12Extrema=MATH_12_DATA[3].sections[3];const math12ExtremaTail=math12Extrema.theory.indexOf('second derivative');math12Extrema.unreviewedTheory='';math12Extrema.sourceBlocks=MATH_12_DATA[3].sourcePages.filter(page=>page.page>=119&&page.page<=126).flatMap(page=>page.page===126?page.blocks.slice(0,page.blocks.findIndex(block=>block.type==='heading'&&block.text==='Exercise 4.3')):page.blocks);math12Extrema.sourceReferences=MATH_12_DATA[3].sourcePages.filter(page=>page.page>=119&&page.page<=126).map(page=>({page:page.page,jpg:page.file,verification:'visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}));math12Extrema.theory=math12Extrema.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n')+'\n\n'+math12Extrema.unreviewedTheory;
const math12Exercise43=[{"id": "ex-4-3-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Find critical values and show where each function increases/decreases: (a) f(x) = x³ + 3x² + 1; (b) f(x) = x³ + 35x² − 125x − 9.375.", "steps": ["(a) Differentiate: f′ = 3x² + 6x = 3x(x + 2). Zeros are −2 and 0.", "The derivative is positive for x < −2 and x > 0, and negative for −2 < x < 0. Thus f increases on (−∞,−2),(0,∞) and decreases on (−2,0).", "The corresponding points are (−2,5) and (0,1); the first is a relative maximum and the second a minimum.", "(b) f′ = 3x² + 70x − 125. Its discriminant is 70² + 4·3·125 = 6400 = 80².", "Roots are (−70 ± 80)/6, namely −25 and 5/3; f′ = 3(x + 25)(x − 5/3).", "The upward quadratic is positive outside these roots and negative between them, so f increases on (−∞,−25),(5/3,∞) and decreases on (−25,5/3).", "The key’s extra extrema-coordinate statements for part (b) disagree with this source polynomial; the interval calculation follows the independently factored derivative."], "solution": "(a) Differentiate: f′ = 3x² + 6x = 3x(x + 2). Zeros are −2 and 0.\nThe derivative is positive for x < −2 and x > 0, and negative for −2 < x < 0. Thus f increases on (−∞,−2),(0,∞) and decreases on (−2,0).\nThe corresponding points are (−2,5) and (0,1); the first is a relative maximum and the second a minimum.\n(b) f′ = 3x² + 70x − 125. Its discriminant is 70² + 4·3·125 = 6400 = 80².\nRoots are (−70 ± 80)/6, namely −25 and 5/3; f′ = 3(x + 25)(x − 5/3).\nThe upward quadratic is positive outside these roots and negative between them, so f increases on (−∞,−25),(5/3,∞) and decreases on (−25,5/3).\nThe key’s extra extrema-coordinate statements for part (b) disagree with this source polynomial; the interval calculation follows the independently factored derivative.", "answer": "(a) Critical −2,0; increasing outside [−2,0], decreasing (−2,0). (b) Critical −25,5/3; increasing (−∞,−25),(5/3,∞), decreasing (−25,5/3).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 126, "jpg": "067_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "The key’s extra extrema-coordinate statements in part (b) are inconsistent with the source polynomial; derivative zeros and intervals are independently checked."}, {"id": "ex-4-3-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Find critical values: (a) f(x) = 2x³ − 3x² − 72x + 15; (b) \\(f(x)=\\frac13x^3-x^2-15x+6\\); (c) \\(f(x)=6x^{2/3}-4x\\); (d) \\(f(x)=3x^{4/3}-12x^{1/3}\\).", "steps": ["All four real source functions are defined at zero, including the cube-root powers; a critical value has zero derivative or no derivative at a defined point.", "(a) f′ = 6x² − 6x − 72 = 6(x − 4)(x + 3), giving −3 and 4.", "(b) f′ = x² − 2x − 15 = (x − 5)(x + 3), giving −3 and 5.", "(c) For x ≠ 0, f′ = 4/∛x − 4. It is zero when ∛x = 1, so x = 1.", "At 0, 6x²/³ − 4x is defined but its derivative fails to exist; include 0 as well as 1.", "(d) For x ≠ 0, f′ = (4x − 4)/(∛x)². Its only zero is 1.", "The function is defined but nondifferentiable at 0, so part (d) also has critical values 0 and 1."], "solution": "All four real source functions are defined at zero, including the cube-root powers; a critical value has zero derivative or no derivative at a defined point.\n(a) f′ = 6x² − 6x − 72 = 6(x − 4)(x + 3), giving −3 and 4.\n(b) f′ = x² − 2x − 15 = (x − 5)(x + 3), giving −3 and 5.\n(c) For x ≠ 0, f′ = 4/∛x − 4. It is zero when ∛x = 1, so x = 1.\nAt 0, 6x²/³ − 4x is defined but its derivative fails to exist; include 0 as well as 1.\n(d) For x ≠ 0, f′ = (4x − 4)/(∛x)². Its only zero is 1.\nThe function is defined but nondifferentiable at 0, so part (d) also has critical values 0 and 1.", "answer": "(a) −3,4; (b) −3,5; (c) 0,1; (d) 0,1.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 126, "jpg": "067_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-3-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Determine relative maximum, minimum or neither at the given critical values: (a) f(x) = (x³ − 3x + 1)⁷ = 0 at x = 1,x = −1; (b) f(x) = (x⁴ − 4x + 2)⁵ at x = 1; (c) f(x) = (x² − 4)⁴(x² − 1)³ at x = 1,x = 2; (d) f(x) = ∛(x³ − 48) at x = 4.", "steps": ["(a) The printed “= 0” is inconsistent with its listed points: (1³ − 3·1 + 1)⁷ = −1 and ((−1)³ − 3(−1) + 1)⁷ = 2187, not zero. Classify the stated function rather than treating that extra equality as a true value.", "For f = (x³ − 3x + 1)⁷, f′ = 21(x² − 1)(x³ − 3x + 1)⁶. Near −1 the sign changes + to −, giving a maximum at (−1,2187); near 1 it changes − to +, giving a minimum at (1,−1).", "(b) Put u = x⁴ − 4x + 2. At 1, u = −1,u′ = 0,u″ = 12. For u⁵, f″(1) = 5u⁴u″ = 60 > 0, giving a relative minimum f(1) = −1.", "(c) Near 1, (x² − 4)⁴ is positive and (x² − 1)³ changes sign from negative to positive. Its cubic-order crossing is a stationary inflection, not an extremum; f(1) = 0.", "Near 2, (x² − 1)³ is positive and (x² − 4)⁴ is nonnegative with a zero at 2, so f(2) = 0 is a relative minimum.", "(d) For the printed f = ∛(x³ − 48), f′ = \\(\\frac{x^2}{(x^3-48)^{2/3}}\\) where x³ ≠ 48. At x = 4, f′ = 16/16²/³ > 0, so 4 is not even a critical value and is neither maximum nor minimum.", "The key’s minimum at 4 would instead fit the different function ∛(x³ − 48x), whose derivative changes − to + at 4. That missing-x alternative is not silently substituted into the source question.", "The printed cube-root function actually has critical values 0 and ∛48: 0 has zero derivative without an extremum, and ∛48 has an undefined finite derivative without an extremum. It is strictly increasing overall."], "solution": "(a) The printed “= 0” is inconsistent with its listed points: (1³ − 3·1 + 1)⁷ = −1 and ((−1)³ − 3(−1) + 1)⁷ = 2187, not zero. Classify the stated function rather than treating that extra equality as a true value.\nFor f = (x³ − 3x + 1)⁷, f′ = 21(x² − 1)(x³ − 3x + 1)⁶. Near −1 the sign changes + to −, giving a maximum at (−1,2187); near 1 it changes − to +, giving a minimum at (1,−1).\n(b) Put u = x⁴ − 4x + 2. At 1, u = −1,u′ = 0,u″ = 12. For u⁵, f″(1) = 5u⁴u″ = 60 > 0, giving a relative minimum f(1) = −1.\n(c) Near 1, (x² − 4)⁴ is positive and (x² − 1)³ changes sign from negative to positive. Its cubic-order crossing is a stationary inflection, not an extremum; f(1) = 0.\nNear 2, (x² − 1)³ is positive and (x² − 4)⁴ is nonnegative with a zero at 2, so f(2) = 0 is a relative minimum.\n(d) For the printed f = ∛(x³ − 48), f′ = \\(\\frac{x^2}{(x^3-48)^{2/3}}\\) where x³ ≠ 48. At x = 4, f′ = 16/16²/³ > 0, so 4 is not even a critical value and is neither maximum nor minimum.\nThe key’s minimum at 4 would instead fit the different function ∛(x³ − 48x), whose derivative changes − to + at 4. That missing-x alternative is not silently substituted into the source question.\nThe printed cube-root function actually has critical values 0 and ∛48: 0 has zero derivative without an extremum, and ∛48 has an undefined finite derivative without an extremum. It is strictly increasing overall.", "answer": "(a) minimum at 1, maximum at −1, with the printed “= 0” inconsistency explained; (b) minimum at 1; (c) neither at 1, minimum at 2; (d) neither at 4 for the printed function.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 127, "jpg": "068_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "Part (a) prints an extra = 0 although neither listed function value is zero. Part (d) prints ∛(x³ − 48), for which x = 4 is not critical; the key instead matches the different ∛(x³ − 48x). Source equations are retained."}, {"id": "ex-4-3-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "Find all critical points, determine rising/falling and concave up/down intervals, and sketch: (a) f(x) = 2(x + 20)² − 8(x + 20) + 7; (b) \\(f(x)=\\frac13x^3-9x+2\\).", "steps": ["(a) Complete the square: f = 2(x + 18)² − 1. Its derivative is 4x + 72 = 4(x + 18), and f″ = 4.", "The only critical point is (−18,−1), a relative and global minimum.", "The graph falls for x < −18 and rises for x > −18. It is concave up everywhere and never concave down; no inflection exists.", "The source-specific sketch is an upward parabola with this vertex; both branches extend without bound.", "(b) f′ = x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3); f″ = 2x.", "The critical points are (−3,20), a relative maximum, and (3,−16), a relative minimum.", "The function rises on (−∞,−3),(3,∞), and falls on (−3,3). It is concave down for x < 0 and concave up for x > 0.", "The inflection point is (0,2). The dedicated cubic sketch marks both extrema and the change of concavity."], "solution": "(a) Complete the square: f = 2(x + 18)² − 1. Its derivative is 4x + 72 = 4(x + 18), and f″ = 4.\nThe only critical point is (−18,−1), a relative and global minimum.\nThe graph falls for x < −18 and rises for x > −18. It is concave up everywhere and never concave down; no inflection exists.\nThe source-specific sketch is an upward parabola with this vertex; both branches extend without bound.\n(b) f′ = x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3); f″ = 2x.\nThe critical points are (−3,20), a relative maximum, and (3,−16), a relative minimum.\nThe function rises on (−∞,−3),(3,∞), and falls on (−3,3). It is concave down for x < 0 and concave up for x > 0.\nThe inflection point is (0,2). The dedicated cubic sketch marks both extrema and the change of concavity.", "answer": "(a) minimum (−18,−1), down left/up right, concave up everywhere. (b) maximum (−3,20), minimum (3,−16), inflection (0,2); full intervals above.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 127, "jpg": "068_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}, "solutionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 4.3 Question 4(a) — dedicated learning sketch", "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "2(x + 18)² − 1", "polynomial": [647, 72, 2], "xMin": -25, "xMax": -11, "yMin": -5, "yMax": 55, "xTicks": [-24, -22, -20, -18, -16, -14, -12], "yTicks": [0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50], "points": [[-18, -1, "(−18,−1)"]]}}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 4.3 Question 4(b) — dedicated learning sketch", "diagram": {"type": "source-function-graph", "title": "x³/3 − 9x + 2", "polynomial": [2, -9, 0, 0.3333333333333333], "xMin": -6, "xMax": 6, "yMin": -30, "yMax": 30, "xTicks": [-6, -3, 0, 3, 6], "yTicks": [-30, -20, -10, 0, 10, 20, 30], "points": [[-3, 20, "(−3,20)"], [0, 2, "(0,2)"], [3, -16, "(3,−16)"]]}}]}, {"id": "ex-4-3-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "Find all relative extrema: (a) f(x) = x³ − 3x² + 1; (b) f(x) = x³ + 6x² + 9x + 2.", "steps": ["(a) f′ = 3x² − 6x = 3x(x − 2), so critical values are 0 and 2.", "f″ = 6x − 6, giving f″(0) = −6 < 0 and f″(2) = 6 > 0.", "Thus relative maximum is (0,1) and relative minimum is (2,−3).", "(b) f′ = 3x² + 12x + 9 = 3(x + 1)(x + 3), so critical values are −3 and −1.", "The second derivative is 6x + 12: −6 at −3 and 6 at −1.", "Thus relative maximum is (−3,2), relative minimum (−1,−2).", "Each cubic is defined and differentiable everywhere, so there are no additional critical values from derivative failure."], "solution": "(a) f′ = 3x² − 6x = 3x(x − 2), so critical values are 0 and 2.\nf″ = 6x − 6, giving f″(0) = −6 < 0 and f″(2) = 6 > 0.\nThus relative maximum is (0,1) and relative minimum is (2,−3).\n(b) f′ = 3x² + 12x + 9 = 3(x + 1)(x + 3), so critical values are −3 and −1.\nThe second derivative is 6x + 12: −6 at −3 and 6 at −1.\nThus relative maximum is (−3,2), relative minimum (−1,−2).\nEach cubic is defined and differentiable everywhere, so there are no additional critical values from derivative failure.", "answer": "(a) maximum (0,1), minimum (2,−3); (b) maximum (−3,2), minimum (−1,−2).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 127, "jpg": "068_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-3-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "(a) Suppose f(x) is a differential function with derivative f′(x) = (x − 1)²(x − 2)(x − 4)(x + 5)⁴. Find all critical values and classify each as a relative maximum, minimum or neither. (b) Suppose f(x) is a differential function with derivative \\(f′(x)=\\frac{(2x-1)(x+3)}{(x-1)^2}\\). Find all critical values and classify each as maximum, minimum or neither.", "steps": ["(a) The derivative zeros are −5,1,2,4. The powers (x + 5)⁴ and (x − 1)² are even, so they do not change derivative sign when crossed.", "Away from its zeros, the sign is that of (x − 2)(x − 4): positive for x < 2, negative for 2 < x < 4, positive for x > 4.", "Therefore 2 is a relative maximum, 4 a relative minimum, and −5,1 are neither.", "Function values are not supplied, so heights of these extrema cannot be determined from the derivative alone without an integration constant.", "(b) The denominator is positive for x ≠ 1. Derivative zeros are −3 and 1/2.", "For x < −3 the numerator is positive; for −3 < x < 1/2 it is negative; for x > 1/2 it is positive, separately on either side of 1.", "The sign changes + to − at −3, giving a maximum; − to + at 1/2 gives a minimum.", "The supplied derivative is undefined at 1 and behaves as 4/(x − 1)² there, so any antiderivative diverges near 1. No finite differentiable extension at 1 is possible. Treat 1 as outside the stated differentiable domain, not a critical value or a “neither” extremum."], "solution": "(a) The derivative zeros are −5,1,2,4. The powers (x + 5)⁴ and (x − 1)² are even, so they do not change derivative sign when crossed.\nAway from its zeros, the sign is that of (x − 2)(x − 4): positive for x < 2, negative for 2 < x < 4, positive for x > 4.\nTherefore 2 is a relative maximum, 4 a relative minimum, and −5,1 are neither.\nFunction values are not supplied, so heights of these extrema cannot be determined from the derivative alone without an integration constant.\n(b) The denominator is positive for x ≠ 1. Derivative zeros are −3 and 1/2.\nFor x < −3 the numerator is positive; for −3 < x < 1/2 it is negative; for x > 1/2 it is positive, separately on either side of 1.\nThe sign changes + to − at −3, giving a maximum; − to + at 1/2 gives a minimum.\nThe supplied derivative is undefined at 1 and behaves as 4/(x − 1)² there, so any antiderivative diverges near 1. No finite differentiable extension at 1 is possible. Treat 1 as outside the stated differentiable domain, not a critical value or a “neither” extremum.", "answer": "(a) maximum at 2, minimum at 4, neither at −5,1. (b) maximum at −3, minimum at 1/2; 1 is a domain exclusion.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 127, "jpg": "068_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "In part (b), the derivative pole at 1 cannot be made into a finite differentiable function point. It is excluded, rather than called a critical value with no extremum."}, {"id": "ex-4-3-q7", "qNo": "7", "question": "A company has found through experience that increasing its advertising also increases its sales up to a point. The company believes that the mathematical model connecting profit in hundreds of dollars P(x) and expenditures on advertising in thousands of dollars x is: P(x) = 80 + 108x − x³, 0 ≤ x ≤ 10. (a) Find the advertising expenditure leading to maximum profit. (b) Find the maximum profit.", "steps": ["Profit P is in hundreds of dollars, while x is advertising in thousands of dollars. Keep the two unit scales separate.", "Differentiate: P′ = 108 − 3x². The zeros are ±6, but only 6 lies in [0,10].", "For 0 ≤ x < 6, P′ > 0; for 6 < x ≤ 10, P′ < 0. The interior maximum is at x = 6.", "Check the closed-interval candidates: P(0) = 80, P(6) = 512, P(10) = 160.", "The largest value is 512 hundreds of dollars, or $51,200.", "The advertising expenditure x = 6 means $6,000, not $600.", "The second derivative P″(6) = −36 provides a local maximum check; endpoint comparison establishes the maximum on the whole source interval."], "solution": "Profit P is in hundreds of dollars, while x is advertising in thousands of dollars. Keep the two unit scales separate.\nDifferentiate: P′ = 108 − 3x². The zeros are ±6, but only 6 lies in [0,10].\nFor 0 ≤ x < 6, P′ > 0; for 6 < x ≤ 10, P′ < 0. The interior maximum is at x = 6.\nCheck the closed-interval candidates: P(0) = 80, P(6) = 512, P(10) = 160.\nThe largest value is 512 hundreds of dollars, or $51,200.\nThe advertising expenditure x = 6 means $6,000, not $600.\nThe second derivative P″(6) = −36 provides a local maximum check; endpoint comparison establishes the maximum on the whole source interval.", "answer": "Advertising $6,000; maximum profit $51,200.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 127, "jpg": "068_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-3-q8", "qNo": "8", "question": "The total profit P(x) in thousands of dollars from the sale of x hundred thousands of automobile tires is approximated by P(x) = −x³ + 9x² + 120x − 400, 3 ≤ x ≤ 15. Find the number of hundred thousands of tires that must be sold to maximize profit. Find the maximum profit.", "steps": ["Here x counts hundred thousands of tires and P counts thousands of dollars, with 3 ≤ x ≤ 15.", "P′ = −3x² + 18x + 120 = −3(x − 10)(x + 4).", "The only derivative zero in the source interval is x = 10; −4 is excluded.", "Check P(3) = 14, P(10) = 700 and P(15) = 50, all in thousands of dollars.", "The greatest is 700 at x = 10. The derivative changes from positive to negative there.", "Ten hundred thousands of tires is 1,000,000 tires.", "The maximum profit is $700,000. The second derivative −6x + 18 is −42 at x = 10, confirming a local maximum."], "solution": "Here x counts hundred thousands of tires and P counts thousands of dollars, with 3 ≤ x ≤ 15.\nP′ = −3x² + 18x + 120 = −3(x − 10)(x + 4).\nThe only derivative zero in the source interval is x = 10; −4 is excluded.\nCheck P(3) = 14, P(10) = 700 and P(15) = 50, all in thousands of dollars.\nThe greatest is 700 at x = 10. The derivative changes from positive to negative there.\nTen hundred thousands of tires is 1,000,000 tires.\nThe maximum profit is $700,000. The second derivative −6x + 18 is −42 at x = 10, confirming a local maximum.", "answer": "1,000,000 tires; maximum profit $700,000.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 127, "jpg": "068_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}}, {"id": "ex-4-3-q9", "qNo": "9", "question": "The percent of concentration of a drug in the bloodstream x hours after administration is \\(K(x)=\\frac{4x}{3x^2+27}\\). (a) On what time intervals is the concentration increasing? (b) On what intervals is it decreasing? (c) Find when concentration is a maximum. (d) Find maximum concentration.", "steps": ["Use physical time x ≥ 0. K(x) is expressed as a percent, not as a dimensionless fraction.", "Quotient rule gives K′ = [4(3x² + 27) − 24x²]/(3x² + 27)² = 4(9 − x²)/[3(x² + 9)²].", "The denominator is positive. For 0 < x < 3 the numerator is positive, so concentration increases.", "For x > 3 the numerator is negative, so it decreases.", "The maximum occurs at x = 3 hours: K(3) = 12/54 = 2/9 percent ≈ 0.222222%.", "As a proportion of the bloodstream, divide by 100: 1/450 ≈ 0.00222222. The key’s 0.22% = 0.0022 is a rounded version.", "K(0) = 0 and K tends to zero as time grows, so the interior maximum is global for x ≥ 0."], "solution": "Use physical time x ≥ 0. K(x) is expressed as a percent, not as a dimensionless fraction.\nQuotient rule gives K′ = [4(3x² + 27) − 24x²]/(3x² + 27)² = 4(9 − x²)/[3(x² + 9)²].\nThe denominator is positive. For 0 < x < 3 the numerator is positive, so concentration increases.\nFor x > 3 the numerator is negative, so it decreases.\nThe maximum occurs at x = 3 hours: K(3) = 12/54 = 2/9 percent ≈ 0.222222%.\nAs a proportion of the bloodstream, divide by 100: 1/450 ≈ 0.00222222. The key’s 0.22% = 0.0022 is a rounded version.\nK(0) = 0 and K tends to zero as time grows, so the interior maximum is global for x ≥ 0.", "answer": "Increasing (0,3), decreasing (3,∞); maximum at 3 h of 2/9 percent ≈ 0.222222%.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 127, "jpg": "068_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}, "sourceClarification": "The source concentration is measured in percent; the key rounds 2/9 percent to 0.22 percent. Exact percentage and dimensionless proportion are both provided."}, {"id": "ex-4-3-q10", "qNo": "10", "question": "A diesel generator burns fuel at \\(G(x)=\\frac1{48}\\left(\\frac{300}{x}+2x\\right)\\) gallons/hour when producing x thousand kilowatt hours of electricity. Fuel costs $2.25/gallon, and the generator operates for 32 hours. Find x leading to minimum total cost, and find that minimum cost.", "steps": ["Multiply gallons/hour by 32 hours and $2.25/gallon: C(x) = G(x)·32·2.25.", "The multiplier 32·2.25/48 is 1.5, so C = 1.5(300/x + 2x) = 450/x + 3x dollars, x > 0.", "Differentiate: C′ = −450/x² + 3. Set it to zero: x² = 150.", "The only allowed root is x = √150 = 5√6 ≈ 12.24745, in the source’s thousand-kilowatt-hour units.", "The second derivative C″ = 900/x³ > 0 establishes a minimum.", "The cost grows without bound near zero and as x grows, making the interior minimum global.", "C(√150) = 450/√150 + 3√150 = 6√150 = 30√6 ≈ $73.48469, or $73.48 to cents."], "solution": "Multiply gallons/hour by 32 hours and $2.25/gallon: C(x) = G(x)·32·2.25.\nThe multiplier 32·2.25/48 is 1.5, so C = 1.5(300/x + 2x) = 450/x + 3x dollars, x > 0.\nDifferentiate: C′ = −450/x² + 3. Set it to zero: x² = 150.\nThe only allowed root is x = √150 = 5√6 ≈ 12.24745, in the source’s thousand-kilowatt-hour units.\nThe second derivative C″ = 900/x³ > 0 establishes a minimum.\nThe cost grows without bound near zero and as x grows, making the interior minimum global.\nC(√150) = 450/√150 + 3√150 = 6√150 = 30√6 ≈ $73.48469, or $73.48 to cents.", "answer": "x = 5√6 ≈ 12.24745 source units; minimum cost 30√6 ≈ $73.48.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 127, "jpg": "068_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source question subparts and exponents checked against JPG/PDF"}], "answerKeyReference": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 172, "printedPages": [335, 336], "scope": "Exercise 4.3 Questions 1–10 only; source mismatches separately clarified, answer pages not transcribed or marked complete"}}];
