const math12Review5=[{"id": "ex-5-review-i", "qNo": "i", "question": "A quantity having magnitude and direction is called: (a) vector (b) scalar (c) velocity (d) derivative", "answer": "Option (a)", "steps": ["Magnitude together with direction defines a vector.", "A scalar has no associated direction.", "Velocity is an example of a vector, but the general category requested is vector.", "Therefore select (a)."], "solution": "Magnitude together with direction defines a vector.\nA scalar has no associated direction.\nVelocity is an example of a vector, but the general category requested is vector.\nTherefore select (a).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}, {"id": "ex-5-review-ii", "qNo": "ii", "question": "A quantity having magnitude only and no direction is called: (a) vector (b) scalar (c) velocity (d) derivative", "answer": "Option (b)", "steps": ["The question asks for a general type of quantity.", "A scalar is specified by magnitude alone.", "Vectors and velocity include direction; derivative describes an operation rather than this classification.", "Therefore select (b)."], "solution": "The question asks for a general type of quantity.\nA scalar is specified by magnitude alone.\nVectors and velocity include direction; derivative describes an operation rather than this classification.\nTherefore select (b).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}, {"id": "ex-5-review-iii", "qNo": "iii", "question": "If \\(F⃗=t^2î+tĵ-\\sin(t)k̂\\) and \\(G⃗=tî+t^{-1}ĵ+5k̂\\), then F⃗ × G⃗ = (a) \\((5t^2+\\frac{\\sin t}t)î-(5t^2+t\\sin t)ĵ-(t+t^2)k̂\\) (b) \\((5t+\\frac{\\sin t}t)î+(5t^2+t\\sin t)ĵ+(t^2-t)k̂\\) (c) \\((5t+\\frac{\\sin t}t)î-(5t^2+t\\sin t)ĵ+(t-t^2)k̂\\) (d) \\((5t^2+\\frac{\\sin t}t)î-(5t^2+t\\sin t)ĵ+(t-t^2)k̂\\)", "answer": "Option (c)", "steps": ["Use F⃗ = (t²,t,−sin t), G⃗ = (t,1/t,5), with t ≠ 0.", "The first cross component is 5t + sin t/t.", "The second is −t sin t − 5t².", "The third is t²/t − t² = t−t².", "These ordered components match (c)."], "solution": "Use F⃗ = (t²,t,−sin t), G⃗ = (t,1/t,5), with t ≠ 0.\nThe first cross component is 5t + sin t/t.\nThe second is −t sin t − 5t².\nThe third is t²/t − t² = t−t².\nThese ordered components match (c).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}, {"id": "ex-5-review-iv", "qNo": "iv", "question": "Let \\(F⃗=t^2î+tĵ-\\sin(t)k̂\\) and \\(G⃗=tî+t^{-1}ĵ+5k̂\\), then F⃗ · G⃗ = (a) \\(t^3+1+5\\sin t\\) (b) \\(t^2-1+4\\sin t\\) (c) \\(3t^2-1+4\\sin t\\) (d) \\(t^3+1-5\\sin t\\)", "answer": "Option (d)", "steps": ["The dot product adds corresponding component products.", "They are t²·t = t³, t·t⁻¹ = 1, and (−sin t)·5 = −5sin t.", "The sum is t³+1−5sin t on t ≠ 0.", "This matches (d)."], "solution": "The dot product adds corresponding component products.\nThey are t²·t = t³, t·t⁻¹ = 1, and (−sin t)·5 = −5sin t.\nThe sum is t³+1−5sin t on t ≠ 0.\nThis matches (d).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}, {"id": "ex-5-review-v", "qNo": "v", "question": "If \\(F⃗(t)=(t+3)î+2t^2ĵ-(1-t)k̂\\), then \\(\\lim_{t\\to5}F⃗(-2)\\) is: (a) 8î+50ĵ+4k̂ (b) 8î+10ĵ−4k̂ (c) 8î+60ĵ−4k̂ (d) 8î+50ĵ+4k̂", "answer": "No correct printed option", "steps": ["The printed argument is −2, independent of the limiting variable t.", "Substitute −2: F⃗(−2) = î+8ĵ−3k̂.", "The limit of this constant vector is î+8ĵ−3k̂. None of the four options gives it.", "If the intended expression was lim(t→5)F⃗(t), continuity would give F⃗(5) = 8î+50ĵ+4k̂.", "That different expression matches both (a) and (d), which are identical. The original argument is retained."], "solution": "The printed argument is −2, independent of the limiting variable t.\nSubstitute −2: F⃗(−2) = î+8ĵ−3k̂.\nThe limit of this constant vector is î+8ĵ−3k̂. None of the four options gives it.\nIf the intended expression was lim(t→5)F⃗(t), continuity would give F⃗(5) = 8î+50ĵ+4k̂.\nThat different expression matches both (a) and (d), which are identical. The original argument is retained.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}, {"id": "ex-5-review-vi", "qNo": "vi", "question": "If \\(F⃗=t^3î-(3+t^2)k̂\\), then F⃗′(−2) is: (a) 4î−12k̂ (b) 12î−4k̂ (c) 12î+4k̂ (d) 12î−8k̂", "answer": "Option (c)", "steps": ["Differentiate t³ to get 3t².", "Differentiate −3−t² to get −2t.", "At −2, these values are 12 and 4.", "Thus F⃗′(−2) = 12î+4k̂, option (c)."], "solution": "Differentiate t³ to get 3t².\nDifferentiate −3−t² to get −2t.\nAt −2, these values are 12 and 4.\nThus F⃗′(−2) = 12î+4k̂, option (c).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}, {"id": "ex-5-review-vii", "qNo": "vii", "question": "If r⃗ = 2î+3ĵ+4k̂, then |r⃗| = (a) \\(\\sqrt{29}\\) (b) \\(\\sqrt{30}\\) (c) 5 (d) 6", "answer": "Option (a)", "steps": ["Square the three components: 4,9,16.", "Their sum is 29.", "Magnitude is the nonnegative square root √29.", "Therefore select (a)."], "solution": "Square the three components: 4,9,16.\nTheir sum is 29.\nMagnitude is the nonnegative square root √29.\nTherefore select (a).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}, {"id": "ex-5-review-viii", "qNo": "viii", "question": "If r⃗ = 5t²î+3tĵ+k̂, then the velocity vector is: (a) 10tî+3ĵ (b) 5tî+3k̂ (c) 2tî+3ĵ (d) 10tî−3k̂", "answer": "Option (a)", "steps": ["Velocity is the derivative of the position vector.", "The component derivatives are 10t,3,0.", "Thus v⃗ = 10tî+3ĵ.", "Select (a)."], "solution": "Velocity is the derivative of the position vector.\nThe component derivatives are 10t,3,0.\nThus v⃗ = 10tî+3ĵ.\nSelect (a).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}, {"id": "ex-5-review-ix", "qNo": "ix", "question": "If velocity v⃗ = sin(t)î−2cos(t)ĵ+4k̂, then acceleration a⃗ = (a) −sin(t)î+2cos(t)ĵ−4k̂ (b) cos(t)î−2sin(t)ĵ+4k̂ (c) −cos(t)î+2sin(t)ĵ (d) cos(t)î−2sin(t)ĵ", "answer": "No correct printed option", "steps": ["Acceleration is the derivative of velocity.", "The derivative of sin t is cos t.", "The derivative of −2cos t is +2sin t; the derivative of the constant 4 is zero.", "Therefore a⃗ = cos tî+2sin tĵ.", "No printed option has both correct signs and a zero k̂ component. Option (d) would need its minus sign changed to plus."], "solution": "Acceleration is the derivative of velocity.\nThe derivative of sin t is cos t.\nThe derivative of −2cos t is +2sin t; the derivative of the constant 4 is zero.\nTherefore a⃗ = cos tî+2sin tĵ.\nNo printed option has both correct signs and a zero k̂ component. Option (d) would need its minus sign changed to plus.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}, {"id": "ex-5-review-x", "qNo": "x", "question": "\\(\\lim_{t\\to t_0}(C\\cdot F⃗(t))\\) = (a) \\(C\\cdot F⃗(t)\\) (b) \\(C\\cdot F⃗(t_0)\\) (c) \\(C+F⃗(t_0)\\) (d) \\(C/F⃗(t_0)\\)", "answer": "b, if F⃗ is continuous at t₀", "steps": ["For a constant scalar C, the general limit is C times lim(t→t₀)F⃗(t), provided that limit exists.", "To replace this limit by F⃗(t₀), continuity at t₀ is required.", "Under that condition the result is C·F⃗(t₀), option (b).", "If C instead denotes a constant vector and the dot is a dot product, the same continuity condition gives C·F⃗(t₀).", "The printed question does not explicitly state the continuity assumption; without it, (b) need not hold."], "solution": "For a constant scalar C, the general limit is C times lim(t→t₀)F⃗(t), provided that limit exists.\nTo replace this limit by F⃗(t₀), continuity at t₀ is required.\nUnder that condition the result is C·F⃗(t₀), option (b).\nIf C instead denotes a constant vector and the dot is a dot product, the same continuity condition gives C·F⃗(t₀).\nThe printed question does not explicitly state the continuity assumption; without it, (b) need not hold.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 140, "jpg": "074_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all four source options checked; inconsistent questions explained separately"}]}];
