const math12Exercise62=[{"id": "ex-6-2-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Evaluate by substitution: (a) \\(\\int\\sin^4x\\cos x\\,dx\\); (b) \\(\\int\\sqrt[3]{\\sin^2x}\\cos x\\,dx\\); (c) \\(\\int\\frac{\\sin x\\ln(\\cos x)}{\\cos x}\\,dx\\); (d) \\(\\int e^x\\sin(e^x)\\,dx\\); (e) \\(\\int\\frac{\\cot\\sqrt x}{\\sqrt x}\\,dx\\); (f) \\(\\int\\frac{\\sin x-\\cos x}{\\sin x+\\cos x}\\,dx\\).", "steps": ["(a) Let u = sin x, du = cos x dx. Integrate u⁴: sin⁵x/5+C.", "(b) With u = sin x, the integrand becomes ∛(u²) du = |u|^(2/3) du. A real antiderivative is (3/5)u|u|^(2/3)+C, giving (3/5)sin x|sin x|^(2/3)+C, valid even at sine zeros.", "(c) The printed ln(cos x) requires cos x > 0. Set u = ln(cos x), du = −sin x dx/cos x. Integrate −u du: −[ln(cos x)]²/2+C on each original-domain interval.", "(d) Set u = eˣ, du = eˣdx. Integrate sin u: −cos(eˣ)+C.", "(e) Set u = √x, dx = 2u du, with x > 0. The integral becomes 2∫cot u du = 2ln|sin√x|+C, where sin√x ≠ 0.", "(f) Set u = sin x+cos x. Then du = (cos x−sin x)dx, the negative of the numerator. The result is −ln|sin x+cos x|+C, where sin x+cos x ≠ 0.", "Differentiate each final expression on its original real domain. A logarithm-of-absolute-value extension in (c) would not change the source domain cos x > 0."], "solution": "(a) Let u = sin x, du = cos x dx. Integrate u⁴: sin⁵x/5+C.\n(b) With u = sin x, the integrand becomes ∛(u²) du = |u|^(2/3) du. A real antiderivative is (3/5)u|u|^(2/3)+C, giving (3/5)sin x|sin x|^(2/3)+C, valid even at sine zeros.\n(c) The printed ln(cos x) requires cos x > 0. Set u = ln(cos x), du = −sin x dx/cos x. Integrate −u du: −[ln(cos x)]²/2+C on each original-domain interval.\n(d) Set u = eˣ, du = eˣdx. Integrate sin u: −cos(eˣ)+C.\n(e) Set u = √x, dx = 2u du, with x > 0. The integral becomes 2∫cot u du = 2ln|sin√x|+C, where sin√x ≠ 0.\n(f) Set u = sin x+cos x. Then du = (cos x−sin x)dx, the negative of the numerator. The result is −ln|sin x+cos x|+C, where sin x+cos x ≠ 0.\nDifferentiate each final expression on its original real domain. A logarithm-of-absolute-value extension in (c) would not change the source domain cos x > 0.", "answer": "(a) sin⁵x/5+C; (b) (3/5)sin x|sin x|^(2/3)+C; (c) −[ln(cos x)]²/2+C; (d) −cos(eˣ)+C; (e) 2ln|sin√x|+C; (f) −ln|sin x+cos x|+C.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 162, "jpg": "085_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all seventeen printed subparts checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-2-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Use suitable substitutions and tables: (a) \\(\\int\\frac{dx}{x^2+16}\\); (b) \\(\\int\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2x+1}\\,dx\\); (c) \\(\\int\\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{e^{2x}-4}}\\); (d) \\(\\int\\frac{2x+5}{x^2+4x+5}\\,dx\\); (e) \\(\\int\\frac{2+x}{\\sqrt{4-2x-x^2}}\\,dx\\).", "steps": ["(a) Put u = x/4. Then dx/(x²+16) = (1/4)du/(u²+1), giving (1/4)arctan(x/4)+C.", "(b) Put u = cos x, du = −sin x dx. Integrate −du/(1+u²): −arctan(cos x)+C.", "(c) The real denominator requires e²ˣ > 4, so x > ln 2. Put u = eˣ/2 > 1, dx = du/u.", "Then the integral is (1/2)∫du/[u√(u²−1)] = (1/2)arcsec u+C = (1/2)arccos(2e⁻ˣ)+C on x > ln 2.", "(d) Complete the square: x²+4x+5 = (x+2)²+1. Split the numerator into (2x+4)+1.", "The first integral is ln(x²+4x+5); the second is arctan(x+2). Sum: ln(x²+4x+5)+arctan(x+2)+C, for all real x.", "(e) Put u = x+1. Then the denominator is √(5−u²) and the numerator is u+1.", "Split into ∫u/√(5−u²) du + ∫du/√(5−u²). The result is −√(4−2x−x²)+arcsin((x+1)/√5)+C, on −1−√5 < x < −1+√5."], "solution": "(a) Put u = x/4. Then dx/(x²+16) = (1/4)du/(u²+1), giving (1/4)arctan(x/4)+C.\n(b) Put u = cos x, du = −sin x dx. Integrate −du/(1+u²): −arctan(cos x)+C.\n(c) The real denominator requires e²ˣ > 4, so x > ln 2. Put u = eˣ/2 > 1, dx = du/u.\nThen the integral is (1/2)∫du/[u√(u²−1)] = (1/2)arcsec u+C = (1/2)arccos(2e⁻ˣ)+C on x > ln 2.\n(d) Complete the square: x²+4x+5 = (x+2)²+1. Split the numerator into (2x+4)+1.\nThe first integral is ln(x²+4x+5); the second is arctan(x+2). Sum: ln(x²+4x+5)+arctan(x+2)+C, for all real x.\n(e) Put u = x+1. Then the denominator is √(5−u²) and the numerator is u+1.\nSplit into ∫u/√(5−u²) du + ∫du/√(5−u²). The result is −√(4−2x−x²)+arcsin((x+1)/√5)+C, on −1−√5 < x < −1+√5.", "answer": "(a) arctan(x/4)/4+C; (b) −arctan(cos x)+C; (c) arccos(2e⁻ˣ)/2+C; (d) ln(x²+4x+5)+arctan(x+2)+C; (e) −√(4−2x−x²)+arcsin((x+1)/√5)+C.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 162, "jpg": "085_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all seventeen printed subparts checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-2-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Evaluate using integration by parts: (a) \\(\\int x^2e^{2x}\\,dx\\); (b) \\(\\int x\\cos x\\,dx\\); (c) \\(\\int e^{2x}\\sin x\\,dx\\); (d) \\(\\int e^x\\cos x\\,dx\\); (e) \\(\\int\\sin^{-1}x\\,dx\\); (f) \\(\\int e^{3t}\\sin(e^{3t})\\,dt\\).", "steps": ["(a) Choose u = x²,dv = e²ˣdx, giving v = e²ˣ/2 and du = 2x dx. The first step is x²e²ˣ/2−∫xe²ˣdx.", "Apply parts again to ∫xe²ˣdx: xe²ˣ/2−e²ˣ/4. Thus (a) = e²ˣ(x²/2−x/2+1/4)+C.", "(b) Choose u = x,dv = cos x dx. The answer is xsin x−∫sin x dx = xsin x+cos x+C.", "(c) Let I = ∫e²ˣsin x dx and J = ∫e²ˣcos x dx. Parts gives I = −e²ˣcos x+2J and J = e²ˣsin x−2I.", "Substitute and collect: 5I = e²ˣ(2sin x−cos x). Thus (c) = e²ˣ(2sin x−cos x)/5+C.", "(d) Applying parts twice with exponent coefficient 1 gives eˣ(sin x+cos x)/2+C.", "(e) Use u = arcsin x,dv = dx. Then ∫arcsin x dx = xarcsin x−∫x/√(1−x²) dx = xarcsin x+√(1−x²)+C, for −1 < x < 1.", "(f) Substitution is more direct: u = e³ᵗ gives du = 3e³ᵗdt. Thus ∫e³ᵗsin(e³ᵗ)dt = −cos(e³ᵗ)/3+C. If demonstrating parts, take u = sin(e³ᵗ),dv = e³ᵗdt; the resulting integral returns to the same substitution.", "Differentiate (f): (−1/3)(−sin(e³ᵗ))(3e³ᵗ) = e³ᵗsin(e³ᵗ), confirming the factor 1/3."], "solution": "(a) Choose u = x²,dv = e²ˣdx, giving v = e²ˣ/2 and du = 2x dx. The first step is x²e²ˣ/2−∫xe²ˣdx.\nApply parts again to ∫xe²ˣdx: xe²ˣ/2−e²ˣ/4. Thus (a) = e²ˣ(x²/2−x/2+1/4)+C.\n(b) Choose u = x,dv = cos x dx. The answer is xsin x−∫sin x dx = xsin x+cos x+C.\n(c) Let I = ∫e²ˣsin x dx and J = ∫e²ˣcos x dx. Parts gives I = −e²ˣcos x+2J and J = e²ˣsin x−2I.\nSubstitute and collect: 5I = e²ˣ(2sin x−cos x). Thus (c) = e²ˣ(2sin x−cos x)/5+C.\n(d) Applying parts twice with exponent coefficient 1 gives eˣ(sin x+cos x)/2+C.\n(e) Use u = arcsin x,dv = dx. Then ∫arcsin x dx = xarcsin x−∫x/√(1−x²) dx = xarcsin x+√(1−x²)+C, for −1 < x < 1.\n(f) Substitution is more direct: u = e³ᵗ gives du = 3e³ᵗdt. Thus ∫e³ᵗsin(e³ᵗ)dt = −cos(e³ᵗ)/3+C. If demonstrating parts, take u = sin(e³ᵗ),dv = e³ᵗdt; the resulting integral returns to the same substitution.\nDifferentiate (f): (−1/3)(−sin(e³ᵗ))(3e³ᵗ) = e³ᵗsin(e³ᵗ), confirming the factor 1/3.", "answer": "(a) e²ˣ(x²/2−x/2+1/4)+C; (b) xsin x+cos x+C; (c) e²ˣ(2sin x−cos x)/5+C; (d) eˣ(sin x+cos x)/2+C; (e) xarcsin x+√(1−x²)+C; (f) −cos(e³ᵗ)/3+C.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 162, "jpg": "085_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all seventeen printed subparts checked"}]}];
const math12Ex62=MATH_12_DATA[5].exercises.find(ex=>ex.title==='Exercise 6.2');math12Ex62.unreviewedProblems=math12Ex62.problems;math12Ex62.problems=math12Exercise62;math12Ex62.pageStart=math12Ex62.pageEnd=162;const math12Integration162=MATH_12_DATA[5].sourcePages.find(p=>p.page===162);math12Ex62.sourceContextBlocks=math12Integration162.blocks.slice(math12Integration162.blocks.findIndex(b=>b.type==='heading'&&b.text==='History'));math12Parts.sourceBlocks.push(...math12Integration162.blocks.slice(0,math12Integration162.blocks.findIndex(b=>b.type==='heading'&&b.text==='Exercise 6.2')));math12Parts.sourceReferences.push({page:162,jpg:'085_page_2.jpg',verification:'ILATE mnemonic/table checked'});math12Parts.theory=math12Parts.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');
const math12Exercise63=[{"id": "ex-6-3-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Evaluate after partial fractions: (a) \\(\\int\\frac1{x(x-3)}\\,dx\\); (b) \\(\\int\\frac{3x^2+2x-1}{x(x+1)}\\,dx\\); (c) \\(\\int\\frac{4x^3+4x^2+x-1}{x^2(x+1)^2}\\,dx\\); (d) \\(\\int\\frac1{x^3-1}\\,dx\\); (e) \\(\\int\\frac{x^4-x^2+2}{x^2(x-1)}\\,dx\\); (f) \\(\\int\\frac1{x^2-1}\\,dx\\); (g) \\(\\int\\frac{x-3}{2x^2-x-1}\\,dx\\); (h) \\(\\int\\frac{x^2-1}{x^2-2x-15}\\,dx\\); (i) \\(\\int\\frac{x}{(x+1)(x^2+1)}\\,dx\\); (j) \\(\\int\\frac{x^2+2}{(x^2+1)^2}\\,dx\\).", "steps": ["Perform long division when needed; include all repeated factor powers and preserve exclusions from the original denominator.", "(a) 1/[x(x−3)] = −1/(3x)+1/[3(x−3)]. Integrate: (1/3)ln|(x−3)/x|+C, x ≠ 0,3.", "(b) Division and decomposition give 3−1/x+0/(x+1). Integrate: 3x−ln|x|+C. The original expression still excludes x = 0 and −1, despite cancellation of x+1.", "(c) The decomposition is 3/x−1/x²+1/(x+1)−2/(x+1)². Integrate: 3ln|x|+1/x+ln|x+1|+2/(x+1)+C, excluding 0,−1.", "(d) Factor x³−1 = (x−1)(x²+x+1). The decomposition is 1/[3(x−1)]−(x+2)/[3(x²+x+1)].", "Write the quadratic numerator as −(2x+1)/6−1/2. Integrate to (1/3)ln|x−1|−(1/6)ln(x²+x+1)−(1/√3)arctan((2x+1)/√3)+C, x ≠ 1.", "(e) Long division gives x+1, and the remaining decomposition is −2/x−2/x²+2/(x−1). Integrate: x²/2+x−2ln|x|+2/x+2ln|x−1|+C, excluding 0,1.", "(f) 1/(x²−1) = 1/[2(x−1)]−1/[2(x+1)]. Integrate: (1/2)ln|(x−1)/(x+1)|+C, excluding ±1.", "(g) Factor 2x²−x−1 = (x−1)(2x+1). Decompose as −2/[3(x−1)]+7/[3(2x+1)]. Integrate: −(2/3)ln|x−1|+(7/6)ln|2x+1|+C, excluding 1,−1/2.", "(h) Division gives 1+3/(x−5)−1/(x+3). Integrate: x+3ln|x−5|−ln|x+3|+C, excluding 5,−3.", "(i) Decompose as −1/[2(x+1)]+(x+1)/[2(x²+1)]. Integrate: −(1/2)ln|x+1|+(1/4)ln(x²+1)+(1/2)arctan x+C, x ≠ −1.", "(j) (x²+2)/(x²+1)² = 1/(x²+1)+1/(x²+1)². Integrate: (3/2)arctan x+x/[2(x²+1)]+C for all real x.", "Each decomposition was checked by clearing its denominator and comparing every polynomial coefficient; differentiating the integrated terms reproduces the original rational function on its domain."], "solution": "Perform long division when needed; include all repeated factor powers and preserve exclusions from the original denominator.\n(a) 1/[x(x−3)] = −1/(3x)+1/[3(x−3)]. Integrate: (1/3)ln|(x−3)/x|+C, x ≠ 0,3.\n(b) Division and decomposition give 3−1/x+0/(x+1). Integrate: 3x−ln|x|+C. The original expression still excludes x = 0 and −1, despite cancellation of x+1.\n(c) The decomposition is 3/x−1/x²+1/(x+1)−2/(x+1)². Integrate: 3ln|x|+1/x+ln|x+1|+2/(x+1)+C, excluding 0,−1.\n(d) Factor x³−1 = (x−1)(x²+x+1). The decomposition is 1/[3(x−1)]−(x+2)/[3(x²+x+1)].\nWrite the quadratic numerator as −(2x+1)/6−1/2. Integrate to (1/3)ln|x−1|−(1/6)ln(x²+x+1)−(1/√3)arctan((2x+1)/√3)+C, x ≠ 1.\n(e) Long division gives x+1, and the remaining decomposition is −2/x−2/x²+2/(x−1). Integrate: x²/2+x−2ln|x|+2/x+2ln|x−1|+C, excluding 0,1.\n(f) 1/(x²−1) = 1/[2(x−1)]−1/[2(x+1)]. Integrate: (1/2)ln|(x−1)/(x+1)|+C, excluding ±1.\n(g) Factor 2x²−x−1 = (x−1)(2x+1). Decompose as −2/[3(x−1)]+7/[3(2x+1)]. Integrate: −(2/3)ln|x−1|+(7/6)ln|2x+1|+C, excluding 1,−1/2.\n(h) Division gives 1+3/(x−5)−1/(x+3). Integrate: x+3ln|x−5|−ln|x+3|+C, excluding 5,−3.\n(i) Decompose as −1/[2(x+1)]+(x+1)/[2(x²+1)]. Integrate: −(1/2)ln|x+1|+(1/4)ln(x²+1)+(1/2)arctan x+C, x ≠ −1.\n(j) (x²+2)/(x²+1)² = 1/(x²+1)+1/(x²+1)². Integrate: (3/2)arctan x+x/[2(x²+1)]+C for all real x.\nEach decomposition was checked by clearing its denominator and comparing every polynomial coefficient; differentiating the integrated terms reproduces the original rational function on its domain.", "answer": "(a) (1/3)ln|(x−3)/x|+C; (b) 3x−ln|x|+C; (c) 3ln|x|+ln|x+1|+1/x+2/(x+1)+C; (d) (1/3)ln|x−1|−(1/6)ln(x²+x+1)−arctan((2x+1)/√3)/√3+C; (e) x²/2+x−2ln|x|+2/x+2ln|x−1|+C; (f) (1/2)ln|(x−1)/(x+1)|+C; (g) −(2/3)ln|x−1|+(7/6)ln|2x+1|+C; (h) x+3ln|x−5|−ln|x+3|+C; (i) −(1/2)ln|x+1|+(1/4)ln(x²+1)+(1/2)arctan x+C; (j) (3/2)arctan x+x/[2(x²+1)]+C.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 165, "jpg": "087_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all ten rational subparts and three applied source questions inspected"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-3-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "The rate at which the body eliminates a drug (in milliliters per hour) is given by \\(R(t)/dt=60t/[(t+1)^2(t+2)]\\), where t is the number of hours since the drug was administered. If R(0) = 0 is the current drug elimination, how much of the drug is eliminated during the first hour after it was administered? The fourth hour, after it was administered?", "steps": ["Interpret the printed rate R(t)/dt as dR/dt; as written it is not derivative notation. Let R be cumulative millilitres eliminated and t ≥ 0.", "Decompose 60t/[(t+1)²(t+2)] = 120/(t+1)−60/(t+1)²−120/(t+2).", "Integrate: R(t) = 120ln(t+1)+60/(t+1)−120ln(t+2)+C.", "Use R(0) = 0: 60−120ln 2+C = 0, so C = 120ln 2−60.", "The first hour is the interval [0,1]: R(1)−R(0) = 120ln(4/3)−30 ≈ 4.521848694 ml.", "The fourth hour is [3,4], not [0,4]. It eliminates R(4)−R(3) = 120ln(25/24)−3 ≈ 1.898639342 ml.", "Both quantities are positive because the rate is nonnegative for t ≥ 0; report approximately 4.52 ml and 1.90 ml."], "solution": "Interpret the printed rate R(t)/dt as dR/dt; as written it is not derivative notation. Let R be cumulative millilitres eliminated and t ≥ 0.\nDecompose 60t/[(t+1)²(t+2)] = 120/(t+1)−60/(t+1)²−120/(t+2).\nIntegrate: R(t) = 120ln(t+1)+60/(t+1)−120ln(t+2)+C.\nUse R(0) = 0: 60−120ln 2+C = 0, so C = 120ln 2−60.\nThe first hour is the interval [0,1]: R(1)−R(0) = 120ln(4/3)−30 ≈ 4.521848694 ml.\nThe fourth hour is [3,4], not [0,4]. It eliminates R(4)−R(3) = 120ln(25/24)−3 ≈ 1.898639342 ml.\nBoth quantities are positive because the rate is nonnegative for t ≥ 0; report approximately 4.52 ml and 1.90 ml.", "answer": "First hour: 120ln(4/3)−30 ≈ 4.52 ml. Fourth hour: 120ln(25/24)−3 ≈ 1.90 ml.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 165, "jpg": "087_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all ten rational subparts and three applied source questions inspected"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-3-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "The rate of change of the voting population of a city with respect to time t (in years) is estimated to be \\(dN/dt=100t/(1+t^2)^2\\), where N(t) is in thousands. If N(0) is the current voting population, then how much will this population increase during the next 3 years?", "steps": ["The requested increase is N(3)−N(0) = ∫₀³100t/(1+t²)² dt, measured in thousands.", "Set u = 1+t², du = 2t dt.", "Then the antiderivative is 50∫u⁻² du = −50/u+C = −50/(1+t²)+C.", "At 3, this is −5+C; at 0, it is −50+C.", "Subtract: N(3)−N(0) = 45 thousands.", "Thus the voting population increases by 45,000 people, independent of its current absolute size.", "The rate is positive for t > 0, so the sign and unit conversion agree with the model."], "solution": "The requested increase is N(3)−N(0) = ∫₀³100t/(1+t²)² dt, measured in thousands.\nSet u = 1+t², du = 2t dt.\nThen the antiderivative is 50∫u⁻² du = −50/u+C = −50/(1+t²)+C.\nAt 3, this is −5+C; at 0, it is −50+C.\nSubtract: N(3)−N(0) = 45 thousands.\nThus the voting population increases by 45,000 people, independent of its current absolute size.\nThe rate is positive for t > 0, so the sign and unit conversion agree with the model.", "answer": "45,000 people.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 165, "jpg": "087_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all ten rational subparts and three applied source questions inspected"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-3-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "An oil tanker aground on a reef is losing oil and producing an oil slick that is radiating outward at a rate approximated by \\(dr/dt=100/\\sqrt{t^2+9}\\), t ≥ 0. where r is the radius (in feet) of the circular slick after t minutes. Find the radius of the slick after 4 minutes if the radius is r = 0 when t = 0.", "steps": ["Integrate the radial growth rate from 0 to 4 minutes: r(4) = ∫₀⁴100/√(t²+9) dt.", "A primitive is 100ln(t+√(t²+9))+C.", "Enforce r(0) = 0: the logarithm at 0 is ln 3, so C = −100ln 3.", "Therefore r(t) = 100ln[(t+√(t²+9))/3], t ≥ 0.", "At 4, √(16+9) = 5 and the logarithm argument is (4+5)/3 = 3.", "Hence r(4) = 100ln 3 ≈ 109.861228867 feet, or 109.86 feet.", "This is a radius, not an area. The equivalent formula 100asinh(t/3) has derivative 100/√(t²+9) and starts at zero."], "solution": "Integrate the radial growth rate from 0 to 4 minutes: r(4) = ∫₀⁴100/√(t²+9) dt.\nA primitive is 100ln(t+√(t²+9))+C.\nEnforce r(0) = 0: the logarithm at 0 is ln 3, so C = −100ln 3.\nTherefore r(t) = 100ln[(t+√(t²+9))/3], t ≥ 0.\nAt 4, √(16+9) = 5 and the logarithm argument is (4+5)/3 = 3.\nHence r(4) = 100ln 3 ≈ 109.861228867 feet, or 109.86 feet.\nThis is a radius, not an area. The equivalent formula 100asinh(t/3) has derivative 100/√(t²+9) and starts at zero.", "answer": "100ln 3 ≈ 109.86 feet.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 165, "jpg": "087_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all ten rational subparts and three applied source questions inspected"}]}];
