const math12ReviewedIntegrationNums=new Set(math12IntegrationExamples.map(e=>e.num)),math12SeenIntegrationNums=new Set();MATH_12_DATA[5].unreviewedDuplicateExamples=[];MATH_12_DATA[5].workedExamples=MATH_12_DATA[5].workedExamples.filter(e=>{if(!math12ReviewedIntegrationNums.has(e.num))return true;if(math12SeenIntegrationNums.has(e.num)){MATH_12_DATA[5].unreviewedDuplicateExamples.push(e);return false;}math12SeenIntegrationNums.add(e.num);return true;});
const math12Exercise64=[{"id": "ex-6-4-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Evaluate the following definite integrals: (a) \\(\\int_3^4 5x\\,dx\\); (b) \\(\\int_{12}^{20}x^3\\,dx\\); (c) \\(\\int_1^2(2x^{-2}-3)\\,dx\\); (d) \\(\\int_1^4 3\\sqrt x\\,dx\\); (e) \\(\\int_2^3 12(x^2-4)^5x\\,dx\\); (f) \\(\\int_{-1}^1\\frac{e^{-x}-e^x}{(e^{-x}+e^x)^2}\\,dx\\); (g) \\(\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\cos(\\frac x2+\\pi)\\,dx\\); (h) \\(\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\sec^2\\theta\\,d\\theta\\).", "steps": ["(a) A primitive is 5x²/2. Its endpoint difference is (5/2)(16−9) = 35/2 = 17.5.", "(b) Use x⁴/4: (20⁴−12⁴)/4 = (160000−20736)/4 = 34816.", "(c) The source exponent is −2, not +2. A primitive is −2/x−3x. At 2 this is −7; at 1 it is −5, so the difference is −2.", "(d) Integrate 3x^(1/2) to 2x^(3/2). The value is 2(8−1) = 14.", "(e) Put u = x²−4, du = 2x dx. The transformed integral is 6∫₀⁵u⁵du = [u⁶]₀⁵ = 15625.", "(f) The denominator is positive and even; the numerator is odd. The symmetric integral is zero. Independently, a primitive is 1/(e⁻ˣ+eˣ), whose endpoint values at ±1 are equal.", "(g) A primitive is 2sin(x/2+π). Subtract its values at π and π/2: −2−(−√2) = √2−2. This negative signed value is appropriate because the integrand is negative on the interval.", "(h) A primitive is tan θ. The value is tan(π/4)−tan 0 = 1. All trigonometric angles are in radians."], "solution": "(a) A primitive is 5x²/2. Its endpoint difference is (5/2)(16−9) = 35/2 = 17.5.\n(b) Use x⁴/4: (20⁴−12⁴)/4 = (160000−20736)/4 = 34816.\n(c) The source exponent is −2, not +2. A primitive is −2/x−3x. At 2 this is −7; at 1 it is −5, so the difference is −2.\n(d) Integrate 3x^(1/2) to 2x^(3/2). The value is 2(8−1) = 14.\n(e) Put u = x²−4, du = 2x dx. The transformed integral is 6∫₀⁵u⁵du = [u⁶]₀⁵ = 15625.\n(f) The denominator is positive and even; the numerator is odd. The symmetric integral is zero. Independently, a primitive is 1/(e⁻ˣ+eˣ), whose endpoint values at ±1 are equal.\n(g) A primitive is 2sin(x/2+π). Subtract its values at π and π/2: −2−(−√2) = √2−2. This negative signed value is appropriate because the integrand is negative on the interval.\n(h) A primitive is tan θ. The value is tan(π/4)−tan 0 = 1. All trigonometric angles are in radians.", "answer": "(a) 17.5; (b) 34816; (c) −2; (d) 14; (e) 15625; (f) 0; (g) √2−2; (h) 1.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 175, "jpg": "076_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source expressions, shaded intervals and rate units visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-4-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Evaluate the following definite integrals: (a) \\(\\int_1^2\\frac{5t^2-3t+18}{t(9-t^2)}\\,dt\\); (b) \\(\\int_1^2\\frac4{t^3+4t}\\,dt\\).", "steps": ["Both denominators are nonzero on [1,2], so ordinary definite integration is valid.", "(a) Decompose (5t²−3t+18)/[t(9−t²)] = 2/t+3/(3−t)−4/(3+t). Multiplication back gives 18−3t+5t².", "A primitive on [1,2] is 2ln t−3ln(3−t)−4ln(3+t).", "Its endpoint difference is 2ln 2−3ln(1/2)−4ln(5/4) = 5ln 2−4ln(5/4) ≈ 2.573161698.", "(b) Factor t³+4t = t(t²+4). Decompose 4/[t(t²+4)] = 1/t−t/(t²+4).", "A primitive is ln t−(1/2)ln(t²+4).", "Evaluate from 1 to 2: ln 2−(1/2)ln 8+(1/2)ln 5 = (1/2)ln(5/2) ≈ 0.458145366."], "solution": "Both denominators are nonzero on [1,2], so ordinary definite integration is valid.\n(a) Decompose (5t²−3t+18)/[t(9−t²)] = 2/t+3/(3−t)−4/(3+t). Multiplication back gives 18−3t+5t².\nA primitive on [1,2] is 2ln t−3ln(3−t)−4ln(3+t).\nIts endpoint difference is 2ln 2−3ln(1/2)−4ln(5/4) = 5ln 2−4ln(5/4) ≈ 2.573161698.\n(b) Factor t³+4t = t(t²+4). Decompose 4/[t(t²+4)] = 1/t−t/(t²+4).\nA primitive is ln t−(1/2)ln(t²+4).\nEvaluate from 1 to 2: ln 2−(1/2)ln 8+(1/2)ln 5 = (1/2)ln(5/2) ≈ 0.458145366.", "answer": "(a) 5ln 2−4ln(5/4) ≈ 2.573161698; (b) (1/2)ln(5/2) ≈ 0.458145366.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 175, "jpg": "076_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source expressions, shaded intervals and rate units visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-4-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Use definite integral to find out the area between the curve f(x) and the x-axis over the indicated interval [a,b]: (a) f(x) = 4−x², [0,3]; (b) f(x) = x²−5x+6, [0,3]; (c) f(x) = x²−6x+8, [0,4]; (d) f(x) = 5x−x², [1,3].", "steps": ["Find roots inside each interval; geometric area is the integral of the absolute value, so split whenever the sign changes.", "(a) 4−x² changes sign at 2 in [0,3]. Use F(x) = 4x−x³/3. The areas are F(2)−F(0) = 16/3 and −[F(3)−F(2)] = 7/3. Total: 23/3 square units.", "(b) x²−5x+6 = (x−2)(x−3), positive on [0,2] and negative on [2,3]. Use F(x) = x³/3−5x²/2+6x.", "F(0) = 0,F(2) = 14/3,F(3) = 9/2. Total area is 14/3+(14/3−9/2) = 29/6 square units.", "(c) x²−6x+8 = (x−2)(x−4), positive on [0,2] and negative on [2,4]. Use F(x) = x³/3−3x²+8x.", "F(0) = 0,F(2) = 20/3,F(4) = 16/3. Total area is 20/3+(20/3−16/3) = 8 square units.", "(d) 5x−x² = x(5−x) is positive throughout [1,3]. Its area is [5x²/2−x³/3]₁³ = 34/3 square units."], "solution": "Find roots inside each interval; geometric area is the integral of the absolute value, so split whenever the sign changes.\n(a) 4−x² changes sign at 2 in [0,3]. Use F(x) = 4x−x³/3. The areas are F(2)−F(0) = 16/3 and −[F(3)−F(2)] = 7/3. Total: 23/3 square units.\n(b) x²−5x+6 = (x−2)(x−3), positive on [0,2] and negative on [2,3]. Use F(x) = x³/3−5x²/2+6x.\nF(0) = 0,F(2) = 14/3,F(3) = 9/2. Total area is 14/3+(14/3−9/2) = 29/6 square units.\n(c) x²−6x+8 = (x−2)(x−4), positive on [0,2] and negative on [2,4]. Use F(x) = x³/3−3x²+8x.\nF(0) = 0,F(2) = 20/3,F(4) = 16/3. Total area is 20/3+(20/3−16/3) = 8 square units.\n(d) 5x−x² = x(5−x) is positive throughout [1,3]. Its area is [5x²/2−x³/3]₁³ = 34/3 square units.", "answer": "(a) 23/3; (b) 29/6; (c) 8; (d) 34/3 square units.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 175, "jpg": "076_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source expressions, shaded intervals and rate units visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-4-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "Setup definite integrals in problems a to d that represent the indicated shaded areas:", "steps": ["Read the shaded endpoints from each dedicated graph, then split at its x-intercept.", "(a) y = (e²−eˣ)/2 is positive from 1 to 2 and negative from 2 to 3. Area = ∫₁²(e²−eˣ)/2 dx − ∫₂³(e²−eˣ)/2 dx.", "(b) y = eˣ−e changes sign at 1. The shaded interval is [0,2]. Area = −∫₀¹(eˣ−e) dx + ∫₁²(eˣ−e) dx.", "(c) y = 2−x²/2 changes sign at 2. The shaded interval is [0,3]. Area = ∫₀²(2−x²/2) dx − ∫₂³(2−x²/2) dx.", "(d) y = x²−2x changes sign at 0 and 2. The source shading ends at 2, so use ∫₋₁⁰(x²−2x) dx − ∫₀²(x²−2x) dx; there is no shaded [2,3] region in this exercise.", "For optional checks, evaluating these expressions gives (a) (e³−2e²+e)/2; (b) (e−1)²; (c) 23/6; (d) 8/3 square units.", "The problem requests setup; every expression above adds nonnegative geometric pieces rather than taking one unsplit signed integral."], "solution": "Read the shaded endpoints from each dedicated graph, then split at its x-intercept.\n(a) y = (e²−eˣ)/2 is positive from 1 to 2 and negative from 2 to 3. Area = ∫₁²(e²−eˣ)/2 dx − ∫₂³(e²−eˣ)/2 dx.\n(b) y = eˣ−e changes sign at 1. The shaded interval is [0,2]. Area = −∫₀¹(eˣ−e) dx + ∫₁²(eˣ−e) dx.\n(c) y = 2−x²/2 changes sign at 2. The shaded interval is [0,3]. Area = ∫₀²(2−x²/2) dx − ∫₂³(2−x²/2) dx.\n(d) y = x²−2x changes sign at 0 and 2. The source shading ends at 2, so use ∫₋₁⁰(x²−2x) dx − ∫₀²(x²−2x) dx; there is no shaded [2,3] region in this exercise.\nFor optional checks, evaluating these expressions gives (a) (e³−2e²+e)/2; (b) (e−1)²; (c) 23/6; (d) 8/3 square units.\nThe problem requests setup; every expression above adds nonnegative geometric pieces rather than taking one unsplit signed integral.", "answer": "(a) ∫₁²(e²−eˣ)/2 dx−∫₂³(e²−eˣ)/2 dx; (b) −∫₀¹(eˣ−e) dx+∫₁²(eˣ−e) dx; (c) ∫₀²(2−x²/2) dx−∫₂³(2−x²/2) dx; (d) ∫₋₁⁰(x²−2x) dx−∫₀²(x²−2x) dx.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 175, "jpg": "076_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source expressions, shaded intervals and rate units visually checked"}], "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 6.4, Question 4 (a)", "diagram": {"type": "source-integral-function", "title": "Exercise 6.4, Question 4 (a) — original shaded intervals", "polynomial": [3.694528049465325], "regions": [[1, 2], [2, 3]], "xMin": -0.5, "xMax": 3.8, "yMin": -7, "yMax": 9, "xTicks": [0, 1, 2, 3], "yTicks": [-4, 4, 8], "equation": "y = (e² − eˣ)/2", "points": [], "expMultiplier": -0.5}}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 6.4, Question 4 (b)", "diagram": {"type": "source-integral-function", "title": "Exercise 6.4, Question 4 (b) — original shaded intervals", "polynomial": [-2.718281828459045], "regions": [[0, 1], [1, 2]], "xMin": -0.5, "xMax": 3.3, "yMin": -2.5, "yMax": 6, "xTicks": [0, 1, 2, 3], "yTicks": [2, 4], "equation": "y = eˣ − e", "points": [], "expMultiplier": 1}}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 6.4, Question 4 (c)", "diagram": {"type": "source-integral-function", "title": "Exercise 6.4, Question 4 (c) — original shaded intervals", "polynomial": [2, 0, -0.5], "regions": [[0, 2], [2, 3]], "xMin": -2.5, "xMax": 4.5, "yMin": -3, "yMax": 5, "xTicks": [-2, 0, 2, 4], "yTicks": [-2, 2, 4], "equation": "y = 2 − 0.5x²", "points": []}}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 6.4, Question 4 (d)", "diagram": {"type": "source-integral-function", "title": "Exercise 6.4, Question 4 (d) — original shaded intervals", "polynomial": [0, -2, 1], "regions": [[-1, 0], [0, 2]], "xMin": -1.5, "xMax": 4.5, "yMin": -1.5, "yMax": 4.5, "xTicks": [-1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "yTicks": [1, 3], "equation": "f(x) = x² − 2x", "points": []}}]}, {"id": "ex-6-4-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "An oil tanker is leaking oil at a rate given in barrels per hour by \\(dL/dt=80\\ln(t+1)/(t+1)\\). Where t is the time in hours after the tanker has hit hidden rock (when t = 0). (a) Find the total number of barrels that the ship will leak on the first day. (b) Find the total number of barrels that the ship will leak on the second day. (c) What is happening over the long run to the amount of oil leaked per day?", "steps": ["The rate uses hours. Therefore the first day is [0,24], and the second day is [24,48].", "Put u = ln(t+1),du = dt/(t+1). Integrate the rate: a cumulative primitive is 40[ln(t+1)]².", "The first-day leakage is 40[ln 25]²−40[ln 1]² = 40[ln 25]² ≈ 414.446463 barrels.", "The second-day leakage is 40([ln 49]²−[ln 25]²) ≈ 191.404146 barrels.", "The instantaneous rate 80ln(t+1)/(t+1) tends to zero as t tends to infinity.", "The amount leaked in a later 24-hour interval is 40([ln(t+25)]²−[ln(t+1)]²), which also tends to zero; daily leakage declines in the long run.", "The idealized cumulative leakage still grows without bound as 40ln²(t+1), so diminishing daily leakage does not imply a finite total in this mathematical model."], "solution": "The rate uses hours. Therefore the first day is [0,24], and the second day is [24,48].\nPut u = ln(t+1),du = dt/(t+1). Integrate the rate: a cumulative primitive is 40[ln(t+1)]².\nThe first-day leakage is 40[ln 25]²−40[ln 1]² = 40[ln 25]² ≈ 414.446463 barrels.\nThe second-day leakage is 40([ln 49]²−[ln 25]²) ≈ 191.404146 barrels.\nThe instantaneous rate 80ln(t+1)/(t+1) tends to zero as t tends to infinity.\nThe amount leaked in a later 24-hour interval is 40([ln(t+25)]²−[ln(t+1)]²), which also tends to zero; daily leakage declines in the long run.\nThe idealized cumulative leakage still grows without bound as 40ln²(t+1), so diminishing daily leakage does not imply a finite total in this mathematical model.", "answer": "(a) ≈ 414.45 barrels; (b) ≈ 191.40 barrels; (c) daily leakage tends to zero, while the idealized cumulative total diverges.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 175, "jpg": "076_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source expressions, shaded intervals and rate units visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-4-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "Use MAPLE command “int” to evaluate (a) f(x) = x²+3x+1 w.r.t. x; (b) f(x) = e²ˣsin x w.r.t. x.", "steps": ["(a) Enter int(x^2+3*x+1,x);.", "Integrate term by term: x² gives x³/3,3x gives 3x²/2, and 1 gives x.", "Expected output: x³/3+3x²/2+x. The full indefinite family adds +C.", "(b) Enter int(exp(2*x)*sin(x),x);.", "A manually checked expected primitive is exp(2*x)*(2*sin(x)−cos(x))/5.", "Differentiate: the product rule yields exp(2*x)[2(2sin x−cos x)+(2cos x+sin x)]/5 = exp(2*x)sin x.", "No limits are specified, so these are indefinite integrals. The outputs are mathematical expectations, not results of an executed Maple session."], "solution": "(a) Enter int(x^2+3*x+1,x);.\nIntegrate term by term: x² gives x³/3,3x gives 3x²/2, and 1 gives x.\nExpected output: x³/3+3x²/2+x. The full indefinite family adds +C.\n(b) Enter int(exp(2*x)*sin(x),x);.\nA manually checked expected primitive is exp(2*x)*(2*sin(x)−cos(x))/5.\nDifferentiate: the product rule yields exp(2*x)[2(2sin x−cos x)+(2cos x+sin x)]/5 = exp(2*x)sin x.\nNo limits are specified, so these are indefinite integrals. The outputs are mathematical expectations, not results of an executed Maple session.", "answer": "(a) x³/3+3x²/2+x+C; (b) e²ˣ(2sin x−cos x)/5+C.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 175, "jpg": "076_page_1.jpg", "verification": "all source expressions, shaded intervals and rate units visually checked"}]}];
