const math12Review6=[{"id": "ex-6-review-i", "qNo": "i", "question": "The process of finding antiderivative is called: (a) differentiation (b) integration (c) probability (d) linear equations", "steps": ["An antiderivative reverses differentiation.", "Finding it is called integration.", "Differentiation finds the derivative rather than the primitive.", "Probability and linear equations describe other topics.", "Select (b)."], "solution": "An antiderivative reverses differentiation.\nFinding it is called integration.\nDifferentiation finds the derivative rather than the primitive.\nProbability and linear equations describe other topics.\nSelect (b).", "answer": "Option (b)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-review-ii", "qNo": "ii", "question": "\\(\\int\\tan\\theta\\,d\\theta\\) = (a) \\(\\ln|\\sin\\theta|+C\\) (b) \\(\\ln|\\cos\\theta|+C\\) (c) \\(-\\ln|\\cos\\theta|+C\\) (d) \\(-\\ln|\\sin\\theta|+C\\)", "steps": ["Write tan θ = sin θ/cos θ.", "Set u = cos θ, du = −sin θ dθ.", "The integral becomes −∫du/u.", "Thus the answer is −ln|cos θ|+C, option (c).", "It holds on each interval where cos θ ≠ 0, using radians."], "solution": "Write tan θ = sin θ/cos θ.\nSet u = cos θ, du = −sin θ dθ.\nThe integral becomes −∫du/u.\nThus the answer is −ln|cos θ|+C, option (c).\nIt holds on each interval where cos θ ≠ 0, using radians.", "answer": "Option (c)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-review-iii", "qNo": "iii", "question": "\\(\\int dx/\\sqrt{a^2-x^2}\\) = (a) \\(\\ln|x+\\sqrt{x^2+a^2}|+C\\) (b) \\(\\sin^{-1}(a/x)+C\\) (c) \\(\\sin^{-1}(x/a)+C\\) (d) \\(\\cos^{-1}(x/a)+C\\)", "steps": ["For a > 0 and |x| < a, put u = x/a.", "Then dx = a du and √(a²−x²) = a√(1−u²).", "The integral becomes ∫du/√(1−u²) = arcsin u+C.", "Return to x to obtain arcsin(x/a)+C, option (c) under the usual a > 0 convention.", "For arbitrary nonzero a, use arcsin(x/|a|)+C; if a < 0 the printed (c) instead differentiates with the wrong sign."], "solution": "For a > 0 and |x| < a, put u = x/a.\nThen dx = a du and √(a²−x²) = a√(1−u²).\nThe integral becomes ∫du/√(1−u²) = arcsin u+C.\nReturn to x to obtain arcsin(x/a)+C, option (c) under the usual a > 0 convention.\nFor arbitrary nonzero a, use arcsin(x/|a|)+C; if a < 0 the printed (c) instead differentiates with the wrong sign.", "answer": "c, assuming a > 0", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-review-iv", "qNo": "iv", "question": "\\(\\int\\frac1{\\sqrt{t^2-36}}\\,dx\\) = (a) \\(\\frac1{2a}\\ln|\\frac{t-6}{t+6}|+C\\) (b) \\(\\frac12\\ln|\\frac{t-6}{t+6}|+C\\) (c) \\(-\\frac12(\\ln|t/6+1|-\\ln|t/6-1|+C)\\) (d) \\(\\frac16(\\ln|t/6+1|+6)+C\\)", "steps": ["The source integrand uses t but the differential is dx; this mismatch must be stated rather than silently repaired.", "If t is independent of x and |t| > 6, the literal x-antiderivative is x/√(t²−36)+C.", "If the intended differential was dt, a primitive is ln|t+√(t²−36)|+C on each of t > 6 and t < −6.", "Differentiate the logarithm: its derivative is 1/√(t²−36) on those open intervals.", "The printed logarithms of (t−6)/(t+6) differentiate to rational terms proportional to 1/(t²−36), not the square-root integrand.", "Neither the literal nor the intended square-root answer appears among the choices. The unrelated a in option (a) does not fix the mismatch."], "solution": "The source integrand uses t but the differential is dx; this mismatch must be stated rather than silently repaired.\nIf t is independent of x and |t| > 6, the literal x-antiderivative is x/√(t²−36)+C.\nIf the intended differential was dt, a primitive is ln|t+√(t²−36)|+C on each of t > 6 and t < −6.\nDifferentiate the logarithm: its derivative is 1/√(t²−36) on those open intervals.\nThe printed logarithms of (t−6)/(t+6) differentiate to rational terms proportional to 1/(t²−36), not the square-root integrand.\nNeither the literal nor the intended square-root answer appears among the choices. The unrelated a in option (a) does not fix the mismatch.", "answer": "No correct printed option", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-review-v", "qNo": "v", "question": "\\(\\int(x^3-4)\\,dx\\) = (a) \\(x^4/4-4x+C\\) (b) \\(x^3/3-4x+C\\) (c) \\(-x^4/4+4x+C\\) (d) \\(x^4/4+4x^3/3-4x^2/2-4x+C\\)", "steps": ["Apply the power rule to x³: its integral is x⁴/4.", "Integrate the constant −4 to −4x.", "Add the arbitrary constant C.", "The result x⁴/4−4x+C matches (a).", "Differentiate to recover x³−4."], "solution": "Apply the power rule to x³: its integral is x⁴/4.\nIntegrate the constant −4 to −4x.\nAdd the arbitrary constant C.\nThe result x⁴/4−4x+C matches (a).\nDifferentiate to recover x³−4.", "answer": "Option (a)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-review-vi", "qNo": "vi", "question": "\\(\\int f(x)g′(x)\\,dx\\) = (a) \\(f′(x)g(x)+\\int g′(x)f(x)dx\\) (b) \\(f(x)g(x)-\\int g(x)f′(x)dx\\) (c) \\(f′(x)g(x)-\\int g′(x)f′(x)dx\\) (d) \\(f(x)g(x)-\\int g(x)f(x)dx\\)", "steps": ["The product rule is (fg)′ = f′g+fg′.", "Rearrange: fg′ = (fg)′−gf′.", "Integrate both sides: ∫fg′dx = fg−∫gf′dx.", "This is option (b), whose second integrand contains f′.", "The visually checked option (d) contains f without the prime and is not the general parts rule."], "solution": "The product rule is (fg)′ = f′g+fg′.\nRearrange: fg′ = (fg)′−gf′.\nIntegrate both sides: ∫fg′dx = fg−∫gf′dx.\nThis is option (b), whose second integrand contains f′.\nThe visually checked option (d) contains f without the prime and is not the general parts rule.", "answer": "Option (b)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-review-vii", "qNo": "vii", "question": "\\(\\int\\tan^4x\\,dx\\) = (a) \\((2/3)\\tan^2x+x-\\tan x+C\\) (b) \\((1/3)\\tan^3x+x-\\tan x+C\\) (c) \\((3/4)\\tan^2x-x+\\tan x+C\\) (d) \\(3\\tan^3x+x+\\tan x+C\\)", "steps": ["Use tan⁴x = tan²x(sec²x−1).", "For ∫tan²x sec²x dx, set u = tan x and obtain tan³x/3.", "The subtracted integral ∫tan²x dx = ∫(sec²x−1)dx = tan x−x.", "Therefore the primitive is tan³x/3−tan x+x+C.", "This matches (b), on intervals excluding cosine zeros."], "solution": "Use tan⁴x = tan²x(sec²x−1).\nFor ∫tan²x sec²x dx, set u = tan x and obtain tan³x/3.\nThe subtracted integral ∫tan²x dx = ∫(sec²x−1)dx = tan x−x.\nTherefore the primitive is tan³x/3−tan x+x+C.\nThis matches (b), on intervals excluding cosine zeros.", "answer": "Option (b)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-review-viii", "qNo": "viii", "question": "\\(\\int\\frac{x+8}{x^2-64}\\,dx\\) = (a) \\(\\ln|x-8|+C\\) (b) \\((1/2)\\ln|x^2-64|+(1/2)|x/8+1|-(1/2)\\ln|x/2-1|+C\\) (c) \\((1/2)\\ln|x^2-64|-(1/2)\\ln|x/8+1|+(1/2)\\ln|x/8-1|+C\\) (d) \\((1/2)\\ln|x/8+1|+(1/2)\\ln|x/8-1|+C\\)", "steps": ["Factor x²−64 = (x−8)(x+8). The original function excludes x = ±8.", "On that domain the fraction simplifies to 1/(x−8).", "A primitive is ln|x−8|+C, option (a).", "In (c), expand ln|x²−64| as ln|x−8|+ln|x+8|, and expand the scaled arguments using ln|x/8±1| = ln|x±8|−ln 8.", "The x+8 terms and scaling constants cancel; (c) also equals ln|x−8|+C.", "Thus both (a) and (c) are correct on the original domain. The malformed (b) lacks ln in its middle term and uses x/2−1 in its last term."], "solution": "Factor x²−64 = (x−8)(x+8). The original function excludes x = ±8.\nOn that domain the fraction simplifies to 1/(x−8).\nA primitive is ln|x−8|+C, option (a).\nIn (c), expand ln|x²−64| as ln|x−8|+ln|x+8|, and expand the scaled arguments using ln|x/8±1| = ln|x±8|−ln 8.\nThe x+8 terms and scaling constants cancel; (c) also equals ln|x−8|+C.\nThus both (a) and (c) are correct on the original domain. The malformed (b) lacks ln in its middle term and uses x/2−1 in its last term.", "answer": "a and c", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-review-ix", "qNo": "ix", "question": "\\(\\int_0^2e^{2x}\\,dx\\) = (a) \\((e^4-1)/2\\) (b) \\((e^3-1)/2\\) (c) \\((e^2-1)/4\\) (d) \\((e^4+1)/2\\)", "steps": ["A primitive is e²ˣ/2.", "At 2, it gives e⁴/2.", "At 0, it gives 1/2.", "Subtract to obtain (e⁴−1)/2.", "Select (a); this definite answer has no arbitrary constant."], "solution": "A primitive is e²ˣ/2.\nAt 2, it gives e⁴/2.\nAt 0, it gives 1/2.\nSubtract to obtain (e⁴−1)/2.\nSelect (a); this definite answer has no arbitrary constant.", "answer": "Option (a)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-6-review-x", "qNo": "x", "question": "\\(\\int_0^2x^3\\,dx\\) = (a) 1 (b) 2 (c) 3 (d) 4", "steps": ["A primitive is x⁴/4.", "At 2 its value is 16/4 = 4.", "At 0 its value is zero.", "The endpoint difference is 4.", "Select (d)."], "solution": "A primitive is x⁴/4.\nAt 2 its value is 16/4 = 4.\nAt 0 its value is zero.\nThe endpoint difference is 4.\nSelect (d).", "answer": "Option (d)", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 176, "jpg": "076_page_2.jpg", "verification": "all source choices enlarged and visually checked; invalid and duplicate choices explained"}]}];
