MATH_12_DATA[6].sourcePages=[{"file": "077_page_2.jpg", "page": 178, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Unit 7 — PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "By the end of this unit, the students will be able to:"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.1 Division of a line segment"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Recall distance formula to calculate distance between two points given in Cartesian plane."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find coordinates of a point that divides the line segment in given ratio (internally and externally)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Show that the medians and angle bisectors of a triangle are concurrent."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.2 Slope of a straight line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Define the slope of a line."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Derive formula to find the slope of a line passing through two points."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the condition that two straight lines with given slopes may be parallel to each other, or perpendicular to each other."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.3 Equation of a straight line parallel to Co-ordinate axes"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the equation of a straight line parallel to y-axis and at a distance a from it, or x-axis and at a distance b from it."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.4 Standard form of equation of a straight line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Define intercepts of a straight line. Derive equation of a straight line in slope-intercept form, point-slope form, two-point form, intercepts form, symmetric form, normal form."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Show that a linear equation in two variables represents a straight line."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Reduce the general form of the equation of a straight line to the other standard forms."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.5 Distance of a point from a line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Recognize a point with respect to position of a line."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the perpendicular distance from a point to the given straight lines."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.6 Angle between lines"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the angle between two coplanar intersecting straight lines."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the equation of family of lines passing through the point of intersection of two given lines."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Calculate angles of the triangle when the slopes of the sides are given."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.7 Concurrency of straight lines"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the condition of concurrency of three straight lines."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the equation of median, altitude and right bisector of a triangle."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Show that three right bisectors, three medians, or three altitudes, of a triangle are concurrent."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.8 Area of a triangular region"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find area of a triangular region whose vertices are given."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.9 Homogenous equation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Recognize homogeneous linear and quadratic equations in two variables."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Investigate that the 2nd degree homogeneous equation in two variables x and y represents a pair of straight lines through the origin and find acute angle between them."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Introduction"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "We are familiar about Cartesian coordinate system, we have learnt about it in our previous classes. This Cartesian coordinate system may be helpful to know the slope formula, Pythagoras theorem and distance formula. In this lesson we will learn in details and write the equations involving arbitrary points. Most of the geometric ideas can be expressed using algebraic equations. Analytic geometry is defined as:"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "“The study of relationship between geometry and algebra is called analytic geometry”."}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/figure-7-1.jpg", "caption": "Figure 7.1", "alt": "Source illustration of a cyclist ascending a sloping line beside a shaded right triangle"}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "All nine objective groups and introduction transcribed; dedicated original cyclist illustration retained", "objectives": [["7.1 Division of a line segment", ["Recall distance formula to calculate distance between two points given in Cartesian plane.", "Find coordinates of a point that divides the line segment in given ratio (internally and externally).", "Show that the medians and angle bisectors of a triangle are concurrent."]], ["7.2 Slope of a straight line", ["Define the slope of a line.", "Derive formula to find the slope of a line passing through two points.", "Find the condition that two straight lines with given slopes may be parallel to each other, or perpendicular to each other."]], ["7.3 Equation of a straight line parallel to Co-ordinate axes", ["Find the equation of a straight line parallel to y-axis and at a distance a from it, or x-axis and at a distance b from it."]], ["7.4 Standard form of equation of a straight line", ["Define intercepts of a straight line. Derive equation of a straight line in slope-intercept form, point-slope form, two-point form, intercepts form, symmetric form, normal form.", "Show that a linear equation in two variables represents a straight line.", "Reduce the general form of the equation of a straight line to the other standard forms."]], ["7.5 Distance of a point from a line", ["Recognize a point with respect to position of a line.", "Find the perpendicular distance from a point to the given straight lines."]], ["7.6 Angle between lines", ["Find the angle between two coplanar intersecting straight lines.", "Find the equation of family of lines passing through the point of intersection of two given lines.", "Calculate angles of the triangle when the slopes of the sides are given."]], ["7.7 Concurrency of straight lines", ["Find the condition of concurrency of three straight lines.", "Find the equation of median, altitude and right bisector of a triangle.", "Show that three right bisectors, three medians, or three altitudes, of a triangle are concurrent."]], ["7.8 Area of a triangular region", ["Find area of a triangular region whose vertices are given."]], ["7.9 Homogenous equation", ["Recognize homogeneous linear and quadratic equations in two variables.", "Investigate that the 2nd degree homogeneous equation in two variables x and y represents a pair of straight lines through the origin and find acute angle between them."]]], "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 77, "side": "right", "reason": "Full spread and duplicate 093_page_2.jpg both inspected visually"}}, {"file": "078_page_1.jpg", "page": 179, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "For example to calculate the slope/gradient between two given points, the numerator is the difference in the y-coordinates some times called it “Rise” and the denominator is the difference between x-coordinates, some time called it “run” e.g."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\mathrm{Slope\\ between\\ two\\ points}=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\\frac{\\mathrm{Rise}}{\\mathrm{Run}}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Do You Know?"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Analytic Geometry was independent and simultaneous invention of Pierre De Fermat and Rene Descartes. The fundamental idea of Analytic Geometry and the representation of curved lines by algebraic equations relating two variables say, x and y was given in seventeenth century by them."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.1 Division of a line segment"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "We are familiar with the set of real numbers as well as with several of its subsets, including natural numbers and real numbers. The real numbers can easily be visualized by using a one dimensional coordinate system call real number line."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.1.1 Calculation of distance between two given points"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The study of plane analytic geometry is greatly facilitated by the use of vectors. The distance between any two given points can be calculated by using the distance formula."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂) are two points in the xy-plane and θ is the angle in between the positive directions of the x and y axes, then, PQ is the directed line segment associated to initial point P(x₁,y₁) and terminal point Q(x₂,y₂)."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.2", "diagram": {"type": "source-line-vector-geometry", "kind": "distance", "title": "Figure 7.2 — Position vectors and coordinate projections for the distance formula"}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The components of the directed line segment PQ are:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(OP+PQ=OQ\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(PQ=OQ-OP,\\quad\\mathrm{position\\ vectors}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=(x_2,y_2)-(x_1,y_1)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(PQ=(x_2-x_1,y_2-y_1)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=(x_2-x_1)î+(y_2-y_1)ĵ\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Squaring both side of the directed line segment PQ to obtain"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((PQ)^2=[(x_2-x_1)î+(y_2-y_1)ĵ]^2,\\quad(a+b)^2=a^2+b^2+2ab\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=(x_2-x_1)^2î\\cdot î+(y_2-y_1)^2ĵ\\cdot ĵ+2(x_2-x_1)(y_2-y_1)î\\cdot ĵ\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2+2(x_2-x_1)(y_2-y_1)|î||ĵ|\\cos\\theta\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(î\\cdot î=ĵ\\cdot ĵ=1,\\quad î\\cdot ĵ=|î||ĵ|\\cos\\theta,\\quad |î|=|ĵ|=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2+2(x_2-x_1)(y_2-y_1)\\cos\\theta\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2+2(x_2-x_1)(y_2-y_1)\\cos\\frac\\pi2,\\quad\\theta=\\frac\\pi2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((PQ)^2=(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2,\\quad\\cos\\frac\\pi2=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(|PQ|^2=(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2,\\quad(PQ)^2=|PQ|^2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Pythagoras Theorem"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂) are the two points in the xy-plane, then the distance d between the given two points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂) is obtained by applying the theorem of Pythagoras to triangle PQR:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((PQ)^2=(PR)^2+(QR)^2=(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(PQ=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}=d,\\ \\mathrm{say}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The slope quotient requires x₂ ≠ x₁; a vertical line has no finite slope. In the vector proof the square means the dot product PQ·PQ, not an undefined product of vectors. Cartesian axes are perpendicular, so θ = π/2. The source calls the Pythagoras triangle PQR, while its small drawing labels the horizontal foot r; these identify the same right-angle foot."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source distance and section-formula proof; coordinate figures reconstructed natively", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 78, "side": "left", "reason": "All proof lines, notes and labels visually inspected"}}, {"file": "078_page_2.jpg", "page": 180, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(|PQ|=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}=d,\\ \\mathrm{say}\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This is the distance from point P(x₁,y₁) to point Q₂(x₂,y₂) in the Cartesian coordinate plane."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Note"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The distance from the origin O(0,0) to point P(x₁,y₁) is obtained by inserting x₂ = y₂ = 0 in result (1):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=|OP|=\\sqrt{x_1^2+y_1^2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The distance from the origin O(0,0) to point Q(x₂,y₂) is obtained by inserting x₁ = y₁ = 0 in result (1):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=|OQ|=\\sqrt{x_2^2+y_2^2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If the line segment PQ is horizontal, then the distance from the point P(x₁,y₁) to point Q(x₂,y₂) is obtained by inserting y₁ = y₂ in result (1):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=|PQ|=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If the line segment PQ is vertical, then the distance from the point P(x₁,y₁) to point Q(x₂,y₂) is obtained by inserting x₁ = x₂ in result (1):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=|PQ|=\\sqrt{(y_2-y_1)^2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 1"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the distance between the two points P(3,−2) and Q(−1,−5)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "P(x₁,y₁) = (3,−2), Q(x₂,y₂) = (−1,−5) is used to obtain the distance d in between the two points P and Q:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}=\\sqrt{(-1-3)^2+[(-5)-(-2)]^2}=\\sqrt{(-4)^2+(-3)^2}=\\sqrt{25}=5\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.1.2 Co-ordinates of a point that divides the line segment in given ratio (internally and externally)"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Take P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂) are the initial and terminal points of a line segment PQ and R(x,y) is a point that divides PQ in the ratio m₁:m₂. If r₁, r₂ and r are the position vectors of P, Q and R, then"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(r_1=(x_1,y_1)=x_1î+y_1ĵ,\\quad r_2=(x_2,y_2)=x_2î+y_2ĵ,\\quad r=(x,y)=xî+yĵ\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\mathrm{If}\\ \\frac{PR}{RQ}=\\frac{m_1}{m_2},\\ \\mathrm{then}\\ PR=\\frac{m_1}{m_1+m_2}PQ=\\frac{m_1}{m_1+m_2}(OQ-OP)=\\frac{m_1}{m_1+m_2}(r_2-r_1)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(OP+PQ=OQ\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(OP+PR=r_1+\\frac{m_1}{m_1+m_2}(r_2-r_1),\\quad OP+PR=OR\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "then the position vector of OR is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(OR=OP+PR=r_1+\\frac{m_1}{m_1+m_2}(r_2-r_1)=\\frac{r_1m_1+r_1m_2+r_2m_1-r_1m_1}{m_1+m_2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(r=\\frac{m_2r_1+m_1r_2}{m_1+m_2},\\quad OR=r\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.3", "diagram": {"type": "source-line-vector-geometry", "kind": "division", "title": "Figure 7.3 — Internal division of PQ by R in the ratio m₁:m₂"}}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((x,y)=\\frac{m_2(x_1,y_1)+m_1(x_2,y_2)}{m_1+m_2},\\quad\\mathrm{components\\ form}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Equating x and y components to obtain the coordinates of R(x,y)"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((x,y)=\\left(\\frac{m_1x_2+m_2x_1}{m_1+m_2},\\frac{m_1y_2+m_2y_1}{m_1+m_2}\\right)\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "that divides the line segment PQ in the ratio m₁:m₂."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The source distance sentence labels the endpoint Q₂ once; it is the same endpoint Q. The horizontal and vertical distances simplify to |x₂−x₁| and |y₂−y₁|, preserving nonnegative distance. This derivation is internal division with m₁,m₂ > 0; the denominator m₁+m₂ must be nonzero. External division follows on the next source page."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source distance and section-formula proof; coordinate figures reconstructed natively", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 78, "side": "right", "reason": "All proof lines, notes and labels visually inspected"}}, {"file": "079_page_1.jpg", "page": 181, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If R is the midpoint of the line segment PQ, then, m₁ = m₂ and the coordinates of the midpoint R of the line segment PQ are:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((x,y)=\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2}2,\\frac{y_1+y_2}2\\right)\\)", "number": "A"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The coordinates of the point that divides the line segment PQ joining two points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂) externally in the ratio m₁:m₂ (m₁ or m₂ is negative) are:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((x,y)=\\left(\\frac{m_1x_2-m_2x_1}{m_1-m_2},\\frac{m_1y_2-m_2y_1}{m_1-m_2}\\right)\\)", "number": "B"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the coordinates of the point which divides the line segment PQ joining the two points (a) P(1,2) and Q(3,4) in the ratio 5:7. (b) P(3,4) and Q(−6,2) in the ratio 3:−2."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "a. If R(x,y) is a point that divides the line segment PQ in the ratio 5:7, then, the coordinates of R(x,y) is obtained through result (B):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((x,y)=\\left(\\frac{m_1x_2+m_2x_1}{m_1+m_2},\\frac{m_1y_2+m_2y_1}{m_1+m_2}\\right),\\quad m_1=5,m_2=7,P(1,2),Q(3,4)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\left(\\frac{5(3)+7(1)}{5+7},\\frac{5(4)+7(2)}{5+7}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{11}6,\\frac{17}6\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "b. If R(x,y) is a point that divides the line segment PQ in the ratio 3:−2, then the coordinates of R(x,y) is obtained through result (B):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((x,y)=\\left(\\frac{m_1x_2+m_2x_1}{m_1+m_2},\\frac{m_1y_2+m_2y_1}{m_1+m_2}\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\left(\\frac{3(-6)+(-2)(3)}{3-2},\\frac{3(2)+(-2)(4)}{3-2}\\right)=(-24,-2),\\quad m_1=3,m_2=-2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The box mixes two ratio conventions. With positive external lengths p:q, use (pQ−qP)/(p−q), p ≠ q. With signed weights m₁:m₂, use (m₁Q+m₂P)/(m₁+m₂), m₁+m₂ ≠ 0. Example 2 correctly uses the latter with 3:−2; its reference to (B) in the internal part should refer to the internal formula. Do not insert a negative weight into the external-minus formula a second time."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.1.3 The medians and angle bisectors of a triangle are concurrent"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "i. The medians of a triangle are concurrent"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Proof: If A(x₁,y₁), B(x₂,y₂) and C(x₃,y₃) are the vertices of a triangle ABC and P, Q and R are the midpoints of the sides AB, BC and CA, then, the coordinates of the midpoint Q through mid point formula."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(Q\\left(\\frac{x_2+x_3}2,\\frac{y_2+y_3}2\\right),\\quad m_1=m_2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.4", "diagram": {"type": "source-triangle-concurrency", "kind": "medians", "title": "Figure 7.4 — Three medians meeting at G"}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If G(x,y) is the centroid (in centre) of the triangle ABC, then, the coordinates of the point G that divides the median AQ in the ratio m₁:m₂ = 2:1 are:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(G(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{2((x_2+x_3)/2)+x_1}{2+1},\\frac{2((y_2+y_3)/2)+y_1}{2+1}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}3,\\frac{y_1+y_2+y_3}3\\right)\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Similarly, the coordinates of the point G(x,y) that divides the medians BR and CP each in the ratio 2:1 are respectively:"}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete section examples and concurrency proofs; native median/bisector figures", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 79, "side": "left", "reason": "Full source equations, ratio signs and centroid/incenter wording inspected"}}, {"file": "079_page_2.jpg", "page": 182, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(G(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{2((x_1+x_3)/2)+x_2}{2+1},\\frac{2((y_1+y_3)/2)+y_2}{2+1}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}3,\\frac{y_1+y_2+y_3}3\\right)\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(G(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{2((x_1+x_2)/2)+x_3}{2+1},\\frac{2((y_1+y_2)/2)+y_3}{2+1}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}3,\\frac{y_1+y_2+y_3}3\\right)\\)", "number": "iii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Therefore, the point G(x,y) lies on each median and consequently the medians of the triangle ABC are concurrent."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 3"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the centroid of the triangle ABC, whose vertices are A(3,−5), B(−7,4) and C(10,−2)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let A(3,−5), B(−7,4) and C(10,−2) are the vertices of the triangle ABC, If G(x,y) is the centroid of the triangle ABC; then, the coordinates of the point G(x,y) are:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(G(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}3,\\frac{y_1+y_2+y_3}3\\right)=\\left(\\frac{3-7+10}3,\\frac{-5+4-2}3\\right)=(2,-1)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "ii. The bisectors of a triangle are concurrent"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Proof: If ABC is a triangle with vertices A(x₁,y₁), B(x₂,y₂) and C(x₃,y₃), whose lengths are |AB| = c, |BC| = a and |CA| = b, then, the position vectors of A, B and C are respectively:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(r_1=(x_1,y_1)=x_1î+y_1ĵ,\\quad r_2=(x_2,y_2)=x_2î+y_2ĵ,\\quad r_3=(x_3,y_3)=x_3î+y_3ĵ\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Consider AD, BE and CF are the internal bisectors of the angles A, B and C that meet at centroid G. This is shown in Figure 7.5."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.5", "diagram": {"type": "source-triangle-concurrency", "kind": "bisectors", "title": "Figure 7.5 — Internal angle bisectors meeting at G"}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If AD is the internal bisector of angle A, then:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{BD}{DC}=\\frac{BA}{AC}\\ \\mathrm{or}\\ \\frac{BD}{DC}=\\frac cb\\ \\Rightarrow\\ BD:DC=c:b\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This means that D divides BC internally in the ratio c:b and the position vector of D is therefore:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{cr_3+br_2}{c+b}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\mathrm{Again},\\ \\frac{BD}c=\\frac{DC}b=\\frac{BD+DC}{c+b}=\\frac a{c+b}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ BD=\\frac{ac}{c+b}\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If BG is the internal bisector of the angle B, then,"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{DG}{AG}=\\frac{BD}{AB}=\\frac{ac/(b+c)}c=\\frac a{b+c}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ DG:GA=a:(b+c)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The position vector of G(x,y) is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(r=\\frac{ar_1+(b+c)((cr_3+br_2)/(b+c))}{a+b+c}=\\frac{ar_1+br_2+cr_3}{a+b+c}=\\frac{a(x_1,y_1)+b(x_2,y_2)+c(x_3,y_3)}{a+b+c}\\)", "number": "iii"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The medians meet at the centroid, whereas the internal angle bisectors meet at the incenter. The source calls both G and incorrectly uses “centroid (in centre)” or “centroid” in these passages; they are generally different points. For a nondegenerate triangle all side lengths a,b,c are positive. In triangle ABD the angle-bisector theorem yields AG:GD = AB:BD, equivalent to the displayed DG:AG; the common point is the incenter. Its coordinate formula continues on page 183."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete section examples and concurrency proofs; native median/bisector figures", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 79, "side": "right", "reason": "Full source equations, ratio signs and centroid/incenter wording inspected"}}, {"file": "080_page_1.jpg", "page": 183, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "The coordinates of the centroid G(x,y) is obtained from equation (iii) by equating the x and y components:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(G(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{ax_1+bx_2+cx_3}{a+b+c},\\frac{ay_1+by_2+cy_3}{a+b+c}\\right)\\)", "number": "iv"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Similarly, the internal bisector of the angle C also passes through the point G(x,y). Thus, the angle bisectors of a triangle ABC are concurrent and G(x,y) is the point of concurrency."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The weighted point in this formula is the incenter, rather than the centroid named in the source."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 4"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Muhammad Ayaan has a triangular piece of backyard where he wants to build a swimming pool. How can he find the largest circular pool that can be built there?"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The largest possible circular pool would have the same size as the largest circle that can be inscribed in the triangular backyard. The largest circle that can be inscribed in a triangle is incircle. This can be determined by finding the point of concurrency of the angle bisectors of each corner of the backyard and then making a circle with this point as center and the shortest distance from this point to the boundary as radius."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 5"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the length JO."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.6", "diagram": {"type": "source-incenter-length", "title": "Figure 7.6 — Triangle LMN, its incenter O and perpendicular feet I, J and H; LO = 13, LH = 12, ON = 8"}}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Here, O is the point of concurrency of the three angle bisectors of ΔLMN and therefore is the incenter. The incenter is equidistant from the sides of the triangle. That is, JO = HO = IO."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "We have the measures of two sides of the right triangle ΔHOL, so it is possible to find the length of the third side. Use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the length HO."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(HO=\\sqrt{(LO)^2-(HL)^2}=\\sqrt{13^2-12^2}=\\sqrt{169-144}=\\sqrt{25}=5\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Since JO = HO, the length JO also equals 5 units."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Exercise 7.1"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "1. The three points are A(−1,3), B(2,1) and C(5,−1). Show that |AB|+|BC| = |AC|."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "2. In each case, find the midpoint of the line segment PQ joining the two points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂): (a) P(10,20), Q(−12,−8); (b) P(a,−b), Q(−a,b); (c) P(1/2,−1/4), Q(3/5,4/7)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "3. In each case, find the coordinates of the point R(x,y) which divides the line segment PQ joining the two points: (a) P(1,2), Q(3,4) in the ratio 5:7; (b) P(3,4), Q(−6,2) in the ratio 3:−2; (c) P(−6,7), Q(5,−4) in the ratio 2/7:1."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "4. In each case, in what ratio is the line segment PQ (joining the two points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂)) divided by the point R(x,y): (a) P(8,10), Q(−12,6), R(−4/7,58/7); (b) P(−2,4), Q(3,6), R(4/5,3/5)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "5. Find the centroid of the triangle ABC, whose vertices are the following: (a) A(4,−2), B(−2,4), C(5,5); (b) A(3,5), B(4,6), C(3,−1); (c) A(1,1), B(−2,−2), C(4,5)."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Question 4(b) gives a point outside line PQ: its x and y coordinates yield incompatible division parameters. Its printed question is preserved and the absence of a ratio is explained in the learning solution."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete proof continuation, Examples 4/5 and all five exercise groups; native incenter figure", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 80, "side": "left", "reason": "All source questions enlarged; fractional ratio 2/7:1 and non-collinear R(4/5,3/5) confirmed"}}, {"file": "080_page_2.jpg", "page": 184, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "7.2 Slope of a Straight Line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The slope of a line is a measure of the when “steepness” of the line, and whether it rises, or falls when moving from left to right. The line from A to B rises up, while the line from C to D goes down are depicted in the Figure 7.6:"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.6", "diagram": {"type": "source-line-slope", "kind": "steepness", "title": "Figure 7.6 — Lines rising and falling from left to right"}}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.2.1 Slope of a line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The graph of a line can be drawn knowing only one point on the line if the “steepness” of the line is known, too."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "“A number that measure the ‘steepness’ of a line is called slope of a line.”"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.7", "diagram": {"type": "source-line-slope", "kind": "rise-run", "title": "Figure 7.7 — Signed rise and positive horizontal run"}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If we move off the line horizontally to the right or move up or down (vertically) to return to the line, then the slope of the line is the “steepness” defined as the ratio of the vertical rise to the horizontal run:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\mathrm{slope}=\\frac{\\mathrm{rise}}{\\mathrm{run}}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "the run is always a movement to the right"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.2.2 Formula to find the slope of a line passing through two points"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Mathematically, if any two points on a line are available, then their join makes a constant angle with a fixed direction and the angle so formed is independent of the choice of the two points on the line. This is a precise way of saying that any line has a constant slope. It is customary to measure the angle θ which a line makes with the positive direction of the x-axis. The quantity tan θ is defined to be the slope of the line and is denoted by m. The slope of a line is also referred to gradient of the line."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For illustration, if A(x₁,y₁) and B(x₂,y₂), where x₁ ≠ x₂, are any two points, then their join develops a line L that makes a constant angle θ with the x-axis. Draw AM, and BN parallel to y-axis and AL parallel to x-axis."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.8", "diagram": {"type": "source-line-slope", "kind": "two-points", "title": "Figure 7.8 — Slope between A(x₁,y₁) and B(x₂,y₂)"}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The slope m of a line L through the two points A(x₁,y₁) and B(x₂,y₂), is therefore:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m=\\tan\\theta=\\frac{LB}{AL}=\\frac{NB-NL}{MN}=\\frac{NB-AM}{ON-OM}=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 6"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the slope m of the line L through the points (a) E(2,4) and F(4,6). (b) M(3,1) and N(−1,3)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "a. The given two points E(2,4) and F(4,6) form a line L, whose slope is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\\frac{6-4}{4-2}=\\frac22=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "b. The given two points M(3,1) and N(−1,3) form a line L, whose slope is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\\frac{3-1}{-1-3}=\\frac2{-4}=-\\frac12\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The standard equation of a line is y = mx+c where m is a slope."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Rise is signed: moving down gives a negative rise and a negative slope. The finite slope and y = mx+c form exclude vertical lines, whose equation is x = constant. The source repeats Figure 7.6 after using that number for the preceding incenter figure; both source captions are preserved."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source slope derivation and parallel/perpendicular proof; five native source figures", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 80, "side": "right", "reason": "All source text, proof fractions and erroneous ordinate/−b labels inspected"}}, {"file": "081_page_1.jpg", "page": 185, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "7.2.3 Condition for two straight lines with given slopes are"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Parallel to each other (b) Perpendicular to each other"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "a. Parallel to each other"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If L₁ and L₂ are the two lines having slopes m₁ and m₂, then the lines L₁ and L₂ are parallel if they make the same angle with the x-axis, that means they have the same slope. Conversely, if two lines L₁ and L₂ have the same slope, then they will make the same angle with the x-axis and the lines L₁ and L₂ are therefore parallel for which:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_1=m_2\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "It is important to note that the lines parallel to x-axis have zero slopes whereas the lines parallel to y-axis have the slope ∞."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "b. Perpendicular to each other"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If L₁ and L₂ are the two perpendicular lines make the angles α and β with the x-axis, then the slopes of the lines L₁ and L₂ are respectively m₁ = tan α and m₂ = tan β. From the Figure 7.9, it is clear that"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.9", "diagram": {"type": "source-line-slope", "kind": "perpendicular", "title": "Figure 7.9 — Two perpendicular lines with inclination angles α and β"}}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac\\pi2=\\beta-\\alpha\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\beta=\\frac\\pi2+\\alpha\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\beta=\\tan(\\frac\\pi2+\\alpha),\\quad\\mathrm{take\\ tan\\ of\\ both\\ sides}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=-\\cot\\alpha=-\\frac1{\\tan\\alpha}\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The given lines L₁ and L₂ are found perpendicular, since the product of their slopes equals −1:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_1m_2=\\tan\\alpha\\tan\\beta=\\tan\\alpha\\left(-\\frac1{\\tan\\alpha}\\right)=-1\\)", "number": "iii"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Equal finite slopes give the same direction; two distinct lines are parallel, and coincident lines also share a slope. A vertical line has undefined finite slope; ∞ is the source’s shorthand. The product criterion m₁m₂ = −1 applies when both slopes are finite. A horizontal line is perpendicular to a vertical line, which needs this separate case."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.3 Equation of a Straight Line Parallel to Co-Ordinate Axes"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.3.1 Equation of a straight line parallel to"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "○ y-axis and at a distance ‘a’ from it. ○ x-axis and at a distance ‘b’ from it."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "i. y-axis and at a distance ‘a’ from it"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let PQ be a straight line parallel to y-axis at a distance ‘a’ units from it see Figure 7.10. This is very clear that all the points on the line PQ have the same ordinate say ‘b’. Therefore, PQ can be considered as the locus of a point at a distance ‘a’ from y-axis and all points on the PQ satisfy the condition x = a. Therefore, the equation of a straight line is parallel to y-axis at a distance ‘a’ from it. e.g. x = a."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.10", "diagram": {"type": "source-line-slope", "kind": "vertical", "title": "Figure 7.10 — Line PQ parallel to the y-axis, at horizontal distance a"}}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "i. If a = 0, then the straight line coincides with the y-axis and its equation becomes x = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "ii. If PQ is parallel and to the left of y-axis at a distance ‘a’, then, its equation is x = −b."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "For the vertical line the fixed coordinate is its abscissa x = a, not the “ordinate b” printed in the paragraph. At distance a > 0 to the left of the y-axis, its equation is x = −a; the source’s final −b is a variable mismatch. Distance a alone allows two sides, x = ±a; Figure 7.10 depicts the right side."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source slope derivation and parallel/perpendicular proof; five native source figures", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 81, "side": "left", "reason": "All source text, proof fractions and erroneous ordinate/−b labels inspected"}}, {"file": "081_page_2.jpg", "page": 186, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Example 7"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the equation of straight line parallel to y-axis at a distance 5 units on the right side of y-axis."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\mathrm{Since},\\quad x=a\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "As, the distance is 5 units to right side of y-axis, so, equation (i) becomes x = 5."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "ii. x-axis and at a distance ‘b’ from it"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let PQ be a straight line parallel to x-axis at a distance ‘b’ units from it see Figure 7.11. This is very clear that all the points on the same ordinate say, ‘b’. Therefore, PQ can be considered as the locus of a point at a distance ‘b’ from x-axis and all points on the PQ satisfy the condition y = b. Therefore, the equation of a straight line is parallel to x-axis at a distance b from it e.g. y = b."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.11", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.11 — horizontal", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[35, 95], [430, 95]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#be123c", "dash": false}, {"points": [[200, 95], [200, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 35, "y": 80, "text": "P"}, {"x": 435, "y": 95, "text": "Q"}, {"x": 210, "y": 175, "text": "b"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "i. If b = 0, then the straight line coincides with the x-axis and its equation becomes y = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "ii. If PQ is parallel and below the x-axis at a distance ‘b’, then its equation is y = −b."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.4 Standard Form of Equation of a Straight Line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Because of their simplicity, linear equation (line) is used in many applications to describe relationships between two variables. We shall see some of these applications in this unit. First, we need to develop some standard forms that are related to linear equations."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "(i) Intercepts of a straight line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "“If a straight line AB intersects x-axis at C and y-axis at D, then OC is called the x-intercept of AB on the x-axis and OD is called the y-intercept of AB on the y-axis."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.12", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.12 — intercepts", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[50, 65], [385, 270]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 83], [80, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 240], [337, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 35, "y": 60, "text": "A"}, {"x": 388, "y": 277, "text": "B"}, {"x": 325, "y": 265, "text": "C"}, {"x": 58, "y": 93, "text": "D"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 8"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the x and y intercepts of a line 2x+4y+6 = 0."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The x-intercept of a line is obtained by putting y = 0 in a line:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(2x+4y+6=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(2x+4(0)=-6\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 2x=-6\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x=-3\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The y-intercept of a line is obtained by putting x = 0 in a line:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(2x+4y+6=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(2(0)+4y=-6\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 4y=-6\\ \\Rightarrow\\ y=-\\frac32\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The general criteria are that a line in two dimensional space can be determined by specifying its slope and just one point."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "(ii) Slope-Intercept Form"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let L be the line see Figure 7.13 develops the y-intercept c on the y-axis. The line L also makes an angle θ with the positive direction of the x-axis that develops a slope m = tan θ. Let P(x,y) be any point on the line L. Draw PM parallel to y-axis and CN parallel to x-axis that give"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.13", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.13 — slope intercept", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[55, 223], [365, 60]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 80], [325, 80], [325, 240], [325, 210], [80, 210]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 210], [325, 80]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 63, "y": 207, "text": "C"}, {"x": 90, "y": 227, "text": "c"}, {"x": 328, "y": 72, "text": "P"}, {"x": 333, "y": 240, "text": "M"}, {"x": 330, "y": 212, "text": "N"}, {"x": 66, "y": 83, "text": "y"}, {"x": 208, "y": 263, "text": "x"}, {"x": 106, "y": 202, "text": "θ"}, {"x": 374, "y": 69, "text": "L"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(CN=OM=x\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(NP=MP-MN=MP-OC=y-c\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "In ΔPCN, the angle is ∠PNC = 90° and the slope of the line L is giving the slope-intercept form of the line L:"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Intercepts are signed coordinates: Example 8 crosses at (−3,0) and (0,−3/2), so its negative signs must be retained. Figure 7.12 illustrates a general line with positive intercepts, rather than that particular example. Slope plus one point specifies a nonvertical line; a vertical line requires its fixed x-coordinate."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete horizontal-line and standard-form source, Examples 7–11, five native diagrams", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 81, "side": "right", "reason": "Full source words/equations and Example 9 negative slope confirmed with enlarged detail"}}, {"file": "082_page_1.jpg", "page": 187, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{NP}{CN}=\\tan\\theta\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\frac{y-c}x=\\tan\\theta\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ y-c=x\\tan\\theta\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ y=x\\tan\\theta+c=mx+c\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If the straight line L passes through the origin (0,0), then c = 0 and the equation of line L becomes y = mx. In y = mx+c, m denotes the slope and c denotes the y-intercept of the line L on the axis of y."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 9"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Determine the slopes of the following lines: (a) x−y = 5. (b) 2x+3y = 6."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "a. For the slope, solve the given line for y to obtain:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x-y=5\\ \\Rightarrow\\ -y=-x+5\\ \\Rightarrow\\ y=x-5\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Thus, the slope of the line is the coefficient of x-term which is m = 1."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "b. For the slope of the line, solve the given line for y to obtain:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(2x+3y=6\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 3y=-2x+6\\ \\Rightarrow\\ y=-\\frac23x+2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Thus, the slope of the line is the coefficient of x-term which is m = −2/3."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 10"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find an equation of the line with slope 4, when the y-intercept is 6."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (i) is used for the assumptions m = 4, c = 6 to obtain the required slope-intercept form of a line:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y=4x+6\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "ii. Point-Slope Form"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If L is a line see Figure 7.14 passing through the point A(x₁,y₁) and P(x,y) is any point on a line L, then, the slope of the line L is giving the point-slope form of a line L:"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.14", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.14 — point slope", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[35, 270], [385, 50]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[180, 179], [180, 240], [330, 240], [330, 85], [180, 85]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[180, 179], [330, 179]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 158, "y": 174, "text": "A"}, {"x": 330, "y": 72, "text": "P"}, {"x": 172, "y": 263, "text": "x₁"}, {"x": 321, "y": 263, "text": "x"}, {"x": 190, "y": 206, "text": "y₁"}, {"x": 340, "y": 179, "text": "y"}, {"x": 390, "y": 48, "text": "L"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m=\\frac{y-y_1}{x-x_1}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 11"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find an equation of a line with slope 4 and passes through the point (2,4)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (ii) is used for the assumptions m = 4, A(x₁,y₁) = A(2,4), to obtain the required point-slope form of a line:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-4=4(x-2)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(-4x+y-4+8=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ -4x+y+4=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 4x-y-4=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "iii. Two-Point Form"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If L is a line, see Figure 7.15 passing through the two points A(x₁,y₁) and B(x₂,y₂), then the slope of the line L is:"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.15", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.15 — two point", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[35, 270], [385, 50]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[180, 179], [180, 240], [330, 240], [330, 85]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[180, 179], [330, 179]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 158, "y": 174, "text": "A"}, {"x": 310, "y": 76, "text": "B"}, {"x": 172, "y": 263, "text": "x₁"}, {"x": 321, "y": 263, "text": "x₂"}, {"x": 190, "y": 206, "text": "y₁"}, {"x": 340, "y": 179, "text": "y₂"}, {"x": 390, "y": 48, "text": "L"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\\)", "number": "iii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If the equation of a line L through the A(x₁,y₁) with slope m is"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\\)", "number": "iv"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "then the equation of a line L through the two points A(x₁,y₁) and B(x₂,y₂) is the equation of the two-point form of a line L:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-y_1=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}(x-x_1)\\)", "number": "v"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The quotient derivations initially require nonzero horizontal differences. After rearrangement, the point-slope equation also includes the given point itself. For distinct points with x₂ = x₁, use the vertical equation x = x₁. A division-free two-point equation valid for any two distinct points is (x₂−x₁)(y−y₁) = (y₂−y₁)(x−x₁)."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete horizontal-line and standard-form source, Examples 7–11, five native diagrams", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 82, "side": "left", "reason": "Full source words/equations and Example 9 negative slope confirmed with enlarged detail"}}, {"file": "082_page_2.jpg", "page": 188, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Example 12"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find an equation of a line that passes through the two points P(−1,−2) and Q(−5,0)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (v) is used for the assumptions P(x₁,y₁) = P(−1,−2), Q(x₂,y₂) = Q(−5,0) to obtain the required two-point form of a line:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-(-2)=\\frac{0-(-2)}{-5-(-1)}[x-(-1)]\\ \\Rightarrow\\ y+2=\\frac2{-4}(x+1)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ -4y-8=2x+2\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 2x+4y+10=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x+2y+5=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "iv. Double-Intercepts Form"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If a line L intersects the x-axis and y-axis at points A and B, then OA = a and OB = b are the x and y intercepts of the line L."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let P(x,y) be any point on the line L, Draw PM parallel to y-axis and PN parallel to x-axis. From the Figure 7.16, the comparison of similar triangles ΔBNP and ΔPMA is giving the equation of double-intercept form of a line L:"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.16", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.16 — intercept form", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[55, 70], [410, 285]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 86], [80, 240], [334, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 188], [250, 188], [250, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[92, 188], [92, 200], [80, 200]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 80, "y": 72, "text": "B"}, {"x": 327, "y": 264, "text": "A"}, {"x": 255, "y": 180, "text": "P"}, {"x": 255, "y": 232, "text": "M"}, {"x": 58, "y": 188, "text": "N"}, {"x": 53, "y": 180, "text": "b"}, {"x": 175, "y": 267, "text": "a"}, {"x": 37, "y": 55, "text": "L"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{NB}{MP}=\\frac{NP}{MA}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{OB-ON}{ON}=\\frac{OM}{OA-OM}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\frac{b-y}y=\\frac x{a-x}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ bx+ay=ab\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{bx}{ab}+\\frac{ay}{ab}=1\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\frac xa+\\frac yb=1\\)", "number": "vi"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 13"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the equation of a line whose x and y intercepts are (3,0) and (0,4) respectively."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (vi) is used for the assumptions a = 3, b = 4 to obtain the required line:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac x3+\\frac y4=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{4x+3y}{12}=1\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 4x+3y=12\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 4x+3y-12=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "v. Symmetric Form"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let a line L through point A(x₁,y₁) makes an angle θ with the positive direction of the x-axis."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If P(x,y) is any point on the line L, then AP = r. If we allow r to vary with any positive or negative values, then P will take any position on the line L. Conversely, if P is given to be any point on the line L, then the unique value of r can be found which in fact is distance of P from A. Thus, it follows that r serves as a parameter of point P."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "To find the coordinates of a point P in terms of the parameter r, let us draw AL and PM parallel to y-axis and AN parallel to x-axis, that give with the following assumptions"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.17", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.17 — symmetric form", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[35, 268], [385, 48]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[180, 177], [180, 240], [330, 240], [330, 82], [180, 82]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[180, 177], [330, 177]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 156, "y": 174, "text": "A"}, {"x": 330, "y": 72, "text": "P"}, {"x": 170, "y": 262, "text": "L"}, {"x": 324, "y": 262, "text": "M"}, {"x": 333, "y": 178, "text": "N"}, {"x": 251, "y": 125, "text": "r"}, {"x": 195, "y": 170, "text": "θ"}, {"x": 105, "y": 229, "text": "θ"}, {"x": 386, "y": 44, "text": "L"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(OM=OL+LM=OL+AN\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(MP=MN+NP=LA+NP\\)", "number": "vii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "develops the parametric equations of a line L through the point A(x₁,y₁) at an angle θ:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(OM=OL+LM=OL+AN,\\quad MP=MN+NP=LA+NP\\)", "number": "viii"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x=x_1+r\\cos\\theta,\\quad\\cos\\theta=\\frac{AN}r\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y=y_1+r\\sin\\theta,\\quad\\sin\\theta=\\frac{NP}r\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The first similar-triangle fraction line prints (OB−ON)/ON, whereas the intended denominator MP = y gives (OB−ON)/MP. The next displayed (b−y)/y and the resulting intercept equation are correct. The divided intercept form requires a,b ≠ 0. The parameter r is a signed distance along a chosen unit direction, rather than an always nonnegative distance; |r| is the ordinary distance from A to P."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete intercept, parametric, symmetric and normal-form source with four native figures", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 82, "side": "right", "reason": "Complete equations, captions and all Examples 12–15 visually inspected"}}, {"file": "083_page_1.jpg", "page": 189, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "The parametric equations automatically give the symmetric form of a line L after simplification:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{x-x_1}{\\cos\\theta}=r,\\quad\\frac{y-y_1}{\\sin\\theta}=r\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\frac{x-x_1}{\\cos\\theta}=\\frac{y-y_1}{\\sin\\theta}=r\\)", "number": "ix"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 14"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the equation of a straight line with inclination 45° and passing through the point (2,√2)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Here we have inclination α = 45° and point (x₁,y₁) = (2,√2). The equation of line in its symmetric form is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{x-x_1}{\\cos\\alpha}=\\frac{y-y_1}{\\sin\\alpha}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Substitute the above values in the formula to get the equation of a straight line."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{x-2}{\\cos45^\\circ}=\\frac{y-\\sqrt2}{\\sin45^\\circ}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ \\sin45^\\circ(x-2)=\\cos45^\\circ(y-\\sqrt2)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(x-2)=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(y-\\sqrt2)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ x-y-2+\\sqrt2=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "vi. Normal Form"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The normal form of a line is the equation of a line in terms of the length of the perpendicular on it from the origin and that perpendicular makes an angle with the x-axis."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.18(a)", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.18(a) — A normal perpendicular to a line", "width": 400, "height": 145, "paths": [{"points": [[70, 110], [280, 110]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[175, 110], [175, 35]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[175, 70], [237, 70]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[175, 96], [190, 96], [190, 110]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 240, "y": 76, "text": "Normal"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If a line L intersects the x-axis and y-axis at points A and B, then OA and OB are the x and y intercepts of the line L. Draw ON perpendicular to line L that provides the perpendicular distance p from the origin on the line L which is denoted by ON = p. If ON makes an angle θ with the positive direction of the x-axis, then the x and y-intercepts of the line L are respectively:"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.18(b)", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.18(b) — normal form", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[45, 55], [405, 295]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 240], [154.615, 128.077]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[164.599, 134.733], [157.943, 144.717], [147.959, 138.061]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 39, "y": 49, "text": "L"}, {"x": 326, "y": 266, "text": "A"}, {"x": 58, "y": 87, "text": "B"}, {"x": 160, "y": 120, "text": "N"}, {"x": 127, "y": 172, "text": "p"}, {"x": 119, "y": 230, "text": "θ"}, {"x": 68, "y": 132, "text": "θ"}, {"x": 213, "y": 265, "text": "m"}, {"x": 64, "y": 179, "text": "n"}, {"x": 102, "y": 193, "text": "P"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\cos\\theta=\\frac p{OA}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ OA=p\\sec\\theta\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\sin\\theta=\\frac p{OB}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ OB=p\\mathrm{cosec}\\theta\\)", "number": "x"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If OA and OB are the x and y-intercepts of a line L, then through result (x), the equation of a normal line L in terms of perpendicular distance p and angle θ is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac x{OA}+\\frac y{OB}=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac x{p\\sec\\theta}+\\frac y{p\\mathrm{cosec}\\theta}=1\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x\\cos\\theta+y\\sin\\theta=p\\)", "number": "xi"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The normal form of a line is also referred to perpendicular form of a line."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 15"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the corresponding equation of a line, if the length of the perpendicular distance from the origin on a line is 3 units that makes an angle of 120°."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (xi) is used for the assumptions p = 3, θ = 120° to obtain the required equation of a line:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x\\cos120^\\circ+y\\sin120^\\circ=3\\ \\Rightarrow\\ -\\frac12x+\\frac{\\sqrt3}2y=3\\ \\Rightarrow\\ -x+\\sqrt3y=6\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x-\\sqrt3y+6=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The symmetric quotients require both sin θ and cos θ to be nonzero. The parametric form x = x₁+r cos θ, y = y₁+r sin θ handles horizontal and vertical lines without division. The normal is a unit vector (cos θ,sin θ), and p ≥ 0 is the distance from the origin. The intercept derivation divides by p, sin θ and cos θ, but the final normal equation remains valid when p = 0 or a coordinate direction is zero; derive those cases directly with the normal dot product."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete intercept, parametric, symmetric and normal-form source with four native figures", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 83, "side": "left", "reason": "Complete equations, captions and all Examples 12–15 visually inspected"}}, {"file": "099_page_2.jpg", "page": 190, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "7.4.2 A linear equation in two variables is a straight line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A first degree polynomial p₁(x) = a₁x+a₀ is rearranged to obtain an equation of the form"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(-a_1x+p_1(x)-a_0=0,\\quad\\mathrm{with}\\ -a_1=a,p_1(x)=y,-a_0=b\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+by+c=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "is then called the general equation of the straight line. Here a, b and c are constants while x and y are variables."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The first degree polynomial p₁(x) is also called the linear algebraic equation and is denoted by p₁(x) = f(x)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Consider, If P(x₁,y₁), Q(x₂,y₂) and R(x₃,y₃) are the three points on the locus represented by the straight line"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+by+c=0\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "then, P(x₁,y₁) on the line (i) gives:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax_1+by_1+c=0\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Q(x₂,y₂) on the line (i) gives:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax_2+by_2+c=0\\)", "number": "iii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "R(x₃,y₃) on the line (i) gives:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax_3+by_3+c=0\\)", "number": "iv"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The three lines from equation (ii) to equation (iv) develops a homogeneous system of three linear equations in the unknowns a, b and c:"}, {"type": "matrixProduct", "matrices": [[["\\(x_1\\)", "\\(y_1\\)", "1"], ["\\(x_2\\)", "\\(y_2\\)", "1"], ["\\(x_3\\)", "\\(y_3\\)", "1"]], [["a"], ["b"], ["c"]], [["0"], ["0"], ["0"]]], "operators": ["", " = "], "suffix": "⇒ Ax = 0 (v)", "caption": "Source homogeneous three-by-three system"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The homogeneous system of linear equations (v) defines a nontrivial solution only if the determinant of coefficient matrix A of the system (v) is zero:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(det(A)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(x_1\\)", "\\(y_1\\)", "1"], ["\\(x_2\\)", "\\(y_2\\)", "1"], ["\\(x_3\\)", "\\(y_3\\)", "1"]], "suffix": "= 0 (vi)", "caption": "Coordinate determinant for three collinear points"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x_1(y_2-y_3)-y_1(x_2-x_3)+(x_2y_3-x_3y_2)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Equation (vi) is rearranged to obtain:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x_1(y_2-y_3)+x_2(y_3-y_1)+x_3(y_1-y_2)=0\\)", "number": "vii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Multiply both sides of equation (vii) by 1/2 to obtain the area of the triangle formed by P, Q and R that equals zero:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac12[x_1(y_2-y_3)+x_2(y_3-y_1)+x_3(y_1-y_2)]=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\mathrm{Area\\ of\\ the\\ triangle}\\ PQR=0\\)", "number": "viii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Since the three points P, Q and R lying on the locus (i) are collinear. Hence, the locus (i) represents a straight line."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "A genuine line requires (a,b) ≠ (0,0). Otherwise 0x+0y+c = 0 gives the whole plane when c = 0, or no points when c ≠ 0. In the opening rearrangement y = a₁x+a₀, the consistent coefficients are a = −a₁, b = 1, c = −a₀; the printed assignment −a₀ = b does not match the following general form. A polynomial is a function; an equation is an equality involving it. The coordinate determinant gives signed twice-area; geometric area is its absolute value divided by 2. Zero still proves collinearity. The source uses x in Ax = 0 for the coefficient column (a,b,c), distinct from the point coordinate x."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.4.3 General form of a straight line is reducible to other standard forms"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Any standard form of a line can also be determined from the general form of a line (i)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "i. To reduce the general form (i) to the slope intercept form of a line, we need to involve the following steps:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+by+c=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(by=-ax-c\\)", "number": null}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source general-line proof, native system/determinant and all standard-form conversion chains", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 99, "side": "right", "reason": "All source coefficients, matrix rows, signed distance and normalization wording visually inspected"}}, {"file": "100_page_1.jpg", "page": 191, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y=-\\frac abx-\\frac cb\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=mx+c_1,\\quad\\mathrm{slope}=m=-\\frac ab,\\quad y\\mathrm{-intercept}=c_1=-\\frac cb\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "ii. To reduce the general form (i) to the double-intercept form, we need to involve the following steps:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+by+c=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+by=-c\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{ax}{-c}+\\frac{by}{-c}=1,\\quad\\mathrm{dividing\\ by}\\ -c\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac x{-c/a}+\\frac y{-c/b}=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac x{a_1}+\\frac y{b_1}=1,\\quad x\\mathrm{-intercept}=a_1=-\\frac ca,\\quad y\\mathrm{-intercept}=b_1=-\\frac cb\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "iii. To reduce the general form (i) to the normal form, we need to involve the following steps:"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "From the Figure 7.18(b), the angles along the positive directions of the x and y-axis are the following:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\cos\\theta=\\frac pm,\\quad\\sin\\theta=\\frac pn\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The values of cos θ and sin θ are used in the trigonometric identity cos²θ+sin²θ = 1 to obtain:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{p^2}{m^2}+\\frac{p^2}{n^2}=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(p^2\\left(\\frac1{m^2}+\\frac1{n^2}\\right)=1\\)", "number": "ix"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(p^2\\left(\\frac{m^2+n^2}{m^2n^2}\\right)=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(p^2=\\frac{m^2n^2}{m^2+n^2}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ p=\\pm\\frac{mn}{\\sqrt{m^2+n^2}}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This p is the perpendicular distance from the origin to the line x/m+y/n = 1 (i.e. nx+my−mn = 0). Of course, the perpendicular distance from the origin to the line ax+by+c = 0 must be:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(p=\\frac{|c|}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}\\)", "number": "x"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For converting the general form (i) to normal form, divide the line ax+by+c = 0 by 1/√(a²+b²) to obtain the conversion of the general form (i) in the normal form:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{ax}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}+\\frac{by}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}+\\frac c{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}=0\\)", "number": "xi"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Slope-intercept conversion requires b ≠ 0. The displayed double-intercept divisions require a,b,c all nonzero; a line through the origin does not have this divided form. The symbols m,n in the normal proof are intercepts, not slopes. As an ordinary distance p is nonnegative, so use |mn|/√(m²+n²), not an arbitrary ± value. The last instruction says divide by 1/√(a²+b²), but its displayed result actually divides by √(a²+b²). Normalize the coefficient vector (a,b), then reverse its sign when needed so that the right-hand side p is nonnegative: for c ≠ 0 multiply the equation by −sign(c)/√(a²+b²). If c = 0 either unit normal gives p = 0. The condition (a,b) ≠ (0,0) is essential."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source general-line proof, native system/determinant and all standard-form conversion chains", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 100, "side": "left", "reason": "All source coefficients, matrix rows, signed distance and normalization wording visually inspected"}}, {"file": "100_page_2.jpg", "page": 192, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Exercise 7.2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "1. Find the equation of lines that are represented on the coordinate planes: (a), (b), (c), as shown in the source graphs."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 7.2 Question 1(a)", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Coordinate line with source intercepts a", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[65, 168], [457, 168]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[289, 270], [289, 32]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[65, 219], [373.0, 32.0]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#be123c", "dash": false}, {"points": [[149, 164], [149, 172]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[285, 83], [293, 83]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 462.59999999999997, "y": 168, "text": "x"}, {"x": 289, "y": 23.5, "text": "y"}, {"x": 139, "y": 158, "text": "-5"}, {"x": 266, "y": 88, "text": "5"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 7.2 Question 1(b)", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Coordinate line with source intercepts b", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[65, 168], [457, 168]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[289, 270], [289, 32]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[270.3333333333333, 32.0], [401.0, 270.0]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#be123c", "dash": false}, {"points": [[345, 164], [345, 172]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[285, 66], [293, 66]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 462.59999999999997, "y": 168, "text": "x"}, {"x": 289, "y": 23.5, "text": "y"}, {"x": 335, "y": 158, "text": "2"}, {"x": 266, "y": 71, "text": "6"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 7.2 Question 1(c)", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Coordinate line with source intercepts c", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[65, 168], [457, 168]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[289, 270], [289, 32]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[65, 117.0], [401.0, 270.0]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#be123c", "dash": false}, {"points": [[177, 164], [177, 172]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[285, 219], [293, 219]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 462.59999999999997, "y": 168, "text": "x"}, {"x": 289, "y": 23.5, "text": "y"}, {"x": 167, "y": 158, "text": "-4"}, {"x": 266, "y": 224, "text": "-3"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "2. In each case, find the slope, if it is defined: (a) (−5,4) and (3,6); (b) 4x+7y = 1; (c) Parallel to 2y−4x = 7; (d) Perpendicular to 6x = y−3."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "3. What are the x- and y-intercepts for each of the following lines? (a) y = 2x+6; (b) y = −3x+9; (c) y = x+2."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "4. In each case, show that the pair of lines are parallel or perpendicular or neither: (a) x−2y−6 = 0, 2x+y−5 = 0; (b) 3x+4y−8 = 0, x+3y−2 = 0; (c) 2x−y−7 = 0, 4x−2y−5 = 0; (d) 2x−5y−7 = 0, 6x−15y+5 = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "5. In each case, find the equation of a line that passes through the pair of points: (a) O(0,0) and A(2,6); (b) E(1,0) and F(2,5); (c) I(1,1) and J(3,3)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "6. In each case, find the equation of a line that passes through the point A(x₁,y₁) having slope m: (a) A(1,2), m = 4; (b) A(−1,−2), m = −1/2; (c) A(−3,5), m = −3; (d) A(7,−8), m = 5."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "7. In each case, find the equation of a line that exists the y-intercept c and slope m: (a) c = 2, m = 2; (b) c = 4, m = 8; (c) c = −4, m = 1/2."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "8. Transform the equation 7x−10y+13 = 0 into: (a) slope intercept form; (b) symmetric form; (c) normal form."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Project"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "How can you calculate the midpoint between your home and school/college? Calculate this distance and write the procedure."}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/unit7-home-school-project.jpg", "caption": "Project — home and school/college", "alt": "Original illustration of a home and school/college connected by a distance line"}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "All eight source exercise groups, three reconstructed coordinate graphs and exact source project illustration", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 100, "side": "right", "reason": "All question signs, intercept labels, points and project text visually checked"}}, {"file": "101_page_1.jpg", "page": 193, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "7.5 Distance of a point to a line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "In Euclidean geometry, the distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance from a given point to any point on an infinite straight line. It is the perpendicular distance of the point to the line, the length of the line segment which joins the point to nearest point on the line."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.5.1 Position of a point with respect to a line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "To show that the point P(x₁,y₁) is on one side or on the other side of the straight line ax+by+c = 0 according as the expression ax₁+by₁+c < 0 or ax₁+by₁+c > 0, the procedure developed is as under:"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let AB be the straight line ax+by+c = 0 and P(x₁,y₁) is a point above the line AB Figure 7.18 and P(x₁,y₁) is also a point below the line AB Figure 7.19. From P draw perpendicular PM on the x-axis that cuts the line AB at a point Q whose coordinates are Q(x₁,y₂)."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.18", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.18 — point above a line at the same x-coordinate", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[45, 85], [430, 250]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[220, 85], [220, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 160], [220, 160]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}, {"points": [[80, 85], [220, 85]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 37, "y": 76, "text": "A"}, {"x": 436, "y": 254, "text": "B"}, {"x": 225, "y": 77, "text": "P(x₁,y₁)"}, {"x": 225, "y": 153, "text": "Q(x₁,y₂)"}, {"x": 214, "y": 265, "text": "M"}, {"x": 58, "y": 90, "text": "y₁"}, {"x": 58, "y": 165, "text": "y₂"}, {"x": 214, "y": 285, "text": "x₁"}], "points": [[220, 85], [220, 160]]}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If Q(x₁,y₂) lies on the line AB, then it give:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+by+c=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax_1+by_2+c=0,\\quad Q(x_1,y_2)\\ \\mathrm{lies\\ on}\\ AB\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(by_2=-(ax_1+c)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y_2=-\\left(\\frac{ax_1+c}b\\right)\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If P lies above AB as in Figure 7.18, then:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(MP-MQ>0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y_1-y_2>0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y_1+\\frac{ax_1+c}b>0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\frac{ax_1+by_1+c}b>0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax_1+by_1+c>0,\\quad b>0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.19", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.19 — point below a line at the same x-coordinate", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[45, 85], [430, 250]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[220, 210], [220, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 160], [220, 160]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}, {"points": [[80, 210], [220, 210]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 37, "y": 76, "text": "A"}, {"x": 436, "y": 254, "text": "B"}, {"x": 225, "y": 202, "text": "P(x₁,y₁)"}, {"x": 225, "y": 153, "text": "Q(x₁,y₂)"}, {"x": 214, "y": 265, "text": "M"}, {"x": 58, "y": 215, "text": "y₁"}, {"x": 58, "y": 165, "text": "y₂"}, {"x": 214, "y": 285, "text": "x₁"}], "points": [[220, 210], [220, 160]]}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If P lies below AB as in Figure 7.19, then:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(MP-MQ<0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y_1-y_2<0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y_1+\\frac{ax_1+c}b<0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\frac{ax_1+by_1+c}b<0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax_1+by_1+c<0,\\quad b>0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Hence P lies on one side or on the other side of the line ax+by+c = 0 according as ax₁+by₁+c > 0 or ax₁+by₁+c < 0."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The above/below interpretation explicitly assumes b > 0. For b < 0 reverse the sign comparison, or compare (ax₁+by₁+c)/b with zero. For b = 0 the line is vertical, so describe left/right rather than above/below. An expression equal to zero means the point lies on the line. For any genuine line, two off-line points lie on the same side exactly when the two evaluated expressions have the same sign; multiplying the entire equation by −1 swaps both signs without changing that conclusion. Figure 7.18 repeats a caption number used earlier for normal-form diagrams."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 16"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Determine whether the point P(10,−6) is above or below the line 9x+10y−3 = 0. Show that the point and the origin lie on the same or on the opposite sides of the given line."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The given line 9x+10y−3 = 0 is compared to the line ax+by+c = 0 to obtain the coefficient of y is b = 10 > 0:"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "i. The given point P(10,−6) is substituted in the given line to obtain:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(9(10)+10(-6)-3=90-63=27>0\\)", "number": null}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source side-of-line and normal-projection proof; three native source figures; angle opening retained", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 101, "side": "left", "reason": "All inequalities, b > 0 assumptions and inconsistent point-coordinate subscripts enlarged and checked"}}, {"file": "101_page_2.jpg", "page": 194, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "Thus, the point P(10,−6) is above the given line 9x+10y−3 = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "ii. The given point P(10,−6) and the origin O(0,0) are substituted in the given line to obtain:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(9(10)+10(-6)-3=90-63=27>0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(9(0)+10(0)-3=-3<0,\\quad\\mathrm{opposite\\ in\\ signs}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Hence, the point P(10,−6) and the origin O(0,0) lie on the opposite side of the given line 9x+10y−3 = 0."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.5.2 Perpendicular distance from a point to the given straight line"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If Q(x₁,y₁) is any point on a line"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+by+c=0\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "and n = (a,b) is a nonzero vector perpendicular to the line (i) at a point Q(x₁,y₁), then the distance D is the scalar projection of a vector QP (associated to any point (P(x₁,y₁)) onto n:"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.20", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.20 — nonzero normal n = (a,b), a point Q on the line, point P off the line and its perpendicular distance D", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 285], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[40, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[60, 154.05], [450, 270]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[240, 207.57], [350, 100]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[311.684, 228.879], [350, 100]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#be123c", "dash": false}, {"points": [[240, 207.57], [270, 106.66]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#0284c7", "dash": false}, {"points": [[315.675, 215.445], [329.109, 219.438], [325.118, 232.872]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 78, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 455, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 265, "text": "O"}, {"x": 351, "y": 94, "text": "P"}, {"x": 199, "y": 235, "text": "Q(x₀,y₀)"}, {"x": 320, "y": 166, "text": "D"}, {"x": 201, "y": 96, "text": "n = (a,b)"}, {"x": 334, "y": 294, "text": "ax+by+c = 0"}], "points": [[240, 207.57], [350, 100]]}}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(D=|\\mathrm{proj}_nQP|=\\frac{|QP\\cdot n|}{|n|}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=\\frac{|(x_0-x_1,y_0-y_1)\\cdot(a,b)|}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}},\\quad n=(a,b),|n|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=\\frac{|a(x_0-x_1)+b(y_0-y_1)|}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=\\frac{|ax_0+by_0-ax_1-by_1|}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}=\\frac{|ax_0+by_0+c|}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "the perpendicular distance from a line ax+by+c = 0 to a point P(x₁,y₁)."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The source alternates the coordinates of Q and P: its opening assigns both (x₁,y₁), its formula uses (x₀,y₀) for the off-line point, and its diagram labels Q(x₀,y₀). To avoid this mismatch, take Q(u,v) on the line and P(s,t) as the given point. Since au+bv+c = 0, |(P−Q)·(a,b)|/√(a²+b²) = |as+bt+c|/√(a²+b²). The denominator is nonzero because (a,b) ≠ (0,0); the absolute value converts the signed projection into nonnegative distance."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 17"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the perpendicular distance from a line 7x+3y−9 = 0 to a point P(2,3)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (ii) is used for the assumptions P(x₁,y₁) = P(2,3), c = −9, a = 7, b = 3 to obtain the perpendicular distance d from the line 7x+3y−9 = 0 to the point P(2,3)."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=\\frac{|ax_1+by_1+c|}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}=\\frac{|7(2)+3(3)-9|}{\\sqrt{7^2+3^2}}=\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{58}}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.6 Angle between Lines"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If the two lines are available, then the angle between these two lines can found as follows:"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.6.1 The angle between two coplanar intersecting straight lines"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The unit vectors are the vectors lie in the same directions of the given lines. The unit vectors along the line AB and CD are respectively u = (cos θ₁,sin θ₁) and v = (cos θ₂,sin θ₂)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The unit vector u of a line AB is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(u=(\\cos\\theta_1,\\sin\\theta_1),\\quad\\cos\\theta_1=\\frac1{\\sec\\theta_1}\\)", "number": null}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source side-of-line and normal-projection proof; three native source figures; angle opening retained", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 101, "side": "right", "reason": "All inequalities, b > 0 assumptions and inconsistent point-coordinate subscripts enlarged and checked"}}, {"file": "102_page_1.jpg", "page": 195, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(u=\\left(\\frac1{\\sec\\theta_1},\\frac{\\tan\\theta_1}{\\sec\\theta_1}\\right),\\quad|u|=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}},\\frac{m_1}{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}}\\right),\\quad\\sec^2\\theta_1=1+\\tan^2\\theta_1=1+m_1^2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.21", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.21 — intersecting lines AB and CD with inclination angles θ₁, θ₂ and angle θ at P", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[225, 300], [225, 35]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[45, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[160, 60], [310, 300]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[70, 300], [410, 60]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 223, "y": 25, "text": "y"}, {"x": 456, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 198, "y": 263, "text": "O"}, {"x": 141, "y": 53, "text": "A"}, {"x": 316, "y": 306, "text": "B"}, {"x": 414, "y": 55, "text": "C"}, {"x": 51, "y": 310, "text": "D"}, {"x": 237, "y": 174, "text": "P"}, {"x": 270, "y": 231, "text": "θ₁"}, {"x": 169, "y": 231, "text": "θ₂"}, {"x": 145, "y": 262, "text": "F"}, {"x": 274, "y": 262, "text": "E"}, {"x": 240, "y": 205, "text": "θ"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The unit vector v of a line CD is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(v=(\\cos\\theta_2,\\sin\\theta_2),\\quad|v|=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\left(\\frac1{\\sec\\theta_2},\\frac{\\tan\\theta_2}{\\sec\\theta_2}\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}},\\frac{m_1}{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}}\\right),\\quad\\sec^2\\theta_2=1+\\tan^2\\theta_2=1+m_2^2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The angle of intersection between the lines AB and CD is the angle of intersection in between their unit vector u and v that can be found by taking the dot product in between the unit vectors u and v:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\cos\\theta=\\frac{u\\cdot v}{|u||v|},\\quad|u|=|v|=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=u\\cdot v\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}},\\frac{m_1}{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+m_2^2}},\\frac{m_2}{\\sqrt{1+m_2^2}}\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\frac{m_1m_2}{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}\\sqrt{1+m_2^2}}+\\frac{m_1m_2}{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}\\sqrt{1+m_2^2}}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\frac{1+m_1m_2}{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}\\sqrt{1+m_2^2}}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The standard form of the angle is obtained if"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan^2\\theta=\\sec^2\\theta-1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\frac1{\\cos^2\\theta}-1,\\quad\\mathrm{use\\ value\\ of}\\ \\cos\\theta\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\frac{(1+m_1^2)(1+m_2^2)}{(1+m_1m_2)^2}-1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\frac{(1+m_1^2)(1+m_2^2)-(1+m_1m_2)^2}{(1+m_1m_2)^2}=\\frac{(m_1-m_2)^2}{(1+m_1m_2)^2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{(m_1-m_2)^2}{(1+m_1m_2)^2}}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\pm\\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The source first substitutes m₁ into the v expression, where m₂ is needed. Its expanded dot product then repeats m₁m₂ in both numerator terms; the first term must be 1. The following final dot formula is correct for the right-pointing unit directions (1,mᵢ)/√(1+mᵢ²). Writing 1/sec θᵢ = 1/√(1+mᵢ²) assumes cos θᵢ > 0; other orientations may reverse a direction. The smaller unsigned line angle α satisfies cos α = |1+m₁m₂|/√[(1+m₁²)(1+m₂²)]. When the denominator 1+m₁m₂ is zero, α = 90° and the tangent quotient is undefined."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source unit-vector/dot/tangent derivation, reminder, example and native intersecting-lines figure", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 102, "side": "left", "reason": "All vector component indices, repeated dot numerators and cross sign visually checked"}}, {"file": "102_page_2.jpg", "page": 196, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If (m₁−m₂)/(1+m₁m₂) is positive, then result (i) gives the acute angle between the lines AB and CD."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If (m₁−m₂)/(1+m₁m₂) is negative, then result (i) gives the obtuse angle between the lines AB and CD."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If one of the given lines is parallel to the y-axis, the angle θ is not possible to obtain by formula:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta=\\pm\\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Because 90° is the angle made by that line with the positive x-axis and tan90° = ∞. In such a case, the angle between the lines will be calculated by drawing the figure."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The lines are parallel, if the cross product in between the unit vector u and v is zero:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(u\\times v=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}},\\frac{m_1}{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}}\\right)\\times\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+m_2^2}},\\frac{m_2}{\\sqrt{1+m_2^2}}\\right)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{m_1-m_2}{\\sqrt{(1+m_1^2)(1+m_2^2)}}k̂=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ m_1-m_2=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ m_1=m_2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "where k is normal to the plane of the lines."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The lines are perpendicular, if the dot product in between the unit vectors u and v is zero:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(u\\cdot v=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}},\\frac{m_1}{\\sqrt{1+m_1^2}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+m_2^2}},\\frac{m_2}{\\sqrt{1+m_2^2}}\\right)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{1+m_1m_2}{\\sqrt{(1+m_1^2)(1+m_2^2)}}=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ 1+m_1m_2=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ m_1m_2=-1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "A tangent quotient alone does not select an acute or obtuse angle without a branch and orientation convention. For the smaller unsigned angle use α = atan(|(m₁−m₂)/(1+m₁m₂)|), with α = 90° for zero denominator. Its supplementary angle is 180°−α. The printed cross product has reversed sign: with the displayed ordered vectors u×v = (m₂−m₁)k̂/√[(1+m₁²)(1+m₂²)], although its zero test still gives m₁ = m₂. The vertical case can also be calculated using direction vectors, without a finite-slope quotient: for any two nonzero directions d₁,d₂, cos α = |d₁·d₂|/(|d₁||d₂|). This covers horizontal/vertical and all orientations."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 18"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the angle from the line 7x+3y−9 = 0 to the line 5x−2y+2 = 0."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The slope of a line 7x+3y−9 = 0 is m₁ = −7/3."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The slope of a line 5x−2y+2 = 0 is m₂ = 5/2."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If θ is the angle from first line to line second, then"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta=\\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}=\\frac{-7/3-5/2}{1+(-7/3)(5/2)}=\\frac{-29}{6(1-35/6)}=\\frac{-29}{-29}=1\\ \\Rightarrow\\theta=\\tan^{-1}(1)=45^\\circ\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The angle θ = 45° is acute."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The source chooses the acute angle 45°. The lines also form a supplementary 135° angle. A directed “from first to second” angle needs chosen orientations and rotation sense, which the question does not specify, so the learning solution states the smaller unsigned angle and its supplement."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source unit-vector/dot/tangent derivation, reminder, example and native intersecting-lines figure", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 102, "side": "right", "reason": "All vector component indices, repeated dot numerators and cross sign visually checked"}}, {"file": "103_page_1.jpg", "page": 197, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "7.6.2 The equation of family of lines passing through the point of intersection of two given lines"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Suppose, the two lines are"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(L_1:a_1x+b_1y+c_1=0\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(L_2:a_2x+b_2y+c_2=0\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "and P(x₁,y₁) is their point of intersection. The given lines L₁ and L₂ are used to obtain a first degree equation of a straight line in x and y:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((a_1x+b_1y+c_1)+\\lambda(a_2x+b_2y+c_2)=0,\\quad\\lambda\\ \\mathrm{is\\ constant}\\)", "number": "iii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The coordinates of a point P will reduce each line in (iii) to zero, since, by hypothesis, P is the point of intersection, i.e., it lies on each line. Therefore P satisfies (iii) and represents the family of lines through the point of intersection of L₁ = 0 and L₂ = 0."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.22", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.22 — two intersecting lines L₁,L₂ and another member L₁+λL₂ = 0", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[225, 300], [225, 35]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[45, 240], [450, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[120, 60], [380, 300]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[50, 260], [260, 189.230769], [440, 128.571429]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[190, 295.846154], [260, 189.230769], [330, 82.615385]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 223, "y": 25, "text": "y"}, {"x": 456, "y": 246, "text": "x"}, {"x": 197, "y": 263, "text": "O"}, {"x": 360, "y": 283, "text": "A"}, {"x": 380, "y": 306, "text": "L₁"}, {"x": 443, "y": 131, "text": "L₂"}, {"x": 335, "y": 65, "text": "L₁+λL₂ = 0"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 19"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Develop the family of lines through the point of intersection of the lines 2x−3y+4 = 0 and 2x+y−1 = 0. Find the line from the family of lines which is (a) parallel to the line whose slope is m₁ = −2/3. (b) perpendicular to the line 4x+3y−1 = 0."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (iii) is used for the lines 2x−3y+4 = 0, 2x+y−1 = 0 to obtain the family of lines:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((2x-3y+4)+\\lambda(2x+y-1)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((2+2\\lambda)x+(-3+\\lambda)y+(4-\\lambda)=0\\)", "number": "iv"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The slope of the family of lines is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((2+2\\lambda)x+(-3+\\lambda)y+(4-\\lambda)=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ (-3+\\lambda)y=-(2+2\\lambda)x-(4-\\lambda)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y=\\frac{-(2+2\\lambda)x}{-3+\\lambda}-\\frac{4-\\lambda}{-3+\\lambda},\\quad m_2=\\frac{-(2+2\\lambda)}{-3+\\lambda}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "a. The family of lines (iv) is parallel to the line with slope m₁ = −2/3 if and only if their slopes are equal:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{-(2+2\\lambda)}{-3+\\lambda}=-\\frac23\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 6+6\\lambda=-6+2\\lambda\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 4\\lambda=-12\\ \\Rightarrow\\lambda=-3\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The value of λ = −3 is used in (iv) to obtain the particular line from the family of lines (iv):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((2x-3y+4)-3(2x+y-1)=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 2x-3y+4-6x-3y+3=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ -4x-6y+7=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 4x+6y-7=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "b. The slope of the given line 4x+3y−1 = 0 is m₃ = −4/3. The family of lines (iv) is perpendicular to the line 4x+3y−1 = 0, if and only if the product of their slopes equals −1:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\left[\\frac{-(2+2\\lambda)}{-3+\\lambda}\\right]\\left(-\\frac43\\right)=-1\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\frac{8+8\\lambda}{-9+3\\lambda}=-1\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 8+8\\lambda=9-3\\lambda\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 11\\lambda=1\\ \\Rightarrow\\lambda=\\frac1{11}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The value of λ = 1/11 is used in (iv) to obtain the particular line from the family of lines:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((2x-3y+4)+\\frac1{11}(2x+y-1)=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 22x-33y+44+2x+y-1=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 24x-32y+43=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "For two distinct intersecting lines, every finite λ member passes through their intersection. This normalized expression omits L₂ itself; the complete pencil is αL₁+βL₂ = 0 with (α,β) ≠ (0,0). In this example λ = 3 gives a vertical line, so its slope quotient must not be used. The generic source figure is schematic; the actual example intersection is (−1/8,5/4)."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source family-of-lines and triangle-angle derivations with three native figures and checked branch selection", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 103, "side": "left", "reason": "All λ coefficients, signed inverse tangents and source vertices visually checked"}}, {"file": "103_page_2.jpg", "page": 198, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "7.6.3 The angles of the triangle when the slopes of the sides are given"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If A(x₁,y₁), B(x₂,y₂) and C(x₃,y₃) are the vertices of a triangle ABC and the slopes of the sides AB, BC and CA of the triangle ABC are respectively:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_1=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1},\\quad\\mathrm{slope\\ of\\ side}\\ AB\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_2=\\frac{y_3-y_2}{x_3-x_2},\\quad\\mathrm{slope\\ of\\ side}\\ BC\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_3=\\frac{y_1-y_3}{x_1-x_3},\\quad\\mathrm{slope\\ of\\ side}\\ CA\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.23", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.23 — triangle ABC with interior angles θ₁,θ₂,θ₃", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[100, 260], [430, 260], [290, 60], [100, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 81, "y": 277, "text": "A"}, {"x": 432, "y": 278, "text": "B"}, {"x": 285, "y": 45, "text": "C"}, {"x": 120, "y": 250, "text": "θ₁"}, {"x": 396, "y": 250, "text": "θ₂"}, {"x": 280, "y": 95, "text": "θ₃"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If θ₁, θ₂ and θ₃ are the angles in between their sides AB to AC, BC to BA and CB to CA respectively, then the angles can be found through results tan θ = ±(m₁−m₂)/(1+m₁m₂). The angles from the sides AB to AC, BC to BA, and CA to CB of a triangle ABC are respectively:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta_1=\\frac{m_1-m_3}{1+m_1m_3},\\quad\\tan\\theta_2=\\frac{m_2-m_1}{1+m_1m_2},\\quad\\tan\\theta_3=\\frac{m_3-m_2}{1+m_2m_3}\\)", "number": "v"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 20"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the angles of the triangle ABC, whose vertices are A(−2,−3), B(4,−1) and C(2,3)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If ABC is a triangle and the slopes of the sides AB, BC and CA are respectively:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_1=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\\frac{-1+3}{4+2}=\\frac26=\\frac13,\\quad\\mathrm{slope\\ of\\ side}\\ AB\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_2=\\frac{y_3-y_2}{x_3-x_2}=\\frac{3+1}{2-4}=\\frac4{-2}=-2,\\quad\\mathrm{slope\\ of\\ side}\\ BC\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_3=\\frac{y_1-y_3}{x_1-x_3}=\\frac{-3-3}{-2-2}=\\frac{-6}{-4}=\\frac32,\\quad\\mathrm{slope\\ of\\ side}\\ CA\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.24", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.24 — exact source vertices A(−2,−3), B(4,−1), C(2,3)", "width": 500, "height": 370, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 180], [432, 180]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[212, 300], [212, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[124, 270], [388, 210], [300, 90], [124, 270]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[168, 176], [168, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[256, 176], [256, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[300, 176], [300, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[344, 176], [344, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[388, 176], [388, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 270], [216, 270]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 240], [216, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 210], [216, 210]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 150], [216, 150]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 120], [216, 120]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 90], [216, 90]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 60], [216, 60]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 440.79999999999995, "y": 180, "text": "x"}, {"x": 212, "y": 18.0, "text": "y"}, {"x": 102, "y": 287, "text": "A"}, {"x": 393, "y": 225, "text": "B"}, {"x": 305, "y": 82, "text": "C"}, {"x": 145, "y": 258, "text": "θ₁"}, {"x": 358, "y": 207, "text": "θ₂"}, {"x": 292, "y": 122, "text": "θ₃"}, {"x": 164, "y": 200, "text": "-1"}, {"x": 252, "y": 200, "text": "1"}, {"x": 296, "y": 200, "text": "2"}, {"x": 340, "y": 200, "text": "3"}, {"x": 384, "y": 200, "text": "4"}, {"x": 220, "y": 275, "text": "-3"}, {"x": 220, "y": 245, "text": "-2"}, {"x": 220, "y": 215, "text": "-1"}, {"x": 220, "y": 155, "text": "1"}, {"x": 220, "y": 125, "text": "2"}, {"x": 220, "y": 95, "text": "3"}, {"x": 220, "y": 65, "text": "4"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (v) to obtain the angle θ₁ from the sides AB to AC use:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta_1=\\frac{m_1-m_3}{1+m_1m_3}=\\frac{1/3-3/2}{1+(1/3)(3/2)}=\\frac{2-9}{6(1+1/2)}=\\frac{-7}{6(3/2)}=-\\frac79\\ \\Rightarrow\\theta_1=\\tan^{-1}(-7/9)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (v) to obtain the angle θ₂ from the sides BC to BA use:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta_2=\\frac{m_2-m_1}{1+m_1m_2}=\\frac{-2-1/3}{1+(-2)(1/3)}=\\frac{-7/3}{1/3}=-7\\ \\Rightarrow\\theta_2=\\tan^{-1}(-7)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (v) to obtain the angle θ₃ from the sides CA to CB use:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta_3=\\frac{m_3-m_2}{1+m_2m_3}=\\frac{3/2+2}{1+(-2)(3/2)}=\\frac7{2(1-3)}=\\frac7{-4}=-\\frac74\\ \\Rightarrow\\theta_3=\\tan^{-1}(-7/4)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The three displayed negative principal inverse tangents are not positive interior angles. Interior angles must be selected from the vertex rays, and an obtuse interior angle cannot always be replaced by the smaller angle between its infinite side-lines. Use the dot product of the two outward rays at each vertex, or atan2(|cross|,dot), which returns an interior angle between 0° and 180°. For this example all three ray dot products are positive, giving A = atan(7/9), B = atan(7), C = atan(7/4). Their sum is 180°. This approach also handles vertical sides, where a finite slope formula is unavailable."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source family-of-lines and triangle-angle derivations with three native figures and checked branch selection", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 103, "side": "right", "reason": "All λ coefficients, signed inverse tangents and source vertices visually checked"}}, {"file": "104_page_1.jpg", "page": 199, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Exercise 7.3"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "1. Show that the point P(x₁,y₁) lies above or below the line ax+by+c = 0. Also show that the point P(x₁,y₁) and the origin lie on the same side or on the opposite side of the line ax+by+c = 0: (a) P(4,−5), 4x−3y−17 = 0; (b) P(−3,8), 5x+7y+9 = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "2. In each case, show that the points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂) are on the same side or on the opposite side of the line ax+by+c = 0: (a) P(−4,2), Q(11,−3), 5x+14y−11 = 0; (b) P(−3,5), Q(1,−2), 2x−3y−10 = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "3. In each case, find the perpendicular distance from the line ax+by+c = 0 to a point P(x₁,y₁): (a) P(3,−4), 4x−3y+6 = 0; (b) P(5,8), 3x−2y+7 = 0; (c) P(3,−1), 5x+12y−16 = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "4. In each case, find the angle θ from the line L₁ to line L₂, if the slopes of the lines L₁ and L₂ are the following: (a) L₁:m₁ = 1/2, L₂:m₂ = 3; (b) L₁:m₁ = 2, L₂:m₂ = 3."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "5. In each case, find the angle θ from the line L₁ to the line L₂: (a) L₁ joins (1,2) and (7,−1), L₂ joins (3,2) and (5,6); (b) L₁ joins (2,7) and (7,10), L₂ joins (1,1) and (−5,3). Try to obtain acute angles."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "6. In each case, find the angle θ from the line L₁ to the line L₂: (a) L₁:x−2y+3 = 0, L₂:3x−y+7 = 0; (b) L₁:2x+4y−10 = 0, L₂:5x−3y+1 = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "7. In each case, find the angles of the triangle ABC whose vertices are the following: (a) A(1,2), B(4,2) and C(−2,3); (b) A(3,−4), B(1,5) and C(2,−4)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "8. Find the equation of the straight line from the family of straight lines through the point of intersection of the lines: (a) 2x−3y+4 = 0, 3x+4y−5 = 0 and is perpendicular to the line 6x−7y−18 = 0; (b) 3x−4y+1 = 0, 5x+y−1 = 0 and cuts off equal intercepts from the axes; (c) x−2y = a, x+3y = 2a and is parallel to the line 3x+4y = 0; (d) 2x−y = 0, 3x+2y = 0 and is perpendicular to the line 3x+y−6 = 0."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "History — Pierre de Fermat"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Pierre de Fermat was French lawyer and a mathematician. He was credited for the early development of calculus. In particular he was recognized because of his discovery of an original method of finding the greatest and smallest ordinates of curved lines which are very important for the differential calculus. He also made some contribution to number theory, but a significant contribution to analytical geometry, optics and probability. He became famous in the community of mathematics because of Fermat’s principle for light propagation and his Fermat’s last theorem in the field of number theory. Fermat’s work in analytical geometry was circulated in manuscript form in 1636. He also developed a method for determining maxima, minima and tangents to various curves that was equivalent to differential calculus."}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/fermat-source199.jpg", "caption": "Pierre de Fermat (1607–1665)", "alt": "Original source portrait of Pierre de Fermat"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Question 5(a) describes perpendicular lines, so its angle is 90° despite the request to try acute angles. Triangle Question 7 contains an obtuse angle in each part, requiring vertex-ray calculations. “From line L₁ to L₂” needs a direction convention; the worked solutions distinguish smaller angles, supplements and directed choices."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "All eight exercise groups transcribed with complete worked solutions; source history paragraph and original portrait", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 104, "side": "left", "reason": "All point coordinates, equation constants, signs and parameter-a questions visually checked"}}, {"file": "104_page_2.jpg", "page": 200, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "7.7 Concurrency of Straight Lines"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Before to touch the concurrency of straight lines, we need to develop the concept of intersection of lines. Logically, the solution of the system of lines exists only, if the lines intersect."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For illustration, the two lines x+y = 1 and x−y = 0 is forming the system of two linear equations"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x+y=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x-y=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "that in matrix form is represented by Ax = b:"}, {"type": "matrixProduct", "matrices": [[[1, 1], [1, -1]], [["x"], ["y"]], [[1], [0]]], "operators": ["", "="], "suffix": "", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source matrix equation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The augmented matrix of the system Ax = b is"}, {"type": "matrixProduct", "matrices": [[[1, 1, 1], [1, -1, 0]]], "operators": [], "suffix": "", "prefix": "A/b =", "caption": "Source augmented matrix"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "reduced in an echelon form through row operations"}, {"type": "matrixProduct", "matrices": [[[1, 1, 1], [1, -1, 0]], [[1, 1, 1], [0, -2, -1]]], "operators": ["∼"], "suffix": "by R₂+R₁(−1)", "prefix": "A/b =", "caption": "Source matrix equation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "that gives the system of equations:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x+y=1,\\quad-2y=-1,\\quad y=\\frac12,x=\\frac12\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The second equation is giving y = 1/2 which is used in first equation to obtain x = 1/2. The solution set (x,y) = (1/2,1/2) of the system of two linear equations is unique (one solution set). This solution is the unique point of intersection at which the given two lines intersect."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "It is important to note that the system of two lines"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "• x+y = 1 and x−y = 0 is giving a unique solution set, since the lines are intersecting at just a single point."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "• x+y = 1 and x+y = 0 is not giving a solution set, since the lines are not intersecting, because the lines are parallel."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "• x+y = 1 and 2x+2y = 2 is giving an infinite set of solutions, since the lines are intersecting at more than one points, because the lines are make a sense of coincident lines."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.7.1 Condition of concurrency of three straight lines"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The condition of concurrency of three straight lines is the point of intersection at which the three straight lines intersect. For illustration, if the given three lines are"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(a_1x+b_1y+c_1=0,\\quad a_2x+b_2y+c_2=0,\\quad a_3x+b_3y+c_3=0\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "then, the three lines develop a homogeneous system of three linear equations"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(Ax=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "matrixProduct", "matrices": [[["\\(a_1\\)", "\\(b_1\\)", "\\(c_1\\)"], ["\\(a_2\\)", "\\(b_2\\)", "\\(c_2\\)"], ["\\(a_3\\)", "\\(b_3\\)", "\\(c_3\\)"]], [["x"], ["y"], ["1"]], [["0"], ["0"], ["0"]]], "operators": ["", "="], "suffix": "(ii)", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source matrix equation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "In homogeneous system of three linear equations lines (i), the homogeneous coordinates are used:"}, {"type": "matrixProduct", "matrices": [[["x"], ["y"]], [["x"], ["y"], ["1"]]], "operators": ["="], "suffix": "(iii)", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source homogeneous-coordinate notation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Concurrency means that the three lines must intersect at a point G(x,y), say, that can be found by solving the system of linear equations (i). The system (ii) has a nontrivial solution if the determinant of the coefficient matrix A of the system (ii) is zero:"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Homogeneous coordinates embed an ordinary point (x,y) as the triple (x,y,1); the source equality between a two-entry and three-entry column is notation for this embedding, not an equality of vectors in the same dimension. Determinant zero is necessary but by itself does not guarantee finite concurrency: the nonzero homogeneous solution may have its last coordinate zero. For example x = 0, x = 1 and x = 2 are three parallel lines with determinant zero and no common finite point. If two given lines are nonparallel, find their unique finite intersection and test it in the third line; this proves finite concurrency."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source matrix elimination and concurrency proof, native matrices/determinants and explicit finite-intersection qualification", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 104, "side": "right", "reason": "All source systems, homogeneous coordinates, row operations and determinant expansion inspected"}}, {"file": "105_page_1.jpg", "page": 201, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(a_1\\)", "\\(b_1\\)", "\\(c_1\\)"], ["\\(a_2\\)", "\\(b_2\\)", "\\(c_2\\)"], ["\\(a_3\\)", "\\(b_3\\)", "\\(c_3\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0 (iv)", "caption": "Source determinant condition"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This is the condition of concurrency of three lines."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For required point of concurrency follow the steps given below:"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "• Choose any two lines from the given three system of linear equations (i)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "• Develop the system of these two linear equations."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "• Develop the augmented matrix A/b of the system of two linear equations and reduce it in an echelon form to obtain the point of intersection."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "• Substitute the developed point of intersection in the remaining third line. If the point of intersection satisfies the remaining line, then that point of intersection should be taken as the point of concurrency of the given three lines."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Choose two nonparallel lines for a unique finite intersection. Merely choosing any pair may give parallel or coincident lines, which requires a separate check."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 21"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Show that the three lines x+4y+3 = 0, 5x−4y−5 = 0 and 2x+2y+1 = 0 are concurrent. If these lines are concurrent, then find out the point of concurrency."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The condition of concurrency (iv) in light of the given three lines is going to be zero:"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [[1, 4, 3], [5, -4, -5], [2, 2, 1]], "suffix": "= 1(−4+10)−4(5+10)+3(10+8) = 6−60+54 = 0", "caption": "Source Example 21 determinant expansion"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The given three lines are concurrent. For the point of concurrency G(x,y), choose the first two lines"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x+4y=-3,\\quad5x-4y=5\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "that develops the system of two linear equations, whose augmented matrix A/b is reduced in an echelon form"}, {"type": "matrixProduct", "matrices": [[[1, 4, -3], [5, -4, 5]], [[1, 4, -3], [0, -24, 20]]], "operators": ["∼"], "suffix": "by R₂₁(−5) = R₂+R₁(−5)", "prefix": "A/b =", "caption": "Source matrix equation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "to obtain the reduced system of linear equations:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x+4y=-3,\\quad-24y=20\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The second equation is giving y = −5/6 which is used in first equation to obtain x = 1/3. The third line with substitution of the point of intersection (x,y) = (1/3,−5/6) is going to be zero:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(2x+2y+1=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 2(1/3)+2(-5/6)+1=2/3-5/3+1=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Thus, the given three lines are concurrent at a point G(1/3,−5/6)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.7.2 Equation of median, altitude and right bisector of a triangle"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "A. Equation of median of a triangle"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A median is a line segment from an interior angle of a triangle to the mid point of the opposite side. Look at the following example, the procedure to find the equation of median of a triangle is illustrated in this example."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "A median joins a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side; the source phrase “from an interior angle” means from its vertex. Example 21’s first two lines are nonparallel, and the computed point satisfies the third, establishing actual finite concurrency independently of the determinant test."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source matrix elimination and concurrency proof, native matrices/determinants and explicit finite-intersection qualification", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 105, "side": "left", "reason": "All source systems, homogeneous coordinates, row operations and determinant expansion inspected"}}, {"file": "105_page_2.jpg", "page": 202, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Example 22"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the equation of the median of a triangle having vertices are A(8,−5), B(6,5) and C(−6,9)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If C′ is mid point of side AB of ΔABC Then its co-ordinates are given as"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\left(\\frac{8+6}2,\\frac{-5+5}2\\right)=(7,0)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Since, the median CC′ passes through points C and C′, using the two-point form of the equation of a straight line, the equation of median CC′ can be found as"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{y-0}{9-0}=\\frac{x-7}{-6-7}\\ \\Rightarrow\\frac y9=\\frac{x-7}{-13}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 13y+9x-63=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.25", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.25 — source triangle with three medians and midpoint labels", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[110, 260], [430, 260], [270, 55], [110, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[110, 260], [350, 157.5]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[430, 260], [190, 157.5]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[270, 55], [270, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 72, "y": 280, "text": "A(8,−5)"}, {"x": 408, "y": 280, "text": "B(6,5)"}, {"x": 255, "y": 42, "text": "C(−6,9)"}, {"x": 356, "y": 157, "text": "A′"}, {"x": 163, "y": 157, "text": "B′"}, {"x": 260, "y": 283, "text": "C′"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If A′ is the midpoint of side BC of ΔABC then its coordinates are given as"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\left(\\frac{6-6}2,\\frac{5+9}2\\right)=\\left(\\frac02,\\frac{14}2\\right)=(0,7)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Since, the median AA′ passes through the point A and A′ respectively. By using the two point form of the equation of a straight line, the equation of median AA′ can be found as"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{y-7}{7-(-5)}=\\frac{x-0}{0-8}\\ \\Rightarrow\\frac{y-5}{12}=\\frac x{-8}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ -8y-12x+40=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If B′ is the midpoint of side AC of ΔABC then its coordinate are given as"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\left(\\frac{8-6}2,\\frac{-5+9}2\\right)=\\left(\\frac22,\\frac42\\right)=(1,2)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Since, the median BB′ passes through the point B and B′ respectively. By using the two point form of the equation of a straight line, the equation of median BB′ can be found as"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{y-2}{2-5}=\\frac{x-1}{1-6}\\ \\Rightarrow\\frac{y-2}{-3}=\\frac{x-1}{-5}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 3x-5y+7=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The source AA′ line changes y−7 to y−5 and ends with a wrong constant 40. Through A(8,−5) and A′(0,7), the correct median is 12x+8y−56 = 0, or 3x+2y−14 = 0. The other two source median equations are correct."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "B. Equation of altitude of a triangle"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Altitude of a triangle is the perpendicular drawn from the vertex of the triangle to the opposite side. This is also known as the height of the triangle. Mostly it is used to find the area of the triangle. Look at the following example the procedure to find the equation of altitude is illustrated in example 22."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 23"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the equation of altitude of triangle ABC having vertices are A(−7,4), B(9,6) and C(7,−10)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "First we find slope of side"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(AB=m_1=\\frac{6-4}{9+7}=\\frac2{16}=\\frac18\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.26", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.26 — source altitude construction, with actual perpendicular feet in a schematic triangle", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 110], [425, 60], [340, 285], [80, 110]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 110], [365.3824546240277, 217.81114952463267]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[425, 60], [294.27335199796386, 254.22244846016798]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[340, 285], [309.8107385311664, 76.69409586504834]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 40, "y": 106, "text": "A(−7,4)"}, {"x": 400, "y": 48, "text": "B(9,6)"}, {"x": 318, "y": 307, "text": "C(7,−10)"}, {"x": 371.3824546240277, "y": 224.81114952463267, "text": "A′"}, {"x": 271.27335199796386, "y": 261.222448460168, "text": "B′"}, {"x": 299.8107385311664, "y": 66.69409586504834, "text": "C′"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The altitude CC′ is perpendicular to side AB, so the slope of"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(CC′=\\frac1{m_1}=-8\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Since the altitude CC′ passes through the point C(7,−10), by using the point slope form of the equation of a line, the equation of CC′ is"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-(-10)=-8(x-7)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ y+10=-8x+56\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 8x+y-46=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Which is the equation of the altitude from C to AB."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\mathrm{The\\ slope\\ of\\ side}\\ BC=m_2=\\frac{-10-6}{7-9}=\\frac{-16}{-2}=8\\)", "number": null}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source median/altitude/bisector examples with four native figures, including preserved inconsistent source label", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 105, "side": "right", "reason": "All midpoint coordinates, wrong source constants, sign omissions and figure-label mismatch visually checked"}}, {"file": "106_page_1.jpg", "page": 203, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "The altitude AA′ is perpendicular to side BC, so the slope of AA′ = 1/m₂ = −1/8."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Since, the altitude passes through the point A(−7,4), by using the point slope form of the equation of a line, the equation of AA′ is"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-4=-\\frac18(x+7)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x+8y-27=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Which is the equation of the altitude from A to BC."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\mathrm{The\\ slope\\ of\\ side}\\ AC=m_3=\\frac{-10-(-7)}{7-4}=\\frac{-3}3=-1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The altitude BB′ is perpendicular to side AC. So,"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\mathrm{The\\ slope\\ of}\\ BB′=-\\frac1{m_3}=-\\frac1{-1}=1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "since, the altitude passes through the point B(9,6), By using the point slope form of the equation of a line, the equation of BB′ is"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-6=1(x-9)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x-y-3=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Which is the equation of the altitude from B to AC."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "For perpendicular finite slopes use −1/m, not the positive 1/m printed for CC′ and AA′. From y−4 = −(x+7)/8, the correct rearrangement is x+8y−25 = 0, not the source’s −27. The AC slope calculation mixes x and y coordinates; the correct quotient is (−10−4)/(7−(−7)) = −14/14 = −1. It happens to give the same slope. The altitude paragraph’s reference to example 22 should identify Example 23."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "C. Equation of bisector of a triangle"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The bisector of a triangle is a line perpendicular to the side and passing through its midpoint. The three perpendicular bisectors of the sides of a triangle meet in a single point. The procedure to find the equation of a bisector is illustrate in the following example."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 24"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the equation of the right bisector of a triangle having points are A(−7,4), B(10,8) and C(6,−12)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Since, the equation of a perpendicular bisector is given as,"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-\\frac{y_1+y_2}2=-\\frac{x_2-x_1}{y_2-y_1}\\left(x-\\frac{x_1+x_2}2\\right)\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For bisector of A(−7,4) and B(10,8) put the values in equation (i):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-\\frac{8+4}2=\\frac{10+7}{8-4}\\left(x-\\frac{10-7}2\\right)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ y-6=\\frac{17}4\\left(x-\\frac32\\right)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 34x-8y-3=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For bisector of B(10,8) and C(6,−12) put the values in equation (i):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-\\frac{8-12}2=-\\frac{6-10}{-12-8}\\left(x-\\frac{10+6}2\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ y+2=-\\frac15(x-8)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x+5y+2=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.27", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.27 — original source triangle labels, including A(−8,4) that differs from the question", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[100, 260], [430, 245], [270, 70], [100, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 53, "y": 280, "text": "A(−8,4)"}, {"x": 407, "y": 266, "text": "B(10,8)"}, {"x": 246, "y": 55, "text": "C(6,−12)"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For bisector of A(−7,4) and C(6,−12) put the values in equation (i):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-\\frac{4-12}2=-\\frac{6+7}{-12+4}\\left(x-\\frac{-7+6}2\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ y+4=\\frac{13}8\\left(x+\\frac12\\right)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 26x-16y-51=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The source drops the required minus sign in its AB substitution, so its first final bisector is incorrect. In AC it uses −12+4 instead of −12−4 in the slope denominator, producing an incorrect factor 13/8 rather than 13/16. Correct bisectors for the question’s A(−7,4), B(10,8), C(6,−12) are 34x+8y−99 = 0; x+5y+2 = 0; and 26x−32y−115 = 0. Figure 7.27 labels A(−8,4), differing from the question’s −7; the original label is preserved in the source reconstruction and the learning solution uses the stated question coordinates. “Right bisector” here means perpendicular bisector of a side, distinct from an angle bisector."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.7.3 Show that, three right bisectors, three medians and three altitudes of a triangle are concurrent"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "i. Three right bisectors of a triangle are concurrent"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "To show the concurrency of the three right bisectors of a triangle, the procedure developed is as under:"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let ABC be a triangle, whose vertices are A(x₁,y₁), B(x₂,y₂) and C(x₃,y₃), D, E, F are the midpoints of the sides BC, CA, AB of a triangle ABC whose coordinates are respectively:"}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.28", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.28 — three perpendicular bisectors through midpoints D,E,F meeting at G", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[250, 45], [100, 270], [430, 250], [250, 45]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[265.0, 260.0], [262.3157894736842, 215.71052631578948]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[340.0, 147.5], [262.3157894736842, 215.71052631578948]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[175.0, 157.5], [262.3157894736842, 215.71052631578948]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 245, "y": 30, "text": "A"}, {"x": 82, "y": 286, "text": "B"}, {"x": 435, "y": 265, "text": "C"}, {"x": 257.0, "y": 282.0, "text": "D"}, {"x": 347.0, "y": 147.5, "text": "E"}, {"x": 152.0, "y": 157.5, "text": "F"}, {"x": 268.3157894736842, "y": 210.71052631578948, "text": "G"}], "points": []}}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source median/altitude/bisector examples with four native figures, including preserved inconsistent source label", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 106, "side": "left", "reason": "All midpoint coordinates, wrong source constants, sign omissions and figure-label mismatch visually checked"}}, {"file": "106_page_2.jpg", "page": 204, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(D\\left(\\frac{x_2+x_3}2,\\frac{y_2+y_3}2\\right),\\quad E\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_3}2,\\frac{y_1+y_3}2\\right),\\quad F\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2}2,\\frac{y_1+y_2}2\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If the slope of the side BC and the slope of the right bisector DG of the side BC are respectively:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_1=\\frac{y_3-y_2}{x_3-x_2},\\quad m_2=-\\frac1{m_1}=-\\frac{x_3-x_2}{y_3-y_2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "then, the equation of the right bisector DG is obtained by point-slope form of a line:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\left(y-\\frac{y_2+y_3}2\\right)=-\\frac{x_3-x_2}{y_3-y_2}\\left(x-\\frac{x_2+x_3}2\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((y_3-y_2)\\left(y-\\frac{y_2+y_3}2\\right)=(x_2-x_3)\\left(x-\\frac{x_2+x_3}2\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x(x_2-x_3)+y(y_2-y_3)-\\frac12(y_2^2-y_3^2)-\\frac12(x_2^2-x_3^2)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Similarly, the equations of the right bisectors EG (of side CA), FG (of side AB) are respectively:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x(x_3-x_1)+y(y_3-y_1)-\\frac12(y_3^2-y_1^2)-\\frac12(x_3^2-x_1^2)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x(x_1-x_2)+y(y_1-y_2)-\\frac12(y_1^2-y_2^2)-\\frac12(x_1^2-x_2^2)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The right bisectors DG, EG and FG is concurrent, if the determinant of the coefficient matrix A of the related system of equations of the right bisectors DG, EG and FG equals zero:"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(x_2-x_3\\)", "\\(y_2-y_3\\)", "\\(-\\frac12(y_2^2-y_3^2)-\\frac12(x_2^2-x_3^2)\\)"], ["\\(x_3-x_1\\)", "\\(y_3-y_1\\)", "\\(-\\frac12(y_3^2-y_1^2)-\\frac12(x_3^2-x_1^2)\\)"], ["\\(x_1-x_2\\)", "\\(y_1-y_2\\)", "\\(-\\frac12(y_1^2-y_2^2)-\\frac12(x_1^2-x_2^2)\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0 (i)", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The operation of addition of rows R₁+R₂+R₃ to row R₁ is used to obtain:"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(0\\)", "\\(0\\)", "\\(0\\)"], ["\\(x_3-x_1\\)", "\\(y_3-y_1\\)", "\\(-\\frac12(y_3^2-y_1^2)-\\frac12(x_3^2-x_1^2)\\)"], ["\\(x_1-x_2\\)", "\\(y_1-y_2\\)", "\\(-\\frac12(y_1^2-y_2^2)-\\frac12(x_1^2-x_2^2)\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0 (ii)", "prefix": "∼", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The value of the determinant is zero. Hence, the right bisectors DG, EG and FG of a triangle ABC are concurrent."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "For a nondegenerate triangle the first two side vectors are not parallel, so the corresponding bisectors have a unique finite intersection. Their three equation rows sum to zero, so that intersection also satisfies the third. This supplies the finite-point argument beyond determinant zero. Equivalently, the point equidistant from A and B and from B and C is also equidistant from A and C. The slope derivation divides by coordinate differences; its final dot-product equations remain valid for horizontal/vertical sides."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 25"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let ABC be a triangle with vertices A(0,0), B(8,6) and C(12,0). Show that the right bisectors DG, EG and FG of the triangle ABC are concurrent."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The vertices A(x₁,y₁) = A(0,0), B(x₂,y₂) = B(8,6) and C(x₃,y₃) = C(12,0) of the triangle ABC are used in the determinant (i) to obtain:"}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Full source bisector/altitude proof chains and all determinants reconstructed; malformed source line and determinant typo explicitly retained", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 106, "side": "right", "reason": "All determinant cells inspected; enlarged 205 confirms x₃−y₁ typo and concatenated AD rearrangement"}}, {"file": "107_page_1.jpg", "page": 205, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(x_2-x_3\\)", "\\(y_2-y_3\\)", "\\(-\\frac12(y_2^2-y_3^2)-\\frac12(x_2^2-x_3^2)\\)"], ["\\(x_3-x_1\\)", "\\(y_3-y_1\\)", "\\(-\\frac12(y_3^2-y_1^2)-\\frac12(x_3^2-x_1^2)\\)"], ["\\(x_1-x_2\\)", "\\(y_1-y_2\\)", "\\(-\\frac12(y_1^2-y_2^2)-\\frac12(x_1^2-x_2^2)\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0 ⇒", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(-4\\)", "\\(6\\)", "\\(22\\)"], ["\\(12\\)", "\\(0\\)", "\\(-72\\)"], ["\\(-8\\)", "\\(-6\\)", "\\(50\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ -4(-432)-6(600-576)+22(-72)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\Rightarrow\\ 1728-1728=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The determinant (ii) equals zero. Hence, the right bisectors DG, EG and FG of the triangle ABC are concurrent."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "ii. Three median of a triangle are concurrent"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "See topic 7.1.3 at page no 181."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "iii. Three altitudes of a triangle are concurrent"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let ABC be a triangle, whose vertices are A(x₁,y₁), B(x₂,y₂) and C(x₃,y₃). The altitudes of the triangle ABC are AD, BE and CF."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.29", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.29 — three altitudes AD,BE,CF meeting at G", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[250, 45], [100, 270], [430, 250], [250, 45]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[250, 45], [263.0375114364135, 260.11893870082343]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[100, 270], [296.2546187437017, 97.67887134699362]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[430, 250], [210.76923076923077, 103.84615384615384]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 245, "y": 30, "text": "A"}, {"x": 82, "y": 286, "text": "B"}, {"x": 435, "y": 265, "text": "C"}, {"x": 255.03751143641352, "y": 282.11893870082343, "text": "D"}, {"x": 303.2546187437017, "y": 97.67887134699362, "text": "E"}, {"x": 187.76923076923077, "y": 103.84615384615384, "text": "F"}, {"x": 261.36842105263156, "y": 128.57894736842104, "text": "G"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If the slope of the side BC and the slope of the altitude AD are respectively"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_1=\\frac{y_3-y_2}{x_3-x_2},\\quad m_2=-\\frac1{m_1}=-\\frac{x_3-x_2}{y_3-y_2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "then, the equation of altitude AD is obtained by point-slope form of a line:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((y-y_1)=-\\frac{x_3-x_2}{y_3-y_2}(x-x_1)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((y-y_1)(y_3-y_2)=(x_2-x_3)(x-x_1)x(x_2-x_3)+y(y_2-y_3)-x_1(x_2-x_3)-y_1(y_2-y_3)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Similarly, the equations of altitudes BE and CF are respectively:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x(x_3-x_1)+y(y_3-y_1)-x_2(x_3-x_1)-y_2(y_3-y_1)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x(x_1-x_2)+y(y_1-y_2)-x_3(x_1-x_2)-y_3(y_1-y_2)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The altitudes AD, BE and CF are concurrent, if the determinant of the coefficient matrix A of the related system of equations of the altitudes AD, BE and CF equals zero:"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(x_2-x_3\\)", "\\(y_2-y_3\\)", "\\(-x_1(x_2-x_3)-y_1(y_2-y_3)\\)"], ["\\(x_3-x_1\\)", "\\(y_3-y_1\\)", "\\(-x_2(x_3-y_1)-y_2(y_3-y_1)\\)"], ["\\(x_1-x_2\\)", "\\(y_1-y_2\\)", "\\(-x_3(x_1-x_2)-y_3(y_1-y_2)\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0 (iii)", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The operation of addition of rows R₁+R₂+R₃ to row R₁ is used to obtain:"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(0\\)", "\\(0\\)", "\\(0\\)"], ["\\(x_3-x_1\\)", "\\(y_3-y_1\\)", "\\(-x_2(x_3-y_1)-y_2(y_3-y_1)\\)"], ["\\(x_1-x_2\\)", "\\(y_1-y_2\\)", "\\(-x_3(x_1-x_2)-y_3(y_1-y_2)\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0", "prefix": "∼", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The value of the determinant is zero. Therefore, the altitudes AD, BE and CF of a triangle ABC are concurrent at a point G."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The conclusion drawn from the above results is that the three medians AD, BE and CF of a triangle ABC will also make concurrency at a point say, G(x,y)."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The AD rearrangement joins two algebraic lines without a separator. Its intended two steps are (y−y₁)(y₃−y₂) = (x₂−x₃)(x−x₁), then x(x₂−x₃)+y(y₂−y₃)−x₁(x₂−x₃)−y₁(y₂−y₃) = 0. The source determinant’s second-row constant uses x₃−y₁, where x₃−x₁ is required; its zero-row operation is valid only after that correction. With correct rows, their sum is zero by cancellation of the pairwise dot products. In a nondegenerate triangle two altitude directions are not parallel, giving a unique finite orthocenter; the row dependency then puts it on the third altitude. Medians, internal angle bisectors, perpendicular bisectors and altitudes have their own concurrent centers—centroid, incenter, circumcenter and orthocenter—which are generally different even though the source reuses G."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Full source bisector/altitude proof chains and all determinants reconstructed; malformed source line and determinant typo explicitly retained", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 107, "side": "left", "reason": "All determinant cells inspected; enlarged 205 confirms x₃−y₁ typo and concatenated AD rearrangement"}}, {"file": "107_page_2.jpg", "page": 206, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Example 26"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let ABC be a triangle with vertices A(0,0), B(8,6) and C(12,0). Show that the altitudes AD, BE and CF of the triangle ABC are concurrent."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The vertices A(x₁,y₁) = A(0,0), B(x₂,y₂) = B(8,6) and C(x₃,y₃) = C(12,0) of the triangle ABC are used in the determinant (iii) to obtain:"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(x_2-x_3\\)", "\\(y_2-y_3\\)", "\\(-x_1(x_2-x_3)-y_1(y_2-y_3)\\)"], ["\\(x_3-x_1\\)", "\\(y_3-y_1\\)", "\\(-x_2(x_3-y_1)-y_2(y_3-y_1)\\)"], ["\\(x_1-x_2\\)", "\\(y_1-y_2\\)", "\\(-x_3(x_1-x_2)-y_3(y_1-y_2)\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0 ⇒", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(-4\\)", "\\(6\\)", "\\(0\\)"], ["\\(12\\)", "\\(0\\)", "\\(-96\\)"], ["\\(-8\\)", "\\(-6\\)", "\\(96\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(-4(-576)-6(1152-768)=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 2304-6(384)=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 2304-2304=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The determinant (iii) equals zero. Hence, the altitudes AD, BE and CF of the triangle ABC are concurrent."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "For the stated nondegenerate triangle, the learning solution also finds the common finite orthocenter and tests all three equations. The inherited general determinant’s x₃−y₁ typo was identified in the preceding proof; it has no numerical effect in this particular example because x₁ = y₁ = 0."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.8 Area of a Triangular Region"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let ABC be a triangle whose vertices are P₁(x₁,y₁), P₂(x₂,y₂) and P₃(x₃,y₃). Project P₁A, P₂B and P₃C upon the x-axis that develops the trapezia P₁ACP₃, P₃CBP₂ and P₁ABP₂."}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.30", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.30 — source triangular region and its three perpendicular projections on the x-axis", "width": 540, "height": 350, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 295], [80, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[45, 260], [480, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[130, 170], [440, 105], [210, 65], [130, 170]], "fill": "#dcfce7", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[136.66666666666666, 161.25], [155.83333333333334, 164.58333333333334]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[143.33333333333334, 152.5], [181.66666666666666, 159.16666666666666]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[150.0, 143.75], [207.5, 153.75]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[156.66666666666666, 135.0], [233.33333333333331, 148.33333333333334]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[163.33333333333334, 126.25], [259.1666666666667, 142.91666666666666]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[170.0, 117.5], [285.0, 137.5]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[176.66666666666669, 108.75], [310.83333333333337, 132.08333333333331]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[183.33333333333331, 100.0], [336.66666666666663, 126.66666666666667]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[190.0, 91.25], [362.5, 121.25]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[196.66666666666669, 82.5], [388.33333333333337, 115.83333333333333]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[203.33333333333331, 73.75], [414.16666666666663, 110.41666666666667]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#166534", "dash": false}, {"points": [[130, 170], [130, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}, {"points": [[440, 105], [440, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}, {"points": [[210, 65], [210, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": true}], "labels": [{"x": 77, "y": 22, "text": "y"}, {"x": 486, "y": 267, "text": "x"}, {"x": 57, "y": 285, "text": "O"}, {"x": 104, "y": 174, "text": "P₁"}, {"x": 440, "y": 97, "text": "P₂"}, {"x": 195, "y": 55, "text": "P₃"}, {"x": 107, "y": 289, "text": "A(x₁,0)"}, {"x": 408, "y": 289, "text": "B(x₂,0)"}, {"x": 180, "y": 318, "text": "C(x₃,0)"}, {"x": 132, "y": 230, "text": "y₁"}, {"x": 206, "y": 230, "text": "y₃"}, {"x": 435, "y": 230, "text": "y₂"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The area A of the triangular region P₁P₂P₃ is the sum of the areas of the trapezia P₁ACP₃, P₃CBP₂ and P₁ABP₂ minus the area of the trapezium P₁ABP₂."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(A=\\frac12[(y_1+y_3)(x_3-x_1)]+\\frac12[(y_3+y_2)(x_2-x_3)]-\\frac12[(y_1+y_2)(x_2-x_1)]\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\frac12[x_3y_1-x_1y_1+x_3y_3-x_1y_3+x_2y_3-x_3y_3+x_2y_2-x_3y_2-x_2y_1+x_1y_1-x_2y_2+x_1y_2]\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\frac12[x_1(y_2-y_3)+x_2(y_3-y_1)+x_3(y_1-y_2)]\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(x_1\\)", "\\(y_1\\)", "\\(1\\)"], ["\\(x_2\\)", "\\(y_2\\)", "\\(1\\)"], ["\\(x_3\\)", "\\(y_3\\)", "\\(1\\)"]], "suffix": "(ii)", "prefix": "A = 1/2", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "It is important to note that the area A of the triangular region P₁P₂P₃ equals zero, when the vertices of the triangular region are collinear points."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The paragraph redundantly includes P₁ABP₂ in both the sum and the subtraction; the displayed calculation correctly adds the first two trapezia and subtracts P₁ABP₂ once for the depicted ordering. The general determinant gives signed area. Geometric area is one half the absolute value of the determinant; reversing two vertex rows changes the sign, not the geometric area. The cross-product formula |(P₂−P₁)×(P₃−P₁)|/2 gives the same nonnegative result and handles any coordinates without trapezium-order assumptions."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 27"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the area of the triangular region P₁P₂P₃ whose vertices are P₁(4,−5), P₂(5,−6) and P₃(3,1)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Result (i) is used for points P₁(4,−5), P₂(5,−6), P₃(3,1) to obtain the area of the triangular region P₁P₂P₃:"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(4\\)", "\\(-5\\)", "\\(1\\)"], ["\\(5\\)", "\\(-6\\)", "\\(1\\)"], ["\\(3\\)", "\\(1\\)", "\\(1\\)"]], "suffix": "first row expansion", "prefix": "A = 1/2", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=\\frac12[4(-6-1)+5(5-3)+(5+18)]=\\frac12[-28+10+23]=\\frac52\\ \\mathrm{square\\ units}\\)", "number": null}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete source altitude example and triangle-area proof with exact determinant expansions and native projection diagram", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 107, "side": "right", "reason": "Area expansion enlarged and checked; all source vertices, signs and omitted-absolute-value condition reviewed"}}, {"file": "108_page_1.jpg", "page": 207, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "7.9 Homogeneous Equation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "In general, any line equation in two variables that passes through the origin is called a homogeneous equation."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.9.1 Homogeneous linear and quadratic equation in two variables"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "i. Homogeneous linear equation in two variables"}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/homogeneous207-faint-definition.jpg", "caption": "Unresolved source fragment — definition (i)", "alt": "Faint nonhomogeneous-line definition; its displayed formula cannot be certified from the supplied copies."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The displayed formula in definition (i) remains too faint to transcribe reliably. Its readable wording identifies two variables x and y, c ≠ 0, constants a,b,c and a nonhomogeneous equation of a line. The usual form ax+by+c = 0 is a learning explanation inferred from the following c = 0 discussion, not a certified transcription of the faint formula."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For c = 0, the nonhomogeneous equation (i) gives the homogeneous equation of the form"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+by=0\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "that passes through the origin definitely. This also defines a homogeneous equation of degree 1, since the indices of x and y in every term of (ii) is the same, the degree being 1. For example, the equation of line x+y = 0 is homogeneous line, since it defines a homogeneous equation of degree 1."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "ii. Homogeneous quadratic equation in two variables"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "An equation of the form"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax^2+2hxy+by^2=0,\\quad a\\ne0\\)", "number": "iii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "where a, b, c are constants is called a homogeneous quadratic equation of second degree in variables x and y. The sum of indices of x and y in every term of (iii) is the same, the degree being 2."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For example, the equations 3x²−4xy+5y² = 0 and lx²+mxy+ny² = 0 are homogeneous equations of second degree. The equation 3xy²−4xy+5y² = 0 is nonhomogeneous, since the sum of indices of x and y in every term is not the same."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The quadratic coefficients are a,h,b; the source’s list a,b,c includes an unused c. A nonzero polynomial can be homogeneous even when a = 0. The assumption a ≠ 0 is needed for the subsequent factorization method, not for homogeneity itself. The zero polynomial has no degree 1 or degree 2."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "7.9.2 Second degree homogeneous equations represents pair of straight lines through the origin"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "i. Standard form of second degree homogeneous equation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let L₁:a₁x+b₁y+c₁ = 0 and L₂:a₂x+b₂y+c₂ = 0 be two straight lines. Their joint equation is"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((a_1x+b_1y+c_1)(a_2x+b_2y+c_2)=0\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For c₁ = c₂ = 0, both lines pass through the origin and their joint equation becomes"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((a_1x+b_1y)(a_2x+b_2y)=0\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(a_1a_2x^2+a_1b_2xy+b_1a_2xy+b_1b_2y^2=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(a_1a_2x^2+(a_1b_2+a_2b_1)xy+b_1b_2y^2=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Let a₁a₂ = a, a₁b₂+a₂b₁ = 2h and b₁b₂ = b. Then"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax^2+2hxy+by^2=0\\)", "number": "iv"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Any point P satisfying either of the two lines satisfies their product equation."}], "sourceDefects": ["Definition (i) displayed formula too faint in JPG, full PDF and Word copy; exact fragment retained and usual form identified only as learning inference."], "visualStatus": "Native homogeneous-equation derivations and worked examples; definition (i) on 207 remains faint", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 108, "side": "left", "reason": "Enlarged discriminant, angle branches and both final y factors visually reviewed; Word copy does not resolve faint definition"}, "textStatus": "partial_source_legibility", "sourceNotes": ["Printed 207 is partially legible; do not certify complete transcription."]}, {"file": "108_page_2.jpg", "page": 208, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "ii. Representation as pair of straight lines"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The product of (iv) to constant quantity a/a gives"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac aa[ax^2+2hxy+by^2]=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(a^2x^2+2ahxy+aby^2=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Adding and subtracting h²y² gives"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(a^2x^2+2ahxy+h^2y^2-h^2y^2+aby^2=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((ax+hy)^2-y^2(h^2-ab)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((ax+hy)^2-(y\\sqrt{h^2-ab})^2=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((ax+hy+y\\sqrt{h^2-ab})(ax+hy-y\\sqrt{h^2-ab})=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+(h+\\sqrt{h^2-ab})y=0\\)", "number": "v"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax+(h-\\sqrt{h^2-ab})y=0\\)", "number": "vi"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The printed multiplier a/a equals 1 and cannot produce the next equation. Multiplication by a produces that equation. For a ≠ 0, the product of the two displayed linear factors equals a times the original quadratic, so their zero sets agree."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Note"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The lines (v) and (vi) are real and distant if h²−ab > 0; real and coincident if h²−ab = 0; imaginary if h²−ab < 0."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The source says “distant”; the appropriate term is distinct. With real coefficients and a ≠ 0, a negative discriminant gives complex conjugate linear factors. Its real quadratic zero set contains only the origin; it does not give two real lines."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 28"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the separate equations of the lines represented by 5x²+3xy−8y² = 0."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Compare with ax²+2hxy+by² = 0: a = 5, 2h = 3, h = 3/2, b = −8. Use (v) and (vi):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(5x+\\left[\\frac32+\\sqrt{\\frac94-5(-8)}\\right]y=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(5x+\\left[\\frac32+\\sqrt{\\frac94+40}\\right]y=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(5x+\\left[\\frac32+\\frac{13}2\\right]y=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 5x+8y=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(5x+\\left[\\frac32-\\sqrt{\\frac94-5(-8)}\\right]y=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(5x+\\left[\\frac32-\\frac{13}2\\right]y=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 5x-5y=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x-y=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "iii. Angle between pair of straight lines"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(ax^2+2hxy+by^2=0\\)", "number": "vii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Suppose the separate equations are y−m₁x = 0 and y−m₂x = 0. Their joint equation is"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((y-m_1x)(y-m_2x)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y^2-m_2xy-m_1xy+m_1m_2x^2=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y^2-(m_1+m_2)xy+m_1m_2x^2=0\\)", "number": "viii"}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Native homogeneous-equation derivations and worked examples; definition (i) on 207 remains faint", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 108, "side": "right", "reason": "Enlarged discriminant, angle branches and both final y factors visually reviewed; Word copy does not resolve faint definition"}}, {"file": "109_page_1.jpg", "page": 209, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "Comparing (vii) and (viii) gives"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(b=1,\\quad -(m_1+m_2)=2h\\ \\Rightarrow\\ m_1+m_2=-\\frac{2h}1=-\\frac{2h}b\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_1m_2=\\frac a1=\\frac ab\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta=\\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{(m_1-m_2)^2}}{1+m_1m_2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((m_1-m_2)^2=(m_1+m_2)^2-4m_1m_2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta=\\frac{\\sqrt{(m_1+m_2)^2-4m_1m_2}}{1+m_1m_2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4h^2/b^2-4a/b}}{1+a/b}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{h^2-ab}}{a+b}\\)", "number": "ix"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The pair of lines is perpendicular if θ = 90°, giving a+b = 0. The lines coincide if θ = 0°, giving h²−ab = 0."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The coefficient comparison requires division by b ≠ 0; b = 1 describes the normalized equation. Replacing m₁−m₂ by its absolute value changes a signed angle convention. For a real line pair, the smaller unsigned angle is atan2(2√(h²−ab),|a+b|). If a+b = 0 and h²−ab > 0 it is 90°; a nonzero coincident pair has angle 0°. This version also handles a vertical factor, which the finite-slope derivation excludes."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 29"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the angle between the lines represented by x²−xy−6y² = 0."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Compare with ax²+2hxy+by² = 0: a = 1, h = −1/2, b = −6. Result (ix) gives"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta=\\frac{2\\sqrt{1/4-1(-6)}}{1-6}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{25/4}}{-5}=\\frac{\\sqrt{25}}{-5}=\\frac5{-5}=-1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\theta=\\tan^{-1}(-1)=\\frac{3\\pi}4=135^\\circ\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The principal inverse tangent of −1 is −45°, not 135°. A 135° supplementary intersection angle is valid after an explicit branch choice. Factoring (x−3y)(x+2y) gives slopes 1/3 and −1/2, whose smaller unsigned angle is 45°."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 30"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Find the two first degree straight lines in x and y when the second degree homogeneous equation is 3x²−4xy−3y² = 0. Show that the resultant lines are coincident or perpendicular."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Compare with ax²+2hxy+by² = 0: a = 3, 2h = −4, h = −2, b = −3. Use (v) and (vi):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(3x+[-2+\\sqrt{4+9}]y=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 3x+[-2+\\sqrt{13}]y=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(3x+[-2-\\sqrt{4+9}]y=0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 3x+[-2-\\sqrt{13}]y=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "These two lines are perpendicular, since a+b = 3−3 = 0."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Native homogeneous-equation derivations and worked examples; definition (i) on 207 remains faint", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 109, "side": "left", "reason": "Enlarged discriminant, angle branches and both final y factors visually reviewed; Word copy does not resolve faint definition"}}, {"file": "109_page_2.jpg", "page": 210, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Exercise 7.4"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "1. In each case, find the point of intersection P(x,y) of the pair of lines: (a) 2x+4y−10 = 0, 5x−3y+1 = 0; (b) 2x+y−8 = 0, 3x+2y−2 = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "2. Show that the following lines are concurrent. If the lines are concurrent, then find out the point at which the given lines make concurrency: (a) x−y−2 = 0, 2x−y−5 = 0, 11x−5y−28 = 0; (b) x+2y−3 = 0, 2x−y+4 = 0, x+4y−7 = 0; (c) 3x+2y−1 = 0, 2x−3y+4 = 0, x+y−2 = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "3. If ABC is a triangle with vertices A(0,0), B(8,6) and C(12,0), show that (a) the right bisectors of triangle ABC are concurrent; (b) the altitudes are concurrent; (c) the medians are concurrent."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "4. Find the area of the triangular region whose vertices are: (a) P₁(0,0), P₂(2,4), P₃(−2,2); (b) P₁(−1,−2), P₂(2,5), P₃(5,2); (c) P₁(4,−5), P₂(5,−6), P₃(3,1)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "5. Find the area bounded by triangle ABC whose vertices are: (a) A(−3,6), B(3,2), C(6,0); (b) A(−2,4), B(3,−6), C(1,−2); (c) Are the vertices in parts a and b of triangle ABC collinear?"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "6. Find two first degree straight lines in x and y when the second degree homogeneous equations are: (a) 3x²−2xy−5y² = 0; (b) 4x²−9xy+5y² = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "7. Show that the two first degree straight lines in x and y are coincident, perpendicular or neither when represented by: (a) x²+5xy−y² = 0; (b) 2x²−xy−y² = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "8. Find a joint equation of the straight line that passes through the origin and (a) perpendicular to the lines represented by 3x²−7xy+2y² = 0; (b) perpendicular to the lines represented by x²−2tanθxy−y² = 0; (c) perpendicular to the lines represented by ax²+2hxy+by² = 0."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Question 2(c) is not concurrent with the printed coefficients. The learning solution demonstrates the nonzero third-line residual without changing the question. Question 8 asks for perpendicular lines in all three parts; its singular “straight line” wording describes a joint line-pair equation."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "All eight source groups transcribed with exact coordinates and independently checked progressive solutions", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 109, "side": "right", "reason": "All coefficients visually checked, including perpendicular request in Question 8(a) and failed concurrency in 2(c)"}}, {"file": "110_page_1.jpg", "page": 211, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Review Exercise 7"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "1. Choose the correct option."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "i. The distance between A(7,5) and B(−5,−7) is: (a) 2√37 (b) 12√2 (c) 2√2 (d) 0"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "ii. To find the mid point of a line we use the formula: (a) \\(((x_1-x_2)/2,(y_1-y_2)/2)\\) (b) \\(((m_1x_2-m_2x_1)/2,(m_1y_2-m_2y_1)/2)\\) (c) \\(((x_1+x_2)/2,(y_1+y_2)/2)\\) (d) \\(((m_1x_2+m_2x_1)/2,(m_1y_2+m_2y_1)/2)\\)"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "iii. For points (a,b) and (5,7), the slope of the line is: (a) \\((7-b)/(5-a)\\) (b) 5/7 (c) 2/3 (d) −2/3"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "iv. The y intercept of 2x+4y = −6 is: (a) 3/2 (b) −3/2 (c) 2/3 (d) −2/3"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "v. \\((x-x_1)/(x_2-x_1)=(y-y_1)/(y_2-y_1)\\) is a: (a) slope intercept form (b) two point form (c) point slop form (d) double intercept form"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "vi. The normal form of an equation is: (a) x cosθ+y sinθ = p (b) x cosθ−y sinθ = p (c) x sinθ+y tanθ = 0 (d) x cosθ−y sinθ = 0"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "vii. Two given lines are perpendicular if their slopes m₁ and m₂ are: (a) m₁+m₂ = 1 (b) m₂−m₁ = 0 (c) m₁m₂ = 1 (d) m₁m₂ = −1"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "viii. 5x+7y = 0 is a: (a) homogeneous linear equation (b) non-homogeneous lines equation (c) quadratic equation (d) only linear equation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "ix. The area of a triangular region is A(4,5), B(−7,4) and C(3,1) is: (a) 31 (b) −31 (c) 47 (d) −47"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "x. The angle between two pair of lines can be calculated by using the formula: (a) \\(2\\sqrt{h^2+ab}/(a+b)\\) (b) \\((2-\\sqrt{a^2+hb})/(a+b)\\) (c) \\(2\\sqrt{h^2+pq}/(a-b)\\) (d) \\(2\\sqrt{h^2-ab}/(a+b)\\)"}, {"type": "heading", "text": "History"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Rene Descartes was a French mathematician, Philosopher and scientist. He was a creative mathematician of first order. He developed the techniques that made possible algebraic or analytic geometry. In metaphysics, he provided arguments for the existence of God, to show that the essence of matter is extension and that the essence of mind is thought. He claimed early on to possess a special method, that was variously exhibited in mathematics."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Descartes has been called the father of analytic geometry. He laid the foundation for 17th century continental rationalism. Because of his contribution Descartes was well versed in mathematics. Descartes’s vast influence in mathematics is equally apparent the Cartesian coordinate system named after him. He was also considered one of the key figure in the scientific revolution."}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/descartes-source211.jpg", "caption": "Rene Descartes (1596–1650)", "alt": "Original source portrait of Rene Descartes with printed lifespan"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Question (ix) has no correct printed option; (iii) excludes vertical/coincident endpoints and (x) displays a tangent expression rather than an angle. All source choices are retained."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "All forty source review choices, source history portrait and complete native summary reviewed", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 110, "side": "left", "reason": "Enlarged choices and summary formulas checked, including omitted distance absolute value and incorrect area choices"}}, {"file": "110_page_2.jpg", "page": 212, "title": "PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Summary"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The distance from point P(x₁,y₁) to point Q(x₂,y₂) in the coordinate plane is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=|PQ|=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If L₁ and L₂ are two lines having slopes m₁ and m₂, then these two lines are (a) parallel if and only if they have the same slopes: m₁ = m₂; (b) perpendicular if and only if the product of their slopes equals −1: m₁m₂ = −1."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The equation of a straight line parallel to the x-axis and at a distance a from it, is y = a. The equation of the x-axis is y = 0 and the vector equation of x-axis is r·j = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The equation of a straight line parallel to the y-axis and at a distance b from it, is x = b. The equation of the y-axis is x = 0 and the vector equation of y-axis is r·i = 0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The standard forms of the line are the following:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y=mx+c,\\quad\\mathrm{Slope\\! -\\! Intercept\\ form}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-y_1=m(x-x_1),\\quad\\mathrm{Point\\! -\\! Slope\\ form}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(y-y_1=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}(x-x_1),\\quad\\mathrm{Two\\! -\\! Point\\ form}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac xa+\\frac yb=1,\\quad\\mathrm{Double\\! -\\! Intercept\\ form}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{x-x_1}{\\cos\\theta}=\\frac{y-y_1}{\\sin\\theta}=r,\\quad\\mathrm{Symmetric\\ Form}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x\\cos\\theta+y\\sin\\theta=p,\\quad\\mathrm{Normal\\ Form}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The standard form of a line is ax+by+c = 0. For c = 0, the line is homogeneous that passes through the origin. For c ≠ 0, the line is nonhomogeneous that does not pass through the origin."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The perpendicular distance from a line ax+by+c = 0 to a point P(x₁,y₁) is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(d=\\frac{ax_1+by_1+c}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The angle between the two lines y = m₁x+c₁ and y = m₂x+c₂ is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta=\\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The general equation of a straight line that passes through the point of intersection of the lines a₁x+b₁y+c₁ = 0 and a₂x+b₂y+c₂ = 0 is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\((a_1x+b_1y+c_1)+\\lambda(a_2x+b_2y+c_2)=0,\\quad\\lambda\\ \\mathrm{constant}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The condition of concurrency of the three lines is:"}, {"type": "determinant", "rows": [["\\(a_1\\)", "\\(b_1\\)", "\\(c_1\\)"], ["\\(a_2\\)", "\\(b_2\\)", "\\(c_2\\)"], ["\\(a_3\\)", "\\(b_3\\)", "\\(c_3\\)"]], "suffix": "= 0", "prefix": "", "caption": "Source concurrency determinant"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "An equation of the form ax²+2hxy+by² = 0, a ≠ 0, b, c are constants is called a homogeneous equation of second degree in x and y when the sum of the indices of x and y in every term is the same, the sum being 2."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The angle between two homogeneous straight lines y = m₁x and y = m₂x is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\tan\\theta=\\frac{2\\sqrt{h^2-ab}}{a+b}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "i. The given two straight lines are perpendicular, if the angle between them is 90° that makes a+b = 0. ii. The given two straight lines are coinciding if the angle between them is zero that makes h² = ab."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "These are the printed summary statements. Geometric point-to-line distance needs an absolute numerator. A distance a from the x-axis allows y = ±a, and similarly x = ±b. Slope formulas require finite slopes; intercept and symmetric quotients require nonzero denominators. Normal form takes an oriented unit normal and p ≥ 0. A line requires (a,b) ≠ (0,0). The finite-λ pencil omits L₂ itself. A zero determinant alone also includes parallel lines; a nonparallel pair and a common-point test establish finite concurrency. Quadratic constants are a,h,b, and a = 0 is possible for a homogeneous quadratic. Real line-pair angle formulas require D = h²−ab ≥ 0 and explicit angle branches; the unsigned formula uses |a+b|. The lessons give these conditions and the corresponding derivations without altering the printed statements."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "All forty source review choices, source history portrait and complete native summary reviewed", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 110, "side": "right", "reason": "Enlarged choices and summary formulas checked, including omitted distance absolute value and incorrect area choices"}}];
MATH_12_DATA[6].sourceObjectives=[["7.1 Division of a line segment", ["Recall distance formula to calculate distance between two points given in Cartesian plane.", "Find coordinates of a point that divides the line segment in given ratio (internally and externally).", "Show that the medians and angle bisectors of a triangle are concurrent."]], ["7.2 Slope of a straight line", ["Define the slope of a line.", "Derive formula to find the slope of a line passing through two points.", "Find the condition that two straight lines with given slopes may be parallel to each other, or perpendicular to each other."]], ["7.3 Equation of a straight line parallel to Co-ordinate axes", ["Find the equation of a straight line parallel to y-axis and at a distance a from it, or x-axis and at a distance b from it."]], ["7.4 Standard form of equation of a straight line", ["Define intercepts of a straight line. Derive equation of a straight line in slope-intercept form, point-slope form, two-point form, intercepts form, symmetric form, normal form.", "Show that a linear equation in two variables represents a straight line.", "Reduce the general form of the equation of a straight line to the other standard forms."]], ["7.5 Distance of a point from a line", ["Recognize a point with respect to position of a line.", "Find the perpendicular distance from a point to the given straight lines."]], ["7.6 Angle between lines", ["Find the angle between two coplanar intersecting straight lines.", "Find the equation of family of lines passing through the point of intersection of two given lines.", "Calculate angles of the triangle when the slopes of the sides are given."]], ["7.7 Concurrency of straight lines", ["Find the condition of concurrency of three straight lines.", "Find the equation of median, altitude and right bisector of a triangle.", "Show that three right bisectors, three medians, or three altitudes, of a triangle are concurrent."]], ["7.8 Area of a triangular region", ["Find area of a triangular region whose vertices are given."]], ["7.9 Homogenous equation", ["Recognize homogeneous linear and quadratic equations in two variables.", "Investigate that the 2nd degree homogeneous equation in two variables x and y represents a pair of straight lines through the origin and find acute angle between them."]]];MATH_12_DATA[6].sourceIntroductionBlocks=[{"type": "heading", "text": "Introduction"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "We are familiar about Cartesian coordinate system, we have learnt about it in our previous classes. This Cartesian coordinate system may be helpful to know the slope formula, Pythagoras theorem and distance formula. In this lesson we will learn in details and write the equations involving arbitrary points. Most of the geometric ideas can be expressed using algebraic equations. Analytic geometry is defined as:"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "“The study of relationship between geometry and algebra is called analytic geometry”."}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/figure-7-1.jpg", "caption": "Figure 7.1", "alt": "Source illustration of a cyclist ascending a sloping line beside a shaded right triangle"}];
MATH_12_DATA[6].sections.unshift({id:'sec-7-introduction',title:'Introduction to Analytic Geometry',sourceBlocks:MATH_12_DATA[6].sourceIntroductionBlocks,theory:MATH_12_DATA[6].sourceIntroductionBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n'),sourceReferences:[{page:178,jpg:'077_page_2.jpg',verification:'JPG/PDF and duplicate compared visually'}]});
const math12StraightExamples=[{"num": "1", "title": "Distance Between Two Points", "question": "Find the distance between P(3,−2) and Q(−1,−5).", "steps": ["Identify x₁ = 3, y₁ = −2, x₂ = −1 and y₂ = −5.", "The horizontal displacement is x₂−x₁ = −1−3 = −4.", "The vertical displacement is y₂−y₁ = −5−(−2) = −3.", "Use d = √[(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²].", "Square the displacements: (−4)² = 16 and (−3)² = 9.", "Their sum is 25, so the nonnegative distance is √25 = 5.", "Check with a 3–4–5 right triangle; distance is unchanged if the endpoints are reversed."], "answer": "5 units.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 180, "jpg": "078_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Complete source example checked visually"}]}, {"num": "2", "title": "Section Formula", "question": "Find the dividing point for (a) P(1,2), Q(3,4), ratio 5:7; (b) P(3,4), Q(−6,2), signed ratio 3:−2.", "steps": ["Use R = (m₂P+m₁Q)/(m₁+m₂), which handles internal division and signed external weights.", "For (a), choose m₁ = 5 and m₂ = 7; the denominator is 12.", "Compute x = (7·1+5·3)/12 = 22/12 = 11/6.", "Compute y = (7·2+5·4)/12 = 34/12 = 17/6.", "For (b), use the signed weights m₁ = 3, m₂ = −2, with denominator 1.", "Compute x = (−2·3+3·(−6))/1 = −24 and y = (−2·4+3·2)/1 = −2.", "Check R−P = 3(Q−P): (−27,−6) = 3(−9,−2). Thus R is beyond Q, with signed PR:RQ = 3:−2."], "answer": "(a) (11/6,17/6); (b) (−24,−2).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 181, "jpg": "079_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Source question and full working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "3", "title": "Centroid", "question": "Find the centroid of A(3,−5), B(−7,4), C(10,−2).", "steps": ["The centroid is the common point of the three medians.", "Use G = ((x₁+x₂+x₃)/3,(y₁+y₂+y₃)/3).", "Sum the horizontal coordinates: 3−7+10 = 6.", "Divide by 3 to get x = 2.", "Sum the vertical coordinates: −5+4−2 = −3.", "Divide by 3 to get y = −1, hence G = (2,−1).", "Check the midpoint of BC is (3/2,1). Moving two thirds from A toward this midpoint gives (2,−1), confirming the median ratio 2:1."], "answer": "(2,−1).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 182, "jpg": "079_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Source question and full working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "4", "title": "Largest Circular Pool", "question": "Find the largest circular swimming pool that fits in a triangular backyard.", "steps": ["A fitting circular pool must lie entirely inside the triangle.", "Construct the internal angle bisectors of two corners.", "Their intersection I is the incenter; the third internal bisector passes through it.", "Drop a perpendicular from I to a side; its length r is the same distance to each side.", "Draw the circle with center I and radius r; it is tangent to all three sides.", "To verify it is largest, let Δ be the triangle area and s its semiperimeter. A contained disk of radius ρ has its center at distance at least ρ from every side, so Δ ≥ sρ. The incircle satisfies Δ = sr, hence ρ ≤ r.", "For the ideal geometric model the incircle gives the largest radius; no numerical backyard dimensions are supplied."], "answer": "The incircle, centered at the intersection of the internal angle bisectors.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 183, "jpg": "080_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Source example and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "5", "title": "Incenter Distance", "question": "Find JO in the source triangle LMN, with incenter O, LO = 13 and LH = 12.", "steps": ["O is the common point of the three internal angle bisectors, hence the incenter.", "The perpendicular distances OI, OJ and OH to the three sides are equal.", "Triangle LHO is right-angled at H, with hypotenuse LO = 13 and leg LH = 12.", "Apply LO² = LH²+HO².", "Thus HO² = 13²−12² = 169−144 = 25.", "Take the nonnegative length HO = 5.", "Since JO = HO, JO = 5 units; the unused ON = 8 label is not needed."], "answer": "5 units.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 183, "jpg": "080_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Source example and working visually checked"}], "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.6", "diagram": {"type": "source-incenter-length", "title": "Figure 7.6 — Triangle LMN, its incenter O and perpendicular feet I, J and H; LO = 13, LH = 12, ON = 8"}}]}, {"num": "6", "title": "Slope Between Two Points", "question": "Find the slope through (a) E(2,4), F(4,6); (b) M(3,1), N(−1,3).", "steps": ["Use m = (y₂−y₁)/(x₂−x₁); both horizontal differences are nonzero.", "(a) The vertical difference is 6−4 = 2.", "The horizontal difference is 4−2 = 2, so m = 2/2 = 1.", "(b) The vertical difference is 3−1 = 2.", "The horizontal difference is −1−3 = −4, so m = 2/(−4) = −1/2.", "Reversing both endpoint orders negates numerator and denominator together, leaving each slope unchanged.", "Thus (a) rises from left to right and (b) falls from left to right."], "answer": "(a) 1; (b) −1/2.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 184, "jpg": "080_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Full source question and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "7", "title": "Line Equation and Intercepts", "question": "Find the equation parallel to the y-axis, 5 units to its right.", "steps": ["A line parallel to the y-axis is vertical.", "Every point on it has the same x-coordinate a.", "The y-axis has x = 0.", "Moving 5 units to its right gives a = 5.", "Thus the equation is x = 5; y can be any real number.", "Its horizontal distance to the y-axis is |5| = 5 and its finite slope is undefined."], "answer": "x = 5.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 186, "jpg": "081_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source example words and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "8", "title": "Line Equation and Intercepts", "question": "Find the x and y intercepts of 2x+4y+6 = 0.", "steps": ["At the x-axis, put y = 0.", "Then 2x+6 = 0, so x = −3; the crossing point is (−3,0).", "At the y-axis, put x = 0.", "Then 4y+6 = 0, so y = −6/4 = −3/2; the crossing point is (0,−3/2).", "Check (−3,0): 2(−3)+4(0)+6 = 0.", "Check (0,−3/2): 0+4(−3/2)+6 = 0.", "The signed intercepts are −3 and −3/2; their distances from the origin would be 3 and 3/2."], "answer": "x-intercept −3, y-intercept −3/2.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 186, "jpg": "081_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source example words and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "9", "title": "Line Equation and Intercepts", "question": "Determine the slopes of (a) x−y = 5; (b) 2x+3y = 6.", "steps": ["Rewrite each line in the form y = mx+c.", "(a) x−y = 5 gives −y = 5−x.", "Multiply by −1: y = x−5, hence m = 1.", "(b) 2x+3y = 6 gives 3y = −2x+6.", "Divide by 3: y = −(2/3)x+2, hence m = −2/3.", "The intercept terms −5 and 2 do not affect either slope.", "Check a horizontal increase of 3: y rises by 3 in (a) and falls by 2 in (b)."], "answer": "(a) 1; (b) −2/3.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 187, "jpg": "082_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source example words and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "10", "title": "Line Equation and Intercepts", "question": "Find an equation with slope 4 and y-intercept 6.", "steps": ["Use the slope-intercept form y = mx+c.", "The given slope is m = 4.", "The signed y-intercept is c = 6.", "Substitute both into the form: y = 4x+6.", "At x = 0 the point is (0,6), as required.", "An increase of 1 in x increases y by 4, confirming the slope."], "answer": "y = 4x+6.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 187, "jpg": "082_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source example words and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "11", "title": "Line Equation and Intercepts", "question": "Find an equation with slope 4 through (2,4).", "steps": ["Use y−y₁ = m(x−x₁).", "Set x₁ = 2, y₁ = 4 and m = 4.", "The equation is y−4 = 4(x−2).", "Expand: y−4 = 4x−8, giving y = 4x−4.", "Move terms to one side: 4x−y−4 = 0.", "Check the point: 4·2−4−4 = 0.", "The coefficient of x in y = 4x−4 is 4, confirming the slope."], "answer": "y = 4x−4, or 4x−y−4 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 187, "jpg": "082_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source example words and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "12", "title": "Standard Line Form", "question": "Find the line through P(−1,−2) and Q(−5,0).", "steps": ["The two x-coordinates differ, so compute m = (0−(−2))/(−5−(−1)).", "The numerator is 2 and the denominator is −4, giving m = −1/2.", "Use the point-slope form with P: y+2 = −(x+1)/2.", "Multiply by 2: 2y+4 = −x−1.", "Rearrange to obtain x+2y+5 = 0.", "At P: −1−4+5 = 0.", "At Q: −5+0+5 = 0; both points satisfy the result."], "answer": "x+2y+5 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 188, "jpg": "082_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Complete source question and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "13", "title": "Standard Line Form", "question": "Find the line whose intercept points are (3,0) and (0,4).", "steps": ["The signed intercepts are a = 3 and b = 4, both nonzero.", "Use x/a+y/b = 1.", "Substitute: x/3+y/4 = 1.", "Multiply by 12 to get 4x+3y = 12.", "Rearrange: 4x+3y−12 = 0.", "Check (3,0): 12 = 12; check (0,4): 12 = 12."], "answer": "4x+3y−12 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 188, "jpg": "082_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Complete source question and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "14", "title": "Standard Line Form", "question": "Find the line with inclination 45° through (2,√2).", "steps": ["Inclination 45° gives direction (cos45°,sin45°) = (1/√2,1/√2).", "Both direction components are nonzero, so use (x−2)/cos45° = (y−√2)/sin45°.", "Cross-multiply: sin45°(x−2) = cos45°(y−√2).", "Cancel the common factor 1/√2 to obtain x−2 = y−√2.", "Rearrange: x−y−2+√2 = 0.", "At (2,√2), the left side is 2−√2−2+√2 = 0.", "Solving for y gives y = x−2+√2, whose slope 1 equals tan45°."], "answer": "x−y−2+√2 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 189, "jpg": "083_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Complete source question and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "15", "title": "Standard Line Form", "question": "Find the line at perpendicular distance 3 from the origin, whose normal makes angle 120°.", "steps": ["Use the normal form x cosθ+y sinθ = p.", "Here p = 3 and θ = 120°, describing the normal direction rather than the line inclination.", "cos120° = −1/2 and sin120° = √3/2.", "Substitute: −x/2+√3 y/2 = 3.", "Multiply by 2: −x+√3 y = 6, or x−√3 y+6 = 0.", "Check its distance from the origin: |6|/√(1+3) = 3.", "The nearest point is 3(cos120°,sin120°) = (−3/2,3√3/2), which satisfies the equation."], "answer": "x−√3 y+6 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 189, "jpg": "083_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Complete source question and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "16", "title": "Point Position and Distance", "question": "Determine whether P(10,−6) is above or below 9x+10y−3 = 0, and whether P and the origin are on the same or opposite sides.", "steps": ["Set F(x,y) = 9x+10y−3. The coefficient of y is b = 10 > 0.", "Evaluate F(10,−6) = 90−60−3 = 27 > 0.", "Because b > 0, this positive result means P is above the line.", "Check directly: at x = 10, the line has y = (3−90)/10 = −87/10 = −8.7, while P has y = −6 > −8.7.", "At the origin, F(0,0) = −3 < 0.", "The product of the two evaluated values is 27(−3) = −81 < 0, so the signs are opposite.", "Therefore P is above the line and P and the origin lie on opposite sides."], "answer": "P is above the line; P and the origin are on opposite sides.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 193, "pageEnd": 194, "verification": "Complete source question and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "17", "title": "Point Position and Distance", "question": "Find the perpendicular distance from P(2,3) to 7x+3y−9 = 0.", "steps": ["Read the coefficients a = 7, b = 3, c = −9.", "The normal vector (7,3) is nonzero and has length √(49+9) = √58.", "Evaluate the line expression at P: 7·2+3·3−9 = 14.", "Use d = |ax₁+by₁+c|/√(a²+b²).", "Thus d = 14/√58 units, a positive distance.", "Independently, the perpendicular foot is P−(14/58)(7,3) = (9/29,66/29).", "Check this foot lies on the line: 7(9/29)+3(66/29)−9 = 261/29−9 = 0. Its displacement from P has length 14/√58."], "answer": "14/√58 units.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 194, "pageEnd": 194, "verification": "Complete source question and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "18", "title": "Angle Between Lines", "question": "Find the angle from 7x+3y−9 = 0 to 5x−2y+2 = 0.", "steps": ["Use slopes m₁ = −7/3 and m₂ = 5/2.", "Compute the slope difference m₁−m₂ = −14/6−15/6 = −29/6.", "Compute 1+m₁m₂ = 1−35/6 = −29/6, which is nonzero.", "For the smaller unsigned angle, tan α = |(m₁−m₂)/(1+m₁m₂)| = 1.", "Thus α = 45° and the supplementary intersection angle is 135°.", "Check with directions d₁ = (3,−7) and d₂ = (2,5): |d₁·d₂| = |6−35| = 29.", "Their lengths have product √58·√29 = 29√2, so cos α = 1/√2, confirming 45°.", "The source’s directed wording lacks orientation and rotation sense; the verified result is the acute angle between the unoriented lines, with the supplement stated separately."], "answer": "Acute angle 45°; supplementary angle 135°.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 196, "jpg": "102_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Full source question and working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "19", "title": "Line Family", "question": "Find the family through 2x−3y+4 = 0 and 2x+y−1 = 0; select (a) a line parallel to slope −2/3 and (b) a line perpendicular to 4x+3y−1 = 0.", "steps": ["Form L₁+λL₂ = (2+2λ)x+(−3+λ)y+(4−λ) = 0. The full pencil also includes L₂ itself.", "For λ ≠ 3, its slope is −(2+2λ)/(−3+λ).", "(a) Set this slope equal to −2/3. Cross-multiply to obtain 6+6λ = −6+2λ, hence λ = −3.", "Substitute λ = −3: −4x−6y+7 = 0, equivalent to 4x+6y−7 = 0. Its slope is −2/3.", "(b) The reference line has slope −4/3, so a perpendicular line has slope 3/4.", "Setting −(2+2λ)/(−3+λ) = 3/4 gives −8−8λ = −9+3λ, hence λ = 1/11.", "Substitute and multiply by 11: 24x−32y+43 = 0, whose slope is 3/4.", "Solve the two original lines to get their intersection (−1/8,5/4). Substitution gives zero in both selected equations, independently checking that each belongs to the pencil."], "answer": "Family (2x−3y+4)+λ(2x+y−1) = 0, plus L₂; (a) 4x+6y−7 = 0; (b) 24x−32y+43 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 197, "jpg": "103_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Complete source question, coefficients and vertices checked"}]}, {"num": "20", "title": "Triangle Interior Angles", "question": "Find the interior angles of A(−2,−3), B(4,−1), C(2,3).", "steps": ["At A, take rays AB = (6,2) and AC = (4,6). Their dot product is 36 and cross magnitude is 28.", "Thus A = atan2(28,36) = atan(7/9) ≈ 37.874984°.", "At B, take BA = (−6,−2) and BC = (−2,4). Their dot product is 4 and cross magnitude is 28.", "Thus B = atan2(28,4) = atan(7) ≈ 81.869898°.", "At C, take CA = (−4,−6) and CB = (2,−4). Their dot product is 16 and cross magnitude is 28.", "Thus C = atan2(28,16) = atan(7/4) ≈ 60.255119°.", "All three are positive and their sum is 180° (up to displayed rounding), so they are the interior angles.", "The source’s negative principal inverse tangents are retained in the lesson but are not used as interior-angle answers. Using vertex rays avoids sign and supplementary-angle ambiguity."], "answer": "A ≈ 37.874984°, B ≈ 81.869898°, C ≈ 60.255119°.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 198, "jpg": "103_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Complete source question, coefficients and vertices checked"}], "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.24", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.24 — exact source vertices A(−2,−3), B(4,−1), C(2,3)", "width": 500, "height": 370, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 180], [432, 180]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[212, 300], [212, 30]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[124, 270], [388, 210], [300, 90], [124, 270]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[168, 176], [168, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[256, 176], [256, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[300, 176], [300, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[344, 176], [344, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[388, 176], [388, 184]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 270], [216, 270]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 240], [216, 240]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 210], [216, 210]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 150], [216, 150]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 120], [216, 120]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 90], [216, 90]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[208, 60], [216, 60]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 440.79999999999995, "y": 180, "text": "x"}, {"x": 212, "y": 18.0, "text": "y"}, {"x": 102, "y": 287, "text": "A"}, {"x": 393, "y": 225, "text": "B"}, {"x": 305, "y": 82, "text": "C"}, {"x": 145, "y": 258, "text": "θ₁"}, {"x": 358, "y": 207, "text": "θ₂"}, {"x": 292, "y": 122, "text": "θ₃"}, {"x": 164, "y": 200, "text": "-1"}, {"x": 252, "y": 200, "text": "1"}, {"x": 296, "y": 200, "text": "2"}, {"x": 340, "y": 200, "text": "3"}, {"x": 384, "y": 200, "text": "4"}, {"x": 220, "y": 275, "text": "-3"}, {"x": 220, "y": 245, "text": "-2"}, {"x": 220, "y": 215, "text": "-1"}, {"x": 220, "y": 155, "text": "1"}, {"x": 220, "y": 125, "text": "2"}, {"x": 220, "y": 95, "text": "3"}, {"x": 220, "y": 65, "text": "4"}], "points": []}}]}, {"num": "21", "title": "Finite Concurrency", "question": "Show that x+4y+3 = 0, 5x−4y−5 = 0 and 2x+2y+1 = 0 are concurrent, and find their common point.", "steps": ["Use the first two lines: x+4y = −3 and 5x−4y = 5. Their coefficient determinant is −4−20 = −24 ≠ 0, so they have a unique finite intersection.", "Add the equations to obtain 6x = 2, giving x = 1/3.", "Substitute in x+4y = −3: 4y = −3−1/3 = −10/3, hence y = −5/6.", "Check the first line: 1/3+4(−5/6)+3 = 0.", "Check the second line: 5/3−4(−5/6)−5 = 0.", "Check the third line: 2/3+2(−5/6)+1 = 2/3−5/3+1 = 0.", "Thus all three pass through G(1/3,−5/6), proving finite concurrency.", "The three-by-three determinant also equals 6−60+54 = 0, consistent with the independently verified common point. Determinant zero alone would require the finite-point qualification explained in the lesson."], "answer": "G(1/3,−5/6).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 201, "jpg": "105_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Full source question, matrix reduction and common-point working visually checked"}]}, {"num": "22", "title": "Triangle Medians", "question": "Find the medians of A(8,−5), B(6,5), C(−6,9).", "steps": ["A′, the midpoint of BC, is ((6−6)/2,(5+9)/2) = (0,7).", "The median from A is y+5 = (−3/2)(x−8), giving 3x+2y−14 = 0.", "B′, the midpoint of CA, is ((−6+8)/2,(9−5)/2) = (1,2).", "The median from B is y−2 = (3/5)(x−1), giving 3x−5y+7 = 0.", "C′, the midpoint of AB, is ((8+6)/2,(−5+5)/2) = (7,0).", "The median from C is y = −9(x−7)/13, giving 9x+13y−63 = 0.", "Each equation passes through its stated vertex and opposite-side midpoint. The common centroid is ((8+6−6)/3,(−5+5+9)/3) = (8/3,3), which satisfies all three.", "The source’s AA′ equation uses an incorrect constant; the corrected median is derived from the actual points and checked independently."], "answer": "AA′: 3x+2y−14 = 0; BB′: 3x−5y+7 = 0; CC′: 9x+13y−63 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 202, "pageEnd": 202, "verification": "All source vertices, equations and source errors visually checked"}], "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.25", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.25 — source triangle with three medians and midpoint labels", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[110, 260], [430, 260], [270, 55], [110, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[110, 260], [350, 157.5]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[430, 260], [190, 157.5]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[270, 55], [270, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 72, "y": 280, "text": "A(8,−5)"}, {"x": 408, "y": 280, "text": "B(6,5)"}, {"x": 255, "y": 42, "text": "C(−6,9)"}, {"x": 356, "y": 157, "text": "A′"}, {"x": 163, "y": 157, "text": "B′"}, {"x": 260, "y": 283, "text": "C′"}], "points": []}}]}, {"num": "23", "title": "Triangle Altitudes", "question": "Find the altitudes of A(−7,4), B(9,6), C(7,−10).", "steps": ["AB has slope (6−4)/(9−(−7)) = 1/8, so the altitude from C has slope −8.", "Through C, y+10 = −8(x−7), giving 8x+y−46 = 0.", "BC has slope (−10−6)/(7−9) = 8, so the altitude from A has slope −1/8.", "Through A, y−4 = −(x+7)/8, giving x+8y−25 = 0.", "CA has slope (4−(−10))/(−7−7) = −1, so the altitude from B has slope 1.", "Through B, y−6 = x−9, giving x−y−3 = 0.", "Each equation contains its vertex, and each altitude slope times the corresponding opposite-side slope is −1.", "Solving x+8y = 25 and x−y = 3 gives the orthocenter H(49/9,22/9). It also satisfies 8x+y = 46.", "The source’s AA′ final constant −27 and its mixed-coordinate AC quotient are retained in the lesson and corrected in these verified steps."], "answer": "From A: x+8y−25 = 0; from B: x−y−3 = 0; from C: 8x+y−46 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 202, "pageEnd": 203, "verification": "All source vertices, equations and source errors visually checked"}], "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.26", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.26 — source altitude construction, with actual perpendicular feet in a schematic triangle", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[80, 110], [425, 60], [340, 285], [80, 110]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[80, 110], [365.3824546240277, 217.81114952463267]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[425, 60], [294.27335199796386, 254.22244846016798]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[340, 285], [309.8107385311664, 76.69409586504834]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 40, "y": 106, "text": "A(−7,4)"}, {"x": 400, "y": 48, "text": "B(9,6)"}, {"x": 318, "y": 307, "text": "C(7,−10)"}, {"x": 371.3824546240277, "y": 224.81114952463267, "text": "A′"}, {"x": 271.27335199796386, "y": 261.222448460168, "text": "B′"}, {"x": 299.8107385311664, "y": 66.69409586504834, "text": "C′"}], "points": []}}]}, {"num": "24", "title": "Perpendicular Bisectors", "question": "Find the perpendicular bisectors for A(−7,4), B(10,8), C(6,−12).", "steps": ["A point X(x,y) on the bisector of endpoints U,V satisfies (V−U)·(X−(U+V)/2) = 0. This handles vertical and horizontal sides without dividing by a slope.", "For AB, the midpoint is (3/2,6) and V−U = (17,4).", "Thus 17(x−3/2)+4(y−6) = 0; multiplying by 2 gives 34x+8y−99 = 0.", "For BC, the midpoint is (8,−2) and C−B = (−4,−20).", "Thus −4(x−8)−20(y+2) = 0, giving x+5y+2 = 0.", "For AC, the midpoint is (−1/2,−4) and C−A = (13,−16).", "Thus 13(x+1/2)−16(y+4) = 0; multiplying by 2 gives 26x−32y−115 = 0.", "Each equation contains the midpoint and has a normal parallel to the side, proving perpendicularity.", "The common point is (511/162,−167/162); substituting it in all three equations gives zero. The source’s AB sign and AC denominator errors would fail these checks.", "The source figure labels A(−8,4); this solution follows the question’s A(−7,4) and makes the mismatch explicit."], "answer": "AB: 34x+8y−99 = 0; BC: x+5y+2 = 0; AC: 26x−32y−115 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 203, "pageEnd": 203, "verification": "All source vertices, equations and source errors visually checked"}], "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "Figure 7.27", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Figure 7.27 — original source triangle labels, including A(−8,4) that differs from the question", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[100, 260], [430, 245], [270, 70], [100, 260]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 53, "y": 280, "text": "A(−8,4)"}, {"x": 407, "y": 266, "text": "B(10,8)"}, {"x": 246, "y": 55, "text": "C(6,−12)"}], "points": []}}]}, {"num": "25", "title": "Concurrent Perpendicular Bisectors", "question": "For A(0,0), B(8,6), C(12,0), show that the perpendicular bisectors of all three sides are concurrent.", "steps": ["The midpoint of BC is (10,3), and C−B = (4,−6). Its bisector is 4(x−10)−6(y−3) = 0, or 2x−3y−11 = 0.", "The midpoint of CA is (6,0), and A−C = (−12,0). Its bisector is x = 6.", "The midpoint of AB is (4,3), and B−A = (8,6). Its bisector is 8(x−4)+6(y−3) = 0, or 4x+3y−25 = 0.", "Intersect x = 6 with 2x−3y−11 = 0: 12−3y−11 = 0, giving y = 1/3.", "At G(6,1/3), the third bisector gives 24+1−25 = 0, so all three are concurrent at this finite point.", "The squared distances from G to A, B and C are respectively 36+1/9, 4+289/9 and 36+1/9; all equal 325/9.", "Thus G is the circumcenter, equidistant from all three vertices, independently confirming the bisectors.", "The source determinant is also zero: 1728−144−1584 = 0. The directly found finite intersection supplies the condition that determinant zero alone cannot guarantee for arbitrary parallel lines."], "answer": "Concurrent at the circumcenter G(6,1/3).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"pageStart": 204, "pageEnd": 205, "verification": "All source vertices, determinant entries and expansion visually checked"}]}, {"num": "26", "title": "Concurrent Altitudes", "question": "For A(0,0), B(8,6), C(12,0), show that the three altitudes are concurrent.", "steps": ["BC has direction (4,−6). The altitude from A is 4x−6y = 0, or 2x−3y = 0.", "CA is horizontal, so the altitude from B is vertical: x = 8.", "AB has direction (8,6). The altitude from C is 8(x−12)+6y = 0, or 4x+3y−48 = 0.", "Intersect x = 8 with 2x−3y = 0: y = 16/3.", "At H(8,16/3), the third equation gives 32+16−48 = 0, so all three altitudes share this finite point.", "The equations pass through their respective vertices and their line normals are the corresponding opposite-side directions, confirming perpendicularity.", "The source determinant also equals 2304−6·384 = 0, consistent with the explicit common-point check."], "answer": "Concurrent at the orthocenter H(8,16/3).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 206, "jpg": "107_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Complete source question, exact vertices and determinant calculation visually checked"}]}, {"num": "27", "title": "Triangle Area", "question": "Find the area of P₁(4,−5), P₂(5,−6), P₃(3,1).", "steps": ["Use the geometric-area formula A = |(P₂−P₁)×(P₃−P₁)|/2.", "The first displacement is P₂−P₁ = (1,−1).", "The second displacement is P₃−P₁ = (−1,6).", "The scalar cross product is 1·6−(−1)(−1) = 6−1 = 5.", "Take half its absolute value: A = |5|/2 = 5/2 square units.", "Independently expand the coordinate determinant: 4(−7)+5(6)+3(1) = −28+30+3 = 5, giving the same area.", "The nonzero result confirms that the three points are not collinear; reversing the vertex order would negate the determinant but preserve area."], "answer": "5/2 square units.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 206, "jpg": "107_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Complete source question, exact vertices and determinant calculation visually checked"}]}, {"num": "28", "title": "Homogeneous Line Pair", "question": "Find the separate lines represented by 5x²+3xy−8y² = 0.", "steps": ["Identify a = 5, h = 3/2 and b = −8.", "Compute D = h²−ab = 9/4+40 = 169/4.", "Thus √D = 13/2.", "The plus factor is 5x+(3/2+13/2)y = 5x+8y.", "The minus factor is 5x+(3/2−13/2)y = 5x−5y, equivalent to x−y.", "Check directly: (x−y)(5x+8y) = 5x²+3xy−8y².", "A product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so these two lines give the complete real zero set."], "answer": "5x+8y = 0 and x−y = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 208, "jpg": "108_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Complete source example and final linear factors visually checked"}]}, {"num": "29", "title": "Homogeneous Line Pair", "question": "Find the angle between the lines x²−xy−6y² = 0.", "steps": ["Factor the quadratic: (x−3y)(x+2y) = 0.", "The lines have slopes m₁ = 1/3 and m₂ = −1/2.", "Their slope difference magnitude is 5/6 and 1+m₁m₂ = 5/6.", "Hence tan α = 1 for the smaller unsigned angle α, giving α = 45°.", "The other intersection angle is 180°−45° = 135°.", "Independently, a = 1, h = −1/2, b = −6 gives D = 25/4 and atan2(5,5) = 45°.", "The source’s signed quotient is −1; its 135° answer uses a supplementary branch. The principal arctangent alone returns −45°."], "answer": "Smaller angle 45°; supplementary angle 135°.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 209, "jpg": "109_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Complete source example and final linear factors visually checked"}]}, {"num": "30", "title": "Homogeneous Line Pair", "question": "Find the two first degree straight lines represented by 3x²−4xy−3y² = 0 and determine whether the resultant lines are coincident or perpendicular.", "steps": ["Identify a = 3, h = −2, b = −3.", "Compute D = (−2)²−3(−3) = 13 > 0, so the two real lines are distinct.", "The plus line is 3x+(−2+√13)y = 0.", "The minus line is 3x+(−2−√13)y = 0.", "The product of their linear forms is 9x²−12xy−9y², equal to 3 times the source quadratic.", "The product of their y coefficients is (−2+√13)(−2−√13) = −9.", "Their slopes therefore have product 9/(−9) = −1, proving perpendicularity. Equivalently a+b = 0 gives a right angle."], "answer": "3x+(−2+√13)y = 0 and 3x+(−2−√13)y = 0; angle 90°.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 209, "jpg": "109_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Complete source example and final linear factors visually checked"}]}];
