const math12ReviewedEx71=[{"id": "ex-7-1-1", "qNo": "1", "question": "The three points are A(−1,3), B(2,1) and C(5,−1). Show that |AB|+|BC| = |AC|.", "steps": ["Use d(P,Q) = √[(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²].", "For AB the displacement is (3,−2), so |AB| = √(9+4) = √13.", "For BC the displacement is also (3,−2), so |BC| = √13.", "For AC the displacement is (6,−4), so |AC| = √(36+16) = √52 = 2√13.", "Therefore |AB|+|BC| = √13+√13 = 2√13 = |AC|.", "B = (A+C)/2, so B lies between A and C; this also explains the distance equality."], "solution": "Use d(P,Q) = √[(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²].\nFor AB the displacement is (3,−2), so |AB| = √(9+4) = √13.\nFor BC the displacement is also (3,−2), so |BC| = √13.\nFor AC the displacement is (6,−4), so |AC| = √(36+16) = √52 = 2√13.\nTherefore |AB|+|BC| = √13+√13 = 2√13 = |AC|.\nB = (A+C)/2, so B lies between A and C; this also explains the distance equality.", "answer": "|AB| = |BC| = √13 and |AC| = 2√13.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 183, "jpg": "080_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All five source question groups and exact coordinates visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-1-2", "qNo": "2", "question": "In each case, find the midpoint of the line segment PQ joining the two points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂): (a) P(10,20), Q(−12,−8); (b) P(a,−b), Q(−a,b); (c) P(1/2,−1/4), Q(3/5,4/7).", "steps": ["Use M = ((x₁+x₂)/2,(y₁+y₂)/2).", "(a) x = (10−12)/2 = −1 and y = (20−8)/2 = 6; M = (−1,6).", "(b) The opposite coordinates cancel: (a−a)/2 = 0 and (−b+b)/2 = 0, so M = (0,0).", "(c) x = (1/2+3/5)/2 = (5/10+6/10)/2 = 11/20.", "For (c), y = (−1/4+4/7)/2 = (−7/28+16/28)/2 = 9/56.", "Thus (c) M = (11/20,9/56); doubling each midpoint and subtracting P recovers Q."], "solution": "Use M = ((x₁+x₂)/2,(y₁+y₂)/2).\n(a) x = (10−12)/2 = −1 and y = (20−8)/2 = 6; M = (−1,6).\n(b) The opposite coordinates cancel: (a−a)/2 = 0 and (−b+b)/2 = 0, so M = (0,0).\n(c) x = (1/2+3/5)/2 = (5/10+6/10)/2 = 11/20.\nFor (c), y = (−1/4+4/7)/2 = (−7/28+16/28)/2 = 9/56.\nThus (c) M = (11/20,9/56); doubling each midpoint and subtracting P recovers Q.", "answer": "(a) (−1,6); (b) (0,0); (c) (11/20,9/56).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 183, "jpg": "080_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All five source question groups and exact coordinates visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-1-3", "qNo": "3", "question": "In each case, find the coordinates of the point R(x,y) which divides the line segment PQ joining the two points: (a) P(1,2), Q(3,4) in the ratio 5:7; (b) P(3,4), Q(−6,2) in the ratio 3:−2; (c) P(−6,7), Q(5,−4) in the ratio 2/7:1.", "steps": ["Use the signed-weight formula R = (m₂P+m₁Q)/(m₁+m₂), with nonzero denominator.", "(a) R = (7(1,2)+5(3,4))/12 = (22/12,34/12) = (11/6,17/6).", "(b) The ratio 3:−2 has opposite signed weights; R = (−2(3,4)+3(−6,2))/(3−2) = (−24,−2).", "For (b), R is beyond Q and R−P = 3(Q−P); do not apply the external-minus formula with a second negative sign.", "(c) Multiply both entries of 2/7:1 by 7 to obtain 2:7.", "R = (7(−6,7)+2(5,−4))/9 = ((−42+10)/9,(49−8)/9) = (−32/9,41/9).", "Check (c): R = P+(2/9)(Q−P), placing it within the segment with PR:RQ = 2:7."], "solution": "Use the signed-weight formula R = (m₂P+m₁Q)/(m₁+m₂), with nonzero denominator.\n(a) R = (7(1,2)+5(3,4))/12 = (22/12,34/12) = (11/6,17/6).\n(b) The ratio 3:−2 has opposite signed weights; R = (−2(3,4)+3(−6,2))/(3−2) = (−24,−2).\nFor (b), R is beyond Q and R−P = 3(Q−P); do not apply the external-minus formula with a second negative sign.\n(c) Multiply both entries of 2/7:1 by 7 to obtain 2:7.\nR = (7(−6,7)+2(5,−4))/9 = ((−42+10)/9,(49−8)/9) = (−32/9,41/9).\nCheck (c): R = P+(2/9)(Q−P), placing it within the segment with PR:RQ = 2:7.", "answer": "(a) (11/6,17/6); (b) (−24,−2); (c) (−32/9,41/9).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 183, "jpg": "080_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All five source question groups and exact coordinates visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-1-4", "qNo": "4", "question": "In each case, in what ratio is the line segment PQ (joining the two points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂)) divided by the point R(x,y): (a) P(8,10), Q(−12,6), R(−4/7,58/7); (b) P(−2,4), Q(3,6), R(4/5,3/5).", "steps": ["A dividing point must have R = P+λ(Q−P) with the same λ from both coordinates.", "(a) From x, λ = (−4/7−8)/(−12−8) = (−60/7)/(−20) = 3/7.", "From y, λ = (58/7−10)/(6−10) = (−12/7)/(−4) = 3/7.", "Thus PR:RQ = λ:(1−λ) = 3/7:4/7 = 3:4, internally.", "(b) From x, λ = (4/5+2)/(3+2) = 14/25.", "From y, λ = (3/5−4)/(6−4) = −17/10, which is different.", "Therefore R(4/5,3/5) is not on line PQ and cannot divide this segment in any ratio. The exact printed coordinates are retained; no guessed replacement is used."], "solution": "A dividing point must have R = P+λ(Q−P) with the same λ from both coordinates.\n(a) From x, λ = (−4/7−8)/(−12−8) = (−60/7)/(−20) = 3/7.\nFrom y, λ = (58/7−10)/(6−10) = (−12/7)/(−4) = 3/7.\nThus PR:RQ = λ:(1−λ) = 3/7:4/7 = 3:4, internally.\n(b) From x, λ = (4/5+2)/(3+2) = 14/25.\nFrom y, λ = (3/5−4)/(6−4) = −17/10, which is different.\nTherefore R(4/5,3/5) is not on line PQ and cannot divide this segment in any ratio. The exact printed coordinates are retained; no guessed replacement is used.", "answer": "(a) 3:4 internally; (b) no ratio exists for the printed point.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 183, "jpg": "080_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All five source question groups and exact coordinates visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-1-5", "qNo": "5", "question": "Find the centroid of the triangle ABC, whose vertices are the following: (a) A(4,−2), B(−2,4), C(5,5); (b) A(3,5), B(4,6), C(3,−1); (c) A(1,1), B(−2,−2), C(4,5).", "steps": ["The centroid is G = ((x₁+x₂+x₃)/3,(y₁+y₂+y₃)/3).", "(a) x = (4−2+5)/3 = 7/3 and y = (−2+4+5)/3 = 7/3.", "Therefore (a) G = (7/3,7/3).", "(b) x = (3+4+3)/3 = 10/3 and y = (5+6−1)/3 = 10/3.", "Therefore (b) G = (10/3,10/3).", "(c) x = (1−2+4)/3 = 1 and y = (1−2+5)/3 = 4/3.", "Therefore (c) G = (1,4/3); each result is the average of all three vertex vectors."], "solution": "The centroid is G = ((x₁+x₂+x₃)/3,(y₁+y₂+y₃)/3).\n(a) x = (4−2+5)/3 = 7/3 and y = (−2+4+5)/3 = 7/3.\nTherefore (a) G = (7/3,7/3).\n(b) x = (3+4+3)/3 = 10/3 and y = (5+6−1)/3 = 10/3.\nTherefore (b) G = (10/3,10/3).\n(c) x = (1−2+4)/3 = 1 and y = (1−2+5)/3 = 4/3.\nTherefore (c) G = (1,4/3); each result is the average of all three vertex vectors.", "answer": "(a) (7/3,7/3); (b) (10/3,10/3); (c) (1,4/3).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 183, "jpg": "080_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All five source question groups and exact coordinates visually checked"}]}];
