const math12ReviewedEx72=[{"id": "ex-7-2-1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Find the equation of lines that are represented on the coordinate planes: (a), (b), (c), as shown in the source graphs.", "steps": ["Read the signed intercepts from each source graph; each line has two different crossing points.", "(a) The crossings are (−5,0) and (0,5), so the slope is (5−0)/(0−(−5)) = 1.", "Thus (a) is y = x+5, or x−y+5 = 0.", "(b) The crossings are (2,0) and (0,6), so the slope is (6−0)/(0−2) = −3.", "Thus (b) is y = −3x+6, or 3x+y−6 = 0.", "(c) The crossings are (−4,0) and (0,−3), so the slope is (−3−0)/(0−(−4)) = −3/4.", "Thus (c) is y = −3x/4−3, or 3x+4y+12 = 0. Substitute each pair of crossings to check its equation."], "solution": "Read the signed intercepts from each source graph; each line has two different crossing points.\n(a) The crossings are (−5,0) and (0,5), so the slope is (5−0)/(0−(−5)) = 1.\nThus (a) is y = x+5, or x−y+5 = 0.\n(b) The crossings are (2,0) and (0,6), so the slope is (6−0)/(0−2) = −3.\nThus (b) is y = −3x+6, or 3x+y−6 = 0.\n(c) The crossings are (−4,0) and (0,−3), so the slope is (−3−0)/(0−(−4)) = −3/4.\nThus (c) is y = −3x/4−3, or 3x+4y+12 = 0. Substitute each pair of crossings to check its equation.", "answer": "(a) x−y+5 = 0; (b) 3x+y−6 = 0; (c) 3x+4y+12 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 192, "jpg": "100_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All eight question groups and exact plotted intercepts visually checked"}], "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 7.2 Question 1(a)", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Coordinate line with source intercepts a", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[65, 168], [457, 168]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[289, 270], [289, 32]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[65, 219], [373.0, 32.0]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#be123c", "dash": false}, {"points": [[149, 164], [149, 172]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[285, 83], [293, 83]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 462.59999999999997, "y": 168, "text": "x"}, {"x": 289, "y": 23.5, "text": "y"}, {"x": 139, "y": 158, "text": "-5"}, {"x": 266, "y": 88, "text": "5"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 7.2 Question 1(b)", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Coordinate line with source intercepts b", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[65, 168], [457, 168]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[289, 270], [289, 32]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[270.3333333333333, 32.0], [401.0, 270.0]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#be123c", "dash": false}, {"points": [[345, 164], [345, 172]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[285, 66], [293, 66]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 462.59999999999997, "y": 168, "text": "x"}, {"x": 289, "y": 23.5, "text": "y"}, {"x": 335, "y": 158, "text": "2"}, {"x": 266, "y": 71, "text": "6"}], "points": []}}, {"type": "diagram", "caption": "Exercise 7.2 Question 1(c)", "diagram": {"type": "source-labelled-geometry", "title": "Coordinate line with source intercepts c", "width": 500, "height": 320, "paths": [{"points": [[65, 168], [457, 168]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[289, 270], [289, 32]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[65, 117.0], [401.0, 270.0]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#be123c", "dash": false}, {"points": [[177, 164], [177, 172]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}, {"points": [[285, 219], [293, 219]], "fill": "none", "stroke": "#334155", "dash": false}], "labels": [{"x": 462.59999999999997, "y": 168, "text": "x"}, {"x": 289, "y": 23.5, "text": "y"}, {"x": 167, "y": 158, "text": "-4"}, {"x": 266, "y": 224, "text": "-3"}], "points": []}}]}, {"id": "ex-7-2-2", "qNo": "2", "question": "In each case, find the slope, if it is defined: (a) (−5,4) and (3,6); (b) 4x+7y = 1; (c) Parallel to 2y−4x = 7; (d) Perpendicular to 6x = y−3.", "steps": ["For a nonvertical line, use the coordinate difference quotient or isolate y.", "(a) m = (6−4)/(3−(−5)) = 2/8 = 1/4.", "(b) 4x+7y = 1 becomes y = −4x/7+1/7, so m = −4/7.", "(c) 2y−4x = 7 becomes y = 2x+7/2, whose slope is 2.", "A parallel line has the same slope, so (c) m = 2.", "(d) 6x = y−3 becomes y = 6x+3, whose slope is 6.", "A perpendicular line has slope −1/6 because 6(−1/6) = −1. All four requested slopes are defined."], "solution": "For a nonvertical line, use the coordinate difference quotient or isolate y.\n(a) m = (6−4)/(3−(−5)) = 2/8 = 1/4.\n(b) 4x+7y = 1 becomes y = −4x/7+1/7, so m = −4/7.\n(c) 2y−4x = 7 becomes y = 2x+7/2, whose slope is 2.\nA parallel line has the same slope, so (c) m = 2.\n(d) 6x = y−3 becomes y = 6x+3, whose slope is 6.\nA perpendicular line has slope −1/6 because 6(−1/6) = −1. All four requested slopes are defined.", "answer": "(a) 1/4; (b) −4/7; (c) 2; (d) −1/6.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 192, "jpg": "100_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All eight question groups and exact plotted intercepts visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-2-3", "qNo": "3", "question": "What are the x- and y-intercepts for each of the following lines? (a) y = 2x+6; (b) y = −3x+9; (c) y = x+2.", "steps": ["For an x-intercept set y = 0; for a y-intercept set x = 0.", "(a) 0 = 2x+6 gives x = −3; at x = 0, y = 6.", "The crossing points for (a) are (−3,0) and (0,6).", "(b) 0 = −3x+9 gives x = 3; at x = 0, y = 9.", "The crossing points for (b) are (3,0) and (0,9).", "(c) 0 = x+2 gives x = −2; at x = 0, y = 2.", "The crossing points for (c) are (−2,0) and (0,2). These are signed intercepts, not unsigned distances."], "solution": "For an x-intercept set y = 0; for a y-intercept set x = 0.\n(a) 0 = 2x+6 gives x = −3; at x = 0, y = 6.\nThe crossing points for (a) are (−3,0) and (0,6).\n(b) 0 = −3x+9 gives x = 3; at x = 0, y = 9.\nThe crossing points for (b) are (3,0) and (0,9).\n(c) 0 = x+2 gives x = −2; at x = 0, y = 2.\nThe crossing points for (c) are (−2,0) and (0,2). These are signed intercepts, not unsigned distances.", "answer": "(a) −3,6; (b) 3,9; (c) −2,2 (x-intercept, y-intercept).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 192, "jpg": "100_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All eight question groups and exact plotted intercepts visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-2-4", "qNo": "4", "question": "In each case, show that the pair of lines are parallel or perpendicular or neither: (a) x−2y−6 = 0, 2x+y−5 = 0; (b) 3x+4y−8 = 0, x+3y−2 = 0; (c) 2x−y−7 = 0, 4x−2y−5 = 0; (d) 2x−5y−7 = 0, 6x−15y+5 = 0.", "steps": ["Isolate y in both equations of each pair. Equal finite slopes mean the same direction; product −1 means perpendicular.", "(a) y = x/2−3 and y = −2x+5 have slopes 1/2 and −2. Their product is −1, so they are perpendicular.", "(b) y = −3x/4+2 and y = −x/3+2/3 have slopes −3/4 and −1/3.", "The slopes in (b) differ and their product is 1/4, so they are neither parallel nor perpendicular.", "(c) y = 2x−7 and y = 2x−5/2 have equal slopes 2 and different intercepts; they are parallel distinct lines.", "(d) y = 2x/5−7/5 and y = 2x/5+1/3 have equal slopes 2/5 and different intercepts; they are parallel distinct lines.", "These classifications use the exact printed constants, including the +5 in the final equation."], "solution": "Isolate y in both equations of each pair. Equal finite slopes mean the same direction; product −1 means perpendicular.\n(a) y = x/2−3 and y = −2x+5 have slopes 1/2 and −2. Their product is −1, so they are perpendicular.\n(b) y = −3x/4+2 and y = −x/3+2/3 have slopes −3/4 and −1/3.\nThe slopes in (b) differ and their product is 1/4, so they are neither parallel nor perpendicular.\n(c) y = 2x−7 and y = 2x−5/2 have equal slopes 2 and different intercepts; they are parallel distinct lines.\n(d) y = 2x/5−7/5 and y = 2x/5+1/3 have equal slopes 2/5 and different intercepts; they are parallel distinct lines.\nThese classifications use the exact printed constants, including the +5 in the final equation.", "answer": "(a) perpendicular; (b) neither; (c) parallel; (d) parallel.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 192, "jpg": "100_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All eight question groups and exact plotted intercepts visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-2-5", "qNo": "5", "question": "In each case, find the equation of a line that passes through the pair of points: (a) O(0,0) and A(2,6); (b) E(1,0) and F(2,5); (c) I(1,1) and J(3,3).", "steps": ["Compute the slope for each pair, then use one endpoint in y−y₁ = m(x−x₁).", "(a) m = (6−0)/(2−0) = 3, so the line through the origin is y = 3x.", "It passes through A because 6 = 3·2.", "(b) m = (5−0)/(2−1) = 5; using E gives y−0 = 5(x−1).", "Thus (b) y = 5x−5; F satisfies 5 = 10−5.", "(c) m = (3−1)/(3−1) = 1; using I gives y−1 = x−1.", "Thus (c) y = x, and both I and J satisfy it."], "solution": "Compute the slope for each pair, then use one endpoint in y−y₁ = m(x−x₁).\n(a) m = (6−0)/(2−0) = 3, so the line through the origin is y = 3x.\nIt passes through A because 6 = 3·2.\n(b) m = (5−0)/(2−1) = 5; using E gives y−0 = 5(x−1).\nThus (b) y = 5x−5; F satisfies 5 = 10−5.\n(c) m = (3−1)/(3−1) = 1; using I gives y−1 = x−1.\nThus (c) y = x, and both I and J satisfy it.", "answer": "(a) y = 3x; (b) y = 5x−5; (c) y = x.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 192, "jpg": "100_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All eight question groups and exact plotted intercepts visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-2-6", "qNo": "6", "question": "In each case, find the equation of a line that passes through the point A(x₁,y₁) having slope m: (a) A(1,2), m = 4; (b) A(−1,−2), m = −1/2; (c) A(−3,5), m = −3; (d) A(7,−8), m = 5.", "steps": ["Use the point-slope form y−y₁ = m(x−x₁).", "(a) y−2 = 4(x−1), so y = 4x−2.", "(b) y+2 = −(x+1)/2, so y = −x/2−5/2, or x+2y+5 = 0.", "(c) y−5 = −3(x+3), so y = −3x−4.", "(d) y+8 = 5(x−7), so y = 5x−43.", "Check (a) at x = 1 gives 2, (b) at x = −1 gives −2, (c) at x = −3 gives 5, and (d) at x = 7 gives −8.", "The coefficients of x in these solved equations are the four prescribed slopes."], "solution": "Use the point-slope form y−y₁ = m(x−x₁).\n(a) y−2 = 4(x−1), so y = 4x−2.\n(b) y+2 = −(x+1)/2, so y = −x/2−5/2, or x+2y+5 = 0.\n(c) y−5 = −3(x+3), so y = −3x−4.\n(d) y+8 = 5(x−7), so y = 5x−43.\nCheck (a) at x = 1 gives 2, (b) at x = −1 gives −2, (c) at x = −3 gives 5, and (d) at x = 7 gives −8.\nThe coefficients of x in these solved equations are the four prescribed slopes.", "answer": "(a) y = 4x−2; (b) x+2y+5 = 0; (c) y = −3x−4; (d) y = 5x−43.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 192, "jpg": "100_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All eight question groups and exact plotted intercepts visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-2-7", "qNo": "7", "question": "In each case, find the equation of a line that exists the y-intercept c and slope m: (a) c = 2, m = 2; (b) c = 4, m = 8; (c) c = −4, m = 1/2.", "steps": ["The slope-intercept form is y = mx+c.", "(a) Substitute m = 2 and c = 2: y = 2x+2.", "(b) Substitute m = 8 and c = 4: y = 8x+4.", "(c) Substitute m = 1/2 and c = −4: y = x/2−4.", "At x = 0 these give y = 2,4,−4, respectively, confirming each signed intercept.", "A unit increase in x changes y by 2,8,1/2, respectively, confirming the slopes."], "solution": "The slope-intercept form is y = mx+c.\n(a) Substitute m = 2 and c = 2: y = 2x+2.\n(b) Substitute m = 8 and c = 4: y = 8x+4.\n(c) Substitute m = 1/2 and c = −4: y = x/2−4.\nAt x = 0 these give y = 2,4,−4, respectively, confirming each signed intercept.\nA unit increase in x changes y by 2,8,1/2, respectively, confirming the slopes.", "answer": "(a) y = 2x+2; (b) y = 8x+4; (c) y = x/2−4.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 192, "jpg": "100_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All eight question groups and exact plotted intercepts visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-7-2-8", "qNo": "8", "question": "Transform the equation 7x−10y+13 = 0 into: (a) slope intercept form; (b) symmetric form; (c) normal form.", "steps": ["(a) Isolate y: 10y = 7x+13, hence y = (7/10)x+13/10.", "(b) A point on the line is (1,2), because 7−20+13 = 0.", "A direction vector is (10,7), perpendicular to the coefficient normal (7,−10).", "Normalize the direction by √149: cosθ = 10/√149, sinθ = 7/√149.", "A symmetric form is (x−1)/(10/√149) = (y−2)/(7/√149) = r. Equivalently (x−1)/10 = (y−2)/7 uses a rescaled parameter.", "(c) The unit normal must point from the origin toward the nearest point, giving (−7/√149,10/√149).", "Divide the equation by −√149 to obtain −7x/√149+10y/√149 = 13/√149.", "Its positive right side p = 13/√149 is the perpendicular distance; the normal angle satisfies cosφ = −7/√149 and sinφ = 10/√149.", "Check the nearest point (−91/149,130/149): it satisfies 7x−10y+13 = 0 and has distance 13/√149."], "solution": "(a) Isolate y: 10y = 7x+13, hence y = (7/10)x+13/10.\n(b) A point on the line is (1,2), because 7−20+13 = 0.\nA direction vector is (10,7), perpendicular to the coefficient normal (7,−10).\nNormalize the direction by √149: cosθ = 10/√149, sinθ = 7/√149.\nA symmetric form is (x−1)/(10/√149) = (y−2)/(7/√149) = r. Equivalently (x−1)/10 = (y−2)/7 uses a rescaled parameter.\n(c) The unit normal must point from the origin toward the nearest point, giving (−7/√149,10/√149).\nDivide the equation by −√149 to obtain −7x/√149+10y/√149 = 13/√149.\nIts positive right side p = 13/√149 is the perpendicular distance; the normal angle satisfies cosφ = −7/√149 and sinφ = 10/√149.\nCheck the nearest point (−91/149,130/149): it satisfies 7x−10y+13 = 0 and has distance 13/√149.", "answer": "(a) y = (7/10)x+13/10; (b) (x−1)/(10/√149) = (y−2)/(7/√149); (c) −7x/√149+10y/√149 = 13/√149.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 192, "jpg": "100_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All eight question groups and exact plotted intercepts visually checked"}]}];
