const math12ReviewedEx81=[{"id": "ex-8-1-1", "qNo": "1", "question": "In each case, find an equation of a circle, when the center and radius are: (a) (0,0), r = 4; (b) (3,2), r = 1; (c) (−4,−3), r = 4; (d) (−a,−b), r = a+b.", "steps": ["Use (x−h)²+(y−k)² = r² for center (h,k) and radius r.", "(a) h = k = 0 and r² = 16, giving x²+y² = 16.", "(b) Substitute h = 3, k = 2 and r² = 1: (x−3)²+(y−2)² = 1.", "(c) Substitute h = −4, k = −3 and r² = 16: (x+4)²+(y+3)² = 16.", "(d) Substitute h = −a, k = −b: (x+a)²+(y+b)² = (a+b)².", "The stated radius in (d) requires a+b > 0 for a nondegenerate circle. If a+b = 0 this is a point; if a+b < 0 it cannot be the stated radius, although the squared equation has radius |a+b|."], "solution": "Use (x−h)²+(y−k)² = r² for center (h,k) and radius r.\n(a) h = k = 0 and r² = 16, giving x²+y² = 16.\n(b) Substitute h = 3, k = 2 and r² = 1: (x−3)²+(y−2)² = 1.\n(c) Substitute h = −4, k = −3 and r² = 16: (x+4)²+(y+3)² = 16.\n(d) Substitute h = −a, k = −b: (x+a)²+(y+b)² = (a+b)².\nThe stated radius in (d) requires a+b > 0 for a nondegenerate circle. If a+b = 0 this is a point; if a+b < 0 it cannot be the stated radius, although the squared equation has radius |a+b|.", "answer": "(a) x²+y² = 16; (b) (x−3)²+(y−2)² = 1; (c) (x+4)²+(y+3)² = 16; (d) (x+a)²+(y+b)² = (a+b)², with a+b > 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 221, "jpg": "115_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Every source subpart visually reviewed and exact calculations checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-8-1-2", "qNo": "2", "question": "In each case, determine the equation of a circle using the given information: (a) C(0,0), tangent to x = −5; (b) C(0,0), tangent to y = 6; (c) C(6,−6), circumference passes through the origin; (d) C(−9,−6), circumference passes through (−20,8); (e) C(−5,4), tangent to the x-axis; (f) C(5,3), tangent to the y-axis.", "steps": ["For tangency, the radius equals the perpendicular distance from the center to the line; for a given circumference point it equals the center-to-point distance.", "(a) Distance from (0,0) to x = −5 is 5, giving x²+y² = 25.", "(b) Distance from (0,0) to y = 6 is 6, giving x²+y² = 36.", "(c) The squared distance from (6,−6) to (0,0) is 36+36 = 72, giving (x−6)²+(y+6)² = 72.", "(d) The displacement from (−9,−6) to (−20,8) is (−11,14), whose squared length is 121+196 = 317; hence (x+9)²+(y+6)² = 317.", "(e) Distance from (−5,4) to the x-axis is 4, giving (x+5)²+(y−4)² = 16.", "(f) Distance from (5,3) to the y-axis is 5, giving (x−5)²+(y−3)² = 25. Each tangent line has exactly one circle contact at the perpendicular projection of its center."], "solution": "For tangency, the radius equals the perpendicular distance from the center to the line; for a given circumference point it equals the center-to-point distance.\n(a) Distance from (0,0) to x = −5 is 5, giving x²+y² = 25.\n(b) Distance from (0,0) to y = 6 is 6, giving x²+y² = 36.\n(c) The squared distance from (6,−6) to (0,0) is 36+36 = 72, giving (x−6)²+(y+6)² = 72.\n(d) The displacement from (−9,−6) to (−20,8) is (−11,14), whose squared length is 121+196 = 317; hence (x+9)²+(y+6)² = 317.\n(e) Distance from (−5,4) to the x-axis is 4, giving (x+5)²+(y−4)² = 16.\n(f) Distance from (5,3) to the y-axis is 5, giving (x−5)²+(y−3)² = 25. Each tangent line has exactly one circle contact at the perpendicular projection of its center.", "answer": "(a) x²+y² = 25; (b) x²+y² = 36; (c) (x−6)²+(y+6)² = 72; (d) (x+9)²+(y+6)² = 317; (e) (x+5)²+(y−4)² = 16; (f) (x−5)²+(y−3)² = 25.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 221, "jpg": "115_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Every source subpart visually reviewed and exact calculations checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-8-1-3", "qNo": "3", "question": "In each case, find the center C(−g,−f) and radius r = √(g²+f²−c) of: (a) x²+y²−8x−6y+9 = 0; (b) 4x²+4y²+16x−12y−7 = 0; (c) x²+y²+4x−6y+13 = 0; (d) x²+y²−x−8y+18 = 0.", "steps": ["Normalize the coefficients of x² and y² to 1, then read the center as (−g,−f) and squared radius as g²+f²−c.", "(a) g = −4, f = −3, c = 9, so center (4,3) and r² = 16+9−9 = 16: r = 4.", "(b) Divide by 4: x²+y²+4x−3y−7/4 = 0.", "Thus g = 2, f = −3/2, c = −7/4, giving center (−2,3/2) and r² = 4+9/4+7/4 = 8: r = 2√2.", "(c) Center (−2,3) and r² = 4+9−13 = 0. The completed equation is (x+2)²+(y−3)² = 0, a single point.", "(d) Center (1/2,4) and r² = 1/4+16−18 = −7/4.", "Since a sum of two real squares cannot be negative, (d) has no real circle or real radius. Its formal complex radius is i√7/2; the real locus is empty."], "solution": "Normalize the coefficients of x² and y² to 1, then read the center as (−g,−f) and squared radius as g²+f²−c.\n(a) g = −4, f = −3, c = 9, so center (4,3) and r² = 16+9−9 = 16: r = 4.\n(b) Divide by 4: x²+y²+4x−3y−7/4 = 0.\nThus g = 2, f = −3/2, c = −7/4, giving center (−2,3/2) and r² = 4+9/4+7/4 = 8: r = 2√2.\n(c) Center (−2,3) and r² = 4+9−13 = 0. The completed equation is (x+2)²+(y−3)² = 0, a single point.\n(d) Center (1/2,4) and r² = 1/4+16−18 = −7/4.\nSince a sum of two real squares cannot be negative, (d) has no real circle or real radius. Its formal complex radius is i√7/2; the real locus is empty.", "answer": "(a) C(4,3), r = 4; (b) C(−2,3/2), r = 2√2; (c) point circle (−2,3), r = 0; (d) no real points, formal center (1/2,4).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 221, "jpg": "115_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Every source subpart visually reviewed and exact calculations checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-8-1-4", "qNo": "4", "question": "In each case, find an equation of a circle which passes through the three points: (a) (−3,0), (5,4), (6,−3); (b) (7,−1), (5,3), (−4,6); (c) (1,2), (3,−4), (5,−6).", "steps": ["Use x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c = 0 and substitute the three points to get three linear equations in g,f,c.", "(a) The equations are −6g+c = −9, 10g+8f+c = −41, and 12g−6f+c = −45. Solving gives g = −2, f = 0, c = −21.", "Thus x²+y²−4x−21 = 0, equivalent to (x−2)²+y² = 25. The three squared center distances are 25,25,25.", "(b) The equations are 14g−2f+c = −50, 10g+6f+c = −34, and −8g+12f+c = −52. Solving gives g = 2, f = 3, c = −72.", "Thus x²+y²+4x+6y−72 = 0, equivalent to (x+2)²+(y+3)² = 85. The three squared center distances are 85,85,85.", "(c) The equations are 2g+4f+c = −5, 6g−8f+c = −25, and 10g−12f+c = −61. Solving gives g = −11, f = −2, c = 25.", "Thus x²+y²−22x−4y+25 = 0, equivalent to (x−11)²+(y−2)² = 100. The three squared center distances are 100,100,100.", "Each triple is non-collinear (cross products −60,30,8 respectively), so the linear systems determine unique circles. Direct substitution gives zero in every stated source-point equation."], "solution": "Use x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c = 0 and substitute the three points to get three linear equations in g,f,c.\n(a) The equations are −6g+c = −9, 10g+8f+c = −41, and 12g−6f+c = −45. Solving gives g = −2, f = 0, c = −21.\nThus x²+y²−4x−21 = 0, equivalent to (x−2)²+y² = 25. The three squared center distances are 25,25,25.\n(b) The equations are 14g−2f+c = −50, 10g+6f+c = −34, and −8g+12f+c = −52. Solving gives g = 2, f = 3, c = −72.\nThus x²+y²+4x+6y−72 = 0, equivalent to (x+2)²+(y+3)² = 85. The three squared center distances are 85,85,85.\n(c) The equations are 2g+4f+c = −5, 6g−8f+c = −25, and 10g−12f+c = −61. Solving gives g = −11, f = −2, c = 25.\nThus x²+y²−22x−4y+25 = 0, equivalent to (x−11)²+(y−2)² = 100. The three squared center distances are 100,100,100.\nEach triple is non-collinear (cross products −60,30,8 respectively), so the linear systems determine unique circles. Direct substitution gives zero in every stated source-point equation.", "answer": "(a) x²+y²−4x−21 = 0; (b) x²+y²+4x+6y−72 = 0; (c) x²+y²−22x−4y+25 = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 221, "jpg": "115_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Every source subpart visually reviewed and exact calculations checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-8-1-5", "qNo": "5", "question": "In each case, find an equation of a circle which (a) contains the points (2,6), (6,4) and has its center on 3x+2y−1 = 0; (b) contains the points (4,1), (6,5) and has its center on 4x+y−16 = 0.", "steps": ["Let C(h,k) be the center. Equate its squared distances to the two given points, then combine with the prescribed center line.", "(a) Equidistance from (2,6) and (6,4) simplifies to 2h−k = 3.", "Combine with 3h+2k = 1: k = 2h−3, hence 7h−6 = 1 and (h,k) = (1,−1).", "The squared radius is (2−1)²+(6+1)² = 50. Thus (x−1)²+(y+1)² = 50. The other point also has squared distance 25+25 = 50.", "(b) Equidistance from (4,1) and (6,5) simplifies to h+2k = 11.", "Combine with 4h+k = 16 to get (h,k) = (3,4).", "The squared radius is (4−3)²+(1−4)² = 10, so (x−3)²+(y−4)² = 10. The other point gives 9+1 = 10.", "Both centers satisfy their specified lines, verifying the third condition as well as the two circumference conditions."], "solution": "Let C(h,k) be the center. Equate its squared distances to the two given points, then combine with the prescribed center line.\n(a) Equidistance from (2,6) and (6,4) simplifies to 2h−k = 3.\nCombine with 3h+2k = 1: k = 2h−3, hence 7h−6 = 1 and (h,k) = (1,−1).\nThe squared radius is (2−1)²+(6+1)² = 50. Thus (x−1)²+(y+1)² = 50. The other point also has squared distance 25+25 = 50.\n(b) Equidistance from (4,1) and (6,5) simplifies to h+2k = 11.\nCombine with 4h+k = 16 to get (h,k) = (3,4).\nThe squared radius is (4−3)²+(1−4)² = 10, so (x−3)²+(y−4)² = 10. The other point gives 9+1 = 10.\nBoth centers satisfy their specified lines, verifying the third condition as well as the two circumference conditions.", "answer": "(a) (x−1)²+(y+1)² = 50; (b) (x−3)²+(y−4)² = 10.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 221, "jpg": "115_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Every source subpart visually reviewed and exact calculations checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-8-1-6", "qNo": "6", "question": "Find an equation of a circle which passes through (a) (0,0), (0,3) and 4x−5y = 0 is tangent to it at (0,0); (b) (0,−1), (3,0) and 3x+y = 9 is tangent to it at (3,0).", "steps": ["At a specified tangency point, the radius vector must be parallel to the tangent line’s normal. First check that each claimed contact lies on its line.", "(a) The origin lies on 4x−5y = 0. Equal distances from center (h,k) to (0,0) and (0,3) give k = 3/2.", "Parallelism of (h,k) to normal (4,−5) gives 5h+4k = 0, hence h = −6/5.", "The squared radius is 36/25+9/4 = 369/100. Expanding the standard form gives x²+y²+(12/5)x−3y = 0.", "Both source points satisfy that equation, and the radius dot tangent direction (5,4) is 5(−6/5)+4(3/2) = 0.", "(b) The contact (3,0) lies on 3x+y = 9. Equal distances to (0,−1) and (3,0) give 3h+k = 4.", "Parallelism of (h−3,k) to the normal (3,1) gives h−3 = 3k. Solving yields (h,k) = (3/2,−1/2).", "Its squared radius is 5/2, giving x²+y²−3x+y = 0. Both source points satisfy it, and the radius dot tangent direction (1,−3) is −3/2+3/2 = 0."], "solution": "At a specified tangency point, the radius vector must be parallel to the tangent line’s normal. First check that each claimed contact lies on its line.\n(a) The origin lies on 4x−5y = 0. Equal distances from center (h,k) to (0,0) and (0,3) give k = 3/2.\nParallelism of (h,k) to normal (4,−5) gives 5h+4k = 0, hence h = −6/5.\nThe squared radius is 36/25+9/4 = 369/100. Expanding the standard form gives x²+y²+(12/5)x−3y = 0.\nBoth source points satisfy that equation, and the radius dot tangent direction (5,4) is 5(−6/5)+4(3/2) = 0.\n(b) The contact (3,0) lies on 3x+y = 9. Equal distances to (0,−1) and (3,0) give 3h+k = 4.\nParallelism of (h−3,k) to the normal (3,1) gives h−3 = 3k. Solving yields (h,k) = (3/2,−1/2).\nIts squared radius is 5/2, giving x²+y²−3x+y = 0. Both source points satisfy it, and the radius dot tangent direction (1,−3) is −3/2+3/2 = 0.", "answer": "(a) x²+y²+(12/5)x−3y = 0; (b) x²+y²−3x+y = 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 221, "jpg": "115_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Every source subpart visually reviewed and exact calculations checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-8-1-7", "qNo": "7", "question": "Find an equation of a circle that is concentric to (a) 2x²+2y²+16x−7y = 0 and is tangent to the y-axis; (b) x²+y²−8x+4 = 0 and is tangent to x+2y+6 = 0; (c) x²+y²+6x−10y+33 = 0 and is touching the x-axis.", "steps": ["Concentric circles share a center, but the new radius must be calculated from the specified tangent condition.", "(a) Divide by 2 to obtain x²+y²+8x−(7/2)y = 0, whose center is (−4,7/4).", "Distance to the y-axis is 4, so (x+4)²+(y−7/4)² = 16.", "(b) Completing squares in x²+y²−8x+4 = 0 gives center (4,0).", "Its distance to x+2y+6 = 0 is |4+0+6|/√5 = 2√5, hence (x−4)²+y² = 20.", "(c) Completing squares gives center (−3,5). Distance to the x-axis is 5, so (x+3)²+(y−5)² = 25.", "In every case the new circle’s radius equals the center-to-line distance, establishing tangency while retaining the original center."], "solution": "Concentric circles share a center, but the new radius must be calculated from the specified tangent condition.\n(a) Divide by 2 to obtain x²+y²+8x−(7/2)y = 0, whose center is (−4,7/4).\nDistance to the y-axis is 4, so (x+4)²+(y−7/4)² = 16.\n(b) Completing squares in x²+y²−8x+4 = 0 gives center (4,0).\nIts distance to x+2y+6 = 0 is |4+0+6|/√5 = 2√5, hence (x−4)²+y² = 20.\n(c) Completing squares gives center (−3,5). Distance to the x-axis is 5, so (x+3)²+(y−5)² = 25.\nIn every case the new circle’s radius equals the center-to-line distance, establishing tangency while retaining the original center.", "answer": "(a) (x+4)²+(y−7/4)² = 16; (b) (x−4)²+y² = 20; (c) (x+3)²+(y−5)² = 25.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 221, "jpg": "115_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Every source subpart visually reviewed and exact calculations checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-8-1-8", "qNo": "8", "question": "Find equation of circle which passes through origin and whose intercepts on the coordinate axes are: (a) 3 and 4; (b) 2 and 4.", "steps": ["A circle through the origin has general form x²+y²+Dx+Ey = 0 because its constant term is zero.", "Taking the listed values as the signed x and y intercepts, use the other two points (a,0) and (0,b).", "Substitution gives a²+Da = 0 and b²+Eb = 0. Since these intercepts are nonzero, D = −a and E = −b.", "(a) With intercepts 3 and 4, the equation is x²+y²−3x−4y = 0. Its center is (3/2,2) and radius is 5/2.", "(b) With intercepts 2 and 4, the equation is x²+y²−2x−4y = 0. Its center is (1,2) and radius is √5.", "The origin and both axis-intercept points satisfy their equations. If the source instead intended unsigned intercept lengths, independent sign choices would give additional reflected circles; the positive signed convention is stated here."], "solution": "A circle through the origin has general form x²+y²+Dx+Ey = 0 because its constant term is zero.\nTaking the listed values as the signed x and y intercepts, use the other two points (a,0) and (0,b).\nSubstitution gives a²+Da = 0 and b²+Eb = 0. Since these intercepts are nonzero, D = −a and E = −b.\n(a) With intercepts 3 and 4, the equation is x²+y²−3x−4y = 0. Its center is (3/2,2) and radius is 5/2.\n(b) With intercepts 2 and 4, the equation is x²+y²−2x−4y = 0. Its center is (1,2) and radius is √5.\nThe origin and both axis-intercept points satisfy their equations. If the source instead intended unsigned intercept lengths, independent sign choices would give additional reflected circles; the positive signed convention is stated here.", "answer": "(a) x²+y²−3x−4y = 0; (b) x²+y²−2x−4y = 0, using positive signed intercepts.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 221, "jpg": "115_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Every source subpart visually reviewed and exact calculations checked"}]}];
