math12ODEReviewedExamples.forEach(e=>{e.problem=e.question;e.given=e.question;e.method='Identify derivatives, apply the definition, and verify the original equation and conditions.';e.title='Example '+e.num+' — '+e.title;const old=MATH_12_DATA[9].workedExamples.find(x=>x.num===e.num);if(old){old.unreviewedProblem=old.problem;Object.assign(old,e);}else MATH_12_DATA[9].workedExamples.push(e);});
math12ODEDefinitions.sourceBlocks.push(...MATH_12_DATA[9].sourcePages.find(p=>p.page===289).blocks.slice(0,MATH_12_DATA[9].sourcePages.find(p=>p.page===289).blocks.length-22));math12ODEDefinitions.theory=math12ODEDefinitions.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');const math12ODEFormation=MATH_12_DATA[9].sections.find(s=>s.id==='sec-10-2');math12ODEFormation.unreviewedTheory=math12ODEFormation.theory;
math12ODEFormation.sourceBlocks=[{"type": "heading", "text": "10.2 Formation of Differential Equation"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "In most of the physical situations, we can observe the process but can not worked out directly to the differential equation. As a result, we have a general solution at our disposal before we know the equation of which it is the solution. Let’s begin with the step for forming with differential equation."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "i. Discover the differential equation that describes a specified physical situation. ii. Find either exactly or approximately, the appropriate solution of that equation. iii. Interpret the solution that is found."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Look at the following examples for the concept of formation of differential equation."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 5"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The rate at which the distance travels by Ali is 30 mph. Find the total distance travels by Ali at a time t hours."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If S(t) is the unknown distance travel by you w.r.t. t number of hours, then, the rate at which the distance travels is the first derivative of S(t) with respect to t:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{dS}{dt}=30,\\quad\\mathrm{the\\ per\\ hour\\ speed}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Integrating with respect to t to obtain S(t):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\int\\frac{dS}{dt}=\\int30\\,dt+c\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad S(t)=30t+c\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "S(t) is the distance travels by Ali with respect to t number of hours and the constant quantity c is the fixed distance in this situation."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 6"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The rate at which the animal population is growing at a constant rate 4%. The habitat will support no more than 10,000 animals. There are 3000 animals present now. Find an equation that gives the animal population y w.r.t. x number of years."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If P(x) is the unknown animal population w.r.t. x number of years, then, the rate at which the population grows is the first derivative of P(x) w.r.t. x:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{dP}{dx}=k(N-P)=0.04(10,000-P)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{dP}{10,000-P}=0.04\\,dx\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Here k=0.04 is the constant growth, N=10,000 is the total size of animal population in that habitat. Integrating with respect to x to obtain P(x):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\int\\frac{dP}{10,000-P}=\\int0.04\\,dx+c\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "P(x) is the total population and c, the fixed population that depends on P=3000 when x=0. This problem is the IVP problem with the initial condition P(0)=3000."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The printed equation models growth proportional to the unfilled capacity N−P, not a constant 4% of the current population. Following that equation gives P(x)=10000−7000e^(−0.04x), approaching capacity from below. Constant per-capita 4% growth would instead give P′=0.04P and would not respect the cap indefinitely; logistic growth is yet another model. The integration constant is fixed by the initial population rather than being the population itself. The source integral shorthand in Examples 4–5 means integrate both sides with respect to the independent variable."}];
math12ODEFormation.theory=math12ODEFormation.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');math12ODEFormation.sourceReferences=[{page:289,jpg:'149_page_1.jpg'}];
const math12ReviewedExercise101=[{"id": "ex-10-1-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Find order and degree: (a) y′=x²+y; (b) y″+3y′+11y=3x; (c) y‴+2(y′)³−y=0; (d) yy″+5xy′=4.", "steps": ["Order is the highest derivative order; degree is the exponent of that highest-order derivative in a polynomial-in-derivatives equation.", "(a) The highest derivative y′ is linear: order 1, degree 1.", "(b) y″ is highest and linear: order 2, degree 1.", "(c) y‴ is highest and linear: order 3, degree 1. The cubic lower-order derivative does not set the degree.", "(d) yy″ has highest derivative y″ linearly: order 2, degree 1; its coefficient y is a dependent variable, not another derivative.", "Degree is defined here because all four equations are polynomial in derivatives. It is not the polynomial degree in x or y."], "solution": "Order is the highest derivative order; degree is the exponent of that highest-order derivative in a polynomial-in-derivatives equation.\n(a) The highest derivative y′ is linear: order 1, degree 1.\n(b) y″ is highest and linear: order 2, degree 1.\n(c) y‴ is highest and linear: order 3, degree 1. The cubic lower-order derivative does not set the degree.\n(d) yy″ has highest derivative y″ linearly: order 2, degree 1; its coefficient y is a dependent variable, not another derivative.\nDegree is defined here because all four equations are polynomial in derivatives. It is not the polynomial degree in x or y.", "answer": "(a) 1,1; (b) 2,1; (c) 3,1; (d) 2,1 (order, degree).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 290, "jpg": "149_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Enlarged source exercise expressions checked; Q2(c) exponent remains explicitly partial"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-1-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Show the indicated function solves the equation: (a) y=e^x+e^(2x), y″−3y′+2y=0; (b) y=x−x ln x, xy′+x−y=0; (c) y=(x+c)e^(−x), y′+y=e raised to a smudged source exponent; (d) y=e^x+e^(−x), y″−3y′+2y=0.", "steps": ["(a) y′=e^x+2e^(2x), y″=e^x+4e^(2x). Substitution yields (1−3+2)e^x+(4−6+2)e^(2x)=0.", "(b) For x>0, y′=−ln x. Thus xy′+x−y=−x ln x+x−(x−x ln x)=0.", "(c) Differentiate the readable function: y′=(1−x−c)e^(−x).", "Then y′+y=e^(−x). It solves an equation with right side e^(−x); the smudged printed exponent is not certified, so this is a conditional learning check rather than an exact-source assertion.", "(d) y′=e^x−e^(−x), y″=e^x+e^(−x).", "The printed left side is 6e^(−x), which is never zero for finite real x. Therefore the indicated function does not solve the printed equation.", "A possible corrected equation is y″−y=0, solved by both exponentials. Alternatively the given printed equation is solved by Ce^x+De^(2x), as part (a) illustrates. These repairs are separate from the original question."], "solution": "(a) y′=e^x+2e^(2x), y″=e^x+4e^(2x). Substitution yields (1−3+2)e^x+(4−6+2)e^(2x)=0.\n(b) For x>0, y′=−ln x. Thus xy′+x−y=−x ln x+x−(x−x ln x)=0.\n(c) Differentiate the readable function: y′=(1−x−c)e^(−x).\nThen y′+y=e^(−x). It solves an equation with right side e^(−x); the smudged printed exponent is not certified, so this is a conditional learning check rather than an exact-source assertion.\n(d) y′=e^x−e^(−x), y″=e^x+e^(−x).\nThe printed left side is 6e^(−x), which is never zero for finite real x. Therefore the indicated function does not solve the printed equation.\nA possible corrected equation is y″−y=0, solved by both exponentials. Alternatively the given printed equation is solved by Ce^x+De^(2x), as part (a) illustrates. These repairs are separate from the original question.", "answer": "(a),(b) verified; (c) gives RHS e^(−x), printed exponent unresolved; (d) fails printed equation with residual 6e^(−x).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 290, "jpg": "149_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Enlarged source exercise expressions checked; Q2(c) exponent remains explicitly partial"}], "sourceQuestionBlocks": [{"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/ode290-q2c-exponent.jpg", "caption": "Unresolved Exercise 10.1 Q2(c) source exponent", "alt": "Small right-side exponent is smudged across full PDF and JPG; Word text does not resolve it."}], "sourceStatus": "partial_source_legibility"}, {"id": "ex-10-1-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Find particular solutions: (a) xy=c, y(2)=1; (b) y=x−x ln x+c, y(1)=2; (c) sin(xy)+y=c, y(π/4)=1; (d) y²/x=x²/2+c, y(1)=1.", "steps": ["(a) Substitute x=2,y=1: c=2, so y=2/x on an interval excluding zero, with the initial point on x>0.", "(b) At x=1, ln1=0, so 2=1+c and c=1. Hence y=x−x ln x+1 for x>0.", "(c) At x=π/4,y=1: c=1+sin(π/4)=1+√2/2. Thus sin(xy)+y=1+√2/2.", "This implicit equation defines the local branch through (π/4,1): the derivative with respect to y is 1+x cos(xy), which is nonzero there.", "(d) At x=1,y=1, c=1−1/2=1/2. Thus y²=(x³+x)/2.", "Choose the positive branch y=√((x³+x)/2) on x>0 to satisfy y(1)=1; the negative branch fails this initial value.", "Each answer uses the stated family and condition. The question does not supply the underlying differential equation for these families, so no extra one is asserted as source."], "solution": "(a) Substitute x=2,y=1: c=2, so y=2/x on an interval excluding zero, with the initial point on x>0.\n(b) At x=1, ln1=0, so 2=1+c and c=1. Hence y=x−x ln x+1 for x>0.\n(c) At x=π/4,y=1: c=1+sin(π/4)=1+√2/2. Thus sin(xy)+y=1+√2/2.\nThis implicit equation defines the local branch through (π/4,1): the derivative with respect to y is 1+x cos(xy), which is nonzero there.\n(d) At x=1,y=1, c=1−1/2=1/2. Thus y²=(x³+x)/2.\nChoose the positive branch y=√((x³+x)/2) on x>0 to satisfy y(1)=1; the negative branch fails this initial value.\nEach answer uses the stated family and condition. The question does not supply the underlying differential equation for these families, so no extra one is asserted as source.", "answer": "(a) y=2/x; (b) y=x−x ln x+1; (c) sin(xy)+y=1+√2/2; (d) y=√((x³+x)/2), x>0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 290, "jpg": "149_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Enlarged source exercise expressions checked; Q2(c) exponent remains explicitly partial"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-1-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "Solve IVPs: (a) y′=cos x, y(0)=1; (b) y′=x², y(0)=1; (c) y′=1/x², y(2)=0; (d) y′=2xy², y(3)=−1.", "steps": ["(a) Integrate y′=cos x: y=sin x+C. The initial value gives C=1, hence y=sin x+1.", "(b) Integrate y′=x²: y=x³/3+C. Since y(0)=1, y=x³/3+1.", "(c) Integrate x^(−2): y=−1/x+C. Set y(2)=0 to get C=1/2, hence y=1/2−1/x on x>0.", "(d) Since y(3)=−1≠0, separate dy/y²=2x dx. Integrate: −1/y=x²+C.", "At x=3,y=−1: 1=9+C, so C=−8. Thus y=1/(8−x²).", "The interval containing 3 is (√8,∞); the formula has poles at ±√8 and cannot define a solution across them. The equilibrium y=0 fails this initial value.", "Differentiate each result and substitute its initial point. For (d), y′=2x/(8−x²)²=2xy² and y(3)=−1."], "solution": "(a) Integrate y′=cos x: y=sin x+C. The initial value gives C=1, hence y=sin x+1.\n(b) Integrate y′=x²: y=x³/3+C. Since y(0)=1, y=x³/3+1.\n(c) Integrate x^(−2): y=−1/x+C. Set y(2)=0 to get C=1/2, hence y=1/2−1/x on x>0.\n(d) Since y(3)=−1≠0, separate dy/y²=2x dx. Integrate: −1/y=x²+C.\nAt x=3,y=−1: 1=9+C, so C=−8. Thus y=1/(8−x²).\nThe interval containing 3 is (√8,∞); the formula has poles at ±√8 and cannot define a solution across them. The equilibrium y=0 fails this initial value.\nDifferentiate each result and substitute its initial point. For (d), y′=2x/(8−x²)²=2xy² and y(3)=−1.", "answer": "(a) y=sin x+1; (b) y=x³/3+1; (c) y=1/2−1/x, x>0; (d) y=1/(8−x²), x>√8.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 290, "jpg": "149_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Enlarged source exercise expressions checked; Q2(c) exponent remains explicitly partial"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-1-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "Suppose a student carrying Corona Virus returns to an isolated college campus of 1000 students. The spread rate is proportional to both the number x infected and the number not infected. Find the number infected after 6 days, given that after 4 days it is 50.", "steps": ["Use this textbook model with fixed population 1000 and no recovery/removal. Let x(t) denote the infected count at time t in days.", "The wording gives x′=k x(1000−x). Interpret the returning student as the sole initially infected student: x(0)=1.", "For 0<x<1000, separate dx/[x(1000−x)]=k dt. Partial fractions give (1/1000)ln[x/(1000−x)]=kt+C.", "Hence x(t)=1000/(1+A e^(−1000kt)). From x(0)=1, A=999.", "Use x(4)=50: 50=1000/(1+999 e^(−4000k)), so e^(−4000k)=19/999 and k=ln(999/19)/4000.", "At six days, e^(−6000k)=(19/999)^(3/2). Thus x(6)=1000/[1+999(19/999)^(3/2)]≈276.2217, about 276 students.", "This is a continuous model estimate, not a clinical forecast. If the source did not intend exactly one initial infection, another initial count would be needed to determine k uniquely. Substitution verifies the initial count, day-four count, and logistic rate."], "solution": "Use this textbook model with fixed population 1000 and no recovery/removal. Let x(t) denote the infected count at time t in days.\nThe wording gives x′=k x(1000−x). Interpret the returning student as the sole initially infected student: x(0)=1.\nFor 0<x<1000, separate dx/[x(1000−x)]=k dt. Partial fractions give (1/1000)ln[x/(1000−x)]=kt+C.\nHence x(t)=1000/(1+A e^(−1000kt)). From x(0)=1, A=999.\nUse x(4)=50: 50=1000/(1+999 e^(−4000k)), so e^(−4000k)=19/999 and k=ln(999/19)/4000.\nAt six days, e^(−6000k)=(19/999)^(3/2). Thus x(6)=1000/[1+999(19/999)^(3/2)]≈276.2217, about 276 students.\nThis is a continuous model estimate, not a clinical forecast. If the source did not intend exactly one initial infection, another initial count would be needed to determine k uniquely. Substitution verifies the initial count, day-four count, and logistic rate.", "answer": "Assuming x(0)=1: approximately 276 students after six days.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 290, "jpg": "149_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Enlarged source exercise expressions checked; Q2(c) exponent remains explicitly partial"}]}];
