math12ODESolutions.sourceBlocks.push(...[{"type": "paragraph", "text": "The initial condition N(0)=N₀ is used in equation (ii) to obtain c:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\ln N_0=0+c\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad c=\\ln N_0\\)", "number": "iii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Use c in equation (ii) to obtain a particular solution:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\ln N=kt+\\ln N_0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\ln\\frac{N}{N_0}=kt\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\frac{N}{N_0}=e^{kt}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad N=N_0e^{kt}\\)", "number": "iv"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Use N(1)=5N₀/2 to obtain k:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac52N_0=N_0e^k\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad e^k=\\frac52\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k=\\ln\\frac52=0.9163\\)", "number": "v"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Use k in equation (iv) to obtain a particular solution:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(N=N_0e^{0.9163t}\\)", "number": "vi"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The condition N=4N₀, when bacteria have quadrupled, obtains the time:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(4N_0=N_0e^{0.9163t}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad4=e^{0.9163t}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad0.9163t=\\ln4\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad t=\\frac{\\ln4}{0.9163}=1.51\\ \\mathrm{hr}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "At this time the bacteria is four times the original number."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Assume N₀>0 and use k=ln(5/2) without rounding until the final answer: t=ln4/ln(5/2)≈1.5129416 hours. The source four-decimal k is an approximation. This is the stipulated exponential model; its constant per-capita rate is distinct from Example 6’s capacity-gap model."}]);
math12ODESolutions.theory=math12ODESolutions.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');math12ODESolutions.sourceReferences.push({page:295,jpg:'152_page_1.jpg'});const math12ODETrajectories=MATH_12_DATA[9].sections.find(s=>s.id==='sec-10-4');math12ODETrajectories.unreviewedTheory=math12ODETrajectories.theory;
math12ODETrajectories.sourceBlocks=[{"type": "heading", "text": "10.4 Orthogonal Trajectories"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Our experience with first order differential equations has taught us that such equations often have general solutions containing a single arbitrary constant. Each solution defines a corresponding set of integral curves. A nonempty set of plane curves defined by a differential equation involving just one parameter (single arbitrary constant) is commonly called a one-parameter family of curves. Of special importance in certain applications are those one-parameter families of curves which are orthogonal trajectories of one another."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The curves of a family F(x,y,c₁) are said to be orthogonal trajectories of curves of a family G(x,y,c₂), if and only if each curve of either family is intersected by at least one curve of the other family and at every point of intersection of a curve of F with a curve of G, the two curves are perpendicular."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "10.4.1 Orthogonal trajectories of the given family of curves"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Two families F(x,y,c₁) and G(x,y,c₂) are perpendicular at a point of intersection if and only if their tangents are perpendicular there. If tangent lines L₁,L₂ are perpendicular, the product of their slopes equals −1:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_1m_2=-1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "m₁ and m₂ are the slopes of the two tangent lines L₁ and L₂."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(m_1=-\\frac1{m_2}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)_G=-\\frac1{\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)_F}\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This is called the differential equation of orthogonal trajectories. If one family of curves F is given, the other family G can be found by solving equation (i)."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The negative-reciprocal formula applies when both slopes are finite and nonzero. Horizontal and vertical tangents are also perpendicular, handled using tangent vectors with dot product zero or by swapping the local dependent variable. Orthogonality requires regular intersections with defined tangents; singular points need separate treatment."}];
math12ODETrajectories.theory=math12ODETrajectories.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');math12ODETrajectories.sourceReferences=[{page:295,jpg:'152_page_1.jpg'}];
