const math12ReviewedExercise102=[{"id": "ex-10-2-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Find general solutions: (a) (y′)²=1−y²; (b) e^x y′+y²=0; (c) √(1−x²) dy=√(1−y²) dx; (d) cosec²x dy+sec y dx=0.", "steps": ["(a) On a branch with |y|<1, choose y′=±√(1−y²). Integration gives arcsin y=±x+C locally, hence sinusoidal arcs with derivative sign consistent with the chosen branch.", "The squared equation also has constant solutions y=1 and y=−1. Smooth solutions may join sinusoidal arcs to waits at these endpoints, so a single sine family does not exhaust every solution.", "(b) y′=−e^(−x)y². Retain y=0; for y≠0 integrate dy/y²=−e^(−x)dx to obtain −1/y=e^(−x)+C. Thus y=1/(A−e^(−x)), excluding denominator zeros.", "(c) On |x|<1, |y|<1, separate dy/√(1−y²)=dx/√(1−x²). Hence arcsin y=arcsin x+C, with both principal ranges respected.", "The source radicals are real only for |x|≤1 and |y|≤1. Also retain constant y=±1, which division removes. The unsquared equation requires nonnegative y′ in the interior; unrestricted sine branches would add false solutions.", "(d) The original equation requires sin x≠0 and cos y≠0. Multiply the slope equation by cos y: cos y dy=−sin²x dx.", "Integrate: sin y=−x/2+sin(2x)/4+C. Choose implicit branches with cos y≠0 and intervals excluding sin x=0; differentiating recovers the original equation."], "solution": "(a) On a branch with |y|<1, choose y′=±√(1−y²). Integration gives arcsin y=±x+C locally, hence sinusoidal arcs with derivative sign consistent with the chosen branch.\nThe squared equation also has constant solutions y=1 and y=−1. Smooth solutions may join sinusoidal arcs to waits at these endpoints, so a single sine family does not exhaust every solution.\n(b) y′=−e^(−x)y². Retain y=0; for y≠0 integrate dy/y²=−e^(−x)dx to obtain −1/y=e^(−x)+C. Thus y=1/(A−e^(−x)), excluding denominator zeros.\n(c) On |x|<1, |y|<1, separate dy/√(1−y²)=dx/√(1−x²). Hence arcsin y=arcsin x+C, with both principal ranges respected.\nThe source radicals are real only for |x|≤1 and |y|≤1. Also retain constant y=±1, which division removes. The unsquared equation requires nonnegative y′ in the interior; unrestricted sine branches would add false solutions.\n(d) The original equation requires sin x≠0 and cos y≠0. Multiply the slope equation by cos y: cos y dy=−sin²x dx.\nIntegrate: sin y=−x/2+sin(2x)/4+C. Choose implicit branches with cos y≠0 and intervals excluding sin x=0; differentiating recovers the original equation.", "answer": "Local sinusoidal arcs plus y=±1; y=1/(A−e^(−x)) plus zero; arcsin y−arcsin x=C plus y=±1; sin y=−x/2+sin2x/4+C, with stated domains.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Reduce to separable form and solve: (a) y′=(y+x)²; (b) y′=tan(x+y)−1; (c) y′=y/x+y²/x²; (d) y′=y/x+x²/y².", "steps": ["(a) Set u=x+y, so u′=1+y′=1+u². Separate to get arctan u=x+C, hence y=tan(x+C)−x away from tangent poles.", "(b) Set u=x+y: u′=tan u. For sin u≠0, separate cot u du=dx and integrate ln|sin u|=x+C.", "Thus sin(x+y)=Ae^x locally with cos(x+y)≠0. Include A=0, giving u=nπ and y=nπ−x, the equilibrium-u solutions discarded by division.", "(c) Set u=y/x, x≠0. Then u+xu′=u+u², so xu′=u². For u≠0, integrate −1/u=ln|x|+C.", "Hence y=−x/(ln|x|+C), with no denominator zero; also include y=0.", "(d) With u=y/x and x,y≠0, xu′=1/u². Integrate u²du=dx/x to get u³=3ln|x|+C.", "Thus y=x·cuberoot(3ln|x|+C) on intervals where this cube-root factor is nonzero. Differentiation checks all four equations and their original denominator restrictions."], "solution": "(a) Set u=x+y, so u′=1+y′=1+u². Separate to get arctan u=x+C, hence y=tan(x+C)−x away from tangent poles.\n(b) Set u=x+y: u′=tan u. For sin u≠0, separate cot u du=dx and integrate ln|sin u|=x+C.\nThus sin(x+y)=Ae^x locally with cos(x+y)≠0. Include A=0, giving u=nπ and y=nπ−x, the equilibrium-u solutions discarded by division.\n(c) Set u=y/x, x≠0. Then u+xu′=u+u², so xu′=u². For u≠0, integrate −1/u=ln|x|+C.\nHence y=−x/(ln|x|+C), with no denominator zero; also include y=0.\n(d) With u=y/x and x,y≠0, xu′=1/u². Integrate u²du=dx/x to get u³=3ln|x|+C.\nThus y=x·cuberoot(3ln|x|+C) on intervals where this cube-root factor is nonzero. Differentiation checks all four equations and their original denominator restrictions.", "answer": "(a) tan(x+C)−x; (b) sin(x+y)=Ae^x; (c) −x/(ln|x|+C), plus zero; (d) x·cuberoot(3ln|x|+C), retaining denominators.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Solve homogeneous equations: (a) y′=(x+y)/(x−y); (b) y′=(xy−y²)/x²; (c) y′=(x²+3y²)/(2xy); (d) y′=(xy+y²)/(x²+xy+y²).", "steps": ["(a) Put u=y/x, x≠0: xu′=(1+u²)/(1−u). Integrate (1−u)du/(1+u²)=dx/x.", "This gives arctan u−(1/2)ln(1+u²)=ln|x|+C, or arctan(y/x)−ln√(x²+y²)=C on a continuous local angle chart. Retain x−y≠0.", "(b) Put u=y/x: xu′=−u². For u≠0 integrate 1/u=ln|x|+C, hence y=x/(ln|x|+C). Also include y=0; original x≠0 remains.", "(c) Put u=y/x with x,y≠0: xu′=(1+u²)/(2u). Integrate 2u du/(1+u²)=dx/x.", "Thus ln(1+u²)=ln|x|+C, giving x²+y²=A|x|³, A>0, on intervals with y≠0. There are no real constant-u roots since 1+u²>0.", "(d) Put u=y/x: xu′=−u³/(1+u+u²). For u≠0 integrate −(u^(−3)+u^(−2)+u^(−1))du=dx/x.", "Hence 1/(2u²)+1/u−ln|u|=ln|x|+C, or x²/(2y²)+x/y−ln|y|=C. Also retain y=0 for x≠0.", "The denominator x²+xy+y² vanishes only at the origin. The last implicit expression can extend across x=0,y≠0; direct differentiation verifies the original equation there as well."], "solution": "(a) Put u=y/x, x≠0: xu′=(1+u²)/(1−u). Integrate (1−u)du/(1+u²)=dx/x.\nThis gives arctan u−(1/2)ln(1+u²)=ln|x|+C, or arctan(y/x)−ln√(x²+y²)=C on a continuous local angle chart. Retain x−y≠0.\n(b) Put u=y/x: xu′=−u². For u≠0 integrate 1/u=ln|x|+C, hence y=x/(ln|x|+C). Also include y=0; original x≠0 remains.\n(c) Put u=y/x with x,y≠0: xu′=(1+u²)/(2u). Integrate 2u du/(1+u²)=dx/x.\nThus ln(1+u²)=ln|x|+C, giving x²+y²=A|x|³, A>0, on intervals with y≠0. There are no real constant-u roots since 1+u²>0.\n(d) Put u=y/x: xu′=−u³/(1+u+u²). For u≠0 integrate −(u^(−3)+u^(−2)+u^(−1))du=dx/x.\nHence 1/(2u²)+1/u−ln|u|=ln|x|+C, or x²/(2y²)+x/y−ln|y|=C. Also retain y=0 for x≠0.\nThe denominator x²+xy+y² vanishes only at the origin. The last implicit expression can extend across x=0,y≠0; direct differentiation verifies the original equation there as well.", "answer": "(a) arctan(y/x)−ln√(x²+y²)=C locally; (b) x/(ln|x|+C) plus zero; (c) x²+y²=A|x|³; (d) x²/(2y²)+x/y−ln|y|=C plus zero.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "Reduce to homogeneous form and solve: (a) xy′=y+√(x²+y²), y(4)=3; (b) (x⁴+y⁴) dx=2x³y dy, y(1)=0.", "steps": ["(a) Near x=4>0 put u=y/x. Then xu′=√(1+u²). Integrate asinh u=ln x+C, equivalently u+√(1+u²)=Ax with A>0.", "At x=4,y=3, u=3/4 and u+√(1+u²)=2, so A=1/2. Thus y+√(x²+y²)=x²/2.", "Solve the relation: y=x²/4−1. Check √(x²+y²)=x²/4+1, so xy′=x²/2=y+√(x²+y²), and y(4)=3. The polynomial solution satisfies the source equation even at x=0.", "(b) A finite derivative at (1,0) would require 1+0=2·1³·0·y′=0, a contradiction. Therefore no classical differentiable y(x) solution satisfies this IVP.", "A geometric integral curve can still have a vertical tangent there. Put z=y²: x⁴+z²=x³z′. Then u=z/x² gives xu′=(u−1)².", "For u≠1 integrate −1/(u−1)=ln|x|+C. Using u(1)=0 gives C=1. Thus y²=x² ln x/(1+ln x).", "For x just above 1, the geometric branches are y=±x√(ln x/(1+ln x)); their derivatives diverge at x=1. They satisfy the differential form as a curve, but do not provide a differentiable y(x) IVP at that endpoint.", "The divided-out u=1 curves y=±x solve the differential form but fail y(1)=0. Keep the distinction between a geometric integral curve and a classical function solution."], "solution": "(a) Near x=4>0 put u=y/x. Then xu′=√(1+u²). Integrate asinh u=ln x+C, equivalently u+√(1+u²)=Ax with A>0.\nAt x=4,y=3, u=3/4 and u+√(1+u²)=2, so A=1/2. Thus y+√(x²+y²)=x²/2.\nSolve the relation: y=x²/4−1. Check √(x²+y²)=x²/4+1, so xy′=x²/2=y+√(x²+y²), and y(4)=3. The polynomial solution satisfies the source equation even at x=0.\n(b) A finite derivative at (1,0) would require 1+0=2·1³·0·y′=0, a contradiction. Therefore no classical differentiable y(x) solution satisfies this IVP.\nA geometric integral curve can still have a vertical tangent there. Put z=y²: x⁴+z²=x³z′. Then u=z/x² gives xu′=(u−1)².\nFor u≠1 integrate −1/(u−1)=ln|x|+C. Using u(1)=0 gives C=1. Thus y²=x² ln x/(1+ln x).\nFor x just above 1, the geometric branches are y=±x√(ln x/(1+ln x)); their derivatives diverge at x=1. They satisfy the differential form as a curve, but do not provide a differentiable y(x) IVP at that endpoint.\nThe divided-out u=1 curves y=±x solve the differential form but fail y(1)=0. Keep the distinction between a geometric integral curve and a classical function solution.", "answer": "(a) y=x²/4−1; (b) no classical differentiable y(x) IVP; geometric curve y²=x² ln x/(1+ln x) has a vertical tangent at (1,0).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "The slope at P(x,y) is (y−1)/(1−x). Find the curve through P(4,−3).", "steps": ["Write y′=(y−1)/(1−x), retaining x≠1.", "The constant y=1 is a solution, but does not pass through (4,−3).", "For y≠1, separate dy/(y−1)=dx/(1−x).", "Integrate ln|y−1|=−ln|1−x|+C, hence (y−1)(1−x)=K.", "At (4,−3), K=(−4)(−3)=12. Thus y=1+12/(1−x).", "The interval containing x=4 is x>1. Differentiate y′=12/(1−x)², equal to (y−1)/(1−x); y(4)=−3."], "solution": "Write y′=(y−1)/(1−x), retaining x≠1.\nThe constant y=1 is a solution, but does not pass through (4,−3).\nFor y≠1, separate dy/(y−1)=dx/(1−x).\nIntegrate ln|y−1|=−ln|1−x|+C, hence (y−1)(1−x)=K.\nAt (4,−3), K=(−4)(−3)=12. Thus y=1+12/(1−x).\nThe interval containing x=4 is x>1. Differentiate y′=12/(1−x)², equal to (y−1)/(1−x); y(4)=−3.", "answer": "y=1+12/(1−x), x>1.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "Find the solution curve of xyy′=3y²+x² through P(−1,2).", "steps": ["Use z=y², giving z′=2yy′. The equation becomes z′−6z/x=2x with x≠0.", "Multiply by integrating factor x^(−6): (z x^(−6))′=2x^(−5).", "Integrate: z x^(−6)=−(1/2)x^(−4)+C, hence y²=Cx⁶−x²/2.", "Use x=−1,y=2: 4=C−1/2, so C=9/2.", "Choose the positive branch through (−1,2): y=√((9x⁶−x²)/2). Its interval is x<−1/√3.", "At the endpoint y=0, the original equation would demand 0=x²≠0, so the classical solution cannot include that endpoint. Differentiating y² verifies 2xyy′=27x⁶−x², or xyy′=3y²+x² after substitution."], "solution": "Use z=y², giving z′=2yy′. The equation becomes z′−6z/x=2x with x≠0.\nMultiply by integrating factor x^(−6): (z x^(−6))′=2x^(−5).\nIntegrate: z x^(−6)=−(1/2)x^(−4)+C, hence y²=Cx⁶−x²/2.\nUse x=−1,y=2: 4=C−1/2, so C=9/2.\nChoose the positive branch through (−1,2): y=√((9x⁶−x²)/2). Its interval is x<−1/√3.\nAt the endpoint y=0, the original equation would demand 0=x²≠0, so the classical solution cannot include that endpoint. Differentiating y² verifies 2xyy′=27x⁶−x², or xyy′=3y²+x² after substitution.", "answer": "y=√((9x⁶−x²)/2), x<−1/√3.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q7", "qNo": "7", "question": "Find the real portion of the solution curve of x e^(y/x) dx+y dx=x dy through P(1,0).", "steps": ["Divide the source form by x dx with x≠0: y′=e^(y/x)+y/x.", "Set u=y/x, so y′=u+xu′ and xu′=e^u.", "Separate e^(−u)du=dx/x and integrate −e^(−u)=ln|x|+C.", "At (1,0), u=0 gives C=−1. Thus e^(−u)=1−ln x on the positive-x chart.", "For a real solution require 1−ln x>0, so 0 < x < e. Taking logs gives y=−x ln(1−ln x).", "Differentiate: y′=−ln(1−ln x)+1/(1−ln x)=y/x+e^(y/x). Also y(1)=0, confirming the entire real interval through the initial point."], "solution": "Divide the source form by x dx with x≠0: y′=e^(y/x)+y/x.\nSet u=y/x, so y′=u+xu′ and xu′=e^u.\nSeparate e^(−u)du=dx/x and integrate −e^(−u)=ln|x|+C.\nAt (1,0), u=0 gives C=−1. Thus e^(−u)=1−ln x on the positive-x chart.\nFor a real solution require 1−ln x>0, so 0 < x < e. Taking logs gives y=−x ln(1−ln x).\nDifferentiate: y′=−ln(1−ln x)+1/(1−ln x)=y/x+e^(y/x). Also y(1)=0, confirming the entire real interval through the initial point.", "answer": "y=−x ln(1−ln x), 0 < x < e.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q8", "qNo": "8", "question": "A particle moves along the x-axis with velocity equal to half its abscissa minus three times the time. At t=2,x=−4. Determine its motion along the x-axis.", "steps": ["Translate the velocity statement into x′=x/2−3t.", "Try a linear particular solution x=at+b: a=(at+b)/2−3t. Coefficients give a=6,b=12.", "The homogeneous equation x′=x/2 has solution Ce^(t/2). Thus x(t)=6t+12+Ce^(t/2).", "Use x(2)=−4: −4=24+Ce, so C=−28/e.", "Therefore x(t)=6t+12−28e^((t−2)/2).", "Differentiate: x′=6−14e^((t−2)/2)=x/2−3t, and x(2)=−4. The quantity is signed position, rather than cumulative distance."], "solution": "Translate the velocity statement into x′=x/2−3t.\nTry a linear particular solution x=at+b: a=(at+b)/2−3t. Coefficients give a=6,b=12.\nThe homogeneous equation x′=x/2 has solution Ce^(t/2). Thus x(t)=6t+12+Ce^(t/2).\nUse x(2)=−4: −4=24+Ce, so C=−28/e.\nTherefore x(t)=6t+12−28e^((t−2)/2).\nDifferentiate: x′=6−14e^((t−2)/2)=x/2−3t, and x(2)=−4. The quantity is signed position, rather than cumulative distance.", "answer": "x(t)=6t+12−28e^((t−2)/2).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q9", "qNo": "9", "question": "Oil consumption rate (billions of barrels) is dx/dt=1.2e^(0.04t), with t=0 corresponding to 1990. Find oil used from 1990 to 1995 and over t=8 years.", "steps": ["The total oil used during an interval is the integral of the specified consumption rate, in billions of barrels.", "For T years since 1990, integrate ∫₀ᵀ1.2e^(0.04t)dt=[30e^(0.04t)]₀ᵀ.", "Hence the amount is 30(e^(0.04T)−1). The subtraction removes the initial antiderivative constant.", "From 1990 to 1995, T=5: amount=30(e^0.2−1)≈6.64208 billion barrels.", "For t=8 years, amount=30(e^0.32−1)≈11.31383 billion barrels.", "Differentiating the cumulative expression recovers 1.2e^(0.04T), and at T=0 the used amount is zero."], "solution": "The total oil used during an interval is the integral of the specified consumption rate, in billions of barrels.\nFor T years since 1990, integrate ∫₀ᵀ1.2e^(0.04t)dt=[30e^(0.04t)]₀ᵀ.\nHence the amount is 30(e^(0.04T)−1). The subtraction removes the initial antiderivative constant.\nFrom 1990 to 1995, T=5: amount=30(e^0.2−1)≈6.64208 billion barrels.\nFor t=8 years, amount=30(e^0.32−1)≈11.31383 billion barrels.\nDifferentiating the cumulative expression recovers 1.2e^(0.04T), and at T=0 the used amount is zero.", "answer": "About 6.64208 and 11.31383 billion barrels, respectively.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q10", "qNo": "10", "question": "Infection rate (people per month) is dI/dt=100t/(t²+1), with t months since outbreak. Find total infected people over the first four months.", "steps": ["The additional number infected over the first four months is the integral of the source rate from t=0 to t=4.", "Set u=t²+1, so du=2t dt.", "Integrate 100t/(t²+1): an antiderivative is 50ln(t²+1).", "Evaluate [50ln(t²+1)]₀⁴=50ln17−50ln1=50ln17.", "This is approximately 141.66067, about 142 people as a continuous-model estimate.", "An initial infected count is not supplied: the integral gives new infections over the interval; a cumulative total including existing cases would additionally require I(0). Differentiation verifies the given rate."], "solution": "The additional number infected over the first four months is the integral of the source rate from t=0 to t=4.\nSet u=t²+1, so du=2t dt.\nIntegrate 100t/(t²+1): an antiderivative is 50ln(t²+1).\nEvaluate [50ln(t²+1)]₀⁴=50ln17−50ln1=50ln17.\nThis is approximately 141.66067, about 142 people as a continuous-model estimate.\nAn initial infected count is not supplied: the integral gives new infections over the interval; a cumulative total including existing cases would additionally require I(0). Differentiation verifies the given rate.", "answer": "50ln17≈141.66067 additional infections, about 142.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}, {"id": "ex-10-2-q11", "qNo": "10 (repeated)", "question": "Determine orthogonal trajectories: (a) y=cx³; (b) xy=c; (c) y=cxe^x; (d) y²=x²+C. [The source repeats question number 10.]", "steps": ["At regular intersections replace the family slope by its negative reciprocal, using tangent vectors for horizontal/vertical exceptions.", "(a) From y=cx³, y′=3y/x. Orthogonal y′=−x/(3y) integrates to x²+3y²=K. Regular trajectories have K>0.", "(b) From xy=c, y′=−y/x. Orthogonal y′=x/y integrates to y²−x²=K. Restrict to regular intersections of the original family.", "(c) From y=cxe^x, y′=y(x+1)/x. Orthogonal y′=−x/[(x+1)y].", "Integrate y dy=−[1−1/(x+1)]dx to obtain y²+2x−2ln|x+1|=K on x≠−1.", "Also the vertical line x=−1 is an orthogonal trajectory, because every original-family tangent there is horizontal. The common origin of the original family is singular as a family and needs exclusion from regular orthogonality claims.", "(d) From y²=x²+C, y′=x/y. Orthogonal y′=−y/x integrates to xy=K. For K=0, the coordinate axes give the horizontal/vertical exceptional trajectories away from singular intersections.", "Differentiate each implicit result to recover its orthogonal slope. At slope exceptions test perpendicular tangent vectors rather than divide by zero."], "solution": "At regular intersections replace the family slope by its negative reciprocal, using tangent vectors for horizontal/vertical exceptions.\n(a) From y=cx³, y′=3y/x. Orthogonal y′=−x/(3y) integrates to x²+3y²=K. Regular trajectories have K>0.\n(b) From xy=c, y′=−y/x. Orthogonal y′=x/y integrates to y²−x²=K. Restrict to regular intersections of the original family.\n(c) From y=cxe^x, y′=y(x+1)/x. Orthogonal y′=−x/[(x+1)y].\nIntegrate y dy=−[1−1/(x+1)]dx to obtain y²+2x−2ln|x+1|=K on x≠−1.\nAlso the vertical line x=−1 is an orthogonal trajectory, because every original-family tangent there is horizontal. The common origin of the original family is singular as a family and needs exclusion from regular orthogonality claims.\n(d) From y²=x²+C, y′=x/y. Orthogonal y′=−y/x integrates to xy=K. For K=0, the coordinate axes give the horizontal/vertical exceptional trajectories away from singular intersections.\nDifferentiate each implicit result to recover its orthogonal slope. At slope exceptions test perpendicular tangent vectors rather than divide by zero.", "answer": "(a) x²+3y²=K; (b) y²−x²=K; (c) y²+2x−2ln|x+1|=K plus x=−1; (d) xy=K, including axis exceptions.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 297, "jpg": "153_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions and initial values checked; repeated question number retained"}]}];
const math12Ex102=MATH_12_DATA[9].exercises.find(e=>e.title==='Exercise 10.2');math12Ex102.unreviewedProblems=math12Ex102.problems;math12Ex102.problems=math12ReviewedExercise102;math12Ex102.pageStart=math12Ex102.pageEnd=297;
const math12ReviewedReview10=[{"id": "review-10-i", "qNo": "i", "question": "The order of 3y′+2y=5 is: (a) 1; (b) 2; (c) 3; (d) 4.", "steps": ["The equation contains y and the first derivative y′.", "There is no second or higher derivative.", "Order records the highest derivative present.", "The coefficient 3 does not make the order 3.", "Therefore the order is 1.", "Select (a)."], "solution": "The equation contains y and the first derivative y′.\nThere is no second or higher derivative.\nOrder records the highest derivative present.\nThe coefficient 3 does not make the order 3.\nTherefore the order is 1.\nSelect (a).", "answer": "(a) 1.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 298, "jpg": "153_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All four source choices checked visually; literal stem mismatch retained"}]}, {"id": "review-10-ii", "qNo": "ii", "question": "The degree of (y″)²−y′+y=0 is: (a) 1; (b) 2; (c) 3; (d) 4.", "steps": ["The highest-order derivative is y″.", "The equation is polynomial in its derivatives.", "The highest derivative appears squared.", "Hence its degree is 2, even though the lower derivative is linear.", "Order is also 2 here, but order and degree measure different things.", "Select (b)."], "solution": "The highest-order derivative is y″.\nThe equation is polynomial in its derivatives.\nThe highest derivative appears squared.\nHence its degree is 2, even though the lower derivative is linear.\nOrder is also 2 here, but order and degree measure different things.\nSelect (b).", "answer": "(b) 2.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 298, "jpg": "153_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All four source choices checked visually; literal stem mismatch retained"}]}, {"id": "review-10-iii", "qNo": "iii", "question": "A solution depending on an arbitrary constant quantity is called: (a) exact solution; (b) particular; (c) general solution; (d) none of these.", "steps": ["A family parametrized by an arbitrary constant is the general solution in this first-order context.", "A particular solution fixes that constant using a condition.", "An exact solution is an expression without numerical approximation, a different distinction.", "The printed choices include the usual term general solution.", "No constant is fixed in the stem.", "Select (c)."], "solution": "A family parametrized by an arbitrary constant is the general solution in this first-order context.\nA particular solution fixes that constant using a condition.\nAn exact solution is an expression without numerical approximation, a different distinction.\nThe printed choices include the usual term general solution.\nNo constant is fixed in the stem.\nSelect (c).", "answer": "(c) general solution.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 298, "jpg": "153_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All four source choices checked visually; literal stem mismatch retained"}]}, {"id": "review-10-iv", "qNo": "iv", "question": "The solution of y′=sin x at y(0)=1 is: (a) y=sin x+2; (b) y=−sin x+2; (c) y=cos x+2; (d) y=−cos x+2.", "steps": ["Integrate sin x: y=−cos x+C.", "At x=0, y=1 and cos0=1.", "Thus 1=−1+C, giving C=2.", "Therefore y=−cos x+2.", "Differentiate to get sin x and evaluate y(0)=1.", "Select (d)."], "solution": "Integrate sin x: y=−cos x+C.\nAt x=0, y=1 and cos0=1.\nThus 1=−1+C, giving C=2.\nTherefore y=−cos x+2.\nDifferentiate to get sin x and evaluate y(0)=1.\nSelect (d).", "answer": "(d) y=−cos x+2.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 298, "jpg": "153_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All four source choices checked visually; literal stem mismatch retained"}]}, {"id": "review-10-v", "qNo": "v", "question": "The solution of yy′=cos x at y(0)=−1 is: (a) y=−√(2sin x+1); (b) y=√(2sin x+1); (c) y=−√(2cos x+1); (d) y=√(2cos x+1).", "steps": ["Separate y dy=cos x dx.", "Integrate y²/2=sin x+C.", "At x=0,y=−1, C=1/2.", "Hence y²=2sin x+1. Choose the negative square root to satisfy the initial sign.", "The solution is y=−√(2sin x+1) on the interval (−π/6,7π/6) containing zero, where the radicand is positive.", "At its zero endpoints yy′=cos x cannot hold with a finite derivative, since cos x is nonzero. Select (a)."], "solution": "Separate y dy=cos x dx.\nIntegrate y²/2=sin x+C.\nAt x=0,y=−1, C=1/2.\nHence y²=2sin x+1. Choose the negative square root to satisfy the initial sign.\nThe solution is y=−√(2sin x+1) on the interval (−π/6,7π/6) containing zero, where the radicand is positive.\nAt its zero endpoints yy′=cos x cannot hold with a finite derivative, since cos x is nonzero. Select (a).", "answer": "(a) y=−√(2sin x+1), on the interval through zero with positive radicand.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 298, "jpg": "153_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All four source choices checked visually; literal stem mismatch retained"}]}, {"id": "review-10-vi", "qNo": "vi", "question": "The differential equation for orthogonal trajectories of x²+y²=c is: (a) dy/dx=cx; (b) y=cx; (c) dy/dx=ct; (d) cy=dy/dθ.", "steps": ["Differentiate the circle equation: 2x+2yy′=0.", "At points with finite nonzero slope, circle slope is −x/y.", "The orthogonal slope is y/x, so the differential equation is y′=y/x.", "Integrating gives radial lines y=Cx, with vertical x=0 handled separately.", "Choice (b) gives the trajectory family, rather than the requested differential equation. None of the printed options is the correct general differential equation.", "If the intended request was the finite-slope trajectory family, (b) is the intended answer; for the literal stem there is no correct option."], "solution": "Differentiate the circle equation: 2x+2yy′=0.\nAt points with finite nonzero slope, circle slope is −x/y.\nThe orthogonal slope is y/x, so the differential equation is y′=y/x.\nIntegrating gives radial lines y=Cx, with vertical x=0 handled separately.\nChoice (b) gives the trajectory family, rather than the requested differential equation. None of the printed options is the correct general differential equation.\nIf the intended request was the finite-slope trajectory family, (b) is the intended answer; for the literal stem there is no correct option.", "answer": "No correct differential-equation option; (b) is the finite-slope trajectory family.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 298, "jpg": "153_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All four source choices checked visually; literal stem mismatch retained"}]}, {"id": "review-10-vii", "qNo": "vii", "question": "Orthogonal trajectories of y²=4ax are: (a) y²=k−2x²; (b) x²=k−2y²; (c) x²=k+2y²; (d) y²=2x²−k.", "steps": ["Differentiate with a fixed: 2yy′=4a.", "Eliminate a using a=y²/(4x), giving y′=y/(2x) at regular points.", "The orthogonal slope is −2x/y.", "Separate y dy=−2x dx and integrate y²/2=−x²+C.", "Thus y²+2x²=k, or y²=k−2x². Use k>0 for nondegenerate regular trajectories.", "Select (a); horizontal/vertical cases follow from perpendicular tangent vectors."], "solution": "Differentiate with a fixed: 2yy′=4a.\nEliminate a using a=y²/(4x), giving y′=y/(2x) at regular points.\nThe orthogonal slope is −2x/y.\nSeparate y dy=−2x dx and integrate y²/2=−x²+C.\nThus y²+2x²=k, or y²=k−2x². Use k>0 for nondegenerate regular trajectories.\nSelect (a); horizontal/vertical cases follow from perpendicular tangent vectors.", "answer": "(a) y²=k−2x².", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 298, "jpg": "153_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All four source choices checked visually; literal stem mismatch retained"}]}];
const math12Rev10=MATH_12_DATA[9].exercises.find(e=>e.title==='Review Exercise 10');math12Rev10.unreviewedProblems=math12Rev10.problems;math12Rev10.problems=math12ReviewedReview10;math12Rev10.pageStart=math12Rev10.pageEnd=298;
MATH_12_DATA[9].sourceSummaryBlocks=[{"type": "heading", "text": "Summary"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A differential equation is an equation that involves the derivatives of an unknown function (dependent variable) of one or more variables (independent variables)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The order of a differential equation is the order of the highest-order derivative which appears in the equation."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The degree of a differential equation is the power of the highest-order derivative which appears in the equation."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A solution of an ordinary differential in one dependent variable y on an interval I is a function y(x) which, when substituted for the dependent variable y and its derivatives y′,y″,…, reduces the differential equation to an identity in the independent variable x over interval I."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A differential equation is linear in a set of one or more of its dependent variables if and only if each term of the equation which contains a variable of the set or any of its derivatives is of the first degree in those variables and their derivatives."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A function f(x,y) is homogeneous function of degree n in variables x and y if and only if for all values of the variables x,y and every positive λ, the identity is true:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f(\\lambda x,\\lambda y)=\\lambda^n f(x,y),\\quad n=1,2,3,\\ldots\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The differential equation:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{f(x,y)}{g(x,y)}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "is called a homogeneous differential equation if it defines a homogeneous function of degree zero."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Degree requires polynomial dependence on derivatives under the stated convention. For linearity, dependent variables and their derivatives appear only as a linear combination, with coefficients depending on independent variables; a product such as yy′ is nonlinear even though each factor separately has power one. Homogeneity of a first-order ratio is different from homogeneous linear equations. Homogeneous functions can also have degree zero or other real degrees on appropriate scaling domains; keep denominator and scaling-domain conditions."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "History"}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/ode299-jacob-bernoulli.jpg", "caption": "Jacob Bernoulli (1654–1705)", "alt": "Source portrait of Jacob Bernoulli."}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/ode299-johann-bernoulli.jpg", "caption": "Johann Bernoulli (1667–1748)", "alt": "Source portrait of Johann Bernoulli."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Jacob Bernoulli and Johann Bernoulli we two Swiss mathematicians. They were first interpreters of Leibniz’s version of differential equations. Both brothers orgue about Newton’s theories and maintained that the Newton’s theory of fluxions was plagiarized from the Leibniz’s original work. Both brothers worked hard using differential calculus to disprove Newton’s principle. The could not accept the theory which Newton had given, that earth and other plants roate around the sun in elliptical orbits. The first book on differential calculus was written on the bases of Bernoulli brothers ideas by Gabriele Manfred in 1707. Most of the publications were made on differential equations and partial differential equations in 18th century."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The historical passage, including printed spellings and claims, is retained as source text. It is not independently verified history; its allegations should not be treated as established facts merely because the textbook states them."}];MATH_12_DATA[9].sourceSummaryReferences=[{page:299,jpg:"154_page_1.jpg"}];
MATH_12_DATA[10].sourcePages=[{"file": "154_page_2.jpg", "page": 300, "title": "PARTIAL DIFFERENTIATION", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Introduction"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The goal of this unit is to extend the methods of single variable differential calculus to functions of two variables. In many practical situations, the value of one quantity may depend on the values of two or more others. For example, the amount of water in a reservoir may depend on the amount of rainfall and on the amount of water consumed by local residents. The current in an electrical circuit may vary with the electromotive force, the capacitance, the resistance, and the impedance in the circuit. The flow of blood from an artery into a small capillary may depend on the diameter of the capillary and the pressure in both the artery and the capillary. The output of a factory may depend on the amount of capital invested in the plant and on the size of the labor force. We will analyse such situations using functions of several variables."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "In many problems involving functions of several variables, the goal is to find the derivative of the function with respect to one of its variables when all the others are held constant. In this unit, we need to develop the concept and shall see how it can be used to find slopes and rates of change in case of two variables function."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "11.1 Differentiation of the function of two variables"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "In the real world, physical quantities often depend on two or more variables. For example, we might be concerned with the temperature on a metal plate at various points at time t. The locations of temperature on the plate are given as ordered pairs (x,y), so that the temperature T can be considered as a function of two location variables x and y, as well as a time variable t. The notation of a function of single variable, we might extend this as T(x,y,t). We begin our study of function of two variables by examining this notation and a few other basic concepts."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For illustration, if a company produces x items at a cost of 10 rupees per item, then total cost C(x) of producing x items is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(C(x)=10x\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The cost is a function of one independent variable, the number of items produced. If the company wants to produce two products, with x of one at 10 rupees each and y of another at 15 rupees each, the total cost is a function of two independent variables x and y:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(C(x,y)=10x+15y\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "When x=5 and y=12, the total cost is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(C(5,12)=10(5)+15(12)=230\\ \\mathrm{rupees}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "11.1.1 Function of two variables"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A function z=f(x,y) is a function of two variables x and y, if for each given pair (x,y), we determine a single value of z. Where x,y and z are real variables."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete native two-variable examples, domain/range and marginal profit explanation", "objectives": [["11.1 Differentiation of function of two variables", ["Define a function of two variables.", "Define partial derivative.", "Find partial derivatives of a function of two variables."]], ["11.2 Euler’s Theorem", ["Define a homogeneous function of degree n.", "State and prove Euler’s theorem on homogeneous functions.", "Verify Euler’s theorem for homogeneous functions of different degrees (simple cases).", "Use MAPLE command diff to find partial derivatives."]]], "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 154, "side": "right", "reason": "All objectives, real-domain inequality, cost and profit coefficients visually checked"}}, {"file": "155_page_1.jpg", "page": 301, "title": "PARTIAL DIFFERENTIATION", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "The real numbers x and y are independent variables; z is the dependent variable. The set of all ordered pairs of real numbers (x,y) such that f(x,y) is a real number is the domain of f, and the set of all values of f(x,y) is the range."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 1"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Show z=f(x,y)=√(1−x+y) is a function of two independent variables x,y. Find its domain and range."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For this function, show the transformation of two independent variables (inputs) x and y gives just a single dependent variable z. For x=2,y=1:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z=f(2,1)=\\sqrt{1-2+1}=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This gives just one real value z=0, and the function is therefore declared a function of two independent variables x,y. Its domain is the set of all ordered pairs for which the square root is defined:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(1-x+y\\ge0\\quad\\mathrm{or}\\quad y\\ge x-1\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Since z must be nonnegative, the range is all z≥0."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Functions of more than one independent variable are called multivariate functions."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "A real domain may be specified as a subset D⊆ℝ² rather than automatically being the maximal formula domain. In Example 1, the principal square root gives exactly one output for every pair with y≥x−1; checking a single input illustrates this but does not prove the function property by itself. Every z≥0 is attained, for example at (x,y)=(0,z²−1), which proves the full range."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "11.1.2 Partial derivative"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "To give a clear concept of partial derivative, consider a real-life situation. A small firm makes two products, radios and audiocassette recorders. The profit from these is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(P(x,y)=40x^2-10xy+5y^2-80\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Here x is the number of radios sold and y is the number of recorders sold. How do changes in x or y affect P? Suppose radio sales stay at 10 units and only recorder sales vary. Management wants the rate (marginal profit/derivative of the profit function) with which the number of recorders sold changes. Fixing x=10 reduces the two-variable function to a single-variable function:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(P(10,y)=40(10)^2-10(10)y+5y^2-80=3920-100y+5y^2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This gives profit from recorder sales assuming x is fixed at 10 radios. Its ordinary derivative with respect to y is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{d}{dy}P(10,y)=-100+10y\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This represents the per-unit profit from y recorders. The notation d/dy P(10,y) usually stands for an ordinary derivative of a single-variable function. In this two-variable case, the rate with respect to y is a partial derivative, so replace the ordinary derivative in (ii) by ∂/∂y P(10,y):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂ P}{∂ y}=\\frac{∂}{∂ y}P(10,y)=P_y=-100+10y\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Prime notation is not allowed."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Informally, the partial derivative ∂f(x,y)/∂x with respect to x is obtained by keeping x variable and y constant."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The derivative measures marginal change in profit per additional recorder near y, not total profit per unit sold. More precisely, P_y(x,y)=−10x+10y and P_y(10,y)=−100+10y. The restricted function g(y)=P(10,y) has ordinary derivative g′(y)=P_y(10,y); prime notation is valid for g but ambiguous for the unspecialized two-variable P. Unit counts are discrete physically; differentiation treats the polynomial as a continuous approximation."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Complete native two-variable examples, domain/range and marginal profit explanation", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 155, "side": "left", "reason": "All objectives, real-domain inequality, cost and profit coefficients visually checked"}}, {"file": "155_page_2.jpg", "page": 302, "title": "PARTIAL DIFFERENTIATION", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "The partial derivative ∂f(x,y)/∂y with respect to y is obtained by keeping y variable and x constant."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "11.1.3 Partial derivatives of a function of two variables"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If z=f(x,y) is a function of two variables, its first partial derivatives with respect to x and y are fₓ and fᵧ, respectively, defined by:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂}{∂x}f(x,y)=f_x=\\lim_{\\Delta x\\to0}\\frac{f(x+\\Delta x,y)-f(x,y)}{\\Delta x}\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂}{∂y}f(x,y)=f_y=\\lim_{\\Delta y\\to0}\\frac{f(x,y+\\Delta y)-f(x,y)}{\\Delta y}\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Provided the limits exist."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If f(x,y)=x³y+xy², find partial derivatives fₓ and fᵧ."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The partial derivatives w.r.t. x and y are:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=f_x=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^3y+xy^2)=3x^2y+y^2,\\quad y\\ \\mathrm{constant}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=f_y=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^3y+xy^2)=x^3+2xy,\\quad x\\ \\mathrm{constant}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 3"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The function is z=x² sin(3x+y³). Find zₓ and zᵧ at (π/3,0)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "With y constant, use the product and chain rules:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z_x=\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left[x^2\\sin(3x+y^3)\\right]\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z_x=2x\\sin(3x+y^3)+x^2\\cos(3x+y^3)\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y^3)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z_x=2x\\sin(3x+y^3)+x^2\\cos(3x+y^3)(3+0)=2x\\sin(3x+y^3)+3x^2\\cos(3x+y^3)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z_x(\\pi/3,0)=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{3}+0\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi^2}{9}\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{3}+0\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\sin\\pi+\\frac{3\\pi^2}{9}\\cos\\pi=-\\frac{\\pi^2}{3}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "With x constant, the derivative w.r.t. y is:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z_y=\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left[x^2\\sin(3x+y^3)\\right]=x^2\\cos(3x+y^3)\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x+y^3)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z_y=x^2\\cos(3x+y^3)(0+3y^2)=3x^2y^2\\cos(3x+y^3)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z_y(\\pi/3,0)=3\\left(\\frac{\\pi^2}{9}\\right)(0)^2\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{3}\\right)=0\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 4"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Suppose the temperature of water at a point on a river where a nuclear power plant discharges hot waste water is approximated by:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(T(x,y)=2x+5y+xy-40\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "x represents river-water temperature in degrees Celsius before it reaches the plant; y is the number of megawatts (in hundreds) produced by the plant. (a) Find and interpret Tₓ(9,5). (b) Find and interpret Tᵧ(9,5)."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "(a) The partial derivative of (i) w.r.t. x is the rate of change in T with respect to x:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(T_x=2+y,\\quad y\\ \\mathrm{constant}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "For each partial limit the other coordinate is fixed. A product or chain rule still applies to any dependence on the variable being differentiated. In Example 3, trigonometric derivatives and π/3 are in radians. Example 4 continues on printed 303; one y-unit represents 100 megawatts, so units must be retained in the interpretation."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Native partial limits, full product/chain rule calculations and source temperature model", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 155, "side": "right", "reason": "All partial symbols, difference quotient increments, exponents and model coefficients visually checked"}}, {"file": "156_page_1.jpg", "page": 303, "title": "PARTIAL DIFFERENTIATION", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "At x=9,y=5, Tₓ(9,5)=2+y=2+5=7, the approximate change in temperature resulting from a one degree increase in input water temperature, if input electricity y remains constant at 500 megawatts."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "(b) The partial derivative w.r.t. y is Tᵧ=5+x, with x constant."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(T_y(9,5)=5+x=5+9=14\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This is the approximate change in temperature resulting from a one megawatt increase in production of electricity if the input water temperature x remains constant at 9°C."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The source defines y in hundreds of megawatts. Consequently Tᵧ=14°C per 100 MW, equivalently 0.14°C per MW, not 14°C per MW as the interpretation says. Tₓ=7°C of output per 1°C of input at fixed 500 MW. Also T(9,5)=48°C; the derivatives describe changes, not this temperature value."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Exercise 11.1"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "1. For f(x,y)=x²y+xy² and real t, find (a) f(0,0); (b) f(−1,0); (c) f(0,−1); (d) f(t,t); (e) f(t,t²); (f) f(1−t,t)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "2. For f(x,y,z)=x²y e^(2x)+(x+y−z)², find (a) f(0,0,0); (b) f(1,−1,1); (c) f(−1,1,−1); (d) ∂/∂x f(x,x,x); (e) ∂/∂y f(1,y,1); (f) ∂/∂z f(1,1,z²)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "3. Find fₓ,fᵧ: (a) sin(x²)cos y; (b) √(3x²+y⁴); (c) xy³tan⁻¹y; (d) x³+x²y+xy²+y³; (e) sin⁻¹(xy); (f) x²e^(x+y)cos y."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "4. The United States production function was estimated as z=x^0.7 y^0.3, where x is labor and y capital. Find marginal productivity of labor ∂z/∂x and capital ∂z/∂y."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "5. The Canadian production function is z=x^0.4 y^0.6, where x is labor and y capital. Find marginal productivity of labor ∂z/∂x and capital ∂z/∂y."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "6. For f(x,y)=x²y+xy², find fₓ,fᵧ using the definition of partial derivatives."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "History"}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/partial303-euler.jpg", "caption": "Leonhard Euler (1707–1783)", "alt": "Source portrait of Leonhard Euler."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Leonhard Euler was a Swiss Mathematician, Physicist, Astronomer and Engineer. He made the important and influential contributions in many branches of Mathematics, such as calculus, graph theory, topology and analytic number theory. He also made significant contribution in mechanics, fluid dynamics, optics and music theory. He was the first person who introduced f(x) to denoted the function f applied to the argument x. In 1735 he introduced a theorem known by his name Euler theorem."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "This historical passage is retained as printed textbook text; its dates and priority claims are not independently verified here."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Full temperature interpretation, all six native exercise groups and source Euler portrait", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 156, "side": "left", "reason": "Enlarged exercise source checked for signs, input coordinates and exponents; temperature units kept distinct"}}, {"file": "156_page_2.jpg", "page": 304, "title": "PARTIAL DIFFERENTIATION", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "11.2 Euler’s Theorem"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The specialty of Euler’s theorem is to verify the degree of a homogeneous function. The homogeneous function z=f(x,y) is not altered if the real numbers x and y of a function z=f(x,y) are stretched or squeezed by any real scalar quantity λ."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "11.2.1 Homogeneous function of degree n"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A function f(x,y) is said to be a homogeneous function of degree n if, for all values of λ and some constant value n, we have:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f(\\lambda x,\\lambda y)=\\lambda^n f(x,y)\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "For example (a), f(x,y)=3x+4y is homogeneous of degree 1:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f(\\lambda x,\\lambda y)=3(\\lambda x)+4(\\lambda y)=\\lambda(3x)+\\lambda(4y)=\\lambda(3x,4y)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad f(\\lambda x,\\lambda y)=\\lambda f(x,y)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "(b) f(x,y)=3x²+4y² is homogeneous of degree 2:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f(\\lambda x,\\lambda y)=3(\\lambda x)^2+4(\\lambda y)^2=\\lambda^2(3x^2,4y^2)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad f(\\lambda x,\\lambda y)=\\lambda^2f(x,y)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "In general, a function f(x₁,x₂,x₃,…,xₙ) is homogeneous of degree n if for all λ and some constant n:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f(\\lambda x_1,\\lambda x_2,\\ldots,\\lambda x_n)=\\lambda^n f(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Remember"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A function f(x,y) is homogeneous of degree n if it is expressed in the form:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f(x,y)=x^n f\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 5"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Show that f(x,y)=2xy+y² is homogeneous of degree 2."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Verify the identity (i):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f(\\lambda x,\\lambda y)=2(\\lambda x)(\\lambda y)+(\\lambda y)^2=2\\lambda^2xy+\\lambda^2y^2=\\lambda^2(2xy+y^2)=\\lambda^2f(x,y),\\quad n=2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Thus the function is homogeneous of degree 2."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "11.2.2 Verification of Euler’s theorem for homogeneous functions of different degrees"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Statement: If z=f(x,y) is continuously differentiable and homogeneous of degree n, then:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x\\frac{∂z}{∂x}+y\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=nz\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Proof: If z=xⁿf(y/x), its partial derivatives are:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}x^n f\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=nx^{n-1}f\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)+x^nf′\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{y}{x^2}\\right)=nx^{n-1}f\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)-yx^{n-2}f′\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\)", "number": "ii"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=x^nf′\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\left(\\frac1x\\right)=x^{n-1}f′\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\)", "number": "iii"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Homogeneity means values scale by λⁿ, rather than remaining unchanged unless n=0. Use positive λ and a domain preserved by that scaling when n is real; arbitrary negative λ or zero need not be meaningful. The commas in the two source expansions should be plus signs: λ(3x+4y) and λ²(3x²+4y²). In the many-variable formula, use m for the variable count and n for degree to avoid forcing them equal. The one-variable representation is z=xⁿg(y/x), using a new function g, locally on x>0 for general real degree; it is not a universal global formula across x=0."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Full native homogeneity scaling identities and Euler proof, all example calculations", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 156, "side": "right", "reason": "All scaling exponents, chain-rule terms and proof cancellation visually checked"}}, {"file": "157_page_1.jpg", "page": 305, "title": "PARTIAL DIFFERENTIATION", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "Add equation (ii) multiplied by x and equation (iii) multiplied by y to obtain Euler’s method of order n:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x\\frac{∂z}{∂x}+y\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=x\\left[nx^{n-1}f\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)-yx^{n-2}f′\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right]+y\\left[x^{n-1}f′\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right]\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(=nx^nf\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)-yx^{n-1}f′\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)+yx^{n-1}f′\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)=nx^nf\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)=nz\\)", "number": "iv"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "A proof without dividing by x starts from f(λx,λy)=λⁿf(x,y) on an open scaling domain, differentiates with respect to λ, and sets λ=1: xfₓ+yfᵧ=nf. This covers regular points on x=0 as well. The theorem uses degree n, not derivative order n. For the zero function the identity holds for every n, so it does not determine a unique degree."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 6"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Use Euler’s theorem to verify z=f(x,y)=ax²+2bxy+cy² is homogeneous of degree 2."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The homogeneous function and its derivatives are:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z=ax^2+2bxy+cy^2,\\quad\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=2ax+2by,\\quad\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=2bx+2cy\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Use these in Euler’s result (iv):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x\\frac{∂z}{∂x}+y\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=x(2ax+2by)+y(2bx+2cy)=2ax^2+2bxy+2bxy+2cy^2=2(ax^2+2bxy+cy^2)=2z,\\quad n=2\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Euler’s procedure confirms second degree homogeneity."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 7"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If u=tan⁻¹((x³+y³)/(x−y)), show xuₓ+yuᵧ=sin2u."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The function u is not homogeneous, but introduce a new variable z to obtain homogeneous form:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(z=\\tan u=\\frac{x^3+y^3}{x-y}=\\frac{x^3\\left(1+(y/x)^3\\right)}{x(1-y/x)}=x^2f\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This is homogeneous of degree 2. Euler’s theorem gives:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x\\frac{∂z}{∂x}+y\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=2z,\\quad n=2\\)", "number": "i"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The partial derivatives of z=tan u are:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\sec^2u\\frac{∂u}{∂x},\\quad\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\sec^2u\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Substitute in (i):"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\sec^2u\\left(x\\frac{∂u}{∂x}+y\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\right)=2z,\\quad z=\\tan u\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(x\\frac{∂u}{∂x}+y\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=2z\\cos^2u=2\\frac{\\sin u}{\\cos u}\\cos^2u=2\\sin u\\cos u=\\sin2u\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Keep x≠y from the rational expression. For real principal arctangent, cos u≠0, so the chain-rule conversion is valid. Homogeneity applies to z=tan u rather than to u; an arctangent of a positive-degree homogeneous expression generally is not homogeneous. The source x²f(y/x) calculation is a chart with x≠0, but the rational function and Euler identity also apply at x=0,y≠0."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Full native homogeneity scaling identities and Euler proof, all example calculations", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 157, "side": "left", "reason": "All scaling exponents, chain-rule terms and proof cancellation visually checked"}}, {"file": "157_page_2.jpg", "page": 306, "title": "PARTIAL DIFFERENTIATION", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "11.2.3 MAPLE command “diff” to find partial derivatives"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Look at the following example; the use of MAPLE command diff is illustrated."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 8"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Use MAPLE command diff to find partial derivatives: (a) f(x,y)=x³+y³+3xy²+4x²y with respect to x,y; (b) f(x,y)=y sin x+x cos y+x² with respect to x,y."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Command:"}, {"type": "command", "text": "diff(x^3+y^3+3*x*y^2+4*x^2*y,x);", "result": "3x²+3y²+8xy", "number": 1}, {"type": "command", "text": "diff(x^3+y^3+3*x*y^2+4*x^2*y,y);", "result": "3y²+6xy+4x²", "number": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Command:"}, {"type": "command", "text": "diff(y*sin(x)+x*cos(y)+x^2,x);", "result": "ycos(x)+cos(y)+2x", "number": 1}, {"type": "command", "text": "diff(y*sin(x)+x*cos(y)+x^2,y);", "result": "sin(x)−xsin(y)", "number": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Using Palettes: Use the cursor button to select the expression in which you are interested. In this problem, the expression is a partial derivative palette. Click the partial derivative palette, insert the given function, and press ENTER to obtain the partial derivatives of the function:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^3+y^3+3xy^2+4x^2y)=3x^2+3y^2+8xy\\)", "number": "1"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^3+y^3+3xy^2+4x^2y)=3y^2+6xy+4x^2\\)", "number": "2"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The commands and outputs are native transcriptions of printed source results, not commands executed in SpaceBook. They can be verified directly by holding the other variable constant and applying the polynomial/product rules."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Exercise 11.2"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "1. Are these homogeneous? (a) u=sin⁻¹((x+y)/(√x+√y)); (b) z=(x+y)/(√x+√y); (c) z=x³e^(y/x)−3y²√(x²+y²); (d) z=(x²+3y²)^(1/3)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "2. Verify Euler’s theorem: (a) z=ax²+2hxy+by²; (b) z=(x²+xy+y²)^(−1)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "3. If u=f(y/x), show xuₓ+yuᵧ=0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "4. If z=xy f(x/y), show xzₓ+yzᵧ=2z."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "5. (a) If u=tan⁻¹((x²+y²)/(x+y)), show xuₓ+yuᵧ=sin u cos u."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "All Maple commands/results and native exercise opening", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 157, "side": "right", "reason": "Every Maple derivative output and exercise scaling exponent checked visually"}}, {"file": "158_page_1.jpg", "page": 307, "title": "PARTIAL DIFFERENTIATION", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "text": "Exercise 11.2 continued: If u=sin⁻¹((√x−√y)/(√x+√y)), show xuₓ+yuᵧ=0."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "6. Use MAPLE diff: (a) (x³+y³)/(x²+y²) with respect to y; (b) −sin²x/y+x cos y with respect to y; (c) tan x/y−x cot²y with respect to x; (d) (4t³+3s²)/(s−t) with respect to s."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Review Exercise 11"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "1. Choose the correct option."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "i. In z=f(x,y), x and y are: (a) alphanumeric variables; (b) dependent variables; (c) independent variables; (d) dependent constants."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "ii. If f(x,y)=√(x²+y²−1), find f(1,5): (a) 25; (b) 5; (c) 125; (d) 3√3."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "iii. If f(x,y)=x²+y², then fₓ is: (a) 2y; (b) 2x; (c) 2x+2y; (d) 2x+y²."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "iv. diff(1/(x+y),y); is: (a) 1/x²; (b) −1/y²; (c) −1/(x+y)²; (d) 1/(x+y)²."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "v. Euler’s theorem states: (a) zₓ+zᵧ=nz; (b) xzₓ+zᵧ=nz; (c) xzₓ+yzᵧ=nz; (d) −xzₓ+zᵧ=nz."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "vi. Functions of more than one independent variable are called: (a) monomial functions; (b) polynomial functions; (c) multivariate function; (d) none of these."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "vii. If f(x,y,z)=√(x³+y³−z), find f(1,0,1): (a) 1; (b) −1; (c) 0; (d) 2."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "viii. If f(x,y)=cos y·e^(3x), then fₓ is: (a) 3cos y·e^(3x)−sin y; (b) 3cos y·e^(3x); (c) 3e^(3x)cos y; (d) 8cos y·e^(3x) [source label (l)]."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "ix. If f(x,y)=3xe^y+x³y², then fᵧ is: (a) 3xe^y+2x³y; (b) 3x^y e+2y3x² [source label (l)]; (c) 3xe^y−2x³y; (d) 3x²y²+3e^y."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Review (viii) has two equivalent correct choices (b),(c). The last choice in (viii) and second choice in (ix) are labelled (l) in the source; those label anomalies are retained alongside a standard four-choice transcription."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Full exercise continuation, nine review items with all 36 choices, complete summary and source portrait", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 158, "side": "left", "reason": "Enlarged source checked for leading minus, choice labels, repeated correct expressions and history series"}}, {"file": "158_page_2.jpg", "page": 308, "title": "PARTIAL DIFFERENTIATION", "paragraphs": [], "blocks": [{"type": "heading", "text": "Summary"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A function z=f(x,y) is a function of two variables x and y if a unique z is obtained from each ordered pair (x,y). The real numbers x and y are independent variables; z is dependent. The domain is the set of ordered pairs for which f(x,y) is real, and the range is the set of all values of f(x,y)."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A polynomial function in x and y is the sum of functions of the form:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f(x,y)=Cx^my^n\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "If z=f(x,y) is a function of two variables, its first partial derivatives with respect to x and y are defined by:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f_x(x,y)=\\lim_{\\Delta x\\to0}\\frac{f(x+\\Delta x,y)-f(x,y)}{\\Delta x},\\quad f_y(x,y)=\\lim_{\\Delta y\\to0}\\frac{f(x,y+\\Delta y)-f(x,y)}{\\Delta y}\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "A function f(x,y) is homogeneous of degree n in x and y if for all values x,y and every positive λ:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(f(\\lambda x,\\lambda y)=\\lambda^nf(x,y)\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The specialty of Euler’s theorem is to verify the degree of a homogeneous function. The homogeneous function z=f(x,y) is not altered if x,y are stretched or squeezed by any real scalar λ."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "Polynomial exponents m,n are nonnegative integers in this definition. The partial limits must exist in the appropriate domain. Homogeneous values scale by λⁿ rather than staying unchanged unless n=0; positive scaling and domain conditions matter. Euler’s identity is xfₓ+yfᵧ=nf for differentiable homogeneous functions."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "History"}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/partial308-neumann.jpg", "caption": "Carl Neumann (1832–1925)", "alt": "Source portrait of Carl Neumann."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Carl Neumann was German mathematician. He studied physics from his father and later became a mathematician. His father was professor at Konigsberg university. In 1875 be introduce the new standard notation (d-hat) during a lecture on the mechanical theory of heat. The symbol was popularized by his name as Neumann notation or δ (Greek delta). He worked on the drichlet principle and can be considered one of the initiators of the theory of integral equations. The Neumann series, that is analogous to the geometric series:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac1{1-x}=a+x+x^2+x^3+\\ldots\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "is named after him. Carl Neumann also founded a mathematical research journal mathematische annalen. The Neumann boundary condition for different types of ordinary differential equations and partial differential equation is also named after him. He also developed an interest in thermodynamics via the overlap of heat and electricity."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The history wording and claims are source transcription, not independently verified history. The displayed geometric series requires the constant term 1, rather than arbitrary a, and convergence |x|<1: 1/(1−x)=1+x+x²+… ."}], "sourceDefects": [], "visualStatus": "Full exercise continuation, nine review items with all 36 choices, complete summary and source portrait", "alternateVerification": {"pdf": "Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf", "pdfPage": 158, "side": "right", "reason": "Enlarged source checked for leading minus, choice labels, repeated correct expressions and history series"}}];MATH_12_DATA[10].sourceObjectives=[["11.1 Differentiation of function of two variables", ["Define a function of two variables.", "Define partial derivative.", "Find partial derivatives of a function of two variables."]], ["11.2 Euler’s Theorem", ["Define a homogeneous function of degree n.", "State and prove Euler’s theorem on homogeneous functions.", "Verify Euler’s theorem for homogeneous functions of different degrees (simple cases).", "Use MAPLE command diff to find partial derivatives."]]];MATH_12_DATA[10].sourceIntroductionBlocks=[{"type": "heading", "text": "Introduction"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "The goal of this unit is to extend the methods of single variable differential calculus to functions of two variables. In many practical situations, the value of one quantity may depend on the values of two or more others. For example, the amount of water in a reservoir may depend on the amount of rainfall and on the amount of water consumed by local residents. The current in an electrical circuit may vary with the electromotive force, the capacitance, the resistance, and the impedance in the circuit. The flow of blood from an artery into a small capillary may depend on the diameter of the capillary and the pressure in both the artery and the capillary. The output of a factory may depend on the amount of capital invested in the plant and on the size of the labor force. We will analyse such situations using functions of several variables."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "In many problems involving functions of several variables, the goal is to find the derivative of the function with respect to one of its variables when all the others are held constant. In this unit, we need to develop the concept and shall see how it can be used to find slopes and rates of change in case of two variables function."}];
MATH_12_DATA[10].sections.unshift({id:'sec-11-introduction',title:'Introduction to Partial Differentiation',sourceBlocks:MATH_12_DATA[10].sourceIntroductionBlocks,theory:MATH_12_DATA[10].sourceIntroductionBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n'),sourceReferences:[{page:300,jpg:'154_page_2.jpg'}]});const math12PartialFunctions=MATH_12_DATA[10].sections.find(s=>s.id==='sec-11-1');math12PartialFunctions.unreviewedTheory=math12PartialFunctions.theory;math12PartialFunctions.sourceBlocks=MATH_12_DATA[10].sourcePages[0].blocks.slice(MATH_12_DATA[10].sourceIntroductionBlocks.length).concat(MATH_12_DATA[10].sourcePages.filter(p=>p.page>=301&&p.page<=302).flatMap(p=>p.blocks));math12PartialFunctions.theory=math12PartialFunctions.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');math12PartialFunctions.sourceReferences=MATH_12_DATA[10].sourcePages.map(p=>({page:p.page,jpg:p.file}));
const math12PartialReviewedExamples=[{"num": "1", "title": "Function of Two Variables", "question": "Show f(x,y)=√(1−x+y) is a function; find its domain and range.", "steps": ["Use the principal real square root, which assigns exactly one nonnegative output whenever its radicand is nonnegative.", "The input restriction is 1−x+y≥0, equivalently y≥x−1.", "Thus the maximal real domain is the half-plane D={(x,y)∈ℝ²:y≥x−1}, including its boundary.", "For the source illustration (2,1), f(2,1)=√0=0. The formula’s single-valued square root proves the function property for every valid input.", "Every output is nonnegative, so the range is contained in [0,∞).", "Conversely, for any z≥0 choose x=0,y=z²−1. This pair lies in D and f(0,z²−1)=√(z²)=z.", "Therefore every nonnegative value occurs and the range is exactly [0,∞)."], "answer": "Domain y≥x−1; range [0,∞).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 301, "jpg": "155_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Source square-root sign, sample input and inequality checked"}]}, {"num": "2", "title": "Partial Product and Chain Rules", "question": "Find fₓ and fᵧ for f(x,y)=x³y+xy².", "steps": ["To find fₓ, keep y constant while differentiating x.", "The derivative of x³y with respect to x is 3x²y.", "The derivative of xy² with respect to x is y², hence fₓ=3x²y+y².", "To find fᵧ, keep x constant while differentiating y.", "The derivative of x³y is x³ and that of xy² is 2xy, giving fᵧ=x³+2xy.", "These polynomials are differentiable at every real pair (x,y). Applying the rules to each term verifies both derivatives."], "answer": "fₓ=3x²y+y²; fᵧ=x³+2xy.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 302, "jpg": "155_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source derivative factors and point values checked"}]}, {"num": "3", "title": "Partial Product and Chain Rules", "question": "For z=x²sin(3x+y³), find zₓ,zᵧ at (π/3,0).", "steps": ["Keep y fixed for zₓ, and apply the product rule to x² and sin(3x+y³).", "Differentiate the inner expression with respect to x: ∂(3x+y³)/∂x=3.", "Thus zₓ=2x sin(3x+y³)+3x² cos(3x+y³).", "Keep x fixed for zᵧ; the inner y derivative is 3y². Hence zᵧ=3x²y²cos(3x+y³).", "At (π/3,0), the angle is π, so sinπ=0 and cosπ=−1.", "Therefore zₓ=−3(π²/9)=−π²/3 and zᵧ=0 because of the factor y².", "The source evaluation uses radians, as required by the standard trigonometric derivative formulas."], "answer": "zₓ(π/3,0)=−π²/3; zᵧ(π/3,0)=0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 302, "jpg": "155_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source derivative factors and point values checked"}]}, {"num": "4", "title": "Temperature Partial Derivatives", "question": "For T(x,y)=2x+5y+xy−40, find and interpret Tₓ(9,5), Tᵧ(9,5), with y measured in hundreds of megawatts.", "steps": ["For Tₓ hold y constant: derivative of 2x is 2 and of xy is y, so Tₓ=2+y.", "At (9,5), Tₓ=7. A 1°C increase in incoming-water temperature produces a 7°C modeled output change with power fixed at 500 MW.", "For Tᵧ hold x constant: derivative of 5y is 5 and of xy is x, so Tᵧ=5+x.", "At (9,5), Tᵧ=14°C per one y-unit, which is 100 MW.", "Thus the change per 1 MW is 14/100=0.14°C at fixed incoming temperature 9°C. The source one-megawatt wording misses this scale factor.", "For reference T(9,5)=18+25+45−40=48°C; it is a temperature value rather than a marginal change.", "Because T is affine in either input when the other is fixed, these increments are exact within this stipulated model."], "answer": "Tₓ(9,5)=7°C/°C; Tᵧ(9,5)=14°C per 100 MW = 0.14°C/MW.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 302, "jpg": "155_page_2.jpg"}, {"page": 303, "jpg": "156_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Both model and source interpretation checked; scale error explained separately"}]}, {"num": "5", "title": "Homogeneity and Euler’s Theorem", "question": "Show f(x,y)=2xy+y² is homogeneous of degree 2.", "steps": ["Scale both inputs by λ: f(λx,λy)=2(λx)(λy)+(λy)².", "Both terms gain exactly two factors of λ.", "Thus f(λx,λy)=λ²(2xy+y²)=λ²f(x,y).", "This is the defining identity with degree n=2.", "It holds for every real λ for this polynomial, although positive scaling is sufficient for the usual real-domain definition.", "As a check, fₓ=2y, fᵧ=2x+2y and xfₓ+yfᵧ=4xy+2y²=2f."], "answer": "Homogeneous of degree 2.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 304, "jpg": "156_page_2.jpg", "verification": "Full source homogeneity and Euler identity checked"}]}, {"num": "6", "title": "Homogeneity and Euler’s Theorem", "question": "Verify Euler’s theorem for z=ax²+2bxy+cy².", "steps": ["Treat a,b,c as constants. Scaling x,y by λ multiplies every degree-two term by λ².", "Differentiate with y fixed: zₓ=2ax+2by.", "Differentiate with x fixed: zᵧ=2bx+2cy.", "Multiply: xzₓ=2ax²+2bxy and yzᵧ=2bxy+2cy².", "Add: xzₓ+yzᵧ=2ax²+4bxy+2cy²=2z.", "Hence Euler’s identity for degree 2 is verified. If all coefficients vanish, z=0 and the identity holds for every degree; degree 2 is still a valid scaling identity."], "answer": "xzₓ+yzᵧ=2z.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 305, "jpg": "157_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Full source homogeneity and Euler identity checked"}]}, {"num": "7", "title": "Homogeneity and Euler’s Theorem", "question": "For u=arctan((x³+y³)/(x−y)), show xuₓ+yuᵧ=sin2u.", "steps": ["Require x≠y and put z=(x³+y³)/(x−y)=tan u.", "Scaling x,y by λ multiplies the numerator by λ³ and denominator by λ, so z has degree 2.", "Euler’s theorem gives xzₓ+yzᵧ=2z.", "Differentiate z=tan u: zₓ=sec²u·uₓ and zᵧ=sec²u·uᵧ.", "Substitute: sec²u(xuₓ+yuᵧ)=2tan u.", "Multiply by cos²u: xuₓ+yuᵧ=2sin u cos u=sin2u.", "For a direct chain-rule check, uₓ=zₓ/(1+z²), uᵧ=zᵧ/(1+z²), giving 2z/(1+z²)=sin2u."], "answer": "xuₓ+yuᵧ=sin2u, x≠y.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 305, "jpg": "157_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Full source homogeneity and Euler identity checked"}]}, {"num": "8", "title": "MAPLE Partial Derivatives", "question": "Find partials using source Maple diff commands: (a) x³+y³+3xy²+4x²y; (b) y sin x+x cos y+x².", "steps": ["For (a), diff(x^3+y^3+3*x*y^2+4*x^2*y,x); differentiates while y stays constant.", "Term derivatives give fₓ=3x²+0+3y²+8xy.", "The y command gives fᵧ=0+3y²+6xy+4x².", "For (b), diff(y*sin(x)+x*cos(y)+x^2,x); gives fₓ=y cos x+cos y+2x.", "The y command gives fᵧ=sin x−x sin y+0.", "The source palette forms yield the same partials for (a). These outputs are checked by differentiation, with trigonometric inputs in radians, rather than represented as newly executed Maple results."], "answer": "(a) fₓ=3x²+3y²+8xy, fᵧ=3y²+6xy+4x²; (b) fₓ=y cos x+cos y+2x, fᵧ=sin x−x sin y.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 306, "jpg": "157_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All four exact source command outputs checked"}]}];
math12PartialReviewedExamples.forEach(e=>{e.problem=e.question;e.given=e.question;e.method='Identify the independent variables, apply the definition, and verify the result.';e.title='Example '+e.num+' — '+e.title;const old=MATH_12_DATA[10].workedExamples.find(x=>x.num===e.num);if(old){old.unreviewedProblem=old.problem;Object.assign(old,e);}else MATH_12_DATA[10].workedExamples.push(e);});
math12PartialFunctions.sourceBlocks.push(...[{"type": "paragraph", "text": "At x=9,y=5, Tₓ(9,5)=2+y=2+5=7, the approximate change in temperature resulting from a one degree increase in input water temperature, if input electricity y remains constant at 500 megawatts."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "(b) The partial derivative w.r.t. y is Tᵧ=5+x, with x constant."}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(T_y(9,5)=5+x=5+9=14\\)", "number": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "This is the approximate change in temperature resulting from a one megawatt increase in production of electricity if the input water temperature x remains constant at 9°C."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The source defines y in hundreds of megawatts. Consequently Tᵧ=14°C per 100 MW, equivalently 0.14°C per MW, not 14°C per MW as the interpretation says. Tₓ=7°C of output per 1°C of input at fixed 500 MW. Also T(9,5)=48°C; the derivatives describe changes, not this temperature value."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "History"}, {"type": "figure", "src": "assets/math12/partial303-euler.jpg", "caption": "Leonhard Euler (1707–1783)", "alt": "Source portrait of Leonhard Euler."}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Leonhard Euler was a Swiss Mathematician, Physicist, Astronomer and Engineer. He made the important and influential contributions in many branches of Mathematics, such as calculus, graph theory, topology and analytic number theory. He also made significant contribution in mechanics, fluid dynamics, optics and music theory. He was the first person who introduced f(x) to denoted the function f applied to the argument x. In 1735 he introduced a theorem known by his name Euler theorem."}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "This historical passage is retained as printed textbook text; its dates and priority claims are not independently verified here."}]);
math12PartialFunctions.theory=math12PartialFunctions.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');
const math12ReviewedExercise111=[{"id": "ex-11-1-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "For f(x,y)=x²y+xy² and real t, find (a) f(0,0); (b) f(−1,0); (c) f(0,−1); (d) f(t,t); (e) f(t,t²); (f) f(1−t,t).", "steps": ["Use f(x,y)=xy(x+y), substituting the ordered pair without exchanging its coordinates.", "(a) f(0,0)=0; (b) f(−1,0)=0 because y=0.", "(c) f(0,−1)=0 because x=0.", "(d) f(t,t)=t²t+t·t²=2t³.", "(e) f(t,t²)=t²·t²+t·(t²)²=t⁴+t⁵.", "(f) f(1−t,t)=(1−t)t[(1−t)+t]=t−t². Each expression is valid for every real t."], "solution": "Use f(x,y)=xy(x+y), substituting the ordered pair without exchanging its coordinates.\n(a) f(0,0)=0; (b) f(−1,0)=0 because y=0.\n(c) f(0,−1)=0 because x=0.\n(d) f(t,t)=t²t+t·t²=2t³.\n(e) f(t,t²)=t²·t²+t·(t²)²=t⁴+t⁵.\n(f) f(1−t,t)=(1−t)t[(1−t)+t]=t−t². Each expression is valid for every real t.", "answer": "(a) 0; (b) 0; (c) 0; (d) 2t³; (e) t⁴+t⁵; (f) t−t².", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 303, "jpg": "156_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions checked; evaluation/composition ambiguity separately explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-1-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "For f(x,y,z)=x²y e^(2x)+(x+y−z)², find (a) f(0,0,0); (b) f(1,−1,1); (c) f(−1,1,−1); (d) ∂/∂x f(x,x,x); (e) ∂/∂y f(1,y,1); (f) ∂/∂z f(1,1,z²).", "steps": ["The readable source function is x²y e^(2x)+(x+y−z)². (a) f(0,0,0)=0.", "(b) f(1,−1,1)=−e²+(−1)²=1−e². (c) f(−1,1,−1)=e^(−2)+1.", "Before substituting repeated coordinates, the coordinate partials are fₓ=2xy(1+x)e^(2x)+2(x+y−z), fᵧ=x²e^(2x)+2(x+y−z), and f_z=−2(x+y−z).", "(d) If the intended task is fₓ evaluated at (x,x,x), the answer is 2x²(1+x)e^(2x)+2x. The printed notation is ambiguous because x also parameterizes all three inputs.", "If instead one first forms the one-variable function f(x,x,x)=x³e^(2x)+x², its ordinary derivative is (3x²+2x³)e^(2x)+2x. This includes changes in all three coordinates.", "(e) Both interpretations agree here: d/dy f(1,y,1)=e²+2y.", "(f) If the task means f_z evaluated at (1,1,z²), it is −2(2−z²)=2z²−4. If it means differentiate the composite f(1,1,z²), the chain rule gives −4z(2−z²).", "The source uses partial symbols but does not distinguish evaluation from composition; both readings are given explicitly instead of silently changing the notation."], "solution": "The readable source function is x²y e^(2x)+(x+y−z)². (a) f(0,0,0)=0.\n(b) f(1,−1,1)=−e²+(−1)²=1−e². (c) f(−1,1,−1)=e^(−2)+1.\nBefore substituting repeated coordinates, the coordinate partials are fₓ=2xy(1+x)e^(2x)+2(x+y−z), fᵧ=x²e^(2x)+2(x+y−z), and f_z=−2(x+y−z).\n(d) If the intended task is fₓ evaluated at (x,x,x), the answer is 2x²(1+x)e^(2x)+2x. The printed notation is ambiguous because x also parameterizes all three inputs.\nIf instead one first forms the one-variable function f(x,x,x)=x³e^(2x)+x², its ordinary derivative is (3x²+2x³)e^(2x)+2x. This includes changes in all three coordinates.\n(e) Both interpretations agree here: d/dy f(1,y,1)=e²+2y.\n(f) If the task means f_z evaluated at (1,1,z²), it is −2(2−z²)=2z²−4. If it means differentiate the composite f(1,1,z²), the chain rule gives −4z(2−z²).\nThe source uses partial symbols but does not distinguish evaluation from composition; both readings are given explicitly instead of silently changing the notation.", "answer": "(a) 0; (b) 1−e²; (c) 1+e^(−2); (d),(f) have distinct evaluated-partial and composite-derivative readings shown above; (e) e²+2y.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 303, "jpg": "156_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions checked; evaluation/composition ambiguity separately explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-1-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "Find fₓ,fᵧ: (a) sin(x²)cos y; (b) √(3x²+y⁴); (c) xy³tan⁻¹y; (d) x³+x²y+xy²+y³; (e) sin⁻¹(xy); (f) x²e^(x+y)cos y.", "steps": ["For each partial derivative hold the other independent variable fixed.", "(a) fₓ=2x cos(x²)cos y; fᵧ=−sin(x²)sin y.", "(b) At points other than the origin, fₓ=3x/√(3x²+y⁴), fᵧ=2y³/√(3x²+y⁴). At (0,0), the x slice is √3|x|, so fₓ does not exist; the y slice is y², so fᵧ=0.", "(c) fₓ=y³arctan y; fᵧ=x[3y²arctan y+y³/(1+y²)].", "(d) fₓ=3x²+2xy+y²; fᵧ=x²+2xy+3y².", "(e) For |xy|<1, fₓ=y/√(1−x²y²), fᵧ=x/√(1−x²y²). The real function domain is |xy|≤1, but the displayed derivatives are interior formulas.", "(f) fₓ=(2x+x²)e^(x+y)cos y; fᵧ=x²e^(x+y)(cos y−sin y). The exponential and cosine both change with y."], "solution": "For each partial derivative hold the other independent variable fixed.\n(a) fₓ=2x cos(x²)cos y; fᵧ=−sin(x²)sin y.\n(b) At points other than the origin, fₓ=3x/√(3x²+y⁴), fᵧ=2y³/√(3x²+y⁴). At (0,0), the x slice is √3|x|, so fₓ does not exist; the y slice is y², so fᵧ=0.\n(c) fₓ=y³arctan y; fᵧ=x[3y²arctan y+y³/(1+y²)].\n(d) fₓ=3x²+2xy+y²; fᵧ=x²+2xy+3y².\n(e) For |xy|<1, fₓ=y/√(1−x²y²), fᵧ=x/√(1−x²y²). The real function domain is |xy|≤1, but the displayed derivatives are interior formulas.\n(f) fₓ=(2x+x²)e^(x+y)cos y; fᵧ=x²e^(x+y)(cos y−sin y). The exponential and cosine both change with y.", "answer": "All six partial-derivative pairs are given above, including the origin exception in (b) and arcsine domain in (e).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 303, "jpg": "156_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions checked; evaluation/composition ambiguity separately explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-1-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "The United States production function was estimated as z=x^0.7 y^0.3, where x is labor and y capital. Find marginal productivity of labor ∂z/∂x and capital ∂z/∂y.", "steps": ["Use the positive-input production domain x>0,y>0 for these marginal derivatives.", "Hold y constant to differentiate with respect to labor x.", "By the power rule, zₓ=0.7x^(−0.3)y^0.3.", "Hold x constant to differentiate with respect to capital y.", "Then zᵧ=0.3x^0.7 y^(−0.7).", "These measure marginal production per extra labor/capital unit in the stipulated model; derivatives at zero-input boundaries need separate analysis. Check xzₓ+yzᵧ=(0.7+0.3)z=z."], "solution": "Use the positive-input production domain x>0,y>0 for these marginal derivatives.\nHold y constant to differentiate with respect to labor x.\nBy the power rule, zₓ=0.7x^(−0.3)y^0.3.\nHold x constant to differentiate with respect to capital y.\nThen zᵧ=0.3x^0.7 y^(−0.7).\nThese measure marginal production per extra labor/capital unit in the stipulated model; derivatives at zero-input boundaries need separate analysis. Check xzₓ+yzᵧ=(0.7+0.3)z=z.", "answer": "zₓ=0.7x^(−0.3)y^0.3; zᵧ=0.3x^0.7 y^(−0.7), x,y>0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 303, "jpg": "156_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions checked; evaluation/composition ambiguity separately explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-1-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "The Canadian production function is z=x^0.4 y^0.6, where x is labor and y capital. Find marginal productivity of labor ∂z/∂x and capital ∂z/∂y.", "steps": ["Use the positive-input production domain x>0,y>0.", "For the labor derivative, treat y^0.6 as a constant factor.", "Thus zₓ=0.4x^(−0.6)y^0.6.", "For the capital derivative, treat x^0.4 as constant.", "Thus zᵧ=0.6x^0.4 y^(−0.4).", "The exponents sum to 1, so xzₓ+yzᵧ=(0.4+0.6)z=z, independently checking both coefficients."], "solution": "Use the positive-input production domain x>0,y>0.\nFor the labor derivative, treat y^0.6 as a constant factor.\nThus zₓ=0.4x^(−0.6)y^0.6.\nFor the capital derivative, treat x^0.4 as constant.\nThus zᵧ=0.6x^0.4 y^(−0.4).\nThe exponents sum to 1, so xzₓ+yzᵧ=(0.4+0.6)z=z, independently checking both coefficients.", "answer": "zₓ=0.4x^(−0.6)y^0.6; zᵧ=0.6x^0.4 y^(−0.4), x,y>0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 303, "jpg": "156_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions checked; evaluation/composition ambiguity separately explained"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-1-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "For f(x,y)=x²y+xy², find fₓ,fᵧ using the definition of partial derivatives.", "steps": ["For fₓ hold y fixed and use [f(x+h,y)−f(x,y)]/h, h≠0.", "Expand the numerator: [(x+h)²−x²]y+hy²=(2xh+h²)y+hy².", "Divide by h: 2xy+hy+y². As h→0, fₓ=2xy+y².", "For fᵧ hold x fixed and use [f(x,y+k)−f(x,y)]/k, k≠0.", "Expand: x²k+x[(y+k)²−y²]=x²k+2xyk+xk².", "Divide by k: x²+2xy+xk. As k→0, fᵧ=x²+2xy.", "Both limits exist at every real pair and agree with direct polynomial differentiation."], "solution": "For fₓ hold y fixed and use [f(x+h,y)−f(x,y)]/h, h≠0.\nExpand the numerator: [(x+h)²−x²]y+hy²=(2xh+h²)y+hy².\nDivide by h: 2xy+hy+y². As h→0, fₓ=2xy+y².\nFor fᵧ hold x fixed and use [f(x,y+k)−f(x,y)]/k, k≠0.\nExpand: x²k+x[(y+k)²−y²]=x²k+2xyk+xk².\nDivide by k: x²+2xy+xk. As k→0, fᵧ=x²+2xy.\nBoth limits exist at every real pair and agree with direct polynomial differentiation.", "answer": "fₓ=2xy+y²; fᵧ=x²+2xy, from the defining limits.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 303, "jpg": "156_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All source expressions checked; evaluation/composition ambiguity separately explained"}]}];
const math12Ex111=MATH_12_DATA[10].exercises.find(e=>e.title==='Exercise 11.1');math12Ex111.unreviewedProblems=math12Ex111.problems;math12Ex111.problems=math12ReviewedExercise111;math12Ex111.pageStart=math12Ex111.pageEnd=303;
const math12EulerTheorem=MATH_12_DATA[10].sections.find(s=>s.id==='sec-11-2');math12EulerTheorem.unreviewedTheory=math12EulerTheorem.theory;math12EulerTheorem.sourceBlocks=MATH_12_DATA[10].sourcePages.filter(p=>p.page>=304&&p.page<=305).flatMap(p=>p.blocks);math12EulerTheorem.theory=math12EulerTheorem.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');math12EulerTheorem.sourceReferences=MATH_12_DATA[10].sourcePages.filter(p=>p.page>=304&&p.page<=305).map(p=>({page:p.page,jpg:p.file}));
math12EulerTheorem.sourceBlocks.push(...[{"type": "heading", "text": "11.2.3 MAPLE command “diff” to find partial derivatives"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Look at the following example; the use of MAPLE command diff is illustrated."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Example 8"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Use MAPLE command diff to find partial derivatives: (a) f(x,y)=x³+y³+3xy²+4x²y with respect to x,y; (b) f(x,y)=y sin x+x cos y+x² with respect to x,y."}, {"type": "heading", "text": "Solution"}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Command:"}, {"type": "command", "text": "diff(x^3+y^3+3*x*y^2+4*x^2*y,x);", "result": "3x²+3y²+8xy", "number": 1}, {"type": "command", "text": "diff(x^3+y^3+3*x*y^2+4*x^2*y,y);", "result": "3y²+6xy+4x²", "number": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Command:"}, {"type": "command", "text": "diff(y*sin(x)+x*cos(y)+x^2,x);", "result": "ycos(x)+cos(y)+2x", "number": 1}, {"type": "command", "text": "diff(y*sin(x)+x*cos(y)+x^2,y);", "result": "sin(x)−xsin(y)", "number": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "text": "Using Palettes: Use the cursor button to select the expression in which you are interested. In this problem, the expression is a partial derivative palette. Click the partial derivative palette, insert the given function, and press ENTER to obtain the partial derivatives of the function:"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^3+y^3+3xy^2+4x^2y)=3x^2+3y^2+8xy\\)", "number": "1"}, {"type": "command", "text": null, "result": "\\(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^3+y^3+3xy^2+4x^2y)=3y^2+6xy+4x^2\\)", "number": "2"}, {"type": "clarification", "text": "The commands and outputs are native transcriptions of printed source results, not commands executed in SpaceBook. They can be verified directly by holding the other variable constant and applying the polynomial/product rules."}]);
math12EulerTheorem.theory=math12EulerTheorem.sourceBlocks.map(math12BlockText).join('\n\n');math12EulerTheorem.sourceReferences.push({page:306,jpg:'157_page_2.jpg'});
const math12ReviewedExercise112=[{"id": "ex-11-2-q1", "qNo": "1", "question": "Are these homogeneous? (a) u=sin⁻¹((x+y)/(√x+√y)); (b) z=(x+y)/(√x+√y); (c) z=x³e^(y/x)−3y²√(x²+y²); (d) z=(x²+3y²)^(1/3).", "steps": ["Use positive scaling λ and retain each original real domain. A homogeneous function must gain a fixed power λⁿ for all valid scaling comparisons.", "(a) The inner ratio R=(x+y)/(√x+√y) scales as √λ R, but arcsin(√λ R) is not a fixed power multiple of arcsin R. The real arcsine domain is also not preserved by every positive scale.", "For y=0, compare scaling x→4x: at x=1/16 the ratio of function values is arcsin(1/2)/arcsin(1/4), while at x=1/4 it is arcsin(1)/arcsin(1/2)=3. These are unequal, so no fixed degree exists.", "(b) The numerator scales as λ and denominator as √λ, so z has degree 1/2 on x,y≥0 excluding the origin.", "(c) The ratio y/x is unchanged; the first term gains λ³. The second term gains λ²·λ=λ³, so z has degree 3 for x≠0.", "(d) The quadratic scales by λ², so its real cube root scales by λ^(2/3): degree 2/3. The origin is included as a function value, though derivatives there require separate attention."], "solution": "Use positive scaling λ and retain each original real domain. A homogeneous function must gain a fixed power λⁿ for all valid scaling comparisons.\n(a) The inner ratio R=(x+y)/(√x+√y) scales as √λ R, but arcsin(√λ R) is not a fixed power multiple of arcsin R. The real arcsine domain is also not preserved by every positive scale.\nFor y=0, compare scaling x→4x: at x=1/16 the ratio of function values is arcsin(1/2)/arcsin(1/4), while at x=1/4 it is arcsin(1)/arcsin(1/2)=3. These are unequal, so no fixed degree exists.\n(b) The numerator scales as λ and denominator as √λ, so z has degree 1/2 on x,y≥0 excluding the origin.\n(c) The ratio y/x is unchanged; the first term gains λ³. The second term gains λ²·λ=λ³, so z has degree 3 for x≠0.\n(d) The quadratic scales by λ², so its real cube root scales by λ^(2/3): degree 2/3. The origin is included as a function value, though derivatives there require separate attention.", "answer": "(a) not homogeneous; (b) degree 1/2; (c) degree 3; (d) degree 2/3, on the stated domains.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 306, "jpg": "157_page_2.jpg"}, {"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Full continuation and leading minus visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-2-q2", "qNo": "2", "question": "Verify Euler’s theorem: (a) z=ax²+2hxy+by²; (b) z=(x²+xy+y²)^(−1).", "steps": ["(a) Hold coefficients a,h,b constant: zₓ=2ax+2hy and zᵧ=2hx+2by.", "Multiply by x,y and add: xzₓ+yzᵧ=2ax²+4hxy+2by²=2z. This verifies degree 2.", "(b) Put Q=x²+xy+y², which is positive away from the origin. The function z=Q^(−1) has scaling degree −2.", "Differentiate: zₓ=−(2x+y)/Q² and zᵧ=−(x+2y)/Q².", "Then xzₓ+yzᵧ=−(2x²+2xy+2y²)/Q²=−2Q/Q²=−2z.", "Thus Euler’s identity holds for degree −2 on ℝ² excluding (0,0); the denominator zero must not be included."], "solution": "(a) Hold coefficients a,h,b constant: zₓ=2ax+2hy and zᵧ=2hx+2by.\nMultiply by x,y and add: xzₓ+yzᵧ=2ax²+4hxy+2by²=2z. This verifies degree 2.\n(b) Put Q=x²+xy+y², which is positive away from the origin. The function z=Q^(−1) has scaling degree −2.\nDifferentiate: zₓ=−(2x+y)/Q² and zᵧ=−(x+2y)/Q².\nThen xzₓ+yzᵧ=−(2x²+2xy+2y²)/Q²=−2Q/Q²=−2z.\nThus Euler’s identity holds for degree −2 on ℝ² excluding (0,0); the denominator zero must not be included.", "answer": "(a) xzₓ+yzᵧ=2z; (b) xzₓ+yzᵧ=−2z.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 306, "jpg": "157_page_2.jpg"}, {"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Full continuation and leading minus visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-2-q3", "qNo": "3", "question": "If u=f(y/x), show xuₓ+yuᵧ=0.", "steps": ["Assume f is differentiable at y/x and require x≠0.", "Write v=y/x and u=f(v).", "With y fixed, vₓ=−y/x², hence uₓ=−(y/x²)f′(v).", "With x fixed, vᵧ=1/x, hence uᵧ=f′(v)/x.", "Multiply and add: xuₓ+yuᵧ=−(y/x)f′(v)+(y/x)f′(v)=0.", "This also expresses degree-zero homogeneity because the ratio y/x is unchanged under positive scaling."], "solution": "Assume f is differentiable at y/x and require x≠0.\nWrite v=y/x and u=f(v).\nWith y fixed, vₓ=−y/x², hence uₓ=−(y/x²)f′(v).\nWith x fixed, vᵧ=1/x, hence uᵧ=f′(v)/x.\nMultiply and add: xuₓ+yuᵧ=−(y/x)f′(v)+(y/x)f′(v)=0.\nThis also expresses degree-zero homogeneity because the ratio y/x is unchanged under positive scaling.", "answer": "xuₓ+yuᵧ=0, x≠0 and f differentiable.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 306, "jpg": "157_page_2.jpg"}, {"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Full continuation and leading minus visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-2-q4", "qNo": "4", "question": "If z=xy f(x/y), show xzₓ+yzᵧ=2z.", "steps": ["Assume f is differentiable and y≠0. Put v=x/y, z=xyf(v).", "With y fixed, vₓ=1/y. Product and chain rules give zₓ=yf(v)+xf′(v).", "With x fixed, vᵧ=−x/y², giving zᵧ=xf(v)−(x²/y)f′(v).", "Multiply by x: xzₓ=xyf(v)+x²f′(v).", "Multiply by y: yzᵧ=xyf(v)−x²f′(v).", "The chain-rule terms cancel, leaving xzₓ+yzᵧ=2xyf(v)=2z, as required."], "solution": "Assume f is differentiable and y≠0. Put v=x/y, z=xyf(v).\nWith y fixed, vₓ=1/y. Product and chain rules give zₓ=yf(v)+xf′(v).\nWith x fixed, vᵧ=−x/y², giving zᵧ=xf(v)−(x²/y)f′(v).\nMultiply by x: xzₓ=xyf(v)+x²f′(v).\nMultiply by y: yzᵧ=xyf(v)−x²f′(v).\nThe chain-rule terms cancel, leaving xzₓ+yzᵧ=2xyf(v)=2z, as required.", "answer": "xzₓ+yzᵧ=2z, y≠0 and f differentiable.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 306, "jpg": "157_page_2.jpg"}, {"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Full continuation and leading minus visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-2-q5", "qNo": "5", "question": "(a) If u=tan⁻¹((x²+y²)/(x+y)), show xuₓ+yuᵧ=sin u cos u. (b) If u=sin⁻¹((√x−√y)/(√x+√y)), show xuₓ+yuᵧ=0.", "steps": ["(a) Put v=(x²+y²)/(x+y)=tan u, requiring x+y≠0. Numerator degree 2 and denominator degree 1 give v degree 1.", "Euler’s theorem gives xvₓ+yvᵧ=v. Chain rule vₓ=sec²u·uₓ, vᵧ=sec²u·uᵧ gives sec²u(xuₓ+yuᵧ)=tan u.", "Thus xuₓ+yuᵧ=tan u cos²u=sin u cos u.", "(b) For x,y>0 put v=(√x−√y)/(√x+√y). This is homogeneous of degree zero and satisfies |v|<1.", "Hence xvₓ+yvᵧ=0. Since u=arcsin v, uₓ=vₓ/√(1−v²) and uᵧ=vᵧ/√(1−v²).", "Therefore xuₓ+yuᵧ=0 on this differentiable interior. The origin is undefined; square-root/arcsine endpoint axes require separate derivative analysis."], "solution": "(a) Put v=(x²+y²)/(x+y)=tan u, requiring x+y≠0. Numerator degree 2 and denominator degree 1 give v degree 1.\nEuler’s theorem gives xvₓ+yvᵧ=v. Chain rule vₓ=sec²u·uₓ, vᵧ=sec²u·uᵧ gives sec²u(xuₓ+yuᵧ)=tan u.\nThus xuₓ+yuᵧ=tan u cos²u=sin u cos u.\n(b) For x,y>0 put v=(√x−√y)/(√x+√y). This is homogeneous of degree zero and satisfies |v|<1.\nHence xvₓ+yvᵧ=0. Since u=arcsin v, uₓ=vₓ/√(1−v²) and uᵧ=vᵧ/√(1−v²).\nTherefore xuₓ+yuᵧ=0 on this differentiable interior. The origin is undefined; square-root/arcsine endpoint axes require separate derivative analysis.", "answer": "(a) xuₓ+yuᵧ=sin u cos u; (b) xuₓ+yuᵧ=0 in the differentiable interior.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 306, "jpg": "157_page_2.jpg"}, {"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Full continuation and leading minus visually checked"}]}, {"id": "ex-11-2-q6", "qNo": "6", "question": "Use MAPLE diff: (a) (x³+y³)/(x²+y²) with respect to y; (b) −sin²x/y+x cos y with respect to y; (c) tan x/y−x cot²y with respect to x; (d) (4t³+3s²)/(s−t) with respect to s.", "steps": ["These are exact expected derivatives from the source commands; Maple is not executed here. Hold all non-differentiated variables constant.", "(a) Command diff((x^3+y^3)/(x^2+y^2),y); gives [3y²(x²+y²)−2y(x³+y³)]/(x²+y²)², excluding the origin.", "(b) The source leading minus is confirmed by enlargement. Command diff(-sin(x)^2/y+x*cos(y),y); gives sin²x/y²−x sin y, y≠0.", "(c) Command diff(tan(x)/y-x*cot(y)^2,x); gives sec²x/y−cot²y. Keep y≠0, sin y≠0 and cos x≠0.", "(d) Command diff((4*t^3+3*s^2)/(s-t),s); gives [6s(s−t)−(4t³+3s²)]/(s−t)².", "Simplify (d) to (3s²−6st−4t³)/(s−t)², s≠t. Quotient/product/chain rules independently verify all four outputs."], "solution": "These are exact expected derivatives from the source commands; Maple is not executed here. Hold all non-differentiated variables constant.\n(a) Command diff((x^3+y^3)/(x^2+y^2),y); gives [3y²(x²+y²)−2y(x³+y³)]/(x²+y²)², excluding the origin.\n(b) The source leading minus is confirmed by enlargement. Command diff(-sin(x)^2/y+x*cos(y),y); gives sin²x/y²−x sin y, y≠0.\n(c) Command diff(tan(x)/y-x*cot(y)^2,x); gives sec²x/y−cot²y. Keep y≠0, sin y≠0 and cos x≠0.\n(d) Command diff((4*t^3+3*s^2)/(s-t),s); gives [6s(s−t)−(4t³+3s²)]/(s−t)².\nSimplify (d) to (3s²−6st−4t³)/(s−t)², s≠t. Quotient/product/chain rules independently verify all four outputs.", "answer": "(a) [3y²(x²+y²)−2y(x³+y³)]/(x²+y²)²; (b) sin²x/y²−x sin y; (c) sec²x/y−cot²y; (d) (3s²−6st−4t³)/(s−t)².", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 306, "jpg": "157_page_2.jpg"}, {"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "Full continuation and leading minus visually checked"}]}];
