const math12ReviewedReview11=[{"id": "review-11-i", "qNo": "i", "question": "In z=f(x,y), x and y are: (a) alphanumeric variables; (b) dependent variables; (c) independent variables; (d) dependent constants.", "steps": ["The function receives the ordered input pair (x,y).", "The two coordinates can vary independently in its domain.", "The output z is the dependent variable.", "Their role does not depend on letter naming.", "Therefore x and y are independent variables.", "Select (c)."], "solution": "The function receives the ordered input pair (x,y).\nThe two coordinates can vary independently in its domain.\nThe output z is the dependent variable.\nTheir role does not depend on letter naming.\nTherefore x and y are independent variables.\nSelect (c).", "answer": "(c) independent variables.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All choices and label defects visually checked"}]}, {"id": "review-11-ii", "qNo": "ii", "question": "If f(x,y)=√(x²+y²−1), find f(1,5): (a) 25; (b) 5; (c) 125; (d) 3√3.", "steps": ["Substitute the input pair x=1,y=5.", "The radicand is 1²+5²−1.", "This is 1+25−1=25.", "The principal square root √25=5.", "The radicand is nonnegative, so the real output is defined.", "Select (b)."], "solution": "Substitute the input pair x=1,y=5.\nThe radicand is 1²+5²−1.\nThis is 1+25−1=25.\nThe principal square root √25=5.\nThe radicand is nonnegative, so the real output is defined.\nSelect (b).", "answer": "(b) 5.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All choices and label defects visually checked"}]}, {"id": "review-11-iii", "qNo": "iii", "question": "If f(x,y)=x²+y², then fₓ is: (a) 2y; (b) 2x; (c) 2x+2y; (d) 2x+y².", "steps": ["For fₓ hold y constant.", "The derivative of x² is 2x.", "The derivative of the constant y² is zero.", "Hence fₓ=2x.", "The other choices either differentiate y or retain a constant term incorrectly.", "Select (b)."], "solution": "For fₓ hold y constant.\nThe derivative of x² is 2x.\nThe derivative of the constant y² is zero.\nHence fₓ=2x.\nThe other choices either differentiate y or retain a constant term incorrectly.\nSelect (b).", "answer": "(b) 2x.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All choices and label defects visually checked"}]}, {"id": "review-11-iv", "qNo": "iv", "question": "diff(1/(x+y),y); is: (a) 1/x²; (b) −1/y²; (c) −1/(x+y)²; (d) 1/(x+y)².", "steps": ["Keep x fixed and write the function as (x+y)^(−1).", "Differentiate the outer power: −(x+y)^(−2).", "The inner y derivative of x+y is 1.", "Thus the derivative is −1/(x+y)².", "Retain x+y≠0 from the function domain.", "Select (c). This is an expected Maple derivative, independently checked by the chain rule."], "solution": "Keep x fixed and write the function as (x+y)^(−1).\nDifferentiate the outer power: −(x+y)^(−2).\nThe inner y derivative of x+y is 1.\nThus the derivative is −1/(x+y)².\nRetain x+y≠0 from the function domain.\nSelect (c). This is an expected Maple derivative, independently checked by the chain rule.", "answer": "(c) −1/(x+y)².", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All choices and label defects visually checked"}]}, {"id": "review-11-v", "qNo": "v", "question": "Euler’s theorem states: (a) zₓ+zᵧ=nz; (b) xzₓ+zᵧ=nz; (c) xzₓ+yzᵧ=nz; (d) −xzₓ+zᵧ=nz.", "steps": ["For a differentiable degree-n homogeneous z, scaling gives z(λx,λy)=λⁿz(x,y).", "Differentiate this identity with respect to λ.", "The left side is xzₓ(λx,λy)+yzᵧ(λx,λy).", "Set λ=1 to obtain xzₓ+yzᵧ=nz.", "Both coordinate multipliers are required.", "Select (c), on the suitable scaling/differentiability domain."], "solution": "For a differentiable degree-n homogeneous z, scaling gives z(λx,λy)=λⁿz(x,y).\nDifferentiate this identity with respect to λ.\nThe left side is xzₓ(λx,λy)+yzᵧ(λx,λy).\nSet λ=1 to obtain xzₓ+yzᵧ=nz.\nBoth coordinate multipliers are required.\nSelect (c), on the suitable scaling/differentiability domain.", "answer": "(c) xzₓ+yzᵧ=nz.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All choices and label defects visually checked"}]}, {"id": "review-11-vi", "qNo": "vi", "question": "Functions of more than one independent variable are called: (a) monomial functions; (b) polynomial functions; (c) multivariate function; (d) none of these.", "steps": ["The term multivariate refers to several independent inputs.", "Monomial and polynomial describe particular algebraic forms.", "A multivariate function need not be polynomial.", "For instance a two-variable square root can be multivariate.", "The printed choice (c) gives this term.", "Select (c)."], "solution": "The term multivariate refers to several independent inputs.\nMonomial and polynomial describe particular algebraic forms.\nA multivariate function need not be polynomial.\nFor instance a two-variable square root can be multivariate.\nThe printed choice (c) gives this term.\nSelect (c).", "answer": "(c) multivariate function.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All choices and label defects visually checked"}]}, {"id": "review-11-vii", "qNo": "vii", "question": "If f(x,y,z)=√(x³+y³−z), find f(1,0,1): (a) 1; (b) −1; (c) 0; (d) 2.", "steps": ["Substitute x=1,y=0,z=1.", "The radicand is 1³+0³−1.", "It equals zero.", "The principal square root of zero is zero.", "The point lies on the real function-domain boundary, but the function value itself is defined.", "Select (c)."], "solution": "Substitute x=1,y=0,z=1.\nThe radicand is 1³+0³−1.\nIt equals zero.\nThe principal square root of zero is zero.\nThe point lies on the real function-domain boundary, but the function value itself is defined.\nSelect (c).", "answer": "(c) 0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All choices and label defects visually checked"}]}, {"id": "review-11-viii", "qNo": "viii", "question": "If f(x,y)=cos y·e^(3x), then fₓ is: (a) 3cos y·e^(3x)−sin y; (b) 3cos y·e^(3x); (c) 3e^(3x)cos y; (d) 8cos y·e^(3x) [source label (l)].", "steps": ["For the x partial, cos y is constant.", "Differentiate e^(3x) using the chain rule: 3e^(3x).", "Hence fₓ=3cos y·e^(3x).", "Choices (b) and (c) are algebraically identical, differing only in multiplication order.", "Both choices are correct; a unique-answer choice cannot be certified.", "The source labels the last option (l); that label is retained in the transcription."], "solution": "For the x partial, cos y is constant.\nDifferentiate e^(3x) using the chain rule: 3e^(3x).\nHence fₓ=3cos y·e^(3x).\nChoices (b) and (c) are algebraically identical, differing only in multiplication order.\nBoth choices are correct; a unique-answer choice cannot be certified.\nThe source labels the last option (l); that label is retained in the transcription.", "answer": "Both (b) and (c): 3cos y·e^(3x).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All choices and label defects visually checked"}]}, {"id": "review-11-ix", "qNo": "ix", "question": "If f(x,y)=3xe^y+x³y², then fᵧ is: (a) 3xe^y+2x³y; (b) 3x^y e+2y3x² [source label (l)]; (c) 3xe^y−2x³y; (d) 3x²y²+3e^y.", "steps": ["For fᵧ hold x constant.", "The derivative of 3xe^y is 3xe^y.", "The derivative of x³y² is 2x³y.", "Add to obtain fᵧ=3xe^y+2x³y.", "Select (a). The malformed second printed expression is retained as printed rather than silently repaired.", "The fourth option resembles a different derivative and is not the y partial."], "solution": "For fᵧ hold x constant.\nThe derivative of 3xe^y is 3xe^y.\nThe derivative of x³y² is 2x³y.\nAdd to obtain fᵧ=3xe^y+2x³y.\nSelect (a). The malformed second printed expression is retained as printed rather than silently repaired.\nThe fourth option resembles a different derivative and is not the y partial.", "answer": "(a) 3xe^y+2x³y.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 307, "jpg": "158_page_1.jpg", "verification": "All choices and label defects visually checked"}]}];
