const math12ReviewedReview12=[{"id": "review-12-i", "qNo": "i", "question": "Roots of 6x²+5x−6=0 are: (a) {2/3,−2/3}; (b) {2/3,−3/2}; (c) {3/2,−2/3}; (d) {−3/2,−2/3}.", "steps": ["Use the quadratic discriminant: 5²−4·6·(−6)=169.", "Its square root is 13.", "Thus x=(−5±13)/12.", "The two values are 8/12=2/3 and −18/12=−3/2.", "Both satisfy the polynomial; equivalently (3x−2)(2x+3)=0.", "Select (b)."], "solution": "Use the quadratic discriminant: 5²−4·6·(−6)=169.\nIts square root is 13.\nThus x=(−5±13)/12.\nThe two values are 8/12=2/3 and −18/12=−3/2.\nBoth satisfy the polynomial; equivalently (3x−2)(2x+3)=0.\nSelect (b).", "answer": "(b) {2/3,−3/2}.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}, {"id": "review-12-ii", "qNo": "ii", "question": "A root of x²+6x−5=0 is: (a) x≈−3.7187…; (b) x≈0.37187…; (c) x≈−0.37187…; (d) x=4.7847….", "steps": ["Complete the square: (x+3)²=14.", "Hence exact roots are x=−3±√14.", "Their values are approximately 0.7416573868 and −6.7416573868.", "Compare each printed option against these two values.", "None matches either root; the positive option 0.37187 is not the positive root.", "There is no correct printed choice for the stated polynomial."], "solution": "Complete the square: (x+3)²=14.\nHence exact roots are x=−3±√14.\nTheir values are approximately 0.7416573868 and −6.7416573868.\nCompare each printed option against these two values.\nNone matches either root; the positive option 0.37187 is not the positive root.\nThere is no correct printed choice for the stated polynomial.", "answer": "No correct option; roots −3±√14.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}, {"id": "review-12-iii", "qNo": "iii", "question": "Newton-Raphson is also known as: (a) tangent method; (b) second method; (c) diameter method; (d) chord method.", "steps": ["Newton obtains the next iterate from the x-axis intersection of a tangent.", "The tangent at xᵢ has slope f′(xᵢ).", "Its zero gives xᵢ₊₁=xᵢ−f(xᵢ)/f′(xᵢ).", "Thus tangent method names its construction.", "Regula-falsi uses a chord through two sampled endpoints instead.", "Select (a)."], "solution": "Newton obtains the next iterate from the x-axis intersection of a tangent.\nThe tangent at xᵢ has slope f′(xᵢ).\nIts zero gives xᵢ₊₁=xᵢ−f(xᵢ)/f′(xᵢ).\nThus tangent method names its construction.\nRegula-falsi uses a chord through two sampled endpoints instead.\nSelect (a).", "answer": "(a) tangent method.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}, {"id": "review-12-iv", "qNo": "iv", "question": "The Newton formula for x₃ is: (a) x₁−f(x₂)/f′(x₂); (b) x₂−f(x₃)/f′(x₃); (c) x₂−f(x₂)/f′(x₂); (d) x₀+f(x₂)/f′(x₂).", "steps": ["Use the recurrence xᵢ₊₁=xᵢ−f(xᵢ)/f′(xᵢ).", "Set i=2 for the third iterate.", "Then x₃=x₂−f(x₂)/f′(x₂).", "All terms on the right must be evaluated at the previous iterate x₂.", "Require f′(x₂)≠0 and a valid function-domain point.", "Select (c)."], "solution": "Use the recurrence xᵢ₊₁=xᵢ−f(xᵢ)/f′(xᵢ).\nSet i=2 for the third iterate.\nThen x₃=x₂−f(x₂)/f′(x₂).\nAll terms on the right must be evaluated at the previous iterate x₂.\nRequire f′(x₂)≠0 and a valid function-domain point.\nSelect (c).", "answer": "(c) x₂−f(x₂)/f′(x₂).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}, {"id": "review-12-v", "qNo": "v", "question": "If f(a),f(c) have opposite signs, the root lies in: (a) [a,b]; (b) [a,c]; (c) (a,b); (d) (a,c).", "steps": ["A sign change alone is not enough; assume f is continuous on [a,c], as required by the chapter method.", "The intermediate value theorem gives a root between a and c.", "Because opposite signs imply neither endpoint is zero, that root is strictly inside (a,c).", "The standard retained closed bracket for bisection/regula-falsi is [a,c], choice (b).", "Choice (d) is also literally true under these assumptions. Thus the wording does not make a unique mathematical location choice.", "Statements about [a,b] or (a,b) additionally depend on where b lies; for a<c<b those larger intervals contain the same root too. Keep the method’s intended smaller closed bracket distinct from exclusive truth."], "solution": "A sign change alone is not enough; assume f is continuous on [a,c], as required by the chapter method.\nThe intermediate value theorem gives a root between a and c.\nBecause opposite signs imply neither endpoint is zero, that root is strictly inside (a,c).\nThe standard retained closed bracket for bisection/regula-falsi is [a,c], choice (b).\nChoice (d) is also literally true under these assumptions. Thus the wording does not make a unique mathematical location choice.\nStatements about [a,b] or (a,b) additionally depend on where b lies; for a<c<b those larger intervals contain the same root too. Keep the method’s intended smaller closed bracket distinct from exclusive truth.", "answer": "Intended retained bracket (b) [a,c]; (d) also contains the interior root if f is continuous.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}, {"id": "review-12-vi", "qNo": "vi", "question": "Newton-Raphson fails at: (a) stationary point; (b) floating point; (c) continuous point; (d) non-of these.", "steps": ["The update divides by f′(xᵢ).", "At a stationary point, f′=0.", "If f is nonzero there, no finite Newton update is defined.", "If f is already zero, the root has been found and the division should not be attempted.", "Thus the intended failure case is a nonroot stationary point.", "Select (a), with this qualification rather than treating every stationary root as a failure."], "solution": "The update divides by f′(xᵢ).\nAt a stationary point, f′=0.\nIf f is nonzero there, no finite Newton update is defined.\nIf f is already zero, the root has been found and the division should not be attempted.\nThus the intended failure case is a nonroot stationary point.\nSelect (a), with this qualification rather than treating every stationary root as a failure.", "answer": "(a) nonroot stationary point, where f′=0.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}, {"id": "review-12-vii", "qNo": "vii", "question": "Positive root of 3t−cos t−1 using regula-falsi to three decimals: (a) 0.507; (b) 0.670; (c) 0.570; (d) 0.607.", "steps": ["Set f(t)=3t−cos t−1; f(0)=−2,f(1)=2−cos1>0.", "The function is continuous and f′=3+sin t≥2, so the bracketed root is unique.", "Apply chord intersections c=a−(a−b)f(a)/(f(a)−f(b)) and keep opposite-sign endpoints.", "Iterations converge to t≈0.6071016481.", "Substitution leaves a residual near zero; rounding to three decimal places gives 0.607.", "Select (d)."], "solution": "Set f(t)=3t−cos t−1; f(0)=−2,f(1)=2−cos1>0.\nThe function is continuous and f′=3+sin t≥2, so the bracketed root is unique.\nApply chord intersections c=a−(a−b)f(a)/(f(a)−f(b)) and keep opposite-sign endpoints.\nIterations converge to t≈0.6071016481.\nSubstitution leaves a residual near zero; rounding to three decimal places gives 0.607.\nSelect (d).", "answer": "(d) 0.607.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}, {"id": "review-12-viii", "qNo": "viii", "question": "MAPLE command to solve x²−5x+3=0 is: (a) solve(x²−5x+3)=0; (b) fsolve(x²−5x+3=0); (c) Simplon(x²−5x+3=0); (d) Psolve(x²−5x+3=0).", "steps": ["The chapter’s numerical root command is fsolve.", "The equation must be an argument of that command.", "Option (b) has fsolve(x²−5x+3=0).", "The printed (a) places =0 outside the solve call rather than inside its equation argument.", "For a numerical solution the intended choice is (b); no command execution is asserted here.", "The exact roots (5±√13)/2 independently show there are two real polynomial roots."], "solution": "The chapter’s numerical root command is fsolve.\nThe equation must be an argument of that command.\nOption (b) has fsolve(x²−5x+3=0).\nThe printed (a) places =0 outside the solve call rather than inside its equation argument.\nFor a numerical solution the intended choice is (b); no command execution is asserted here.\nThe exact roots (5±√13)/2 independently show there are two real polynomial roots.", "answer": "(b) fsolve(x²−5x+3=0).", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}, {"id": "review-12-ix", "qNo": "ix", "question": "For x−sin x−1/2=0, bisection between 1 and 2 gives: (a) 1.88; (b) 1.48; (c) 1.49; (d) 1.897.", "steps": ["Set f(x)=x−sin x−1/2, with radians.", "f(1)<0 and f(2)>0, so continuity gives a bracketed root.", "On [1,2], f′=1−cos x>0, so the root is unique.", "Repeated bisection gives x≈1.4973003891.", "Among the offered coarse numbers, 1.49 is closest, choice (c).", "It is not the correctly rounded two-decimal value, which is 1.50. The source specifies neither an iteration count nor a tolerance, so the distinction between its closest offered approximation and proper rounding is retained."], "solution": "Set f(x)=x−sin x−1/2, with radians.\nf(1)<0 and f(2)>0, so continuity gives a bracketed root.\nOn [1,2], f′=1−cos x>0, so the root is unique.\nRepeated bisection gives x≈1.4973003891.\nAmong the offered coarse numbers, 1.49 is closest, choice (c).\nIt is not the correctly rounded two-decimal value, which is 1.50. The source specifies neither an iteration count nor a tolerance, so the distinction between its closest offered approximation and proper rounding is retained.", "answer": "Closest offered (c) 1.49; computed root ≈1.4973003891, rounded to two decimals 1.50.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}, {"id": "review-12-x", "qNo": "x", "question": "Bisection is also known as: (a) Newton method; (b) Binary Chopping method; (c) Quaternary method; (d) none of these.", "steps": ["Bisection halves the current bracket at each update.", "It retains one of two halves based on signs.", "This binary halving motivates Binary Chopping.", "It is distinct from Newton tangent iteration.", "The printed choice (b) supplies the corresponding name.", "Select (b)."], "solution": "Bisection halves the current bracket at each update.\nIt retains one of two halves based on signs.\nThis binary halving motivates Binary Chopping.\nIt is distinct from Newton tangent iteration.\nThe printed choice (b) supplies the corresponding name.\nSelect (b).", "answer": "(b) Binary Chopping method.", "progressiveSolution": true, "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning enhancement", "sourceReferences": [{"page": 324, "jpg": "166_page_2.jpg", "verification": "All source choices and wording anomalies checked visually"}]}];
